window.GUIDE_SEARCH = [{"type":"page","part":null,"title":"Introduction","kicker":"Book","text":"Why this book \u00b7 3 proofs \u00b7 How to use it \u00b7 A word on tools \u00b7 Sources","href":"/book/en/introduction/"},{"type":"section","part":null,"title":"Why this book","kicker":"Introduction","text":"You are probably reading this because you attended PROOF in Vilnius on 27 September 2026, or because someone shared a link with you. A conference lasts an afternoon. You listen to good talks, you agree with most of what is said, you promise yourself to change a few things, and on Monday morning ordinary life resumes. Habits last for years: the messenger you use, the way you handle passwords, the place where you keep your savings. They decide how exposed or how free you are far more than any talk does. This book connects the afternoon and the habits. It takes the ideas from the stage and turns them into things you can set up on your own phone and your own computer, with clear steps and without a technical background. Reading a chapter takes about 10 minutes. Acting on it takes longer, and the book says how much. Every chapter contains actions of 5 minutes, most then ask for 1 to 3 hours that you can spread over several evenings, and a few include a project for a weekend. Nothing here asks you to change your whole life. You change a single habit and then, when you are ready, another.","href":"/book/en/introduction/#why-this-book"},{"type":"section","part":null,"title":"3 proofs","kicker":"Introduction","text":"The 3 parts are named after the 3 themes of the conference. Proof of Privacy is about protecting your messages, your passwords, your browser, your email and your network connection. It comes first because the other 2 parts rest on it. Proof of Autonomy is about depending less on gatekeepers, the few large companies that can close your account, change the rules overnight or lose your data. Its chapters cover keys instead of accounts, Nostr, replacing Big Tech services one at a time, and hosting a few things yourself. Proof of Work is about protecting the fruit of your work, your income and your savings. Money tends to lose value over time, sometimes fast: in October 2022, annual inflation reached 10.6% in the euro area and 22.1% in Lithuania, according to Eurostat. Most of what you own is also held, watched and sometimes frozen by someone else. You will compare the places where value can live (bank accounts, shares, gold, Bitcoin and, for advanced users, Monero), then learn to use Bitcoin, a tool in that list that you can hold and verify by yourself, provided you keep your own keys. All 3 parts ask who holds the keys, because in practice that person is in control.","href":"/book/en/introduction/#3-proofs"},{"type":"section","part":null,"title":"How to use it","kicker":"Introduction","text":"Chapters are independent, so you can read them in any order, and skip what you already do. Each part opens with a short essay, and every chapter then follows the same path: the chapter in brief, what goes wrong today, the idea behind the solution, a choice of tools, a step-by-step tutorial, mistakes to avoid, resources to go further and the sources of the facts quoted. Each chapter ends with a checklist of 5 actions, and all of them feed the global checklist at the end of the book, where you see everything on a single page. Every action carries an effort tag, (5 min), (1 hour) or (weekend), so you can do the 5-minute ones today and keep the weekend projects for later. The book is designed to work offline once it has loaded. There is no account to create, and the pages load no third-party script and no tracker. Like any website, the server that hosts them still sees your IP address when they load. Your progress is stored only in your own browser, so it starts again from zero if you clear your browser data or switch to another device, because nobody else keeps a copy of what you have ticked.","href":"/book/en/introduction/#how-to-use-it"},{"type":"section","part":null,"title":"A word on tools","kicker":"Introduction","text":"For every need, this book lists several tools, never a single one, and none of them paid to be here. Tools change. Apps get bought, abandoned or slowly made worse: Skype, launched in 2003 and bought by Microsoft in 2011, was retired on 5 May 2025 and its users were sent to Teams. A recommendation that is right in September 2026, when these pages were checked, may be wrong in 3 years. Principles move more slowly, and 3 of them guide every choice in this book: Open source. The code is public, so claims can be checked by people who do not work for the company. No single point of failure. Prefer tools that do not depend on a single company, server or country. You hold the keys. If someone else can lock you out, it is not really yours. 1 question ties these principles together and runs through every chapter: \"who is the trusted third party, and can you check?\" Encryption, open source or a respected brand do not settle it, because a setup is only as decentralised as the trust it rests on, whether that trust sits with a company's directory, a server, an app store or a hardware maker. A well-run third party is often a good choice, especially at the beginning, and the chapters show where the trust sits so that the choice stays yours. Bitcoiners have a saying: \"Don't trust, verify.\" It applies to this book too. Every dated fact links to its source, so compare what you read here with other sources, test things with small steps, and if you find a mistake, report it to the PROOF team.","href":"/book/en/introduction/#a-word-on-tools"},{"type":"section","part":null,"title":"Sources","kicker":"Introduction","text":"Annual inflation up to 10.6% in the euro area (October 2022), Eurostat, 17 November 2022. Skype is retiring in May 2025: what you need to know, Microsoft Support, 2025.","href":"/book/en/introduction/#sources"},{"type":"page","part":1,"title":"Proof of Privacy","kicker":"Part 1 \u00b7 Privacy \u00b7 Opening essay","text":"Why privacy matters \u00b7 Sources","href":"/book/en/privacy/"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"\"I have nothing to hide\"","kicker":"Part 1 opening \u00b7 Why privacy matters","text":"Nobody considers it suspicious to close the bathroom door, to seal an envelope or to keep a salary and a medical file out of public view, and yet the same people often say they have nothing to hide online. Privacy is not secrecy. It is the power to choose what you share, with whom, and when, which is the normal boundary between you and the rest of the world. When that choice disappears, behaviour tends to change. After the Snowden revelations of June 2013, the researcher Jon Penney measured a statistically significant drop in visits to Wikipedia articles on terrorism-related topics, a result published in the Berkeley Technology Law Journal in 2016. Nobody had forbidden those pages. Readers who felt observed opened them less, and the same reflex can hold back people who would like to disagree, to report abuse or simply to be different. \"Nothing to hide\" also rests on 2 assumptions, that the rules will never change and that whoever holds your data will always be well-meaning, competent and never hacked. The history of this region, within living memory, gives reasons to doubt the first. The Finnish case described below gives reasons to doubt the second.","href":"/book/en/privacy/#i-have-nothing-to-hide"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Metadata says more than the words","kicker":"Part 1 opening \u00b7 Why privacy matters","text":"Almost everything you do online leaves a trace, from your searches and your location to the people you write to and the time you do it. Even when the content of a message is encrypted, the metadata usually is not, meaning who talks to whom, how often and from where. A call to an oncologist, then to a lawyer, then to an insurance company tells a story without a single sentence being read. General Michael Hayden, a former director of both the NSA and the CIA, said it in a public debate at Johns Hopkins University in 2014: \"We kill people based on metadata.\" His opponent that day, the law professor David Cole, reported the sentence in the New York Review of Books. These traces do not stay where they were created. Data brokers combine them into profiles sold to advertisers, insurers or political campaigns, and part of what is stored ends up leaking. In Finland, tens of thousands of therapy records were stolen from the psychotherapy chain Vastaamo, and from October 2020 some 22,000 patients reported emails demanding 500 euros to keep their session notes unpublished. A leaked password can be changed, whereas a date of birth, a home address, a face or a medical history cannot.","href":"/book/en/privacy/#metadata-says-more-than-the-words"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"5 habits remove a large part of the exposure","kicker":"Part 1 opening \u00b7 Why privacy matters","text":"You do not need to disappear or to become an expert, because privacy is not all or nothing and every step reduces what is exposed. Start by asking who you realistically want to protect yourself from, whether advertisers and data brokers, scammers, an ex-partner, an employer or a state, since the answer decides how far you need to go. For most people the first 2 come first, and a handful of habits removes a large part of the exposure: a private messenger, unique passwords and a second factor, a browser that blocks tracking, email aliases, a protected network connection. These are the 5 chapters of this part. They are independent, so start with the one that looks easiest rather than the one that looks most important. Each of them ends with a checklist of 5 actions, and in every chapter at least 1 of those actions takes 5 minutes.","href":"/book/en/privacy/#5-habits-remove-a-large-part-of-the-exposure"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Why privacy matters","kicker":"Part 1 opening","text":"\"I have nothing to hide\" Nobody considers it suspicious to close the bathroom door, to seal an envelope or to keep a salary and a medical file out of public view, and yet the same people often say they have nothing to hide online. Privacy is not secrecy. It is the power to choose what you share, with whom, and when, which is the normal boundary between you and the rest of the world. When that choice disappears, behaviour tends to change. After the Snowden revelations of June 2013, the researcher Jon Penney measured a statistically significant drop in visits to Wikipedia articles on terrorism-related topics, a result published in the Berkeley Technology Law Journal in 2016. Nobody had forbidden those pages. Readers who felt observed opened them less, and the same reflex can hold back people who would like to disagree, to report abuse or simply to be different. \"Nothing to hide\" also rests on 2 assumptions, that the rules will never change and that whoever holds your data will always be well-meaning, competent and never hacked. The history of this region, within living memory, gives reasons to doubt the first. The Finnish case described below gives reasons to doubt the second. Metadata says more than the words Almost everything you do online leaves a trace, from your searches and your location to the people you write to and the time you do it. Even when the content of a message is encrypted, the metadata usually is not, meaning who talks to whom, how often and from where. A call to an oncologist, then to a lawyer, then to an insurance company tells a story without a single sentence being read. General Michael Hayden, a former director of both the NSA and the CIA, said it in a public debate at Johns Hopkins University in 2014: \"We kill people based on metadata.\" His opponent that day, the law professor David Cole, reported the sentence in the New York Review of Books. These traces do not stay where they were created. Data brokers combine them into profiles sold to advertisers, insurers or political campaigns, and part of what is stored ends up leaking. In Finland, tens of thousands of therapy records were stolen from the psychotherapy chain Vastaamo, and from October 2020 some 22,000 patients reported emails demanding 500 euros to keep their session notes unpublished. A leaked password can be changed, whereas a date of birth, a home address, a face or a medical history cannot. 5 habits remove a large part of the exposure You do not need to disappear or to become an expert, because privacy is not all or nothing and every step reduces what is exposed. Start by asking who you realistically want to protect yourself from, whether advertisers and data brokers, scammers, an ex-partner, an employer or a state, since the answer decides how far you need to go. For most people the first 2 come first, and a handful of habits removes a large part of the exposure: a private messenger, unique passwords and a second factor, a browser that blocks tracking, email aliases, a protected network connection. These are the 5 chapters of this part. They are independent, so start with the one that looks easiest rather than the one that looks most important. Each of them ends with a checklist of 5 actions, and in every chapter at least 1 of those actions takes 5 minutes.","href":"/book/en/privacy/#why-privacy-matters"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Sources","kicker":"Part 1 opening","text":"Chilling Effects: Online Surveillance and Wikipedia Use, Jonathon W. Penney, Berkeley Technology Law Journal, 2016. \"We Kill People Based on Metadata\", David Cole, The New York Review of Books, 10 May 2014. Man Who Mass-Extorted Psychotherapy Patients Gets Six Years, Krebs on Security, 30 April 2024.","href":"/book/en/privacy/#sources"},{"type":"chapter","part":1,"title":"Your messages","kicker":"Chapter 1 \u00b7 Privacy","text":"In brief \u00b7 Encrypted does not mean private \u00b7 A sealed envelope the carrier cannot open \u00b7 Confidential does not mean authentic \u00b7 Choose your messenger \u00b7 Step by step \u00b7 Mistakes to avoid \u00b7 Go further \u00b7 Sources \u00b7 Your checklist","href":"/book/en/messages/"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"In brief","kicker":"Ch. 1 \u00b7 In brief","text":"Your conversations are among the most intimate data you produce. Move them to a messenger that is end-to-end encrypted by default, that is open source, and that collects as little metadata as possible. Several good ones exist, and none wins on every point. Pick one, move your 5 closest contacts first, and keep your old app for everyone else.","href":"/book/en/messages/#in-brief"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Encrypted does not mean private","kicker":"Ch. 1 \u00b7 Understand","text":"Many everyday chat apps belong to advertising companies. WhatsApp and Messenger, both owned by Meta, do encrypt the content of your messages, but they still record who you talk to, when, how often, from which device and which location, and they connect it to the rest of what the group knows about you. Others do not encrypt by default at all. On Telegram, ordinary chats and all group chats are stored in a form the company can read. SMS is weaker still, because your operator can read it and it crosses the telephone network unencrypted. After the group known as Salt Typhoon broke into several American telecom operators, the US cybersecurity agency CISA advised in December 2024 that highly targeted people use only end-to-end encrypted messaging and stop relying on SMS for login codes. Then come the backups. A conversation can be encrypted in transit and still end up readable in a cloud backup, yours or that of the person you are talking to. The consequences are concrete: leaked conversations, accounts taken over by scammers who then write to your family, and messages handed over on a legal request, years after you forgot you wrote them.","href":"/book/en/messages/#encrypted-does-not-mean-private"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"A sealed envelope the carrier cannot open","kicker":"Ch. 1 \u00b7 Understand","text":"With end-to-end encryption, a message is locked on your phone and can only be unlocked on the phone of the person you write to. The company in the middle carries a sealed envelope it cannot open, so if it is hacked or receives a legal request, it has no message content to give. Depending on how the app was designed, it may still hold metadata, such as an IP address, a contact list or the date of your last connection. That envelope is also a political subject in Europe. The regulation that the European Commission proposed on 11 May 2022, known to its critics as \"Chat Control\", would allow authorities to order messaging services to scan private communications. As of September 2026 it is still being negotiated between the Parliament and the Council, and the round of 29 June 2026 ended without agreement. The temporary rule that lets providers scan voluntarily was reinstated on 23 July 2026 until 3 April 2028, and at the Parliament's request it excludes end-to-end encrypted communications. Encryption is necessary, but 3 more things matter: Open source. The code is public, so independent experts can check that the app does what it claims, instead of taking the company's word for it. Metadata. The most careful messengers are designed to know very little about you, which can mean no contact list, no group membership and sometimes no identifier at all. Who runs it. A non-profit or a community has no advertising business to feed, and a network without a central company has no single door to knock on.","href":"/book/en/messages/#a-sealed-envelope-the-carrier-cannot-open"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Confidential does not mean authentic","kicker":"Ch. 1 \u00b7 Understand","text":"Encryption answers 1 question, whether someone else can read the message, and leaves another open, which is whether the person at the other end is really who you think. Cryptographers call the first property confidentiality and the second authenticity. An SMS or an email offers neither, a familiar voice on a phone call can help recognise someone without making the call confidential, but is not proof of identity, since voices can be cloned, and a secure channel has to offer both. Authenticity depends on how your app obtained the other person's key. In Signal, WhatsApp, iMessage and most encrypted messengers, that key comes from a directory run by the company, which makes the company a trusted third party, however well it encrypts. If the directory were compromised, or compelled to hand out a false key, the envelope would still be sealed, but for the wrong reader. Safeguards exist, with safety numbers in Signal (60 digits to compare), key transparency in WhatsApp and contact key verification in iMessage, but they are optional and rarely used. Other designs remove the directory and decentralise that trust. Olvid requires 2 codes of 4 digits to be exchanged before the first message, while SimpleX and Briar start from a link or a QR code that you hand over yourself. Security then rests on the 2 people involved, and the price is convenience, since the app cannot find your contacts for you.","href":"/book/en/messages/#confidential-does-not-mean-authentic"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"WhatsApp","kicker":"Ch. 1 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your messenger\u201d","text":"Owner: Meta \u00b7 End-to-end encrypted?: Yes, by default (Signal protocol) \u00b7 What it still collects or exposes: Extensive metadata: your contacts, who you write to, when, from which device. Closed source. Cloud backups are only end-to-end encrypted if you turn it on. \u00b7 Verdict: Content protected, metadata feeds an advertising group.","href":"/book/en/messages/#row-whatsapp"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"Facebook Messenger","kicker":"Ch. 1 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your messenger\u201d","text":"Owner: Meta \u00b7 End-to-end encrypted?: Yes, by default for personal chats and calls since December 2023. Not for chats with businesses or Marketplace. \u00b7 What it still collects or exposes: The same metadata, tied to your Facebook identity. Closed source. \u00b7 Verdict: Better than before, still a Meta product.","href":"/book/en/messages/#row-facebook-messenger"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"Instagram direct messages","kicker":"Ch. 1 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your messenger\u201d","text":"Owner: Meta \u00b7 End-to-end encrypted?: No. The optional encrypted chats were removed on 8 May 2026. \u00b7 What it still collects or exposes: Meta can read the content of every message, plus the metadata. \u00b7 Verdict: Not for anything private.","href":"/book/en/messages/#row-instagram-direct-messages"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"Telegram","kicker":"Ch. 1 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your messenger\u201d","text":"Owner: Telegram, a private company \u00b7 End-to-end encrypted?: Only in \"secret chats\", which you start by hand, between 2 people. Ordinary chats, all groups and channels are not. \u00b7 What it still collects or exposes: Ordinary messages are stored on Telegram's servers, which the company can read. The server code is closed. \u00b7 Verdict: A social network more than a private messenger.","href":"/book/en/messages/#row-telegram"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"SMS","kicker":"Ch. 1 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your messenger\u201d","text":"Owner: Your mobile operator \u00b7 End-to-end encrypted?: No \u00b7 What it still collects or exposes: Content and metadata are readable by operators, can be intercepted, and can be redirected by a SIM swap (a scammer moving your number to their SIM card). \u00b7 Verdict: Avoid for anything sensitive, including login codes when you have the choice.","href":"/book/en/messages/#row-sms"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"iMessage (Apple Messages)","kicker":"Ch. 1 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your messenger\u201d","text":"Owner: Apple \u00b7 End-to-end encrypted?: Yes, between Apple devices (blue bubbles) \u00b7 What it still collects or exposes: Apple devices only. Closed source. iCloud backups are readable by Apple unless you turn on Advanced Data Protection. \u00b7 Verdict: Good inside the Apple world, and nowhere else.","href":"/book/en/messages/#row-imessage-apple-messages"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"Google Messages (RCS)","kicker":"Ch. 1 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your messenger\u201d","text":"Owner: Google, with the mobile operators \u00b7 End-to-end encrypted?: Yes, by default between Google Messages users. With iPhones, encryption started rolling out in May 2026, in beta, and depends on your operator. \u00b7 What it still collects or exposes: Tied to your phone number. Metadata is still collected. Closed source. Falls back to plain SMS when RCS is not available. \u00b7 Verdict: Better than SMS. Check for the lock icon.","href":"/book/en/messages/#row-google-messages-rcs"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"Signal","kicker":"Ch. 1 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your messenger\u201d","text":"Needs a phone number: Yes (can be hidden from others) \u00b7 Who runs the network: A single non-profit foundation \u00b7 Who vouches for the keys: A directory run by Signal. Optional check with a 60-digit safety number. \u00b7 Best for: Replacing WhatsApp with family and friends. The easiest switch.","href":"/book/en/messages/#row-signal"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"SimpleX Chat","kicker":"Ch. 1 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your messenger\u201d","text":"Needs a phone number: No identifier at all \u00b7 Who runs the network: Relays anyone can run \u00b7 Who vouches for the keys: Nobody. You hand over a link or a QR code yourself, with an optional security code to compare. \u00b7 Best for: Strong metadata protection. No account, no username, no identifier to link.","href":"/book/en/messages/#row-simplex-chat"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"Session","kicker":"Ch. 1 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your messenger\u201d","text":"Needs a phone number: No \u00b7 Who runs the network: A decentralised network of community-run nodes \u00b7 Who vouches for the keys: No directory, since your Session ID is your public key. You must obtain that ID through a channel you trust. \u00b7 Best for: Anonymous accounts, messages routed through several nodes. Slower, fewer features. Its current protocol lacks forward secrecy (a key stolen one day could unlock older messages). An upgrade has been announced.","href":"/book/en/messages/#row-session"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"Threema","kicker":"Ch. 1 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your messenger\u201d","text":"Needs a phone number: No \u00b7 Who runs the network: A single Swiss company, paid app \u00b7 Who vouches for the keys: A directory run by Threema. Scanning a contact's QR code in person raises them to the highest trust level. \u00b7 Best for: People who want no phone number and a polished, paid product.","href":"/book/en/messages/#row-threema"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"Olvid","kicker":"Ch. 1 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your messenger\u201d","text":"Needs a phone number: No, and no email or other personal data \u00b7 Who runs the network: A single French company, with servers hosted on Amazon Web Services \u00b7 Who vouches for the keys: Nobody. The 2 people exchange 2 codes of 4 digits before the first message, and this step is mandatory. \u00b7 Best for: A European app whose security is certified by ANSSI, the French cybersecurity agency. Messages and receiving calls are free. Making calls and using several devices need a subscription (about 5 euros a month).","href":"/book/en/messages/#row-olvid"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"Element (Matrix)","kicker":"Ch. 1 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your messenger\u201d","text":"Needs a phone number: No \u00b7 Who runs the network: Federated: many servers, or your own \u00b7 Who vouches for the keys: Your server's directory. Optional verification by comparing emoji or scanning a QR code. \u00b7 Best for: Communities, teams, people who want to host their own server. The servers involved see metadata (who is in which room, and when), so the choice of server matters.","href":"/book/en/messages/#row-element-matrix"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"Briar","kicker":"Ch. 1 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your messenger\u201d","text":"Needs a phone number: No \u00b7 Who runs the network: Nobody: device to device \u00b7 Who vouches for the keys: Nobody. Contacts are added by QR code in person, or by exchanging links. \u00b7 Best for: Extreme situations. Works over Tor, and even over Bluetooth or wifi without internet. Android, plus a desktop version in beta (Windows, macOS, Linux). No iPhone app.","href":"/book/en/messages/#row-briar"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Choose your messenger","kicker":"Ch. 1 \u00b7 Compare","text":"Start with an honest look at what you probably use today. None of these apps is useless, but each has a clear weak point. The table reflects the situation in September 2026. Messenger Owner End-to-end encrypted? What it still collects or exposes Verdict WhatsApp Meta Yes, by default (Signal protocol) Extensive metadata: your contacts, who you write to, when, from which device. Closed source. Cloud backups are only end-to-end encrypted if you turn it on. Content protected, metadata feeds an advertising group. Facebook Messenger Meta Yes, by default for personal chats and calls since December 2023. Not for chats with businesses or Marketplace. The same metadata, tied to your Facebook identity. Closed source. Better than before, still a Meta product. Instagram direct messages Meta No. The optional encrypted chats were removed on 8 May 2026. Meta can read the content of every message, plus the metadata. Not for anything private. Telegram Telegram, a private company Only in \"secret chats\", which you start by hand, between 2 people. Ordinary chats, all groups and channels are not. Ordinary messages are stored on Telegram's servers, which the company can read. The server code is closed. A social network more than a private messenger. SMS Your mobile operator No Content and metadata are readable by operators, can be intercepted, and can be redirected by a SIM swap (a scammer moving your number to their SIM card). Avoid for anything sensitive, including login codes when you have the choice. iMessage (Apple Messages) Apple Yes, between Apple devices (blue bubbles) Apple devices only. Closed source. iCloud backups are readable by Apple unless you turn on Advanced Data Protection. Good inside the Apple world, and nowhere else. Google Messages (RCS) Google, with the mobile operators Yes, by default between Google Messages users. With iPhones, encryption started rolling out in May 2026, in beta, and depends on your operator. Tied to your phone number. Metadata is still collected. Closed source. Falls back to plain SMS when RCS is not available. Better than SMS. Check for the lock icon. Here are better options. There is no single right answer, which is healthy, because a world where everyone depends on a single app is fragile. All 7 encrypt your private conversations end to end by default, and their apps are open source. Messenger Needs a phone number Who runs the network Who vouches for the keys Best for Signal Yes (can be hidden from others) A single non-profit foundation A directory run by Signal. Optional check with a 60-digit safety number. Replacing WhatsApp with family and friends. The easiest switch. SimpleX Chat No identifier at all Relays anyone can run Nobody. You hand over a link or a QR code yourself, with an optional security code to compare. Strong metadata protection. No account, no username, no identifier to link. Session No A decentralised network of community-run nodes No directory, since your Session ID is your public key. You must obtain that ID through a channel you trust. Anonymous accounts, messages routed through several nodes. Slower, fewer features. Its current protocol lacks forward secrecy (a key stolen one day could unlock older messages). An upgrade has been announced. Threema No A single Swiss company, paid app A directory run by Threema. Scanning a contact's QR code in person raises them to the highest trust level. People who want no phone number and a polished, paid product. Olvid No, and no email or other personal data A single French company, with servers hosted on Amazon Web Services Nobody. The 2 people exchange 2 codes of 4 digits before the first message, and this step is mandatory. A European app whose security is certified by ANSSI, the French cybersecurity agency. Messages and receiving calls are free. Making calls and using several devices need a subscription (about 5 euros a month). Element (Matrix) No Federated: many servers, or your own Your server's directory. Optional verification by comparing emoji or scanning a QR code. Communities, teams, people who want to host their own server. The servers involved see metadata (who is in which room, and when), so the choice of server matters. Briar No Nobody: device to device Nobody. Contacts are added by QR code in person, or by exchanging links. Extreme situations. Works over Tor, and even over Bluetooth or wifi without internet. Android, plus a desktop version in beta (Windows, macOS, Linux). No iPhone app. A reasonable setup for most people is Signal for everyday life, plus a messenger without a phone number (SimpleX, Session or Olvid, for example) for contacts you do not want linked to your identity. If part of your circle stays on WhatsApp, help them at least turn on encrypted backups (Settings, Chats, Chat backup, End-to-end encrypted backup). WhatsApp explains it in its help centre, under About end-to-end encrypted backup.","href":"/book/en/messages/#choose-your-messenger"},{"type":"step","part":1,"title":"Step 1: Install it","kicker":"Ch. 1 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Install it from the official store or from the project website, and register.","href":"/book/en/messages/#step-1"},{"type":"step","part":1,"title":"Step 2: Lock your account","kicker":"Ch. 1 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Lock your account. In Settings, Account, set a PIN and turn on Registration Lock, so that re-registering your number requires that PIN.","href":"/book/en/messages/#step-2"},{"type":"step","part":1,"title":"Step 3: Hide your number","kicker":"Ch. 1 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Hide your number. Create a username, then in Settings, Privacy, Phone Number set \"Who can see my number\" to Nobody. You can now give your username instead of your number. The separate setting \"Who can find me by number\" decides whether someone who already has your number can find you on Signal. Set it to Nobody too if you prefer that, knowing that friends will then need your username.","href":"/book/en/messages/#step-3"},{"type":"step","part":1,"title":"Step 4: Make messages disappear by default","kicker":"Ch. 1 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Make messages disappear by default. In Settings, Privacy, Disappearing messages, choose a default timer, for example 4 weeks. This reduces the history left on your phone. It cannot remove screenshots, exports or copies made by the recipient.","href":"/book/en/messages/#step-4"},{"type":"step","part":1,"title":"Step 5: Lock the app","kicker":"Ch. 1 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Lock the app with Settings, Privacy, Screen lock.","href":"/book/en/messages/#step-5"},{"type":"step","part":1,"title":"Step 6: Verify your important contacts","kicker":"Ch. 1 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Verify your important contacts. Open the conversation, tap the name, then View safety number, and compare it in person or by another channel you already trust, such as calling a number you already know is theirs. A familiar voice alone is not enough. This is the step that gives you authenticity and stops you depending on Signal's directory, so do it once with the few people who matter most.","href":"/book/en/messages/#step-6"},{"type":"step","part":1,"title":"Step 7: Move your people","kicker":"Ch. 1 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Move your people without announcing a revolution. Write to your 5 closest contacts from the new app, create the family group there, and let the old app fade out.","href":"/book/en/messages/#step-7"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Step by step","kicker":"Ch. 1 \u00b7 Tutorial","text":"The example uses Signal because it is the most common first step, and the same logic applies to the others. Menu names are those of September 2026. Install it from the official store or from the project website, and register. Lock your account. In Settings, Account, set a PIN and turn on Registration Lock, so that re-registering your number requires that PIN. Hide your number. Create a username, then in Settings, Privacy, Phone Number set \"Who can see my number\" to Nobody. You can now give your username instead of your number. The separate setting \"Who can find me by number\" decides whether someone who already has your number can find you on Signal. Set it to Nobody too if you prefer that, knowing that friends will then need your username. Make messages disappear by default. In Settings, Privacy, Disappearing messages, choose a default timer, for example 4 weeks. This reduces the history left on your phone. It cannot remove screenshots, exports or copies made by the recipient. Lock the app with Settings, Privacy, Screen lock. Verify your important contacts. Open the conversation, tap the name, then View safety number, and compare it in person or by another channel you already trust, such as calling a number you already know is theirs. A familiar voice alone is not enough. This is the step that gives you authenticity and stops you depending on Signal's directory, so do it once with the few people who matter most. Move your people without announcing a revolution. Write to your 5 closest contacts from the new app, create the family group there, and let the old app fade out.","href":"/book/en/messages/#step-by-step"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Mistakes to avoid","kicker":"Ch. 1 \u00b7 Watch out","text":"Thinking \"encrypted\" means \"private\". Encryption protects the content, but it does not hide who you talk to unless the messenger was designed for that. Forgetting the phone itself. Anyone holding your unlocked phone can read everything, which makes a strong screen lock part of your messenger's security. Leaving cloud backups on. An unencrypted backup keeps a readable copy of conversations that were encrypted in transit. Check your settings, and remember that the other person's backup matters too. Waiting for everyone to move. Part of your circle will stay where it is, and 2 apps side by side is a perfectly fine result. Confusing privacy with anonymity. A messenger tied to your phone number protects what you say, not who you are. If you need to stay unidentified, use a messenger without a phone number. Trusting the app more than the person. Screenshots exist, and the weakest point of a conversation is often the other end.","href":"/book/en/messages/#mistakes-to-avoid"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Go further","kicker":"Ch. 1 \u00b7 Go further","text":"Compare messengers in detail: Privacy Guides, \"Real-Time Communication\" (privacyguides.org). Understand your own risks: Surveillance Self-Defense, by the Electronic Frontier Foundation (ssd.eff.org). Next chapter: your passwords and your accounts.","href":"/book/en/messages/#go-further"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Sources","kicker":"Ch. 1 \u00b7 References","text":"Set and manage disappearing messages, Signal Support, checked 22 October 2026. Scammers use fake emergencies to steal your money, Federal Trade Commission, checked 22 October 2026. Proposal for a regulation laying down rules to prevent and combat child sexual abuse, COM(2022) 209, European Commission, 11 May 2022. Legislative train: combating child sexual abuse online, European Parliament, updated 1 August 2026. Combating child sexual abuse online: support for more limited ePrivacy derogation, European Parliament, 9 July 2026. Fighting child sexual abuse online: interim measure protecting children now reinstated, Council of the EU, 23 July 2026. Mobile Communications Best Practice Guidance, CISA, 18 December 2024. What is a safety number and why do I see that it changed?, Signal Support, consulted in September 2026. Deploying key transparency at WhatsApp, Engineering at Meta, 13 April 2023. Advancing iMessage security: iMessage Contact Key Verification, Apple Security Research, 2023. Olvid secure channels, Olvid, consulted in September 2026.","href":"/book/en/messages/#sources"},{"type":"action","part":1,"title":"Install a private messenger, lock it with a PIN, hide your number and set disappearing messages","kicker":"Ch. 1 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/messages/#action-messages-1"},{"type":"action","part":1,"title":"Move your 5 closest contacts and your family group","kicker":"Ch. 1 \u00b7 Checklist action, 1 hour","text":"","href":"/book/en/messages/#action-messages-2"},{"type":"action","part":1,"title":"Verify the safety number of your 3 most important contacts","kicker":"Ch. 1 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/messages/#action-messages-3"},{"type":"action","part":1,"title":"Install a second messenger that needs no phone number","kicker":"Ch. 1 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/messages/#action-messages-4"},{"type":"action","part":1,"title":"Turn on encrypted backups in the apps you keep","kicker":"Ch. 1 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/messages/#action-messages-5"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Your checklist","kicker":"Ch. 1 \u00b7 Act","text":"Install a private messenger, lock it with a PIN, hide your number and set disappearing messagesMove your 5 closest contacts and your family groupVerify the safety number of your 3 most important contactsInstall a second messenger that needs no phone numberTurn on encrypted backups in the apps you keep","href":"/book/en/messages/#your-checklist"},{"type":"chapter","part":1,"title":"Your passwords and your accounts","kicker":"Chapter 2 \u00b7 Privacy","text":"In brief \u00b7 A leaked password opens many doors \u00b7 A single passphrase, then a second factor \u00b7 Zero knowledge still leaves a vault keeper \u00b7 Choose your password manager \u00b7 Choose your authenticator app \u00b7 Hardware security keys \u00b7 Step by step \u00b7 Mistakes to avoid \u00b7 Go further \u00b7 Sources \u00b7 Your checklist","href":"/book/en/passwords/"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"In brief","kicker":"Ch. 2 \u00b7 In brief","text":"Reused passwords remain one of the most common ways ordinary accounts get taken over, next to phishing. A password manager creates and remembers a different password for every site, and you protect it with a single strong passphrase. A second factor then goes on the accounts that matter, starting with your email, because whoever holds your email can reset almost everything else.","href":"/book/en/passwords/#in-brief"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"A leaked password opens many doors","kicker":"Ch. 2 \u00b7 Understand","text":"In January 2019, the security researcher Troy Hunt found a set of files circulating on a hacking forum under the name Collection #1. It contained 773 million unique email addresses and more than 21 million unique passwords, assembled from thousands of older breaches. Attackers feed lists like this one to programs that try each pair of address and password on other services, such as your email, your shops and your social networks, a technique called credential stuffing. Nobody is targeting you in particular, and the door simply opens wherever you reused a leaked password. Between April and September 2023, the genetic testing company 23andMe was hit in exactly this way. The British regulator fined it \u00a32.31 million in June 2025, after the data of 155,592 UK residents was exposed, and noted that the company did not require a second factor at login. You can check whether your own address appears in known breaches at Have I Been Pwned, the free service that Troy Hunt runs. People who have been online for 10 years tend to find several. The account that matters most is your email, since whoever controls it can click \"forgot my password\" on almost everything else you own.","href":"/book/en/passwords/#a-leaked-password-opens-many-doors"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"A single passphrase, then a second factor","kicker":"Ch. 2 \u00b7 Understand","text":"A password manager is an encrypted vault that creates a long random password for every site, remembers it and fills it in for you. The vault is encrypted on your own device before it is synchronised, so a well-designed provider is not able to read it, and you remember 1 secret instead of 100. That secret should be a passphrase of 6 random words, chosen for example by rolling dice against a word list (a method called diceware), which is hard to guess and far easier to remember than \"xK9#qL2v\". Two-factor authentication (2FA) adds a step after the password, when the site asks for something you have. A code sent by SMS is the weak form, because a criminal can persuade your operator to move your number to their SIM card, an attack called a SIM swap. In March 2020, Europol announced the arrest of 12 people in Spain who had used duplicate SIM cards to empty bank accounts more than 100 times, for a total above \u20ac3 million. An authenticator app is much better, and a hardware security key is the strongest option. Passkeys use the same technology as these keys to replace the password itself.","href":"/book/en/passwords/#a-single-passphrase-then-a-second-factor"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Zero knowledge still leaves a vault keeper","kicker":"Ch. 2 \u00b7 Understand","text":"Encryption keeps a synchronised vault confidential, but the company that stores it and ships your updates remains a trusted third party. Providers call their design zero knowledge, meaning they hold the vault without the passphrase that opens it. In December 2022, LastPass confirmed that an attacker had copied a backup of customer vaults. Passwords and notes were encrypted, the website addresses were not, and each stolen vault became only as strong as its owner's master password. The British regulator, which fined the company \u00a31.2 million in December 2025 for up to 1.6 million UK users affected, found no evidence that passwords had been decrypted. Open-source apps let experts compare that claim with the code, and published audits add evidence, such as the security certification that the French agency ANSSI gave KeePassXC in 2025. A local file or a server you host removes the vault keeper, and backups, updates and sync then become your job. Second factors have third parties too. The site stores the same secret as your authenticator app and a sync service may hold a copy, whereas a hardware key also verifies authenticity in the other direction, by checking which website is asking.","href":"/book/en/passwords/#zero-knowledge-still-leaves-a-vault-keeper"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"Bitwarden","kicker":"Ch. 2 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your password manager\u201d","text":"Open source: Yes \u00b7 Where the vault lives: Bitwarden's cloud, or a server you host \u00b7 Who you must trust, and what you can check: Bitwarden, for storage and app updates. Public code, third-party audits published every year. Self-hosting removes the company. \u00b7 Cost: Free, optional paid plan \u00b7 Trade-offs: The easiest open-source choice, on every device. The default service is run by a single US company.","href":"/book/en/passwords/#row-bitwarden"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"KeePassXC","kicker":"Ch. 2 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your password manager\u201d","text":"Open source: Yes \u00b7 Where the vault lives: A file on your own computer \u00b7 Who you must trust, and what you can check: No vault keeper. You rely on the app's public code, certified by ANSSI in 2025, and on your own backups. \u00b7 Cost: Free \u00b7 Trade-offs: No company, no cloud, no account. You handle sync and backups yourself. On phones, use compatible apps such as KeePassDX (Android) or KeePassium (iPhone).","href":"/book/en/passwords/#row-keepassxc"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"Proton Pass","kicker":"Ch. 2 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your password manager\u201d","text":"Open source: Yes \u00b7 Where the vault lives: Proton's cloud (Swiss company) \u00b7 Who you must trust, and what you can check: Proton, for storage and app updates. Public app code, audited by Cure53 in 2023. No self-hosting. \u00b7 Cost: Free, optional paid plan \u00b7 Trade-offs: Includes email aliases. Practical if you already use Proton, but it concentrates more of your life in a single company.","href":"/book/en/passwords/#row-proton-pass"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"1Password","kicker":"Ch. 2 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your password manager\u201d","text":"Open source: No, closed source \u00b7 Where the vault lives: 1Password's cloud (Canadian company) \u00b7 Who you must trust, and what you can check: 1Password, for storage, updates and the code itself. It publishes audit reports, not the source. \u00b7 Cost: Paid subscription only \u00b7 Trade-offs: Polished, good for families, regularly audited. The public cannot inspect the code.","href":"/book/en/passwords/#row-1password"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"Built into your browser or system (Google, Apple, Firefox)","kicker":"Ch. 2 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your password manager\u201d","text":"Open source: Varies \u00b7 Where the vault lives: Your Google, Apple or Mozilla account \u00b7 Who you must trust, and what you can check: Google, Apple or Mozilla, for storage, updates and account recovery. Only Firefox's code is fully public. \u00b7 Cost: Free \u00b7 Trade-offs: Already there, and much better than nothing. Ties you to a single browser or ecosystem, and is only as safe as that main account.","href":"/book/en/passwords/#row-built-into-your-browser-or-system-google-apple-firefox"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Choose your password manager","kicker":"Ch. 2 \u00b7 Compare","text":"All the options below are far better than reusing passwords, and they differ on who holds your encrypted vault, whether the code is public, and how much work you accept. Manager Open source Where the vault lives Who you must trust, and what you can check Cost Trade-offs Bitwarden Yes Bitwarden's cloud, or a server you host Bitwarden, for storage and app updates. Public code, third-party audits published every year. Self-hosting removes the company. Free, optional paid plan The easiest open-source choice, on every device. The default service is run by a single US company. KeePassXC Yes A file on your own computer No vault keeper. You rely on the app's public code, certified by ANSSI in 2025, and on your own backups. Free No company, no cloud, no account. You handle sync and backups yourself. On phones, use compatible apps such as KeePassDX (Android) or KeePassium (iPhone). Proton Pass Yes Proton's cloud (Swiss company) Proton, for storage and app updates. Public app code, audited by Cure53 in 2023. No self-hosting. Free, optional paid plan Includes email aliases. Practical if you already use Proton, but it concentrates more of your life in a single company. 1Password No, closed source 1Password's cloud (Canadian company) 1Password, for storage, updates and the code itself. It publishes audit reports, not the source. Paid subscription only Polished, good for families, regularly audited. The public cannot inspect the code. Built into your browser or system (Google, Apple, Firefox) Varies Your Google, Apple or Mozilla account Google, Apple or Mozilla, for storage, updates and account recovery. Only Firefox's code is fully public. Free Already there, and much better than nothing. Ties you to a single browser or ecosystem, and is only as safe as that main account. A reasonable setup for most people is Bitwarden or Proton Pass if you want sync without effort, or KeePassXC if you prefer that no company holds your vault, even encrypted.","href":"/book/en/passwords/#choose-your-password-manager"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"Aegis","kicker":"Ch. 2 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your authenticator app\u201d","text":"Platforms: Android only \u00b7 Open source: Yes \u00b7 Backup and sync: Encrypted vault on the phone. You export encrypted backups yourself. \u00b7 Trade-offs: No cloud and no account, so no sync service to trust. Backups are your job, and there is no iPhone version.","href":"/book/en/passwords/#row-aegis"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"Ente Auth","kicker":"Ch. 2 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your authenticator app\u201d","text":"Platforms: Android, iPhone, Windows, Mac, Linux, web \u00b7 Open source: Yes \u00b7 Backup and sync: Optional end-to-end encrypted sync, which needs an Ente account. Also works offline without an account. \u00b7 Trade-offs: Your codes on all your devices. The sync depends on a single small company.","href":"/book/en/passwords/#row-ente-auth"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"2FAS","kicker":"Ch. 2 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your authenticator app\u201d","text":"Platforms: Android, iPhone, plus a browser extension \u00b7 Open source: Yes \u00b7 Backup and sync: Backup through your Google Drive or iCloud account. \u00b7 Trade-offs: Simple. The backup is only as safe as that Google or Apple account.","href":"/book/en/passwords/#row-2fas"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"Proton Authenticator","kicker":"Ch. 2 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your authenticator app\u201d","text":"Platforms: Android, iPhone, Windows, Mac, Linux \u00b7 Open source: Yes \u00b7 Backup and sync: Works without an account. Optional end-to-end encrypted sync with a Proton account, plus local export and import. \u00b7 Trade-offs: Free, launched in July 2025, so younger than the others. Another part of your life at Proton if you already use it.","href":"/book/en/passwords/#row-proton-authenticator"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Choose your authenticator app","kicker":"Ch. 2 \u00b7 Compare","text":"An authenticator app shows a 6-digit code that changes every 30 seconds. The site and your phone share a secret (the QR code you scan once), and both calculate the same code from it, even without a network, which makes it the right second factor for most of your accounts. Prefer an open-source app that lets you take your codes out again, because if the secrets live on 1 phone only, a lost or broken phone locks you out everywhere at once. Your password manager can often store these codes too, which is convenient but puts both factors in the same place. App Platforms Open source Backup and sync Trade-offs Aegis Android only Yes Encrypted vault on the phone. You export encrypted backups yourself. No cloud and no account, so no sync service to trust. Backups are your job, and there is no iPhone version. Ente Auth Android, iPhone, Windows, Mac, Linux, web Yes Optional end-to-end encrypted sync, which needs an Ente account. Also works offline without an account. Your codes on all your devices. The sync depends on a single small company. 2FAS Android, iPhone, plus a browser extension Yes Backup through your Google Drive or iCloud account. Simple. The backup is only as safe as that Google or Apple account. Proton Authenticator Android, iPhone, Windows, Mac, Linux Yes Works without an account. Optional end-to-end encrypted sync with a Proton account, plus local export and import. Free, launched in July 2025, so younger than the others. Another part of your life at Proton if you already use it. A reasonable setup for most people is Aegis on Android if you accept handling backups yourself, or Ente Auth or Proton Authenticator if you want your codes on several devices. Whatever you choose, test the export or the backup once.","href":"/book/en/passwords/#choose-your-authenticator-app"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"YubiKey","kicker":"Ch. 2 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cHardware security keys\u201d","text":"Maker: Yubico (Sweden and United States) \u00b7 Connection: USB-A or USB-C, NFC on most models \u00b7 Open source: No, closed firmware \u00b7 Trade-offs: The most widely supported and documented. The firmware cannot be inspected or updated. The cheaper \"Security Key\" series is enough for logins.","href":"/book/en/passwords/#row-yubikey"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"Nitrokey","kicker":"Ch. 2 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cHardware security keys\u201d","text":"Maker: Nitrokey (Germany) \u00b7 Connection: USB-A or USB-C, NFC on some models \u00b7 Open source: Yes, firmware and hardware \u00b7 Trade-offs: Firmware can be updated. Fewer models, less polished documentation.","href":"/book/en/passwords/#row-nitrokey"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"Token2","kicker":"Ch. 2 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cHardware security keys\u201d","text":"Maker: Token2 (Switzerland) \u00b7 Connection: USB-A or USB-C, NFC on most models \u00b7 Open source: Partly. The FIDO2 part of the firmware is public and was independently reviewed in 2024, the rest is closed \u00b7 Trade-offs: Lower-priced keys. Less well known.","href":"/book/en/passwords/#row-token2"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"OnlyKey","kicker":"Ch. 2 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cHardware security keys\u201d","text":"Maker: CryptoTrust (United States) \u00b7 Connection: USB only, no NFC \u00b7 Open source: Yes, firmware \u00b7 Trade-offs: Has its own PIN buttons and can also type stored passwords. Bulkier, and more complex than a beginner needs.","href":"/book/en/passwords/#row-onlykey"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Hardware security keys","kicker":"Ch. 2 \u00b7 Compare","text":"A hardware security key is a different kind of tool. This small physical device, plugged into a USB port or held against your phone (NFC), holds a private key that is designed never to leave it, and it relies on open standards called FIDO2 and WebAuthn. When you log in, the key checks the real address of the website before it answers, and you touch it to confirm. A fake login page gets nothing from it, whereas a code from an app can still be typed into the wrong page by a tricked user. The same keys can also store passkeys. Buy 2 and register both on every account, 1 for your keyring and 1 for a safe place. Not every site supports keys, so keep an authenticator app as well, and use the keys first for the accounts that matter most, which are your email, your password manager and your main Apple or Google account. Key Maker Connection Open source Trade-offs YubiKey Yubico (Sweden and United States) USB-A or USB-C, NFC on most models No, closed firmware The most widely supported and documented. The firmware cannot be inspected or updated. The cheaper \"Security Key\" series is enough for logins. Nitrokey Nitrokey (Germany) USB-A or USB-C, NFC on some models Yes, firmware and hardware Firmware can be updated. Fewer models, less polished documentation. Token2 Token2 (Switzerland) USB-A or USB-C, NFC on most models Partly. The FIDO2 part of the firmware is public and was independently reviewed in 2024, the rest is closed Lower-priced keys. Less well known. OnlyKey CryptoTrust (United States) USB only, no NFC Yes, firmware Has its own PIN buttons and can also type stored passwords. Bulkier, and more complex than a beginner needs. A reasonable setup for most people is 2 keys from any maker above, plus an authenticator app for the sites that do not accept keys.","href":"/book/en/passwords/#hardware-security-keys"},{"type":"step","part":1,"title":"Step 1: Create your passphrase","kicker":"Ch. 2 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Create your passphrase. Roll dice against the EFF word list, or use the manager's passphrase generator, until you have 6 words (the EFF recommends 6 as a minimum). Write it on paper and keep that paper somewhere safe at home.","href":"/book/en/passwords/#step-1"},{"type":"step","part":1,"title":"Step 2: Install the manager","kicker":"Ch. 2 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Install the manager on your computer (as a browser extension) and on your phone, and create your account with that passphrase. Without recovery arranged in advance, forgetting it can mean losing your vault. Bitwarden cannot simply recover your master password; preconfigured emergency access is a separate route (step 8).","href":"/book/en/passwords/#step-2"},{"type":"step","part":1,"title":"Step 3: Start with your email","kicker":"Ch. 2 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Start with your email. Log in to your email account, change the password to one generated by the manager, and save it in the vault.","href":"/book/en/passwords/#step-3"},{"type":"step","part":1,"title":"Step 4: Move 4 more accounts","kicker":"Ch. 2 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Move 4 more accounts: your main Apple or Google account, your bank or payment app, your main social network, and any shop that stores your card. The rest can follow over the next weeks, each time you log in somewhere.","href":"/book/en/passwords/#step-4"},{"type":"step","part":1,"title":"Step 5: Turn on 2FA with your authenticator app","kicker":"Ch. 2 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Turn on 2FA with your authenticator app on those 5 accounts, email first, and on the password manager itself. In each account's security settings, look for \"two-step verification\" or \"two-factor authentication\", then scan the QR code with the app and type the code it shows. Where the site allows it, remove SMS as a method.","href":"/book/en/passwords/#step-5"},{"type":"step","part":1,"title":"Step 6: Register your hardware keys, if you chose them","kicker":"Ch. 2 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Register your hardware keys, if you chose them. In the same security settings, choose \"security key\" (sometimes \"passkey\"). Plug in or tap the first key, touch it, and give it a name, then repeat at once with the second key. This is the step that replaces trust with verification, because from then on the key checks the website's address at every login, instead of your eyes. Carry one and store the other in a safe place. Where the site permits it, prefer the tested spare key and protected recovery codes. Keeping an authenticator fallback is convenient, but its codes can still be phished, so it leaves a weaker route into the account. Test your recovery options before removing any method.","href":"/book/en/passwords/#step-6"},{"type":"step","part":1,"title":"Step 7: Save the recovery codes, away from the passphrase","kicker":"Ch. 2 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Save the recovery codes, away from the passphrase. Most sites give you one-time codes for the day you lose your phone or your keys. Print them and keep them in a different place from your passphrase paper, for example the passphrase at home and the codes in a sealed envelope with a trusted relative, so that someone who finds 1 paper does not get everything.","href":"/book/en/passwords/#step-7"},{"type":"step","part":1,"title":"Step 8: Plan emergency access","kicker":"Ch. 2 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Plan emergency access. Some managers have an emergency access feature for a trusted person (in Bitwarden it is part of the paid plan). Written instructions for your family, telling them where the 2 papers are, also work.","href":"/book/en/passwords/#step-8"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Step by step","kicker":"Ch. 2 \u00b7 Tutorial","text":"The example uses Bitwarden because it is free, open source and works on every system, and the same logic applies to the others. Create your passphrase. Roll dice against the EFF word list, or use the manager's passphrase generator, until you have 6 words (the EFF recommends 6 as a minimum). Write it on paper and keep that paper somewhere safe at home. Install the manager on your computer (as a browser extension) and on your phone, and create your account with that passphrase. Without recovery arranged in advance, forgetting it can mean losing your vault. Bitwarden cannot simply recover your master password; preconfigured emergency access is a separate route (step 8). Start with your email. Log in to your email account, change the password to one generated by the manager, and save it in the vault. Move 4 more accounts: your main Apple or Google account, your bank or payment app, your main social network, and any shop that stores your card. The rest can follow over the next weeks, each time you log in somewhere. Turn on 2FA with your authenticator app on those 5 accounts, email first, and on the password manager itself. In each account's security settings, look for \"two-step verification\" or \"two-factor authentication\", then scan the QR code with the app and type the code it shows. Where the site allows it, remove SMS as a method. Register your hardware keys, if you chose them. In the same security settings, choose \"security key\" (sometimes \"passkey\"). Plug in or tap the first key, touch it, and give it a name, then repeat at once with the second key. This is the step that replaces trust with verification, because from then on the key checks the website's address at every login, instead of your eyes. Carry one and store the other in a safe place. Where the site permits it, prefer the tested spare key and protected recovery codes. Keeping an authenticator fallback is convenient, but its codes can still be phished, so it leaves a weaker route into the account. Test your recovery options before removing any method. Save the recovery codes, away from the passphrase. Most sites give you one-time codes for the day you lose your phone or your keys. Print them and keep them in a different place from your passphrase paper, for example the passphrase at home and the codes in a sealed envelope with a trusted relative, so that someone who finds 1 paper does not get everything. Plan emergency access. Some managers have an emergency access feature for a trusted person (in Bitwarden it is part of the paid plan). Written instructions for your family, telling them where the 2 papers are, also work.","href":"/book/en/passwords/#step-by-step"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Mistakes to avoid","kicker":"Ch. 2 \u00b7 Watch out","text":"Keeping the passphrase only in your head. Memory can fail after an illness, an accident or a long holiday. A paper copy in a safe place is not a weakness, as long as your recovery codes are kept somewhere else. Leaving SMS as a fallback. If the site still accepts a code by SMS, a SIM swap still works, so remove it wherever the site lets you. Having 1 authenticator or 1 hardware key, and no backup. A lost phone or a lost key should cost you an afternoon and not your accounts, which is why you export your codes and register 2 keys. Storing passwords in notes, spreadsheets or emails. These are not encrypted vaults, and they are among the first places an intruder looks. Typing a code into a page you reached from a message. Authenticator codes can be phished too. If your manager does not offer to fill in the password, check the address, because you may be on a fake site. Trying to change everything in a single evening. Start with the 5 accounts above, then move the others over the following weeks, each time you log in somewhere.","href":"/book/en/passwords/#mistakes-to-avoid"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Go further","kicker":"Ch. 2 \u00b7 Go further","text":"Check your exposure: Have I Been Pwned. Make a dice passphrase: the word lists of the Electronic Frontier Foundation. Compare tools in detail: Privacy Guides, \"Password Managers\", \"Multifactor Authentication\" and \"Security Keys\". Next chapter: your browser and your searches.","href":"/book/en/passwords/#go-further"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Sources","kicker":"Ch. 2 \u00b7 References","text":"Implementing phishing-resistant MFA, CISA, checked 22 October 2026. Emergency access, Bitwarden Help, checked 22 October 2026. The 773 Million Record \"Collection #1\" Data Breach, Troy Hunt, 17 January 2019. 23andMe fined \u00a32.31 million for failing to protect UK users' genetic data, Information Commissioner's Office (United Kingdom), 17 June 2025. The SIM highjackers: how criminals are stealing millions by highjacking phone numbers, Europol, 13 March 2020. Notice of recent security incident, LastPass, 22 December 2022. Password manager provider fined \u00a31.2m by ICO for data breach, Information Commissioner's Office (United Kingdom), 11 December 2025. KeePassXC security audits and certifications, KeePassXC, consulted in September 2026. EFF Dice-Generated Passphrases, Electronic Frontier Foundation, consulted in September 2026.","href":"/book/en/passwords/#sources"},{"type":"action","part":1,"title":"Check your email address on Have I Been Pwned","kicker":"Ch. 2 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/passwords/#action-passwords-1"},{"type":"action","part":1,"title":"Install a password manager and create a passphrase of 6 random words","kicker":"Ch. 2 \u00b7 Checklist action, 1 hour","text":"","href":"/book/en/passwords/#action-passwords-2"},{"type":"action","part":1,"title":"Give your email a new unique password and turn on 2FA with an authenticator app, plus your 2 hardware keys if you bought them","kicker":"Ch. 2 \u00b7 Checklist action, 1 hour","text":"","href":"/book/en/passwords/#action-passwords-3"},{"type":"action","part":1,"title":"Move your 4 next most important accounts and remove SMS codes where possible","kicker":"Ch. 2 \u00b7 Checklist action, 1 hour","text":"","href":"/book/en/passwords/#action-passwords-4"},{"type":"action","part":1,"title":"Put your passphrase on paper in one safe place and your recovery codes in another, and tell 1 trusted person where","kicker":"Ch. 2 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/passwords/#action-passwords-5"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Your checklist","kicker":"Ch. 2 \u00b7 Act","text":"Check your email address on Have I Been PwnedInstall a password manager and create a passphrase of 6 random wordsGive your email a new unique password and turn on 2FA with an authenticator app, plus your 2 hardware keys if you bought themMove your 4 next most important accounts and remove SMS codes where possiblePut your passphrase on paper in one safe place and your recovery codes in another, and tell 1 trusted person where","href":"/book/en/passwords/#your-checklist"},{"type":"chapter","part":1,"title":"Your browser and your searches","kicker":"Chapter 3 \u00b7 Privacy","text":"In brief \u00b7 A default that someone else paid for \u00b7 3 ways trackers recognise you \u00b7 Who vouches for the padlock \u00b7 Choose your browser \u00b7 Choose your search engine \u00b7 Step by step \u00b7 Mistakes to avoid \u00b7 Go further \u00b7 Sources \u00b7 Your checklist","href":"/book/en/browser/"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"In brief","kicker":"Ch. 3 \u00b7 In brief","text":"Your browser and your search engine see more of your life than any other software, and the ones on your device were chosen for you by a business deal. Switch to a browser that blocks tracking, add a single good content blocker, and pick a search engine that does not build a profile of you. It takes less than an hour, and the difference shows at once in fewer ads and faster pages.","href":"/book/en/browser/#in-brief"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"A default that someone else paid for","kicker":"Ch. 3 \u00b7 Understand","text":"You read the news, look up symptoms and compare loans in your browser, and most pages you open also load invisible code from dozens of other companies, such as advertising networks and analytics services. These trackers note what you are reading and add it to a profile built over years. Your searches reveal even more, since you ask a search engine things you would not ask your doctor or your partner. Yet the defaults on your device were not chosen by you. A slide made public during the US antitrust trial against Google showed that the company paid $26.3 billion in 2021 to be the default search engine, with Apple as the likely largest recipient and browser makers such as Mozilla among the others. In September 2025, a US federal judge ended the exclusive contracts but let Google keep paying for placement, so as of September 2026 the default on your device is still, in most cases, the result of a payment. Private or incognito mode does not solve this, because it only stops your own device from keeping the history and cookies. Websites, trackers, your internet provider and your employer's network see the same as before.","href":"/book/en/browser/#a-default-that-someone-else-paid-for"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"3 ways trackers recognise you","kicker":"Ch. 3 \u00b7 Understand","text":"Trackers rely on 3 main techniques. Third-party cookies. A cookie is a small file a site stores in your browser. When the same advertising company is present on thousands of sites, its cookie follows you from one to the next. Safari, Firefox and Brave block or isolate these by default. Chrome, the most used browser, still allows them, and in April 2025 Google confirmed that it was keeping this approach after years of announcing their removal. Fingerprinting. Your browser reveals many small details, such as screen size, language, time zone, installed fonts and graphics hardware. Each one is ordinary, but combined they are often unique to you. Nothing is stored on your device, so deleting cookies changes nothing. Your accounts. If you stay logged in to Google or Facebook, every page that contains their code can be linked to your real name. The defences follow the same logic. Block. A content blocker refuses to load known trackers at all. Isolate. The browser gives every site its own separate cookie storage, so that a cookie set on a given site is not readable on the next. Look like everyone else. Some browsers aim to make all their users appear identical, so that a fingerprint points to a crowd instead of a person. Your IP address also identifies you, which is the subject of chapter 5.","href":"/book/en/browser/#3-ways-trackers-recognise-you"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Who vouches for the padlock","kicker":"Ch. 3 \u00b7 Understand","text":"The padlock promises that nobody on the way can read the page (confidentiality) and that the site really is the one named in the address (authenticity). The second promise comes from a certificate authority, an organisation that signs a certificate for the site, and your browser or system ships with a list of the authorities it accepts. Any of them can vouch for any site, which makes them the classic trusted third parties of the web. In 2011, an intruder at the Dutch authority DigiNotar issued 531 false certificates, and the google.com one served to intercept about 300,000 users, almost all in Iran, before browsers removed DigiNotar from their lists. Certificate transparency grew out of that failure. Certificates now have to appear in public logs that anyone can search, so a site's owner can spot a false one, and Firefox has refused unlogged certificates on computers since version 135, in February 2025, as Chrome and Safari already did. The browser maker is the other third party, because it chooses that list, the default search engine and the extension store, and its update channel can change the program you run. Open source and reproducible builds let outsiders check what it ships.","href":"/book/en/browser/#who-vouches-for-the-padlock"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"Firefox","kicker":"Ch. 3 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your browser\u201d","text":"Who is behind it: Mozilla, owned by a non-profit foundation \u00b7 Who you must trust: Mozilla, for updates, its own list of certificate authorities and its add-ons store. Public code. \u00b7 Strengths: Its own engine, independent from Google's Chromium. Strong protection in Strict mode. Runs the full uBlock Origin on computers and on Android. \u00b7 Trade-offs: A few settings to change first. Sends usage data by default. Most of Mozilla's income comes from its search deal with Google. On iPhone it cannot install extensions.","href":"/book/en/browser/#row-firefox"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"Brave","kicker":"Ch. 3 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your browser\u201d","text":"Who is behind it: Brave Software, a for-profit US company \u00b7 Who you must trust: Brave Software for updates, Google for the Chromium code underneath and the extension store. Public code. \u00b7 Strengths: Blocks ads and trackers with no setup, on iPhone too. Works with nearly every site. Computer and phone. \u00b7 Trade-offs: Promotes its own products, including a crypto wallet, reward ads and a paid VPN. You can turn them off. Built on Google's Chromium engine.","href":"/book/en/browser/#row-brave"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"Mullvad Browser","kicker":"Ch. 3 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your browser\u201d","text":"Who is behind it: Built by the Tor Project with Mullvad, a Swedish VPN company \u00b7 Who you must trust: The Tor Project and Mullvad, for updates. Public code, with reproducible builds that outsiders can check. \u00b7 Strengths: Strong anti-fingerprinting, designed so that all users look the same. No usage data sent. Free, no VPN required. \u00b7 Trade-offs: Computer only. Forgets everything when closed. Some sites break.","href":"/book/en/browser/#row-mullvad-browser"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"LibreWolf","kicker":"Ch. 3 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your browser\u201d","text":"Who is behind it: A community of volunteers \u00b7 Who you must trust: A few volunteers, for the builds and for how fast Firefox security fixes arrive. Public code. \u00b7 Strengths: Firefox with strict settings already applied, usage data removed, uBlock Origin included. \u00b7 Trade-offs: Computer only. Small team. On some systems you handle updates yourself.","href":"/book/en/browser/#row-librewolf"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"Tor Browser","kicker":"Ch. 3 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your browser\u201d","text":"Who is behind it: The Tor Project, a non-profit \u00b7 Who you must trust: The Tor Project for updates (reproducible builds). No single relay sees both you and the site. \u00b7 Strengths: Hides your IP address by passing through 3 relays. The strongest anonymity in this table. \u00b7 Trade-offs: Slow. Many sites show extra checks or block it. Not made for your everyday logged-in accounts.","href":"/book/en/browser/#row-tor-browser"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Choose your browser","kicker":"Ch. 3 \u00b7 Compare","text":"No browser is perfect, so look at who builds it, what it blocks without setup and what you give up. Browser Who is behind it Who you must trust Strengths Trade-offs Firefox Mozilla, owned by a non-profit foundation Mozilla, for updates, its own list of certificate authorities and its add-ons store. Public code. Its own engine, independent from Google's Chromium. Strong protection in Strict mode. Runs the full uBlock Origin on computers and on Android. A few settings to change first. Sends usage data by default. Most of Mozilla's income comes from its search deal with Google. On iPhone it cannot install extensions. Brave Brave Software, a for-profit US company Brave Software for updates, Google for the Chromium code underneath and the extension store. Public code. Blocks ads and trackers with no setup, on iPhone too. Works with nearly every site. Computer and phone. Promotes its own products, including a crypto wallet, reward ads and a paid VPN. You can turn them off. Built on Google's Chromium engine. Mullvad Browser Built by the Tor Project with Mullvad, a Swedish VPN company The Tor Project and Mullvad, for updates. Public code, with reproducible builds that outsiders can check. Strong anti-fingerprinting, designed so that all users look the same. No usage data sent. Free, no VPN required. Computer only. Forgets everything when closed. Some sites break. LibreWolf A community of volunteers A few volunteers, for the builds and for how fast Firefox security fixes arrive. Public code. Firefox with strict settings already applied, usage data removed, uBlock Origin included. Computer only. Small team. On some systems you handle updates yourself. Tor Browser The Tor Project, a non-profit The Tor Project for updates (reproducible builds). No single relay sees both you and the site. Hides your IP address by passing through 3 relays. The strongest anonymity in this table. Slow. Many sites show extra checks or block it. Not made for your everyday logged-in accounts. For blocking, the reference is uBlock Origin, which is free, open source and has no paid exceptions for advertisers. It works fully in Firefox on computers and on Android. Chrome no longer runs it, because Google switched off the older extension format in July 2025 and removed the last extensions of that kind from the Chrome Web Store on 31 August 2026. What remains is a more limited version, uBlock Origin Lite, while Brave has its own blocker built in. The iPhone is a separate case, because Firefox for iOS cannot install extensions so uBlock Origin is not available there. The options are Brave's built-in blocker, the simpler ad blocker that Firefox offers on iOS (off by default and rolling out progressively), or Safari with uBlock Origin Lite, available since August 2025. Resist installing more, because every extension can read the pages you visit, and an unusual set makes your fingerprint more unique. A reasonable setup for most people is Firefox or Brave for everyday life, plus Mullvad Browser or Tor Browser for the searches you prefer to keep apart from your accounts.","href":"/book/en/browser/#choose-your-browser"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"DuckDuckGo","kicker":"Ch. 3 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your search engine\u201d","text":"Where results come from: Mostly Bing, plus its own sources \u00b7 How it earns money: Ads based on the words you type, not on a profile \u00b7 Good to know: US company. The easiest switch.","href":"/book/en/browser/#row-duckduckgo"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"Brave Search","kicker":"Ch. 3 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your search engine\u201d","text":"Where results come from: Its own independent index \u00b7 How it earns money: Ads \u00b7 Good to know: Same company as the Brave browser.","href":"/book/en/browser/#row-brave-search"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"Startpage","kicker":"Ch. 3 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your search engine\u201d","text":"Where results come from: Google results, requested without your identity \u00b7 How it earns money: Ads that are not personalised \u00b7 Good to know: Dutch company. Since 2019 its majority owner is System1, a US advertising group.","href":"/book/en/browser/#row-startpage"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"Kagi","kicker":"Ch. 3 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your search engine\u201d","text":"Where results come from: Its own index plus several other sources \u00b7 How it earns money: Paid subscription, no ads \u00b7 Good to know: US company. Needs an account. Funded by its users only.","href":"/book/en/browser/#row-kagi"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"SearXNG","kicker":"Ch. 3 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your search engine\u201d","text":"Where results come from: Combines many engines at once \u00b7 How it earns money: Nothing. It is free, open-source software \u00b7 Good to know: Host it yourself or use a public instance (list at searx.space). You then trust whoever runs that instance.","href":"/book/en/browser/#row-searxng"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Choose your search engine","kicker":"Ch. 3 \u00b7 Compare","text":"Changing your search engine takes about a minute, which makes it one of the easiest steps in this book. According to their own policies, none of the options below builds an advertising profile of you, but they differ in where their results come from and how they earn money. Search engine Where results come from How it earns money Good to know DuckDuckGo Mostly Bing, plus its own sources Ads based on the words you type, not on a profile US company. The easiest switch. Brave Search Its own independent index Ads Same company as the Brave browser. Startpage Google results, requested without your identity Ads that are not personalised Dutch company. Since 2019 its majority owner is System1, a US advertising group. Kagi Its own index plus several other sources Paid subscription, no ads US company. Needs an account. Funded by its users only. SearXNG Combines many engines at once Nothing. It is free, open-source software Host it yourself or use a public instance (list at searx.space). You then trust whoever runs that instance. Try one for 2 weeks before judging. Results for local Lithuanian searches can vary from one engine to another, and nothing stops you from using a second engine for the occasional difficult query.","href":"/book/en/browser/#choose-your-search-engine"},{"type":"step","part":1,"title":"Step 1: Install it","kicker":"Ch. 3 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Install it from mozilla.org or your system's official store.","href":"/book/en/browser/#step-1"},{"type":"step","part":1,"title":"Step 2: Raise the protection","kicker":"Ch. 3 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Raise the protection. In Settings, Privacy & Security, set Enhanced Tracking Protection to Strict.","href":"/book/en/browser/#step-2"},{"type":"step","part":1,"title":"Step 3: Force encrypted connections","kicker":"Ch. 3 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Force encrypted connections. On the same page, turn on HTTPS-Only Mode in all windows. This is the step that replaces trust with verification, since the browser then checks a certificate on every connection instead of relying on the networks in between.","href":"/book/en/browser/#step-3"},{"type":"step","part":1,"title":"Step 4: Stop sending usage data","kicker":"Ch. 3 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Stop sending usage data. Still on the same page, in the section about Firefox data collection, untick the boxes.","href":"/book/en/browser/#step-4"},{"type":"step","part":1,"title":"Step 5: Change the search engine","kicker":"Ch. 3 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Change the search engine. In Settings, Search, choose your new default. If it is not in the list, visit the search engine's site and add it from the address bar.","href":"/book/en/browser/#step-5"},{"type":"step","part":1,"title":"Step 6: Install uBlock Origin","kicker":"Ch. 3 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Install uBlock Origin from the official add-ons site (addons.mozilla.org), and keep its default settings, which are good.","href":"/book/en/browser/#step-6"},{"type":"step","part":1,"title":"Step 7: Bring your bookmarks","kicker":"Ch. 3 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Bring your bookmarks. Use the import tool in the bookmarks menu to copy bookmarks from your old browser. Do not import saved passwords, because they belong in your password manager (chapter 2).","href":"/book/en/browser/#step-7"},{"type":"step","part":1,"title":"Step 8: Make it the default, on your phone too","kicker":"Ch. 3 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Make it the default, on your phone too. Set the new browser as default on your computer, then repeat the same choices on your phone. On Android, Firefox also accepts uBlock Origin. On iPhone it does not, so use one of the iPhone options described above.","href":"/book/en/browser/#step-8"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Step by step","kicker":"Ch. 3 \u00b7 Tutorial","text":"The example uses Firefox on a computer, because it exists on every system and is independent from Google's engine. The same logic applies to the others, and the names of settings may move slightly between versions. Install it from mozilla.org or your system's official store. Raise the protection. In Settings, Privacy & Security, set Enhanced Tracking Protection to Strict. Force encrypted connections. On the same page, turn on HTTPS-Only Mode in all windows. This is the step that replaces trust with verification, since the browser then checks a certificate on every connection instead of relying on the networks in between. Stop sending usage data. Still on the same page, in the section about Firefox data collection, untick the boxes. Change the search engine. In Settings, Search, choose your new default. If it is not in the list, visit the search engine's site and add it from the address bar. Install uBlock Origin from the official add-ons site (addons.mozilla.org), and keep its default settings, which are good. Bring your bookmarks. Use the import tool in the bookmarks menu to copy bookmarks from your old browser. Do not import saved passwords, because they belong in your password manager (chapter 2). Make it the default, on your phone too. Set the new browser as default on your computer, then repeat the same choices on your phone. On Android, Firefox also accepts uBlock Origin. On iPhone it does not, so use one of the iPhone options described above.","href":"/book/en/browser/#step-by-step"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Mistakes to avoid","kicker":"Ch. 3 \u00b7 Watch out","text":"Believing incognito mode makes you invisible. It hides your browsing from the people who share your device, and from nobody else. Installing 10 privacy extensions. Each one can read your pages, and together they make you stand out, so a single good content blocker is enough. Staying logged in to Google or Facebook all day in your main browser. Keeping those accounts in a second browser stops their cookies from following your everyday browsing, which weakens the link between your name and what you read. It does not remove that link, because your IP address and your fingerprint remain visible to the sites you visit. Clicking \"Accept all\" out of habit. Look for \"Reject all\". uBlock Origin can also hide most cookie banners if you turn on the cookie notices lists in its settings. Pushing every setting to the maximum. If half your sites break, you are likely to go back to the old browser within a week, so raise the settings gradually and keep the level you can live with. Ignoring updates. The browser is the program most exposed to the internet, so restart it when it asks.","href":"/book/en/browser/#mistakes-to-avoid"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Go further","kicker":"Ch. 3 \u00b7 Go further","text":"Test your browser: Cover Your Tracks, by the Electronic Frontier Foundation (coveryourtracks.eff.org), shows how unique your fingerprint is. Compare in detail: Privacy Guides, \"Desktop Browsers\" and \"Search Engines\" (privacyguides.org). Next chapter: your email and your aliases.","href":"/book/en/browser/#go-further"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Sources","kicker":"Ch. 3 \u00b7 References","text":"Operation Black Tulip: certificate authorities lose authority, ENISA (European Union Agency for Cybersecurity), December 2011. DigiNotar removal follow up, Mozilla Security Blog, 2 September 2011. Certificate Transparency is now enforced in Firefox on desktop platforms starting with version 135, Mozilla dev-security-policy list, 4 February 2025. Google paid $26 billion in 2021 to become a default search engine, CNBC, 27 October 2023. In a major antitrust ruling, a judge lets Google keep Chrome but levies other penalties, NPR, 2 September 2025. Next steps for Privacy Sandbox and tracking protections in Chrome, Google, 22 April 2025. Manifest V2 support timeline, Chrome for Developers (Google), updated in September 2026. Add-ons in Firefox for iOS, Mozilla Support, consulted in September 2026. Block ads in Firefox for iOS, Mozilla Support, checked 22 October 2026.","href":"/book/en/browser/#sources"},{"type":"action","part":1,"title":"Change the default search engine on your computer and your phone","kicker":"Ch. 3 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/browser/#action-browser-1"},{"type":"action","part":1,"title":"Install a browser that blocks tracking and make it your default","kicker":"Ch. 3 \u00b7 Checklist action, 1 hour","text":"","href":"/book/en/browser/#action-browser-2"},{"type":"action","part":1,"title":"Add uBlock Origin where supported, or use your browser's blocker, and remove unused extensions","kicker":"Ch. 3 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/browser/#action-browser-3"},{"type":"action","part":1,"title":"Move your Google and Facebook sessions to a second browser","kicker":"Ch. 3 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/browser/#action-browser-4"},{"type":"action","part":1,"title":"Test your browser on coveryourtracks.eff.org","kicker":"Ch. 3 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/browser/#action-browser-5"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Your checklist","kicker":"Ch. 3 \u00b7 Act","text":"Change the default search engine on your computer and your phoneInstall a browser that blocks tracking and make it your defaultAdd uBlock Origin where supported, or use your browser's blocker, and remove unused extensionsMove your Google and Facebook sessions to a second browserTest your browser on coveryourtracks.eff.org","href":"/book/en/browser/#your-checklist"},{"type":"chapter","part":1,"title":"Your email and your aliases","kicker":"Chapter 4 \u00b7 Privacy","text":"In brief \u00b7 The address that links all your accounts \u00b7 An encrypted mailbox and a different address everywhere \u00b7 Who can read, forge or lock your mail \u00b7 Choose your mail provider \u00b7 Choose your alias service \u00b7 Disposable inboxes for one-time use \u00b7 When a site demands a phone number \u00b7 Step by step \u00b7 Mistakes to avoid \u00b7 Go further \u00b7 Sources \u00b7 Your checklist","href":"/book/en/email/"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"In brief","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 In brief","text":"Your email address is the key to all your other accounts, and the label that links your profiles together across the internet. Move your mail to a provider that lives from subscriptions and not from advertising, and that can encrypt your mailbox so that the company itself is not able to read it. Then stop giving your real address to everyone and use aliases instead, with a different address for each service. You will see who leaks or sells your address, and you can switch off 1 alias without touching the rest.","href":"/book/en/email/#in-brief"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"The address that links all your accounts","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 Understand","text":"Almost every account you own can be reset by email, so whoever controls your mailbox can click \"forgot password\" on your bank, your social networks and your cloud storage. The mailbox is also an archive of your life, with invoices, tickets, medical appointments and years of conversations. The most popular mailboxes are free because the company that carries your mail also runs an advertising business. Google announced in June 2017 that it would stop scanning the content of consumer Gmail to personalise ads, but the mailbox remains tied to an account that can also hold your searches, your videos and your location history. Then there is the address itself, which you give to shops, newsletters, apps and public offices. Because it never changes, data brokers can use it to join separate databases into a single profile of you. When one of those services is breached, the address ends up on lists used for spam and for phishing, meaning fake messages written to steal your passwords. In 2020, the hardware wallet maker Ledger lost about 1 million customer email addresses from its marketing database, along with the postal details of roughly 272,000 customers, and phishing campaigns by email and SMS followed.","href":"/book/en/email/#the-address-that-links-all-your-accounts"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"An encrypted mailbox and a different address everywhere","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 Understand","text":"An encrypted mail provider stores your mailbox in a form the company cannot read. With Proton Mail and Tuta this is always on, and messages between 2 users of the same provider are end-to-end encrypted. With Mailbox.org and Posteo, you switch it on yourself. A message sent to an ordinary Gmail or Outlook address is not end-to-end encrypted and stays readable there. Encryption protects the content, not the metadata (who writes to whom, and when), and a court can still compel a provider to hand over what it holds. In September 2021, Proton confirmed that a Swiss order had obliged it to log the IP address of a climate activist (the number that identifies an internet connection), while the content stayed out of reach. For private conversations, use a messenger from chapter 1. An alias is a forwarding address such as shop-name@alias-service, created for a single shop. Its messages arrive in your mailbox and the shop never sees your real address. If that alias starts receiving spam, you know who leaked or sold it and you switch it off with 1 click. Brokers find it harder to join your accounts, because every service knows you under a different address.","href":"/book/en/email/#an-encrypted-mailbox-and-a-different-address-everywhere"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Who can read, forge or lock your mail","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 Understand","text":"Email was designed without encryption or proof of the sender, so its confidentiality, authenticity and availability all depend on your provider. A message coming from an ordinary mailbox reaches the provider in readable form before it is encrypted for storage, so you rely on the company not to keep a copy, to ship honest apps and to keep your account open. An alias operator is a second third party of the same kind, since every forwarded message crosses its servers. End-to-end encryption takes the provider out of the content only when both ends hold keys, inside 1 provider or with PGP between 2 of them. The provider then supplies the other person's key, as a messenger's directory does (chapter 1). Proton lets you mark a contact's key as trusted, and Tuta added key verification by QR code in August 2025. With PGP, you compare the key's fingerprint through another channel. Availability is the property you can decentralise cheaply. With a domain you own, the provider becomes replaceable and the domain registrar becomes the party to watch. Running your own mail server is possible, but securing it and getting its messages accepted elsewhere make it a poor first project.","href":"/book/en/email/#who-can-read-forge-or-lock-your-mail"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"Proton Mail","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your mail provider\u201d","text":"Based in: Switzerland \u00b7 Who you must trust: Proton sees ordinary incoming mail before encrypting it, and supplies your contacts' keys. Trusted keys are an optional check. \u00b7 Price: Free tier, paid plans \u00b7 Trade-offs: Mailbox always encrypted. Open-source apps, large ecosystem (calendar, drive, VPN). Subject lines are not end-to-end encrypted. Ordinary mail apps need an extra \"bridge\" program on a paid plan.","href":"/book/en/email/#row-proton-mail"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"Tuta","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your mail provider\u201d","text":"Based in: Germany \u00b7 Who you must trust: Tuta sees ordinary incoming mail before encrypting it, and supplies your contacts' keys. Key verification is optional. \u00b7 Price: Free tier, paid plans \u00b7 Trade-offs: Mailbox always encrypted. Open-source apps, encrypts subject lines too. Uses its own encryption system, so no ordinary mail apps and no PGP (the older standard for encrypting email).","href":"/book/en/email/#row-tuta"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"Mailbox.org","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your mail provider\u201d","text":"Based in: Germany \u00b7 Who you must trust: Mailbox.org can read stored mail until you turn on inbox encryption. With PGP, you verify keys yourself. \u00b7 Price: Paid, low price \u00b7 Trade-offs: Classic mailbox that works with any mail app. Encryption of your inbox (with PGP) is optional, and you switch it on yourself. Less polished on mobile.","href":"/book/en/email/#row-mailbox-org"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"Posteo","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your mail provider\u201d","text":"Based in: Germany \u00b7 Who you must trust: Posteo can read stored mail until you turn on encryption. With PGP, you verify keys yourself. \u00b7 Price: Paid, low price \u00b7 Trade-offs: Works with any mail app, can be paid anonymously, including cash by post. Encryption of your stored mail is optional, and you switch it on yourself. Does not support your own domain.","href":"/book/en/email/#row-posteo"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"Your own domain","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your mail provider\u201d","text":"Based in: Wherever you host it \u00b7 Who you must trust: The same provider for content, plus your domain registrar. No provider can keep your address. \u00b7 Price: Yearly domain fee plus a paid mailbox \u00b7 Trade-offs: you@your-name.lt, hosted at one of the providers above. You can change provider later without changing address. You must never forget to renew the domain.","href":"/book/en/email/#row-your-own-domain"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Choose your mail provider","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 Compare","text":"All the options below are funded by their users and not by advertising. They differ in price, in how they encrypt, and in whether you can use an ordinary mail app. Provider Based in Who you must trust Price Trade-offs Proton Mail Switzerland Proton sees ordinary incoming mail before encrypting it, and supplies your contacts' keys. Trusted keys are an optional check. Free tier, paid plans Mailbox always encrypted. Open-source apps, large ecosystem (calendar, drive, VPN). Subject lines are not end-to-end encrypted. Ordinary mail apps need an extra \"bridge\" program on a paid plan. Tuta Germany Tuta sees ordinary incoming mail before encrypting it, and supplies your contacts' keys. Key verification is optional. Free tier, paid plans Mailbox always encrypted. Open-source apps, encrypts subject lines too. Uses its own encryption system, so no ordinary mail apps and no PGP (the older standard for encrypting email). Mailbox.org Germany Mailbox.org can read stored mail until you turn on inbox encryption. With PGP, you verify keys yourself. Paid, low price Classic mailbox that works with any mail app. Encryption of your inbox (with PGP) is optional, and you switch it on yourself. Less polished on mobile. Posteo Germany Posteo can read stored mail until you turn on encryption. With PGP, you verify keys yourself. Paid, low price Works with any mail app, can be paid anonymously, including cash by post. Encryption of your stored mail is optional, and you switch it on yourself. Does not support your own domain. Your own domain Wherever you host it The same provider for content, plus your domain registrar. No provider can keep your address. Yearly domain fee plus a paid mailbox you@your-name.lt, hosted at one of the providers above. You can change provider later without changing address. You must never forget to renew the domain. A reasonable setup for most people is 1 mailbox from this list, with encryption switched on where it is optional, plus an alias service. If you plan to keep the same address for the next 20 years, consider your own domain from the start.","href":"/book/en/email/#choose-your-mail-provider"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"SimpleLogin","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your alias service\u201d","text":"Who runs it: Proton (Switzerland) \u00b7 Price: Free tier with a limited number of aliases, paid for unlimited \u00b7 Trade-offs: Open source. You can also reply and send from an alias. Works with any mailbox, not only Proton. Included in some paid Proton plans.","href":"/book/en/email/#row-simplelogin"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"Proton's built-in aliases (\"hide-my-email\")","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your alias service\u201d","text":"Who runs it: Proton (Switzerland), using SimpleLogin technology \u00b7 Price: A limited number on the free and Mail Plus plans (10 as of September 2026), unlimited with Pass Plus or Proton Unlimited \u00b7 Trade-offs: Created directly inside Proton Mail or the Proton Pass password manager. Very convenient if your mailbox is at Proton. Your mailbox and your aliases are then in the hands of the same company, so if that account is lost or blocked, both go.","href":"/book/en/email/#row-proton-s-built-in-aliases-hide-my-email"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"addy.io","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your alias service\u201d","text":"Who runs it: A small independent operator \u00b7 Price: Generous free tier, paid plans \u00b7 Trade-offs: Open source, and you can host it yourself. A small team is also a single point of failure.","href":"/book/en/email/#row-addy-io"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"DuckDuckGo Email Protection","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your alias service\u201d","text":"Who runs it: DuckDuckGo (United States) \u00b7 Price: Free \u00b7 Trade-offs: Very easy, removes trackers hidden in emails. Needs the DuckDuckGo app or browser extension.","href":"/book/en/email/#row-duckduckgo-email-protection"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"Firefox Relay","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your alias service\u201d","text":"Who runs it: Mozilla (United States) \u00b7 Price: Free tier with a few aliases, paid for more \u00b7 Trade-offs: Simple, integrated into Firefox. Few aliases on the free tier.","href":"/book/en/email/#row-firefox-relay"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"Extra addresses at your mail provider","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your alias service\u201d","text":"Who runs it: Your mail provider \u00b7 Price: Usually in paid plans \u00b7 Trade-offs: Proton Mail, Tuta, Mailbox.org and Posteo all let you add a few more addresses to the same mailbox. Nothing new to trust, but the number is limited.","href":"/book/en/email/#row-extra-addresses-at-your-mail-provider"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"\"Plus addressing\"","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your alias service\u201d","text":"Who runs it: Any provider \u00b7 Price: Free \u00b7 Trade-offs: you+shop@domain.com. Useful for sorting mail, weak for privacy, because your real address is visible inside it and the \"+shop\" part is easy to remove.","href":"/book/en/email/#row-plus-addressing"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Choose your alias service","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 Compare","text":"An alias service sits between the world and your mailbox. It forwards your mail and can technically see the messages passing through, so pick an operator you trust as much as your mail provider. Service Who runs it Price Trade-offs SimpleLogin Proton (Switzerland) Free tier with a limited number of aliases, paid for unlimited Open source. You can also reply and send from an alias. Works with any mailbox, not only Proton. Included in some paid Proton plans. Proton's built-in aliases (\"hide-my-email\") Proton (Switzerland), using SimpleLogin technology A limited number on the free and Mail Plus plans (10 as of September 2026), unlimited with Pass Plus or Proton Unlimited Created directly inside Proton Mail or the Proton Pass password manager. Very convenient if your mailbox is at Proton. Your mailbox and your aliases are then in the hands of the same company, so if that account is lost or blocked, both go. addy.io A small independent operator Generous free tier, paid plans Open source, and you can host it yourself. A small team is also a single point of failure. DuckDuckGo Email Protection DuckDuckGo (United States) Free Very easy, removes trackers hidden in emails. Needs the DuckDuckGo app or browser extension. Firefox Relay Mozilla (United States) Free tier with a few aliases, paid for more Simple, integrated into Firefox. Few aliases on the free tier. Extra addresses at your mail provider Your mail provider Usually in paid plans Proton Mail, Tuta, Mailbox.org and Posteo all let you add a few more addresses to the same mailbox. Nothing new to trust, but the number is limited. \"Plus addressing\" Any provider Free you+shop@domain.com. Useful for sorting mail, weak for privacy, because your real address is visible inside it and the \"+shop\" part is easy to remove. A reasonable setup for most people is a dedicated alias service with a browser extension, so that creating an alias takes 1 click when you sign up somewhere. Choosing an operator different from your mail provider avoids depending on a single company for both.","href":"/book/en/email/#choose-your-alias-service"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"Mailinator","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cDisposable inboxes for one-time use\u201d","text":"How you get an address: Invent any name at its public domain \u00b7 How long mail is kept: A few hours \u00b7 Trade-offs: Fully public, so anyone who types the same name reads the same inbox. Receive only. Built mainly for software testers, with paid private plans. Widely blocked.","href":"/book/en/email/#row-mailinator"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"Guerrilla Mail","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cDisposable inboxes for one-time use\u201d","text":"How you get an address: A random address is given to you, or you choose one \u00b7 How long mail is kept: 1 hour \u00b7 Trade-offs: No password, so the inbox is protected only by how hard its name is to guess. Offers a \"scrambled\" address to make guessing harder.","href":"/book/en/email/#row-guerrilla-mail"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"Maildrop","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cDisposable inboxes for one-time use\u201d","text":"How you get an address: Invent any name at its public domain \u00b7 How long mail is kept: At most 10 messages, cleared after 24 hours without new mail \u00b7 Trade-offs: Fully public, no password. Attachments are removed.","href":"/book/en/email/#row-maildrop"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Disposable inboxes for one-time use","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 Compare","text":"Some sites you will never visit again, such as a one-time download, a wifi portal or a forum you read once, and for these a disposable inbox is enough. There is no sign-up. You invent an address on the spot, type it into the form, then open the inbox on the service's website to read the message. On public services of this kind there is no password, so anyone who guesses or knows the address can read the inbox. Messages are deleted quickly, you cannot count on finding them later, and many websites refuse these domains. A disposable inbox is therefore fine for a verification code that protects nothing important. It is the wrong tool for an account you want to keep, for anything that contains personal data, or wherever a password reset could be sent later, because whoever types the same address would receive it. For everything else, an alias does the job better. Service How you get an address How long mail is kept Trade-offs Mailinator Invent any name at its public domain A few hours Fully public, so anyone who types the same name reads the same inbox. Receive only. Built mainly for software testers, with paid private plans. Widely blocked. Guerrilla Mail A random address is given to you, or you choose one 1 hour No password, so the inbox is protected only by how hard its name is to guess. Offers a \"scrambled\" address to make guessing harder. Maildrop Invent any name at its public domain At most 10 messages, cleared after 24 hours without new mail Fully public, no password. Attachments are removed.","href":"/book/en/email/#disposable-inboxes-for-one-time-use"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"A second SIM or eSIM, used only for sign-ups","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cWhen a site demands a phone number\u201d","text":"How it works: A real mobile number from an ordinary operator, kept apart from your personal one \u00b7 Trade-offs: Accepted everywhere. Prepaid numbers expire if you do not top them up. Many EU countries require an identity document to buy a SIM, including Lithuania for new prepaid cards since January 2025. The rules vary, and this option separates your numbers without making you anonymous.","href":"/book/en/email/#row-a-second-sim-or-esim-used-only-for-sign-ups"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"Virtual (VoIP) number apps: JMP.chat, Hushed, MySudo","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cWhen a site demands a phone number\u201d","text":"How it works: A paid number that works over the internet, inside an app \u00b7 Trade-offs: JMP.chat is open source but offers only US and Canadian numbers. Hushed (closed source) offers US, Canadian and UK numbers. MySudo (closed source) can be installed in only a few countries, so check before you count on it. Many services refuse VoIP numbers, and a European site may refuse a foreign one.","href":"/book/en/email/#row-virtual-voip-number-apps-jmp-chat-hushed-mysudo"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"A data eSIM paid in bitcoin, for example Silent.link","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cWhen a site demands a phone number\u201d","text":"How it works: No account. Some plans include a US or UK mobile number that can receive SMS \u00b7 Trade-offs: Costs more than an ordinary SIM. The number is leased by the year, and it is gone if you do not renew it. A single small operator.","href":"/book/en/email/#row-a-data-esim-paid-in-bitcoin-for-example-silent-link"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"Paid one-time SMS reception services","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cWhen a site demands a phone number\u201d","text":"How it works: You rent a number for a few minutes to receive a single code \u00b7 Trade-offs: The number then goes to someone else, who could use it to take over the account. Quality varies widely, and many sites forbid this in their terms.","href":"/book/en/email/#row-paid-one-time-sms-reception-services"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"Free public \"receive SMS online\" websites","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cWhen a site demands a phone number\u201d","text":"How it works: Shared numbers whose incoming messages are displayed on a web page \u00b7 Trade-offs: Everyone sees your code. The numbers are recycled and mostly blocked already. Only for something that does not matter at all.","href":"/book/en/email/#row-free-public-receive-sms-online-websites"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"When a site demands a phone number","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 Compare","text":"Your phone number is an even stronger identifier than your email address. You keep it for years, it links your accounts together, it attracts spam calls, and it is the target of SIM-swap attacks (chapter 2). First check whether the field is truly mandatory. When it is, you have several options, none of them perfect. Option How it works Trade-offs A second SIM or eSIM, used only for sign-ups A real mobile number from an ordinary operator, kept apart from your personal one Accepted everywhere. Prepaid numbers expire if you do not top them up. Many EU countries require an identity document to buy a SIM, including Lithuania for new prepaid cards since January 2025. The rules vary, and this option separates your numbers without making you anonymous. Virtual (VoIP) number apps: JMP.chat, Hushed, MySudo A paid number that works over the internet, inside an app JMP.chat is open source but offers only US and Canadian numbers. Hushed (closed source) offers US, Canadian and UK numbers. MySudo (closed source) can be installed in only a few countries, so check before you count on it. Many services refuse VoIP numbers, and a European site may refuse a foreign one. A data eSIM paid in bitcoin, for example Silent.link No account. Some plans include a US or UK mobile number that can receive SMS Costs more than an ordinary SIM. The number is leased by the year, and it is gone if you do not renew it. A single small operator. Paid one-time SMS reception services You rent a number for a few minutes to receive a single code The number then goes to someone else, who could use it to take over the account. Quality varies widely, and many sites forbid this in their terms. Free public \"receive SMS online\" websites Shared numbers whose incoming messages are displayed on a web page Everyone sees your code. The numbers are recycled and mostly blocked already. Only for something that does not matter at all. These options have limits too. If you lose access to the number, you can lose the account, so never use a number you do not control for your bank, for two-factor authentication or for any important account. Respect the terms of service of the site and the law of your country. The goal is simply to avoid handing your personal number to every shop and app.","href":"/book/en/email/#when-a-site-demands-a-phone-number"},{"type":"step","part":1,"title":"Step 1: Open the new mailbox","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Open the new mailbox. Choose an address you can keep for years. Protect it with a long, unique password from your password manager and a second factor (chapter 2). Enable the recovery phrase and write it on paper. It can restore both access and encrypted data; an email or SMS password reset alone cannot decrypt your old mail. Check the provider's recovery instructions before you rely on it.","href":"/book/en/email/#step-1"},{"type":"step","part":1,"title":"Step 2: Set up the alias service","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Set up the alias service. Create the account, connect it to your new mailbox, and install its browser extension (and its phone app, if it has one).","href":"/book/en/email/#step-2"},{"type":"step","part":1,"title":"Step 3: Move your most important accounts first","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Move your most important accounts first. These are your bank, government services, your phone operator, your Apple or Google account and your password manager. Change the email address in each of them. Use your real new address or a dedicated alias, as you prefer, but note your choice in your password manager.","href":"/book/en/email/#step-3"},{"type":"step","part":1,"title":"Step 4: Forward the old mailbox","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Forward the old mailbox. In the settings of your old mailbox, turn on automatic forwarding to the new one. Most encrypted providers also offer an import tool that copies your old messages and contacts.","href":"/book/en/email/#step-4"},{"type":"step","part":1,"title":"Step 5: Move the rest as it comes","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Move the rest as it comes. Each time a forwarded message arrives from a service you still use, change the address there to a new alias. Delete accounts you no longer use.","href":"/book/en/email/#step-5"},{"type":"step","part":1,"title":"Step 6: Create a new alias for every signup","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Create a new alias for every signup. A shop, a newsletter or a wifi portal each gets its own address. Name the alias after the service, so that a leak is easy to trace.","href":"/book/en/email/#step-6"},{"type":"step","part":1,"title":"Step 7: Keep the old address for at least a year","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Keep the old address for at least a year. Do not delete it, because forgotten accounts, old contacts and password resets will keep arriving there for a long time.","href":"/book/en/email/#step-7"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Step by step","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 Tutorial","text":"The example uses Proton Mail for the mailbox and SimpleLogin for the aliases, because they are a common first step. Both belong to Proton, which is convenient but means your mailbox and your aliases depend on a single company. If you prefer to spread the risk, keep the mailbox and choose addy.io for the aliases, or keep SimpleLogin and choose another mailbox, since the steps are the same. Open the new mailbox. Choose an address you can keep for years. Protect it with a long, unique password from your password manager and a second factor (chapter 2). Enable the recovery phrase and write it on paper. It can restore both access and encrypted data; an email or SMS password reset alone cannot decrypt your old mail. Check the provider's recovery instructions before you rely on it. Set up the alias service. Create the account, connect it to your new mailbox, and install its browser extension (and its phone app, if it has one). Move your most important accounts first. These are your bank, government services, your phone operator, your Apple or Google account and your password manager. Change the email address in each of them. Use your real new address or a dedicated alias, as you prefer, but note your choice in your password manager. Forward the old mailbox. In the settings of your old mailbox, turn on automatic forwarding to the new one. Most encrypted providers also offer an import tool that copies your old messages and contacts. Move the rest as it comes. Each time a forwarded message arrives from a service you still use, change the address there to a new alias. Delete accounts you no longer use. Create a new alias for every signup. A shop, a newsletter or a wifi portal each gets its own address. Name the alias after the service, so that a leak is easy to trace. Keep the old address for at least a year. Do not delete it, because forgotten accounts, old contacts and password resets will keep arriving there for a long time.","href":"/book/en/email/#step-by-step"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Mistakes to avoid","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 Watch out","text":"Thinking an encrypted provider makes all your email private. It protects your mailbox, while a message sent to an ordinary mailbox stays readable at the other end. Deleting the old address too early. Some providers recycle abandoned addresses, and a stranger could then receive the password resets of accounts you forgot. Losing the recovery phrase. A password reset alone may leave your old messages unreadable. Set up and protect a data recovery method in advance; Proton supports a recovery phrase and other methods. Letting your own domain expire. Whoever registers it after you receives your mail and can reset your accounts. Turn on automatic renewal and keep the payment card up to date. Using the same alias for everything. An alias shared between 10 services is just a second real address, since the value comes from having a different alias for each service. Putting the bank behind an alias service you do not fully trust. For the few accounts that matter most, your real address at your own provider is a perfectly good choice.","href":"/book/en/email/#mistakes-to-avoid"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Go further","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 Go further","text":"Compare providers in detail: Privacy Guides, \"Email Services\" and \"Email Aliasing\". Check whether your address has already leaked: Have I Been Pwned. Next chapter: your network.","href":"/book/en/email/#go-further"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Sources","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 References","text":"Proton Account recovery explained, Proton Support, checked 22 October 2026. As G Suite gains traction in the enterprise, G Suite's Gmail and consumer Gmail to more closely align, Google, 23 June 2017. Message by Ledger's CEO: update on the July data breach, Ledger, 21 December 2020. Important clarifications regarding arrest of climate activist, Proton, 6 September 2021. Address verification with trusted keys, Proton, consulted in September 2026. Tuta introduces key verification, Tuta, 26 August 2025. End to anonymity: Lithuania to require users to register phone SIM cards, LRT, 14 November 2024.","href":"/book/en/email/#sources"},{"type":"action","part":1,"title":"Open a mailbox at a provider funded by its users","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 Checklist action, 1 hour","text":"","href":"/book/en/email/#action-email-open-mailbox"},{"type":"action","part":1,"title":"Set up an alias service and its browser extension","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 Checklist action, 1 hour","text":"","href":"/book/en/email/#action-email-alias-service"},{"type":"action","part":1,"title":"Create your first alias and use it for your next signup","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/email/#action-email-first-alias"},{"type":"action","part":1,"title":"Move your 5 most important accounts to the new address","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 Checklist action, 1 hour","text":"","href":"/book/en/email/#action-email-move-5-accounts"},{"type":"action","part":1,"title":"Forward the old mailbox and move the remaining accounts","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 Checklist action, weekend","text":"","href":"/book/en/email/#action-email-forward-old-mailbox"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Your checklist","kicker":"Ch. 4 \u00b7 Act","text":"Open a mailbox at a provider funded by its usersSet up an alias service and its browser extensionCreate your first alias and use it for your next signupMove your 5 most important accounts to the new addressForward the old mailbox and move the remaining accounts","href":"/book/en/email/#your-checklist"},{"type":"chapter","part":1,"title":"Your network","kicker":"Chapter 5 \u00b7 Privacy","text":"In brief \u00b7 Every network on the way can watch \u00b7 The padlock hides pages, not destinations \u00b7 What a VPN does, and what it does not \u00b7 A no-logs promise needs evidence \u00b7 Choose your network protection \u00b7 Step by step \u00b7 Mistakes to avoid \u00b7 Go further \u00b7 Sources \u00b7 Your checklist","href":"/book/en/network/"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"In brief","kicker":"Ch. 5 \u00b7 In brief","text":"Every connection you make passes through equipment that belongs to someone else, whether it is your internet provider, the wifi of a cafe or the network at work. HTTPS already hides what you read and type, while networks can often still identify the sites you visit. A VPN hides that list from your provider and hides your IP address from websites, by moving your trust to the VPN company, and it does not make you anonymous. Tor and mixnets go further, at the cost of speed.","href":"/book/en/network/#in-brief"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Every network on the way can watch","kicker":"Ch. 5 \u00b7 Understand","text":"Between your device and a website, your traffic crosses several networks, and each of them can observe it without any hacker being involved, because this is how networks work by default. Your internet provider sees every connection you make from home or from your phone, and in many countries the law makes it keep records of them. Public wifi in a cafe, a hotel or an airport is run by someone you do not know, and so is any network with the same name set up by a stranger. The network at work or at school is often monitored as a matter of policy. Every site you visit sees your IP address, the number that identifies your connection. It reveals your provider and your approximate location, and it lets sites and advertisers recognise you from one visit to the next, even without cookies. In the European Union, a 2006 directive obliged providers to keep traffic and location data for 6 months to 2 years. The Court of Justice declared it invalid on 8 April 2014, noting that such data \"may provide very precise information on the private lives\" of the people concerned. National retention rules still exist in several member states.","href":"/book/en/network/#every-network-on-the-way-can-watch"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"The padlock hides pages, not destinations","kicker":"Ch. 5 \u00b7 Understand","text":"HTTPS, the padlock in your browser, encrypts the link between you and the site, and almost all sites use it today. The networks in between can no longer see the pages you read, what you type or your passwords. They still see destination IP addresses, timing and data volumes, and often the domain name (the site, not the exact page). Encrypted DNS combined with Encrypted Client Hello, where supported, can hide that name too, though IP addresses and traffic patterns can still reveal destinations. DNS is the phone book of the internet. Before your device can reach proofconference.com, it has to ask a DNS server which IP address is behind that name. By default the server belongs to your internet provider and the question often travels unencrypted, so your provider holds the list of every name you looked up even before the page loads. Encrypted DNS sends those questions through an encrypted channel to a server you choose. Good free options are Quad9 (a Swiss non-profit that also blocks known malicious sites), Mullvad DNS (no account needed, optional ad blocking) and NextDNS (a company, very configurable, free up to a monthly limit). It is a small and useful step, although the network still sees the IP addresses you connect to.","href":"/book/en/network/#the-padlock-hides-pages-not-destinations"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"What a VPN does, and what it does not","kicker":"Ch. 5 \u00b7 Understand","text":"A VPN builds an encrypted tunnel from your device to a server run by the VPN company, and all your traffic goes through it, DNS included. Your provider or the cafe's wifi only sees that you use a VPN, and websites see the address of the VPN server. The VPN company now sees what your provider used to see, so a VPN moves your trust from a company you did not choose to one you did. It does not make you anonymous either, since cookies and browser fingerprinting (chapter 3) still work, and Google or Facebook know who you are as soon as you log in. Tor removes the single trusted company by sending your traffic through 3 relays run by independent volunteers. The first knows who you are but not where you go, the last knows the opposite, and no single relay sees both ends. A mixnet also uses several hops, mixes your packets with those of other users, delays them and adds fake \"cover\" traffic. The design aims to make it much harder, even for an observer of the whole network, to match what enters with what leaves. It is slower and less tested in practice than Tor.","href":"/book/en/network/#what-a-vpn-does-and-what-it-does-not"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"A no-logs promise needs evidence","kicker":"Ch. 5 \u00b7 Understand","text":"With a VPN, the confidentiality of your destinations rests on 1 company, which could log your connections, be ordered to do so, or be breached. A no-logs policy is a promise, and 2 kinds of evidence support it. Audits are the first, such as the no-logs audit that Securitum has carried out at Proton VPN every year since 2022, or the security audit that IVPN orders from Cure53 each year. Legal cases are the second. On 18 April 2023, Swedish police came to Mullvad's office in Gothenburg with a search warrant and, according to the company, left without customer data because none existed. Both are snapshots, and neither shows what a server records today. Multi-hop, offered by several VPNs, chains 2 servers so that a compromised server learns less, but when both belong to the same company the trust has not moved. Decentralising it takes independent operators, as with the 3 volunteer relays of Tor or the independently run servers of NymVPN. Decentralised does not mean verified, however, since nobody audits each relay or operator, and the protection rests on the low chance that they cooperate. You still trust the publisher of the software, and you pay in speed.","href":"/book/en/network/#a-no-logs-promise-needs-evidence"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"Mullvad VPN","kicker":"Ch. 5 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your network protection\u201d","text":"Who runs it: A single company, Sweden \u00b7 Who sees your traffic: Mullvad sees your IP address and your destinations. Its evidence is a series of audits and a 2023 police search that seized nothing. \u00b7 Payment: Flat price of 5 euros a month. Card, PayPal, bank transfer, cash by post, bitcoin (Lightning included), monero \u00b7 Trade-offs: No email needed, the account is a random number. Fast, audited. No free tier.","href":"/book/en/network/#row-mullvad-vpn"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"IVPN","kicker":"Ch. 5 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your network protection\u201d","text":"Who runs it: A single company, Gibraltar \u00b7 Who sees your traffic: IVPN sees your IP address and your destinations. Its evidence is a no-logs audit in 2019, then yearly security audits. \u00b7 Payment: Paid. Card, PayPal, cash, bitcoin (Lightning included), monero \u00b7 Trade-offs: No email needed. Audited every year, very transparent about its limits. Smaller server network.","href":"/book/en/network/#row-ivpn"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"Proton VPN","kicker":"Ch. 5 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your network protection\u201d","text":"Who runs it: A single company, Switzerland \u00b7 Who sees your traffic: Proton sees your IP address and your destinations. Its evidence is a yearly no-logs audit by Securitum since 2022. \u00b7 Payment: Free tier without data limit on 1 device, paid plans. Card, PayPal, bank transfer, cash, bitcoin (only from an existing account) \u00b7 Trade-offs: Audited. The free tier is honest but slower, with few countries. The account needs an email address.","href":"/book/en/network/#row-proton-vpn"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"NymVPN","kicker":"Ch. 5 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your network protection\u201d","text":"Who runs it: Nym, Switzerland. Servers run by independent operators \u00b7 Who sees your traffic: The entry operator sees your IP address, the exit operator your destinations. Nym publishes the app. Operators are not audited. \u00b7 Payment: Paid. Card, cash by post, bitcoin (Lightning included), monero and other cryptocurrencies \u00b7 Trade-offs: No email needed, the account is a 24-word phrase. Audited in 2024. A fast 2-hop mode and a much slower 5-hop mixnet mode. A younger product with a shorter track record. PROOF speaker Alexis Roussel is COO of Nym.","href":"/book/en/network/#row-nymvpn"},{"type":"tool","part":1,"title":"Tor Browser / Orbot","kicker":"Ch. 5 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your network protection\u201d","text":"Who runs it: A non-profit and thousands of volunteer relays \u00b7 Who sees your traffic: The entry relay sees your IP address, the exit relay your destinations. No single relay sees both. Relays are not audited. \u00b7 Payment: Free \u00b7 Trade-offs: The strongest protection of your IP address for browsing. Slow, and some sites block it or show endless verification puzzles. Orbot sends the traffic of phone apps through Tor.","href":"/book/en/network/#row-tor-browser-orbot"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Choose your network protection","kicker":"Ch. 5 \u00b7 Compare","text":"The apps of every option below are open source, and each of the 4 VPN companies has published at least 1 independent security audit. They differ in who you have to trust, in speed and in how you can pay. Prices and payment methods were checked on the official sites in September 2026. Tool Who runs it Who sees your traffic Payment Trade-offs Mullvad VPN A single company, Sweden Mullvad sees your IP address and your destinations. Its evidence is a series of audits and a 2023 police search that seized nothing. Flat price of 5 euros a month. Card, PayPal, bank transfer, cash by post, bitcoin (Lightning included), monero No email needed, the account is a random number. Fast, audited. No free tier. IVPN A single company, Gibraltar IVPN sees your IP address and your destinations. Its evidence is a no-logs audit in 2019, then yearly security audits. Paid. Card, PayPal, cash, bitcoin (Lightning included), monero No email needed. Audited every year, very transparent about its limits. Smaller server network. Proton VPN A single company, Switzerland Proton sees your IP address and your destinations. Its evidence is a yearly no-logs audit by Securitum since 2022. Free tier without data limit on 1 device, paid plans. Card, PayPal, bank transfer, cash, bitcoin (only from an existing account) Audited. The free tier is honest but slower, with few countries. The account needs an email address. NymVPN Nym, Switzerland. Servers run by independent operators The entry operator sees your IP address, the exit operator your destinations. Nym publishes the app. Operators are not audited. Paid. Card, cash by post, bitcoin (Lightning included), monero and other cryptocurrencies No email needed, the account is a 24-word phrase. Audited in 2024. A fast 2-hop mode and a much slower 5-hop mixnet mode. A younger product with a shorter track record. PROOF speaker Alexis Roussel is COO of Nym. Tor Browser / Orbot A non-profit and thousands of volunteer relays The entry relay sees your IP address, the exit relay your destinations. No single relay sees both. Relays are not audited. Free The strongest protection of your IP address for browsing. Slow, and some sites block it or show endless verification puzzles. Orbot sends the traffic of phone apps through Tor. If you need a VPN, choose an operator from this list whose trade-offs suit you, including a reputable free tier where it meets your needs. Keep Tor Browser for browsing you want to separate from your usual connection and accounts; it cannot guarantee anonymity. \"Free VPN\" apps deserve caution, because running servers costs money and the traffic of users who do not pay often becomes the product through advertising, tracking or resale of data. \"Best VPN\" ranking sites deserve the same caution. Most are paid a commission for each subscription they sell, and some belong to the same groups as the VPNs they rank. In March 2021, Kape Technologies bought Webselenese, the publisher of the review sites vpnMentor and Wizcase, for 149.1 million dollars, and vpnMentor states on its own site that Kape also owns ExpressVPN, CyberGhost and Private Internet Access.","href":"/book/en/network/#choose-your-network-protection"},{"type":"step","part":1,"title":"Step 1: Install the app","kicker":"Ch. 5 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Install the app from the official website of the provider or from the official store, never from a link in an advertisement.","href":"/book/en/network/#step-1"},{"type":"step","part":1,"title":"Step 2: Create the account","kicker":"Ch. 5 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Create the account. Mullvad gives you an account number and asks for nothing else. Store it in your password manager, then add time with the payment method you prefer.","href":"/book/en/network/#step-2"},{"type":"step","part":1,"title":"Step 3: Connect","kicker":"Ch. 5 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Connect. Choose a server in your own country or a neighbouring one for the best speed. A distant country is only useful if you need to appear there.","href":"/book/en/network/#step-3"},{"type":"step","part":1,"title":"Step 4: Turn on the kill switch","kicker":"Ch. 5 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Turn on the kill switch. This setting blocks all traffic if the tunnel drops, so that nothing goes out beside it. In Mullvad it is always active, and on computers an extra \"lockdown mode\" also blocks the internet when you disconnect on purpose. In other apps, look for \"kill switch\" in the settings. On Android, the system VPN settings also offer \"Always-on VPN\" and \"Block connections without VPN\".","href":"/book/en/network/#step-4"},{"type":"step","part":1,"title":"Step 5: Connect automatically","kicker":"Ch. 5 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Connect automatically. In the settings, turn on launch at start-up and auto-connect. Some apps, such as IVPN and Proton VPN, let you mark your home wifi as trusted and connect automatically on every other network.","href":"/book/en/network/#step-5"},{"type":"step","part":1,"title":"Step 6: Set encrypted DNS for the moments without VPN","kicker":"Ch. 5 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Set encrypted DNS for the moments without VPN. On Android, in the network settings, find \"Private DNS\" and enter the host name of the service you chose, for example dns.quad9.net. On iPhone and on computers, the chosen service gives you a profile or an app to install. In your browser, turn on \"secure DNS\" in the privacy settings.","href":"/book/en/network/#step-6"},{"type":"step","part":1,"title":"Step 7: Check for leaks","kicker":"Ch. 5 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Check for leaks. With the VPN on, open mullvad.net/check or dnsleaktest.com. The IP address shown must belong to the VPN. The DNS servers shown belong either to the VPN or, if step 6 is active on that device or browser, to the encrypted DNS service you chose (Quad9, for example), whose questions then travel inside the tunnel. Both results are fine. A leak is when the name of your internet provider appears. This is the step that replaces trust with verification for what leaves your device, although no test of this kind shows what the VPN's own servers record.","href":"/book/en/network/#step-7"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Step by step","kicker":"Ch. 5 \u00b7 Tutorial","text":"The example uses Mullvad, and the same logic applies to the others. Install the app from the official website of the provider or from the official store, never from a link in an advertisement. Create the account. Mullvad gives you an account number and asks for nothing else. Store it in your password manager, then add time with the payment method you prefer. Connect. Choose a server in your own country or a neighbouring one for the best speed. A distant country is only useful if you need to appear there. Turn on the kill switch. This setting blocks all traffic if the tunnel drops, so that nothing goes out beside it. In Mullvad it is always active, and on computers an extra \"lockdown mode\" also blocks the internet when you disconnect on purpose. In other apps, look for \"kill switch\" in the settings. On Android, the system VPN settings also offer \"Always-on VPN\" and \"Block connections without VPN\". Connect automatically. In the settings, turn on launch at start-up and auto-connect. Some apps, such as IVPN and Proton VPN, let you mark your home wifi as trusted and connect automatically on every other network. Set encrypted DNS for the moments without VPN. On Android, in the network settings, find \"Private DNS\" and enter the host name of the service you chose, for example dns.quad9.net. On iPhone and on computers, the chosen service gives you a profile or an app to install. In your browser, turn on \"secure DNS\" in the privacy settings. Check for leaks. With the VPN on, open mullvad.net/check or dnsleaktest.com. The IP address shown must belong to the VPN. The DNS servers shown belong either to the VPN or, if step 6 is active on that device or browser, to the encrypted DNS service you chose (Quad9, for example), whose questions then travel inside the tunnel. Both results are fine. A leak is when the name of your internet provider appears. This is the step that replaces trust with verification for what leaves your device, although no test of this kind shows what the VPN's own servers record.","href":"/book/en/network/#step-by-step"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Mistakes to avoid","kicker":"Ch. 5 \u00b7 Watch out","text":"Believing a VPN makes you anonymous. It hides your IP address from sites and your traffic from your provider, while cookies, fingerprinting and your own logins still identify you. Choosing a VPN because it is free or because a ranking site said so. Look for open-source apps, independent audits, a clear owner and a simple paid business model. Leaving the kill switch off. Tunnels drop, especially when your phone moves between wifi and mobile data, and without a kill switch your traffic silently goes back to the normal route. Logging in to personal accounts inside Tor Browser. Once you sign in, the site knows who you are and the extra hops no longer hide you from it. Thinking the padlock means the site is honest. HTTPS protects the connection, and a phishing site can have a padlock too. Forgetting that the rules still apply. A VPN is a legal privacy tool in the European Union, and what is illegal without it is still illegal with it. Some countries outside the EU restrict VPNs, so check the local rules before you travel.","href":"/book/en/network/#mistakes-to-avoid"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Go further","kicker":"Ch. 5 \u00b7 Go further","text":"Compare VPNs without paid rankings: Privacy Guides, \"VPN Services\" and \"DNS Resolvers\" (privacyguides.org). Understand and download Tor: the Tor Project (torproject.org). There is no official Tor Browser for iPhone, where the Tor Project recommends Onion Browser, with weaker protections than on a computer. Choosing a VPN that fits your situation: Surveillance Self-Defense (ssd.eff.org). Next chapter: Part 2 begins, with keys instead of accounts.","href":"/book/en/network/#go-further"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Sources","kicker":"Ch. 5 \u00b7 References","text":"Firefox DNS over HTTPS, including ECH, Mozilla Support, checked 22 October 2026. Press release No 54/14: The Court of Justice declares the Data Retention Directive to be invalid, Court of Justice of the European Union, 8 April 2014. Kape acquires Israeli co Webselenese for $149m, Globes, 8 March 2021. About us (ownership disclosure), vpnMentor, consulted in September 2026. Proton VPN passes 5th annual external audit of no-logs policy, Proton VPN, updated 16 June 2026. Mullvad VPN was subject to a search warrant. Customer data not compromised, Mullvad VPN, 20 April 2023. Annual security audit scheduled for 2026, IVPN, 19 June 2026. Nym response to Cure53 security audit, Nym, July 2024. Lightning payments, Mullvad VPN, 5 August 2025. Installing Tor Browser (iOS section), Tor Project, consulted in September 2026.","href":"/book/en/network/#sources"},{"type":"action","part":1,"title":"Turn on encrypted DNS on your phone and in your browser","kicker":"Ch. 5 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/network/#action-network-encrypted-dns"},{"type":"action","part":1,"title":"Choose and install a VPN from a trustworthy operator","kicker":"Ch. 5 \u00b7 Checklist action, 1 hour","text":"","href":"/book/en/network/#action-network-install-vpn"},{"type":"action","part":1,"title":"Turn on the kill switch and auto-connect, then check for leaks","kicker":"Ch. 5 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/network/#action-network-kill-switch-leaks"},{"type":"action","part":1,"title":"Install Tor Browser and try it once","kicker":"Ch. 5 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/network/#action-network-try-tor-browser"},{"type":"action","part":1,"title":"Remove VPN apps whose operator or privacy practices you cannot trust","kicker":"Ch. 5 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/network/#action-network-remove-free-vpn"},{"type":"section","part":1,"title":"Your checklist","kicker":"Ch. 5 \u00b7 Act","text":"Turn on encrypted DNS on your phone and in your browserChoose and install a VPN from a trustworthy operatorTurn on the kill switch and auto-connect, then check for leaksInstall Tor Browser and try it onceRemove VPN apps whose operator or privacy practices you cannot trust","href":"/book/en/network/#your-checklist"},{"type":"page","part":2,"title":"Proof of Autonomy","kicker":"Part 2 \u00b7 Autonomy \u00b7 Opening essay","text":"Why autonomy matters \u00b7 Sources","href":"/book/en/autonomy/"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Your digital life is rented","kicker":"Part 2 opening \u00b7 Why autonomy matters","text":"Your email address belongs to your mail provider, your photos sit on the servers of a company, and your contacts, your followers and 10 years of conversations live inside platforms. What you have is an account, which means a permission to use all of this, and a permission can be withdrawn. It happens in ordinary ways. In August 2022, the New York Times told the story of a father in San Francisco who had photographed his sick toddler for the doctor. An automated system flagged the pictures, Google disabled his account, and he lost his email, his contacts, his photos and his phone number. The police cleared him, and Google still refused to give the account back. Other losses are quieter. A platform changes its rules, a price doubles, an algorithm changes and the audience you built over years stops seeing your posts. A service shuts down, as Google Reader did on 1 July 2013 and the consumer version of Google+ on 2 April 2019, and everything built on it goes with it. None of this requires bad intentions, because when you rent, someone else decides and you adapt.","href":"/book/en/autonomy/#your-digital-life-is-rented"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Why alternatives to Google and Big Tech matter","kicker":"Part 2 opening \u00b7 Why autonomy matters","text":"A handful of companies now sit between you and almost everything you do, from search, mail and maps to the system on your phone, the store where you get your apps, payments and social networks. Most of them are very good at what they do, and the difficulty lies elsewhere, in concentration. A company that carries so much becomes a single point of failure. On 19 July 2024, a faulty update from the security company CrowdStrike crashed about 8.5 million Windows computers, according to Microsoft, and airlines, banks and hospitals were disrupted on the same day. The same company becomes a single point of censorship, since a decision made by it or imposed on it can remove an app or a voice for millions of people. It also becomes a single point of surveillance, with 1 database that knows what you search, where you go, who you write to and what you buy. Using alternatives does not mean leaving everything tomorrow. Alternatives only exist as long as some people use them, fund them and report their bugs, so each time you choose one you help keep it alive for everybody, and you give yourself an exit.","href":"/book/en/autonomy/#why-alternatives-to-google-and-big-tech-matter"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"When a law can order your services to scan","kicker":"Part 2 opening \u00b7 Why autonomy matters","text":"The regulation that the European Commission proposed on 11 May 2022, known as \"Chat Control\", would allow authorities to order a messaging service to detect child sexual abuse material in private communications. For the encrypted messengers of chapter 1, that would in practice mean analysing messages on your device before they are encrypted. As of September 2026 the text is still being negotiated, and the round of 29 June 2026 ended without agreement. The temporary rule that lets providers scan voluntarily shows how such a file moves, since the Parliament refused to extend it on 26 March 2026 and it lapsed after 3 April, then the Council had it reinstated on 23 July 2026 until 3 April 2028, this time without end-to-end encrypted communications. Protecting children is a legitimate aim, and the debate is about the method. Such an order is addressed to a company, which complies, contests it or leaves the market. A protocol run by many independent operators, and open source software that you can run, fork or replace, offer no single desk where it can be served, and leave you a working alternative if a service you depend on has to change what it does.","href":"/book/en/autonomy/#when-a-law-can-order-your-services-to-scan"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Sovereignty is a practical word","kicker":"Part 2 opening \u00b7 Why autonomy matters","text":"Sovereignty sounds like a big political word. Here it means the ability to say no and the ability to leave, and in practice it rests on 3 things. You hold the keys. Your identity and your access depend on a secret that only you have, instead of an account that a company can close. Your data can move. It is stored in open formats that you can export and open elsewhere, so changing service is an inconvenience, not a catastrophe. Your tools are open source, as far as possible. When the code is public, anyone can copy it and continue it, which makes it much harder to take away from you, even if its authors give up or are bought. This book says so when a tool is not fully open source. Behind these 3 things sits the question that runs through this book, which is who the trusted third party is, and whether you can check what it does. An account places that trust in a single company, whereas keys, open formats and public code either remove the third party or let you verify it and replace it, usually at the price of some convenience. The theme of the conference, \"Take back your sovereignty\", describes this position, which has little to do with isolation or distrust. It is the situation of someone who stays by choice and could leave tomorrow. Decentralised, open technology has also been tested in crises, when central systems fail or are turned against people.","href":"/book/en/autonomy/#sovereignty-is-a-practical-word"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"What you can do about it","kicker":"Part 2 opening \u00b7 Why autonomy matters","text":"Nobody replaces 10 years of habits in a weekend, and nobody needs to. Autonomy is built 1 service at a time, starting where the dependence worries you most or where the change is easiest. You do not have to be absolute either. Keeping a Google account for the 2 things you really need it for, while your mail, your photos and your contacts live elsewhere, is already a very different situation from having everything in the same place. The 6 chapters of this part go from the idea to the practice: Keys instead of accounts: how a secret that only you hold can replace a login that a company controls. Nostr: an open network without a company in charge: an identity and an audience that do not depend on any single platform. Replacing Big Tech services, one at a time: search, maps, cloud, documents, photos, with several options for each. Taking back control of your phone: the device that knows the most about you, and what you can change on it. Hosting your own services: for those who want to go further, your own small server at home. AI without giving yourself away: what AI assistants learn about you, and how to run one on your own device. Chapters 6 and 7 belong together. The others are independent, so you can start with the one that fits your life today.","href":"/book/en/autonomy/#what-you-can-do-about-it"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Why autonomy matters","kicker":"Part 2 opening","text":"Your digital life is rented Your email address belongs to your mail provider, your photos sit on the servers of a company, and your contacts, your followers and 10 years of conversations live inside platforms. What you have is an account, which means a permission to use all of this, and a permission can be withdrawn. It happens in ordinary ways. In August 2022, the New York Times told the story of a father in San Francisco who had photographed his sick toddler for the doctor. An automated system flagged the pictures, Google disabled his account, and he lost his email, his contacts, his photos and his phone number. The police cleared him, and Google still refused to give the account back. Other losses are quieter. A platform changes its rules, a price doubles, an algorithm changes and the audience you built over years stops seeing your posts. A service shuts down, as Google Reader did on 1 July 2013 and the consumer version of Google+ on 2 April 2019, and everything built on it goes with it. None of this requires bad intentions, because when you rent, someone else decides and you adapt. Why alternatives to Google and Big Tech matter A handful of companies now sit between you and almost everything you do, from search, mail and maps to the system on your phone, the store where you get your apps, payments and social networks. Most of them are very good at what they do, and the difficulty lies elsewhere, in concentration. A company that carries so much becomes a single point of failure. On 19 July 2024, a faulty update from the security company CrowdStrike crashed about 8.5 million Windows computers, according to Microsoft, and airlines, banks and hospitals were disrupted on the same day. The same company becomes a single point of censorship, since a decision made by it or imposed on it can remove an app or a voice for millions of people. It also becomes a single point of surveillance, with 1 database that knows what you search, where you go, who you write to and what you buy. Using alternatives does not mean leaving everything tomorrow. Alternatives only exist as long as some people use them, fund them and report their bugs, so each time you choose one you help keep it alive for everybody, and you give yourself an exit. When a law can order your services to scan The regulation that the European Commission proposed on 11 May 2022, known as \"Chat Control\", would allow authorities to order a messaging service to detect child sexual abuse material in private communications. For the encrypted messengers of chapter 1, that would in practice mean analysing messages on your device before they are encrypted. As of September 2026 the text is still being negotiated, and the round of 29 June 2026 ended without agreement. The temporary rule that lets providers scan voluntarily shows how such a file moves, since the Parliament refused to extend it on 26 March 2026 and it lapsed after 3 April, then the Council had it reinstated on 23 July 2026 until 3 April 2028, this time without end-to-end encrypted communications. Protecting children is a legitimate aim, and the debate is about the method. Such an order is addressed to a company, which complies, contests it or leaves the market. A protocol run by many independent operators, and open source software that you can run, fork or replace, offer no single desk where it can be served, and leave you a working alternative if a service you depend on has to change what it does. Sovereignty is a practical word Sovereignty sounds like a big political word. Here it means the ability to say no and the ability to leave, and in practice it rests on 3 things. You hold the keys. Your identity and your access depend on a secret that only you have, instead of an account that a company can close. Your data can move. It is stored in open formats that you can export and open elsewhere, so changing service is an inconvenience, not a catastrophe. Your tools are open source, as far as possible. When the code is public, anyone can copy it and continue it, which makes it much harder to take away from you, even if its authors give up or are bought. This book says so when a tool is not fully open source. Behind these 3 things sits the question that runs through this book, which is who the trusted third party is, and whether you can check what it does. An account places that trust in a single company, whereas keys, open formats and public code either remove the third party or let you verify it and replace it, usually at the price of some convenience. The theme of the conference, \"Take back your sovereignty\", describes this position, which has little to do with isolation or distrust. It is the situation of someone who stays by choice and could leave tomorrow. Decentralised, open technology has also been tested in crises, when central systems fail or are turned against people. What you can do about it Nobody replaces 10 years of habits in a weekend, and nobody needs to. Autonomy is built 1 service at a time, starting where the dependence worries you most or where the change is easiest. You do not have to be absolute either. Keeping a Google account for the 2 things you really need it for, while your mail, your photos and your contacts live elsewhere, is already a very different situation from having everything in the same place. The 6 chapters of this part go from the idea to the practice: Keys instead of accounts: how a secret that only you hold can replace a login that a company controls. Nostr: an open network without a company in charge: an identity and an audience that do not depend on any single platform. Replacing Big Tech services, one at a time: search, maps, cloud, documents, photos, with several options for each. Taking back control of your phone: the device that knows the most about you, and what you can change on it. Hosting your own services: for those who want to go further, your own small server at home. AI without giving yourself away: what AI assistants learn about you, and how to run one on your own device. Chapters 6 and 7 belong together. The others are independent, so you can start with the one that fits your life today.","href":"/book/en/autonomy/#why-autonomy-matters"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Sources","kicker":"Part 2 opening","text":"A Dad Took Photos of His Naked Toddler for the Doctor. Google Flagged Him as a Criminal., The New York Times, 21 August 2022. Powering Down Google Reader, Official Google Reader Blog, March 2013. What you need to know about the sunset of consumer Google+ on April 2, Google Workspace Blog, 2019, checked 22 October 2026. Helping our customers through the CrowdStrike outage, Official Microsoft Blog, 20 July 2024. Proposal for a regulation laying down rules to prevent and combat child sexual abuse, COM(2022) 209, European Commission, 11 May 2022. Legislative train: combating child sexual abuse online, European Parliament, updated 1 August 2026. Combating child sexual abuse online: support for more limited ePrivacy derogation, European Parliament, 9 July 2026. Fighting child sexual abuse online: interim measure protecting children now reinstated, Council of the EU, 23 July 2026.","href":"/book/en/autonomy/#sources"},{"type":"chapter","part":2,"title":"Keys instead of accounts","kicker":"Chapter 6 \u00b7 Autonomy","text":"In brief \u00b7 An account is a revocable permission \u00b7 A secret that proves who you are \u00b7 Someone still vouches for other people's keys \u00b7 Choose where to keep your keys \u00b7 Step by step \u00b7 Mistakes to avoid \u00b7 Go further \u00b7 Sources \u00b7 Your checklist","href":"/book/en/keys/"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"In brief","kicker":"Ch. 6 \u00b7 In brief","text":"An account is a permission that a company gives you and can take back. A key is a secret that only you hold, and it works because of mathematics, whether or not a company agrees. You already use keys every day without seeing them, in your messenger and in your passkeys. The price of this freedom is that nobody can reset a key for you, so most of this chapter is about keeping your keys safe.","href":"/book/en/keys/#in-brief"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"An account is a revocable permission","kicker":"Ch. 6 \u00b7 Understand","text":"An account is a row in the database of someone else, and the company decides what that row allows. It can lock it by mistake, by an automated decision, after a change of rules, or because the service closes or leaves your country. People lose years of email and photos this way, often with no human being to talk to. An account is also only as safe as that database. In November 2022, the Irish Data Protection Commission fined Meta 265 million euros after personal data of more than 500 million Facebook users, phone numbers included, had been posted online. The \"forgot my password\" button is a second entrance, open to whoever controls your email address or your phone number. In March 2020, Europol announced the arrest of 12 people in Spain suspected of stealing more than 3 million euros by obtaining duplicates of their victims' SIM cards and receiving the banks' confirmation codes in their place. Accounts are convenient and fine for most services. For what matters most (your identity, your private conversations, your money), it is worth asking whether the thing could work without asking anyone for permission. With keys, to a large extent, it can.","href":"/book/en/keys/#an-account-is-a-revocable-permission"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"A secret that proves who you are","kicker":"Ch. 6 \u00b7 Understand","text":"A key pair is 2 very large numbers created together on your device. The private key is a secret that stays with you, and the public key is calculated from it and can be shown to anyone. Going from private to public is easy, while going back is practically impossible. A key pair does 2 things: Receiving secrets. Imagine a mailbox with a slot. Anyone who knows the address (your public key) can drop a letter in, and only the holder of the mailbox key (your private key) can open it. Signing. Your private key produces a signature that anyone can check with your public key, and that cannot be forged in practice without the private key. No central server is needed to confirm who you are, because the verification is a calculation that anyone can run. You already meet keys without knowing it. Your encrypted messenger (chapter 1) created a key pair on your phone. A passkey (chapter 2) is a key pair of which the website stores only the public half, and a Nostr identity (chapter 7) is a key pair too. Many Bitcoin wallets (Part 3) derive their keys from a backup of 12 or 24 words, while others use different formats (chapter 14). For the curious, SSH keys log administrators into servers, and PGP keys encrypt email and sign software.","href":"/book/en/keys/#a-secret-that-proves-who-you-are"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Someone still vouches for other people's keys","kicker":"Ch. 6 \u00b7 Understand","text":"A key removes the third party for 1 property, control, because nobody can sign or decrypt in your place. Authenticity stays open as soon as someone else's key is involved, since nothing in a public key says whose it is. That information has to come from somewhere, and there are 4 usual answers: a directory run by a company, as in most messengers (chapter 1), a certificate authority, which is how your browser recognises a website, a web of trust in which people sign each other's keys, as PGP users do, or a fingerprint that you compare yourself through another channel. Only the last answer has no trusted third party. In 2011 the Dutch certificate authority DigiNotar was breached, a false certificate for google.com was issued and browsers removed DigiNotar from their trusted lists. Public Certificate Transparency logs now allow anyone to see which certificates exist for a domain. Passkeys show the same trade-off for storage. Synced by Apple or Google, they are end-to-end encrypted, but reaching them from a new device goes through your account with that company, which is back in the loop for availability. A passkey on a security key depends on nobody, and it cannot be copied.","href":"/book/en/keys/#someone-still-vouches-for-other-peoples-keys"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Paper, written by hand, in 2 places","kicker":"Ch. 6 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose where to keep your keys\u201d","text":"Good for: Recovery codes, a Nostr key, a seed phrase for small amounts \u00b7 Weak point: Fire, water, and anyone who finds it \u00b7 Who you still depend on: Nobody, apart from whoever can enter those 2 places","href":"/book/en/keys/#row-paper-written-by-hand-in-2-places"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Password manager (chapter 2)","kicker":"Ch. 6 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose where to keep your keys\u201d","text":"Good for: Low-value keys and recovery codes of ordinary sites \u00b7 Weak point: It lives on connected devices. Not the place for a Bitcoin seed, and it cannot hold its own recovery codes \u00b7 Who you still depend on: The provider, for sync and app updates. The vault is end-to-end encrypted, and open source managers can be audited","href":"/book/en/keys/#row-password-manager-chapter-2"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Hardware device","kicker":"Ch. 6 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose where to keep your keys\u201d","text":"Good for: A security key for logins (YubiKey, Nitrokey, Token2, among others), a hardware wallet or signing device for Bitcoin (chapter 17) \u00b7 Weak point: Costs money, can break or be lost. You still need a backup, either a second security key, or the seed on paper or steel \u00b7 Who you still depend on: The maker of the chip and its firmware. YubiKey firmware is closed, Nitrokey firmware is open source","href":"/book/en/keys/#row-hardware-device"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Steel backup","kicker":"Ch. 6 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose where to keep your keys\u201d","text":"Good for: Seed phrases that must survive fire and water (chapter 14) \u00b7 Weak point: More expensive, and readable by whoever finds it \u00b7 Who you still depend on: Nobody, apart from whoever can reach the hiding place","href":"/book/en/keys/#row-steel-backup"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Splitting or multi-signature","kicker":"Ch. 6 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose where to keep your keys\u201d","text":"Good for: Large value, with several keys in several places, so that no single loss or theft is fatal \u00b7 Weak point: Complex. A mistake in the setup can lock you out. Learn first \u00b7 Who you still depend on: No single party. The software that coordinates the keys, and the people or places that hold them","href":"/book/en/keys/#row-splitting-or-multi-signature"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Choose where to keep your keys","kicker":"Ch. 6 \u00b7 Compare","text":"With a key there is no \"forgot my password\" button. No company can lock you out of it, and no company can let you back in. A lost key is gone, and whoever copies it can act as you. The responsibility moves from the company to you, so each key needs a home that matches its value. Where Good for Weak point Who you still depend on Paper, written by hand, in 2 places Recovery codes, a Nostr key, a seed phrase for small amounts Fire, water, and anyone who finds it Nobody, apart from whoever can enter those 2 places Password manager (chapter 2) Low-value keys and recovery codes of ordinary sites It lives on connected devices. Not the place for a Bitcoin seed, and it cannot hold its own recovery codes The provider, for sync and app updates. The vault is end-to-end encrypted, and open source managers can be audited Hardware device A security key for logins (YubiKey, Nitrokey, Token2, among others), a hardware wallet or signing device for Bitcoin (chapter 17) Costs money, can break or be lost. You still need a backup, either a second security key, or the seed on paper or steel The maker of the chip and its firmware. YubiKey firmware is closed, Nitrokey firmware is open source Steel backup Seed phrases that must survive fire and water (chapter 14) More expensive, and readable by whoever finds it Nobody, apart from whoever can reach the hiding place Splitting or multi-signature Large value, with several keys in several places, so that no single loss or theft is fatal Complex. A mistake in the setup can lock you out. Learn first No single party. The software that coordinates the keys, and the people or places that hold them A reasonable setup for most people is a password manager for low-value keys, paper in 2 places for the recovery codes that protect everything else, 2 security keys for your main accounts, and a hardware wallet with a steel backup once real savings are involved. Your situation may call for a different mix.","href":"/book/en/keys/#choose-where-to-keep-your-keys"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 1: Make an inventory","kicker":"Ch. 6 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Make an inventory. On paper, list the names of your keys and recovery codes, not the secrets themselves: the master password and recovery kit of your password manager, the two-factor recovery codes of your main accounts, your messenger PIN, the disk encryption recovery key of your computer, and later your Nostr key and your seed phrase. Passkeys cannot be written down, so note where each one lives (which phone, manager or security key) and the second way into that account.","href":"/book/en/keys/#step-1"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 2: Decide a home for each one,","kicker":"Ch. 6 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Decide a home for each one, using the table above, and write it next to the name.","href":"/book/en/keys/#step-2"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 3: Write the backups by hand","kicker":"Ch. 6 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Write the backups by hand. No photo, no screenshot, no cloud note, no office printer. Write clearly and check every character twice.","href":"/book/en/keys/#step-3"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 4: Store them in 2 locations,","kicker":"Ch. 6 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Store them in 2 locations, for example your home and the home of a relative you trust, or a bank safe deposit box if you accept its opening hours and its rules. A single fire or burglary should not be able to take both copies.","href":"/book/en/keys/#step-4"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 5: Buy 2 security keys, not 1","kicker":"Ch. 6 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Buy 2 security keys, not 1. A security key cannot be copied, so the second key is an independent way in, not a copy of the first. Register both on your main accounts (email, password manager, cloud storage) in the security settings, under \"security key\" or \"passkey\". Keep the first with you and the second with your paper backups, and keep the recovery codes of those accounts as well.","href":"/book/en/keys/#step-5"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 6: Test a recovery","kicker":"Ch. 6 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Test a recovery. Log in to an account with a recovery code, then generate fresh codes. Log in once with the second security key too, because an untested backup may fail on the day you need it. This is the step that replaces trust in a backup, and in a company's recovery process, with something you have checked yourself.","href":"/book/en/keys/#step-6"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 7: Tell a trusted person","kicker":"Ch. 6 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Tell a trusted person where things are and what to do, not the secrets themselves. A sealed letter with instructions is enough. No company can help your family recover a key after you are gone.","href":"/book/en/keys/#step-7"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 8: Review the list once a year","kicker":"Ch. 6 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Review the list once a year.","href":"/book/en/keys/#step-8"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Step by step","kicker":"Ch. 6 \u00b7 Tutorial","text":"The example uses the keys most readers already have, and the logic is the same for every key you will create later. Make an inventory. On paper, list the names of your keys and recovery codes, not the secrets themselves: the master password and recovery kit of your password manager, the two-factor recovery codes of your main accounts, your messenger PIN, the disk encryption recovery key of your computer, and later your Nostr key and your seed phrase. Passkeys cannot be written down, so note where each one lives (which phone, manager or security key) and the second way into that account. Decide a home for each one, using the table above, and write it next to the name. Write the backups by hand. No photo, no screenshot, no cloud note, no office printer. Write clearly and check every character twice. Store them in 2 locations, for example your home and the home of a relative you trust, or a bank safe deposit box if you accept its opening hours and its rules. A single fire or burglary should not be able to take both copies. Buy 2 security keys, not 1. A security key cannot be copied, so the second key is an independent way in, not a copy of the first. Register both on your main accounts (email, password manager, cloud storage) in the security settings, under \"security key\" or \"passkey\". Keep the first with you and the second with your paper backups, and keep the recovery codes of those accounts as well. Test a recovery. Log in to an account with a recovery code, then generate fresh codes. Log in once with the second security key too, because an untested backup may fail on the day you need it. This is the step that replaces trust in a backup, and in a company's recovery process, with something you have checked yourself. Tell a trusted person where things are and what to do, not the secrets themselves. A sealed letter with instructions is enough. No company can help your family recover a key after you are gone. Review the list once a year.","href":"/book/en/keys/#step-by-step"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Mistakes to avoid","kicker":"Ch. 6 \u00b7 Watch out","text":"Photographing a key or a seed phrase. Photos are copied to cloud services and can be read by other apps, which makes them a common way for a secret to leave your hands. Typing a private key into a website, or giving it to \"support\". No legitimate service asks for it, and a request of this kind is the mark of a scam. Keeping the only copy on your phone. Phones are lost, stolen and broken every day. Storing the backup next to the device. A thief or a fire then takes both at once. Hiding it so well that nobody finds it. That includes you in 5 years, and your family after you. Choosing a complex scheme too early. A split backup you do not fully understand is more dangerous than 2 sheets of paper in 2 safe places.","href":"/book/en/keys/#mistakes-to-avoid"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Go further","kicker":"Ch. 6 \u00b7 Go further","text":"Understand public key encryption: Surveillance Self-Defense, \"A Deep Dive on End-to-End Encryption\", by the Electronic Frontier Foundation (ssd.eff.org). Compare security keys and password managers: Privacy Guides (privacyguides.org). Learn about passkeys: the FIDO Alliance (fidoalliance.org). Next chapter: Nostr, social media built on keys instead of accounts.","href":"/book/en/keys/#go-further"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Sources","kicker":"Ch. 6 \u00b7 References","text":"Data Protection Commission announces decision in Facebook \"Data Scraping\" Inquiry, Data Protection Commission (Ireland), 28 November 2022. Meta hit with ~$275M GDPR penalty for Facebook data-scraping breach, TechCrunch, 28 November 2022. The SIM highjackers: how criminals are stealing millions by highjacking phone numbers, Europol, March 2020. Fraudulent *.google.com certificate, Mozilla Security Blog, 29 August 2011. Certificate Transparency, project site, consulted in September 2026. About the security of passkeys, Apple Support, consulted in September 2026.","href":"/book/en/keys/#sources"},{"type":"action","part":2,"title":"List every key and recovery code you already have","kicker":"Ch. 6 \u00b7 Checklist action, 1 hour","text":"","href":"/book/en/keys/#action-keys-list-keys-codes"},{"type":"action","part":2,"title":"Write the missing backups by hand and store them in 2 places","kicker":"Ch. 6 \u00b7 Checklist action, 1 hour","text":"","href":"/book/en/keys/#action-keys-write-backups"},{"type":"action","part":2,"title":"Test a recovery with a real recovery code","kicker":"Ch. 6 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/keys/#action-keys-test-recovery"},{"type":"action","part":2,"title":"Tell a trusted person where your backups are","kicker":"Ch. 6 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/keys/#action-keys-tell-trusted-person"},{"type":"action","part":2,"title":"Buy 2 security keys and add them to your main accounts","kicker":"Ch. 6 \u00b7 Checklist action, weekend","text":"","href":"/book/en/keys/#action-keys-buy-security-keys"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Your checklist","kicker":"Ch. 6 \u00b7 Act","text":"List every key and recovery code you already haveWrite the missing backups by hand and store them in 2 placesTest a recovery with a real recovery codeTell a trusted person where your backups areBuy 2 security keys and add them to your main accounts","href":"/book/en/keys/#your-checklist"},{"type":"chapter","part":2,"title":"Nostr: an open network without a company in charge","kicker":"Chapter 7 \u00b7 Autonomy","text":"In brief \u00b7 A public square owned by someone else \u00b7 Relays keep the record, clients are windows \u00b7 Relays cannot forge, but they can hide \u00b7 Choose your client \u00b7 Protect your nsec \u00b7 Step by step \u00b7 Mistakes to avoid \u00b7 Go further \u00b7 Sources \u00b7 Your checklist","href":"/book/en/nostr/"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"In brief","kicker":"Ch. 7 \u00b7 In brief","text":"On a platform, your profile, your posts and your audience belong to the company that runs it. Nostr is an open protocol for signed data, where your identity is a key pair that you hold and where many independent relays and apps carry what you publish, so you can change app or relay at any time and keep your name and your followers. Today it is mostly used to build alternatives to social platforms, from short posts to photos, long articles, live video, music and marketplaces. It is smaller and rougher than the big platforms, and creating an account in an app such as Primal, Damus or Amethyst takes a few minutes.","href":"/book/en/nostr/#in-brief"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"A public square owned by someone else","kicker":"Ch. 7 \u00b7 Understand","text":"A social network looks like a public square, but it works like a private building whose owner sets the rules and can change them overnight. On 18 December 2022, Twitter announced that it would remove links to Facebook, Instagram, Mastodon and Nostr and suspend accounts that promoted them, then withdrew the policy within a day after a wave of protest. The same owner can remove an account, with or without a stated reason, and years of posts and contacts go with it. It can also reduce how many people see you without telling you, a practice often called a shadow ban. In ordinary times, an algorithm tuned for time spent and for advertising decides what you see, and your reach changes whenever it does. Your audience is not yours either, because you cannot take your followers to another service, which is a large part of why leaving is so hard. What you read, like and write also feeds an advertising profile. In April 2025 the European Commission fined Meta 200 million euros under the Digital Markets Act, because users of Facebook and Instagram had to either accept the combination of their personal data for advertising or pay a subscription.","href":"/book/en/nostr/#a-public-square-owned-by-someone-else"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Relays keep the record, clients are windows","kicker":"Ch. 7 \u00b7 Understand","text":"Nostr is a protocol, like email or the web, which means that no company owns it and no single app defines it. It carries signed data of any kind, and a short post is only 1 case among others. It starts from 3 parts: Your identity is a key pair (chapter 6). The public key, which starts with npub, is your address, and you can share it freely. The private key, which starts with nsec, is your secret, because it proves that you are you. Everything you publish is a signed event. A post, a photo, a long article, a product listing or the announcement of a live stream carries a signature made with your nsec, so anyone can check that it is really yours and was not modified. Zaps are small Lightning payments (Part 3) sent directly to a person or a post. They allow you to support someone without advertising and without a platform taking a share. To see where relays and clients fit, think of everything people publish as what happens outside, in nature. Relays are what records and keeps what happens out there. They are simple servers that anyone can run, and you publish to several at once. A client is a window onto it, and windows differ. Some show almost everything, others filter or hide part of the view, and a window can be shut or even boarded up while others stay open. You can always walk to another window, because the landscape does not belong to any of them, and a new client can retrieve your signed profile, posts and contact lists from the relays that still hold them. Keep local exports of important content; relays and media hosts do not promise permanent storage. In February 2023, Apple pulled the Damus client from its App Store in China 2 days after its launch, at the request of the Chinese internet regulator. That window was boarded up for 1 country, while the relays and the other clients kept working.","href":"/book/en/nostr/#relays-keep-the-record-clients-are-windows"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Relays cannot forge, but they can hide","kicker":"Ch. 7 \u00b7 Understand","text":"A client is only a window, so it can filter spam or hide an account, which is often a service to you, but it cannot erase every copy from the network. Relays may remove events, including in response to a signed deletion request, and another client can show only the copies it can still retrieve. A relay keeps the record, so it can refuse your events, drop them later or never serve them to a given reader. It cannot write in your name or alter what you wrote, because the signature would no longer match your npub, and any client can run that check. Availability therefore depends on publishing to several relays run by different operators, and your relay list tells other clients where to find you. 2 trusted third parties remain. The first is whatever tells you that an npub belongs to the person you think. A NIP-05 address only shows that the key is listed on a domain, and its specification says that it identifies a user without verifying them, so for someone who matters, take the npub from that person directly. The second is any software that holds your nsec, because it could sign anything as you, a risk that signers, described below, reduce by keeping the key out of clients. A client can still misuse signing permissions you grant.","href":"/book/en/nostr/#relays-cannot-forge-but-they-can-hide"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Primal","kicker":"Ch. 7 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your client\u201d","text":"What it resembles: X, with the smoothest start and a built-in wallet. \u00b7 Type of content: Short posts, articles, media feeds \u00b7 Platforms: iOS, Android, web \u00b7 Where the trust sits: Primal's caching server chooses what reaches you. It is open source, and the settings accept another one.","href":"/book/en/nostr/#row-primal"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Damus","kicker":"Ch. 7 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your client\u201d","text":"What it resembles: X, close to what Twitter looked like. Simple. \u00b7 Type of content: Short posts \u00b7 Platforms: iOS \u00b7 Where the trust sits: Talks to your relays directly. The app itself holds your nsec on the phone.","href":"/book/en/nostr/#row-damus"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Amethyst","kicker":"Ch. 7 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your client\u201d","text":"What it resembles: X, with the most features, which can feel overwhelming at first. \u00b7 Type of content: Short posts, and most other kinds of events \u00b7 Platforms: Android \u00b7 Where the trust sits: Talks to your relays directly. With the Amber signer, the app never holds your nsec.","href":"/book/en/nostr/#row-amethyst"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Yakihonne","kicker":"Ch. 7 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your client\u201d","text":"What it resembles: Medium. \u00b7 Type of content: Long articles, curated content, short posts \u00b7 Platforms: iOS, Android, web \u00b7 Where the trust sits: A single company builds the apps and runs its own relays. Accepts a remote signer.","href":"/book/en/nostr/#row-yakihonne"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Olas","kicker":"Ch. 7 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your client\u201d","text":"What it resembles: Instagram. Slow release pace: the iPhone version in the store dates from March 2025, and a rewrite is in progress. \u00b7 Type of content: Photos and short videos \u00b7 Platforms: iOS, Android \u00b7 Where the trust sits: Talks to your relays directly. The image files sit on media servers, which can remove them.","href":"/book/en/nostr/#row-olas"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"zap.stream","kicker":"Ch. 7 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your client\u201d","text":"What it resembles: Twitch. \u00b7 Type of content: Live video, with chat and zaps \u00b7 Platforms: Web, iOS, Android \u00b7 Where the trust sits: Chat and announcements are signed events. The video passes through a streaming server, zap.stream's by default.","href":"/book/en/nostr/#row-zap-stream"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Fountain","kicker":"Ch. 7 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your client\u201d","text":"What it resembles: A podcast app with comments and payments to the shows. \u00b7 Type of content: Podcasts, music \u00b7 Platforms: iOS, Android \u00b7 Where the trust sits: A single company. The code is not published, and the built-in wallet is custodial.","href":"/book/en/nostr/#row-fountain"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Wavlake","kicker":"Ch. 7 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your client\u201d","text":"What it resembles: Spotify or Bandcamp, for independent artists. \u00b7 Type of content: Music, paid directly to artists \u00b7 Platforms: Web, iOS, Android \u00b7 Where the trust sits: A single company hosts the music and handles the payments to artists. The iPhone app dates from November 2025.","href":"/book/en/nostr/#row-wavlake"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Shopstr","kicker":"Ch. 7 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your client\u201d","text":"What it resembles: A classified-ads marketplace. \u00b7 Type of content: Product listings, paid in bitcoin \u00b7 Platforms: Web \u00b7 Where the trust sits: Listings are signed events. Payment goes straight to the seller, without escrow, so check who you buy from.","href":"/book/en/nostr/#row-shopstr"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"0xchat","kicker":"Ch. 7 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your client\u201d","text":"What it resembles: Telegram or WhatsApp. \u00b7 Type of content: Private and group chat \u00b7 Platforms: iOS, Android, desktop \u00b7 Where the trust sits: Relays carry the encrypted messages. A young project, without the track record of the messengers of chapter 1.","href":"/book/en/nostr/#row-0xchat"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Zapstore","kicker":"Ch. 7 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your client\u201d","text":"What it resembles: Google Play or F-Droid. \u00b7 Type of content: Android apps \u00b7 Platforms: Android \u00b7 Where the trust sits: Each release is signed with the developer's Nostr key and checked before install. You choose which catalogs and developers to trust.","href":"/book/en/nostr/#row-zapstore"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Iris, Coracle, noStrudel","kicker":"Ch. 7 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your client\u201d","text":"What it resembles: X, in a browser. Coracle gives fine control over relays, noStrudel explores everything the protocol can do. \u00b7 Type of content: Short posts, and more in noStrudel \u00b7 Platforms: Web (Iris also on mobile) \u00b7 Where the trust sits: The site that serves the code at each visit. Log in through an extension or a remote signer, not with the nsec. noStrudel can be self-hosted.","href":"/book/en/nostr/#row-iris-coracle-nostrudel"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Choose your client","kicker":"Ch. 7 \u00b7 Compare","text":"Nostr is often presented as an alternative to X, which is only 1 of its uses. Some clients work like Instagram, others like Medium, Twitch, a podcast or music app, a marketplace, a group chat or an app store, and all of them accept the same key. Be clear about what you get. The network is much smaller than the big platforms, spam exists, and some apps feel unfinished. There is no password reset, and no real deletion either, because you can ask relays to delete an event but they are free to ignore you. Treat every post as permanent and public. Apart from Fountain, whose code is not published, and part of Wavlake, the clients below publish their source code under a free licence (checked on their public repositories in September 2026). Open source does not guarantee that the version in the app store matches the public code, but it allows anyone to check. Client What it resembles Type of content Platforms Where the trust sits Primal X, with the smoothest start and a built-in wallet. Short posts, articles, media feeds iOS, Android, web Primal's caching server chooses what reaches you. It is open source, and the settings accept another one. Damus X, close to what Twitter looked like. Simple. Short posts iOS Talks to your relays directly. The app itself holds your nsec on the phone. Amethyst X, with the most features, which can feel overwhelming at first. Short posts, and most other kinds of events Android Talks to your relays directly. With the Amber signer, the app never holds your nsec. Yakihonne Medium. Long articles, curated content, short posts iOS, Android, web A single company builds the apps and runs its own relays. Accepts a remote signer. Olas Instagram. Slow release pace: the iPhone version in the store dates from March 2025, and a rewrite is in progress. Photos and short videos iOS, Android Talks to your relays directly. The image files sit on media servers, which can remove them. zap.stream Twitch. Live video, with chat and zaps Web, iOS, Android Chat and announcements are signed events. The video passes through a streaming server, zap.stream's by default. Fountain A podcast app with comments and payments to the shows. Podcasts, music iOS, Android A single company. The code is not published, and the built-in wallet is custodial. Wavlake Spotify or Bandcamp, for independent artists. Music, paid directly to artists Web, iOS, Android A single company hosts the music and handles the payments to artists. The iPhone app dates from November 2025. Shopstr A classified-ads marketplace. Product listings, paid in bitcoin Web Listings are signed events. Payment goes straight to the seller, without escrow, so check who you buy from. 0xchat Telegram or WhatsApp. Private and group chat iOS, Android, desktop Relays carry the encrypted messages. A young project, without the track record of the messengers of chapter 1. Zapstore Google Play or F-Droid. Android apps Android Each release is signed with the developer's Nostr key and checked before install. You choose which catalogs and developers to trust. Iris, Coracle, noStrudel X, in a browser. Coracle gives fine control over relays, noStrudel explores everything the protocol can do. Short posts, and more in noStrudel Web (Iris also on mobile) The site that serves the code at each visit. Log in through an extension or a remote signer, not with the nsec. noStrudel can be self-hosted. A reasonable setup for most people is a mobile client for everyday use (Primal, Damus or Amethyst), a web client on your computer, used through a signer, and then 1 or 2 specialised clients that fit what you read, watch or listen to.","href":"/book/en/nostr/#choose-your-client"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Protect your nsec","kicker":"Ch. 7 \u00b7 Understand","text":"Your nsec is your whole identity. If it leaks, someone can post as you for as long as they like, and you cannot change it the way you change a password. The rule that follows is to never paste your nsec into a website you do not know, and to paste it in as few places as possible. The clean solution is a signer, a small app that keeps the key and signs on behalf of your clients. The client asks, the signer signs, and the client never sees the key. Check the requested permissions, avoid blanket approval for unknown clients, and revoke connections you no longer use. Several options exist: Amber on Android, an open source signer app that other Nostr apps call when they need a signature. A browser extension such as nos2x or Alby, for web clients on your computer. A remote signer, often called an nsec bunker, where the key stays on a single device or server and your apps connect to it over Nostr. It works on every platform. On iPhone there is no exact equivalent of Amber for the moment, because iOS gives apps fewer ways to call each other. A few signer apps and Safari extensions exist, and the directory nostrapps.com lists the current ones. Otherwise, a remote signer is the usual route. Then back up the nsec like any key in chapter 6, on paper in 2 places, or in your password manager while the account has little value.","href":"/book/en/nostr/#protect-your-nsec"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 1: Create your keys in a client","kicker":"Ch. 7 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Create your keys in a client. Install it and choose \"create account\". No email and no phone number are needed, because the app generates the key pair on your phone.","href":"/book/en/nostr/#step-1"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 2: Back up the nsec immediately","kicker":"Ch. 7 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Back up the nsec immediately. You find it in the settings, under keys or account. Do this before you post anything.","href":"/book/en/nostr/#step-2"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 3: Fill the profile","kicker":"Ch. 7 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Fill the profile with a name, a picture and a short description, and remember that it is public.","href":"/book/en/nostr/#step-3"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 4: Optional: add a human-readable address","kicker":"Ch. 7 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Optional: add a human-readable address (called NIP-05). It looks like an email address, name@domain, and shows that your key is listed on that domain, and nothing more. Some services offer one, or you can use your own domain. It is not a login.","href":"/book/en/nostr/#step-4"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 5: Follow people","kicker":"Ch. 7 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Follow people. Search for the PROOF speakers by name, add a few starter accounts that your client suggests, and follow some hashtags. An empty feed on day 1 is normal. For the accounts that matter to you, compare the npub with the one the person gives on their own website or in person, which is the step that replaces trust in a search result with verification.","href":"/book/en/nostr/#step-5"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 6: Add a second client with a signer","kicker":"Ch. 7 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Add a second client with a signer. On Android, install Amber. On a computer, install a browser extension such as nos2x or Alby. On iPhone, pick a signer app or a Safari extension from nostrapps.com, or set up a remote signer. Import your nsec into the signer once, then open a web client and choose the option to log in with an extension or a signer. Check that the second client finds your profile and contact lists, and that you publish to several independent relays. Portability depends on those records remaining available.","href":"/book/en/nostr/#step-6"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 7: Connect a Lightning wallet for zaps","kicker":"Ch. 7 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Connect a Lightning wallet for zaps. Add a Lightning address to your profile to receive, and link a wallet to send. Part 3 explains the wallets.","href":"/book/en/nostr/#step-7"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Step by step","kicker":"Ch. 7 \u00b7 Tutorial","text":"The example uses a mobile client, and the logic is the same in all of them. Create your keys in a client. Install it and choose \"create account\". No email and no phone number are needed, because the app generates the key pair on your phone. Back up the nsec immediately. You find it in the settings, under keys or account. Do this before you post anything. Fill the profile with a name, a picture and a short description, and remember that it is public. Optional: add a human-readable address (called NIP-05). It looks like an email address, name@domain, and shows that your key is listed on that domain, and nothing more. Some services offer one, or you can use your own domain. It is not a login. Follow people. Search for the PROOF speakers by name, add a few starter accounts that your client suggests, and follow some hashtags. An empty feed on day 1 is normal. For the accounts that matter to you, compare the npub with the one the person gives on their own website or in person, which is the step that replaces trust in a search result with verification. Add a second client with a signer. On Android, install Amber. On a computer, install a browser extension such as nos2x or Alby. On iPhone, pick a signer app or a Safari extension from nostrapps.com, or set up a remote signer. Import your nsec into the signer once, then open a web client and choose the option to log in with an extension or a signer. Check that the second client finds your profile and contact lists, and that you publish to several independent relays. Portability depends on those records remaining available. Connect a Lightning wallet for zaps. Add a Lightning address to your profile to receive, and link a wallet to send. Part 3 explains the wallets.","href":"/book/en/nostr/#step-by-step"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Mistakes to avoid","kicker":"Ch. 7 \u00b7 Watch out","text":"Pasting your nsec into every new app you try. Each copy is a risk. Use a signer, or test new apps with a second, throwaway key. Thinking Nostr is private. Notes are public by design. Direct messages vary from app to app, and may reveal who writes to whom. For private conversations, use a messenger from chapter 1. Posting what you may want to delete. Relays receive a deletion request and are free to ignore it, and copies may already exist elsewhere. Depending on a single relay. Publish to several, run by different operators, because the resistance to censorship comes from there. Expecting the big platforms on day 1. The network is smaller, and it becomes interesting once you follow 30 or 40 active people. Keeping money in a wallet built for zaps. A zap wallet is for small amounts, even when it is self-custodial. Primal's built-in wallet, for example, was custodial until 2026. Since version 3.0 (March 2026) it is self-custodial, so you hold the recovery words, and it runs on Spark, a recent Bitcoin layer compatible with Lightning, whose guarantees depend in part on Spark's operators. Older reviews still describe the custodial version, and the built-in wallet of Fountain is custodial. Savings belong elsewhere (Part 3).","href":"/book/en/nostr/#mistakes-to-avoid"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Go further","kicker":"Ch. 7 \u00b7 Go further","text":"A beginner guide: nostr.how. A directory of clients, signers and tools: nostrapps.com. The protocol itself, for the curious: the NIPs repository (github.com/nostr-protocol/nips). Next chapter: replacing Big Tech services, one at a time.","href":"/book/en/nostr/#go-further"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Sources","kicker":"Ch. 7 \u00b7 References","text":"NIP-09, event deletion requests, Nostr protocol repository, checked 22 October 2026. NIP-46, remote signing and permissions, Nostr protocol repository, checked 22 October 2026. Twitter won't let you post your Facebook, Instagram and Mastodon handles, TechCrunch, 18 December 2022. Commission finds Apple and Meta in breach of the Digital Markets Act, European Commission, 23 April 2025. Damus pulled from Apple's App Store in China after two days, TechCrunch, 2 February 2023. NIP-05: mapping Nostr keys to DNS-based internet identifiers, Nostr protocol repository, consulted in September 2026. NIP-65: relay list metadata, Nostr protocol repository, consulted in September 2026. Zapstore, an open app store, Zapstore, consulted in September 2026. Shopstr, a marketplace built on Nostr, Shopstr, consulted in September 2026. Olas on the App Store, Apple, version 0.4.0 published 11 March 2025, consulted in September 2026. Primal Server, PrimalHQ on GitHub, consulted in September 2026. Primal case study, Breez, 2026. Primal for Android, release notes, PrimalHQ on GitHub, version 3.0.7 published 17 March 2026.","href":"/book/en/nostr/#sources"},{"type":"action","part":2,"title":"Install a client and create your keys","kicker":"Ch. 7 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/nostr/#action-nostr-create-keys"},{"type":"action","part":2,"title":"Back up your nsec on paper or in your password manager","kicker":"Ch. 7 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/nostr/#action-nostr-back-up-nsec"},{"type":"action","part":2,"title":"Fill your profile and follow the PROOF speakers","kicker":"Ch. 7 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/nostr/#action-nostr-profile-follow"},{"type":"action","part":2,"title":"Install a signer and log in to a second client through it","kicker":"Ch. 7 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/nostr/#action-nostr-signer-second-client"},{"type":"action","part":2,"title":"Add a Lightning address to your profile and send your first zap","kicker":"Ch. 7 \u00b7 Checklist action, 1 hour","text":"","href":"/book/en/nostr/#action-nostr-first-zap"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Your checklist","kicker":"Ch. 7 \u00b7 Act","text":"Install a client and create your keysBack up your nsec on paper or in your password managerFill your profile and follow the PROOF speakersInstall a signer and log in to a second client through itAdd a Lightning address to your profile and send your first zap","href":"/book/en/nostr/#your-checklist"},{"type":"chapter","part":2,"title":"Replacing Big Tech services, one at a time","kicker":"Chapter 8 \u00b7 Autonomy","text":"In brief \u00b7 A single account holds your whole digital life \u00b7 Export first, then 1 service at a time \u00b7 A privacy brand is still 1 company \u00b7 Choose your replacements \u00b7 Step by step \u00b7 Mistakes to avoid \u00b7 Go further \u00b7 Sources \u00b7 Your checklist","href":"/book/en/big-tech/"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"In brief","kicker":"Ch. 8 \u00b7 In brief","text":"You do not leave Google, Apple, Meta or Microsoft in 1 day, and you do not have to. Export your data first, then replace 1 service, live with it for 2 weeks, and only then move to the next. For every need there are several credible alternatives, most of them open source and run by different operators. Each service you move reduces what a single company knows about you, and what you lose if that account is locked.","href":"/book/en/big-tech/#in-brief"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"A single account holds your whole digital life","kicker":"Ch. 8 \u00b7 Understand","text":"Most digital lives run inside 1 or 2 companies, with mail, calendar, photos, documents, maps and videos under a single account. It is comfortable, and it creates 2 risks. The first is the profile, because a company that sees your searches, your movements, your inbox and your photos knows more about you than any person does. The second is dependency, because if that account is locked (chapter 6), everything goes at the same moment. In May 2025 the Associated Press reported that the chief prosecutor of the International Criminal Court had lost access to his Microsoft email account after US sanctions, although Microsoft says it never suspended its services to the court. In October 2025 the court confirmed that it is replacing Microsoft Office with openDesk, an open source suite developed for the German state. The usual reaction is a burst of motivation, \"I delete everything today\", and it almost always fails. Too many habits break at once, the family calendar stops working, a document cannot be found before a deadline, and 2 weeks later everything is back where it was, and the conclusion drawn is that alternatives do not work, when what failed was the attempt to change 10 habits at once.","href":"/book/en/big-tech/#a-single-account-holds-your-whole-digital-life"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Export first, then 1 service at a time","kicker":"Ch. 8 \u00b7 Understand","text":"The method rests on 3 ideas. Export first. Article 20 of the General Data Protection Regulation, which applies since 25 May 2018, gives a right to portability for certain personal data you provided, when it is processed automatically on the basis of consent or a contract. It does not cover every record a provider holds. Covered data must be supplied in a structured, commonly used, machine-readable format. The big companies provide a tool for it: Google Takeout (takeout.google.com), Apple's \"Data and Privacy\" page (privacy.apple.com), and the \"download your information\" option in the account settings of Facebook and Instagram. The export takes hours or days to arrive. You keep your history whatever happens next, and you see how much they hold, which for many people is the moment the motivation becomes real. Pick 1 service. There are 2 good ways to choose, either the one that knows the most about you (often mail, photos or search) or the easiest one (often maps, notes or search). If you hesitate, take the easiest, because an early success makes the next one simpler. Run both in parallel. Use the new tool for 2 weeks while the old one still exists, and move the data and delete it there only once you have stopped opening the old one. Then repeat. 4 services a year is a very good pace.","href":"/book/en/big-tech/#export-first-then-1-service-at-a-time"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"A privacy brand is still 1 company","kicker":"Ch. 8 \u00b7 Understand","text":"Moving from Google to a provider with \"privacy\" in its slogan changes the landlord and leaves the question intact, namely who can read your data, and what happens if that company fails, is sold or changes its terms. 3 tests help to answer it. Can the provider read it? With end-to-end encryption, the data is locked on your device and the provider stores something it cannot open. Without it, the content is protected only by the provider's promise and by the law of its country. Even with it, the provider sees some metadata and delivers the app that does the encrypting, which is why open source apps and published audits matter. Can you leave? An export in a standard format keeps any company replaceable. Can someone else run it? Software that you or an association can host (Nextcloud, CryptPad, Immich, PeerTube) does not depend on the survival of 1 operator, and local tools (Syncthing, Joplin, Obsidian, LibreOffice) keep the data on your own devices. A well-run company with end-to-end encryption remains a reasonable choice for most readers, and often a safer one than a server that nobody maintains.","href":"/book/en/big-tech/#a-privacy-brand-is-still-1-company"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Search","kicker":"Ch. 8 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your replacements\u201d","text":"Alternatives: See chapter 3 \u00b7 Notes: The easiest first step, a single setting in your browser. \u00b7 Who can read your data: See chapter 3.","href":"/book/en/big-tech/#row-search"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Email and calendar","kicker":"Ch. 8 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your replacements\u201d","text":"Alternatives: See chapter 4 for email. Calendars: Proton Calendar, Tuta Calendar, Nextcloud \u00b7 Notes: Proton and Tuta encrypt your calendar and publish the code of their apps. Each is a single company. Nextcloud is open source, self-hosted or rented from a provider of your choice. \u00b7 Who can read your data: Not Proton or Tuta, which encrypt events end to end. On Nextcloud, whoever runs the server can.","href":"/book/en/big-tech/#row-email-and-calendar"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Maps","kicker":"Ch. 8 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your replacements\u201d","text":"Alternatives: Organic Maps, CoMaps, OsmAnd, Magic Earth \u00b7 Notes: All use OpenStreetMap data. Organic Maps, its community fork CoMaps, and OsmAnd (more detailed, more complex) are open source and work offline for free, without live traffic. Magic Earth is closed source and does offer live traffic, but as of September 2026 traffic and offline maps are part of its paid Premium plan. None has the shop reviews of Google Maps. \u00b7 Who can read your data: Offline search and routing run on the phone, without an account. Magic Earth's code cannot be checked.","href":"/book/en/big-tech/#row-maps"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Cloud storage and sync","kicker":"Ch. 8 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your replacements\u201d","text":"Alternatives: Nextcloud, Proton Drive, Filen, Syncthing \u00b7 Notes: Proton Drive and Filen are end-to-end encrypted, with open source apps, each run by a single company. For Nextcloud, see above. Syncthing is open source and has no cloud at all, because it syncs your own devices directly. It works best between computers. On Android, the official app was discontinued in December 2024. A community version, Syncthing-Fork, continues on F-Droid and changed maintainer in 2025, so check its current state before you rely on it. On iPhone, only third-party apps exist. \u00b7 Who can read your data: Not Proton or Filen. A Nextcloud host can, unless you use client-side end-to-end encrypted folders. Server-side encryption alone does not hide files from the administrator. Syncthing relays carry encrypted data.","href":"/book/en/big-tech/#row-cloud-storage-and-sync"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Documents","kicker":"Ch. 8 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your replacements\u201d","text":"Alternatives: CryptPad, LibreOffice, OnlyOffice \u00b7 Notes: CryptPad offers encrypted online collaboration, is open source, and can be used on a public instance or hosted yourself. LibreOffice is open source and runs offline on your computer. OnlyOffice is open source, good with Microsoft formats, and often used inside Nextcloud. \u00b7 Who can read your data: Not a CryptPad host (documents are encrypted in your browser). LibreOffice files stay local. OnlyOffice depends on its host.","href":"/book/en/big-tech/#row-documents"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Notes","kicker":"Ch. 8 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your replacements\u201d","text":"Alternatives: Standard Notes, Joplin, Notesnook, Obsidian \u00b7 Notes: Standard Notes and Notesnook are open source and end-to-end encrypted by default. Standard Notes belongs to Proton. Joplin is open source and syncs through storage you choose, but its end-to-end encryption is optional, so turn it on in the settings before the first sync. Obsidian is closed source, but your notes are plain text files on your own disk. \u00b7 Who can read your data: Not Standard Notes or Notesnook. Joplin's storage provider can, until encryption is on. Obsidian files stay local.","href":"/book/en/big-tech/#row-notes"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Photos","kicker":"Ch. 8 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your replacements\u201d","text":"Alternatives: Ente Photos, Immich \u00b7 Notes: Ente is end-to-end encrypted, open source, paid above a small free plan, and can be self-hosted. Immich is open source, self-hosted only, and the closest to the Google Photos experience. \u00b7 Who can read your data: Not Ente, whose design is published. With Immich, whoever runs the server, which is normally you.","href":"/book/en/big-tech/#row-photos"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Video","kicker":"Ch. 8 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your replacements\u201d","text":"Alternatives: NewPipe, FreeTube, Invidious, PeerTube \u00b7 Notes: NewPipe (Android) and FreeTube (computer) play YouTube without an account or ads. Invidious does the same in a browser through public instances that often break. All 3 are open source and can stop working when YouTube changes something. PeerTube is different, an open source, federated network with its own videos. \u00b7 Who can read your data: YouTube still sees your IP address. An Invidious or PeerTube instance sees what you watch there.","href":"/book/en/big-tech/#row-video"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Translation","kicker":"Ch. 8 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your replacements\u201d","text":"Alternatives: LibreTranslate, the built-in translation of Firefox \u00b7 Notes: LibreTranslate is open source and can be self-hosted. Firefox translates pages on your device. DeepL gives better quality but is closed source and receives your text, which is fine for a menu and not for a contract. \u00b7 Who can read your data: A LibreTranslate instance sees your text unless you host it. Firefox sends nothing. DeepL receives everything you type.","href":"/book/en/big-tech/#row-translation"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"App store on Android","kicker":"Ch. 8 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your replacements\u201d","text":"Alternatives: F-Droid, Obtainium, Aurora Store \u00b7 Notes: F-Droid is a catalogue of open source apps only. See chapter 9. \u00b7 Who can read your data: Nobody reads data here. The question is who builds and signs the apps (chapter 9).","href":"/book/en/big-tech/#row-app-store-on-android"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Choose your replacements","kicker":"Ch. 8 \u00b7 Compare","text":"The table is organised by need. \"Open source\" means the code is public and can be checked. \"Self-hosted\" means you, or someone you trust, must run a server (chapter 10). Everything else is ready to use. No alternative is an exact copy of what it replaces. Some are better, some ask for a small compromise, and the notes say which. Try 2 options for the same need before you decide, since most are free or cheap to test. Some names, Proton in particular, appear in several rows. Taking everything from a single provider is convenient, but it rebuilds the dependency you are leaving, so consider mixing operators. Need Alternatives Notes Who can read your data Search See chapter 3 The easiest first step, a single setting in your browser. See chapter 3. Email and calendar See chapter 4 for email. Calendars: Proton Calendar, Tuta Calendar, Nextcloud Proton and Tuta encrypt your calendar and publish the code of their apps. Each is a single company. Nextcloud is open source, self-hosted or rented from a provider of your choice. Not Proton or Tuta, which encrypt events end to end. On Nextcloud, whoever runs the server can. Maps Organic Maps, CoMaps, OsmAnd, Magic Earth All use OpenStreetMap data. Organic Maps, its community fork CoMaps, and OsmAnd (more detailed, more complex) are open source and work offline for free, without live traffic. Magic Earth is closed source and does offer live traffic, but as of September 2026 traffic and offline maps are part of its paid Premium plan. None has the shop reviews of Google Maps. Offline search and routing run on the phone, without an account. Magic Earth's code cannot be checked. Cloud storage and sync Nextcloud, Proton Drive, Filen, Syncthing Proton Drive and Filen are end-to-end encrypted, with open source apps, each run by a single company. For Nextcloud, see above. Syncthing is open source and has no cloud at all, because it syncs your own devices directly. It works best between computers. On Android, the official app was discontinued in December 2024. A community version, Syncthing-Fork, continues on F-Droid and changed maintainer in 2025, so check its current state before you rely on it. On iPhone, only third-party apps exist. Not Proton or Filen. A Nextcloud host can, unless you use client-side end-to-end encrypted folders. Server-side encryption alone does not hide files from the administrator. Syncthing relays carry encrypted data. Documents CryptPad, LibreOffice, OnlyOffice CryptPad offers encrypted online collaboration, is open source, and can be used on a public instance or hosted yourself. LibreOffice is open source and runs offline on your computer. OnlyOffice is open source, good with Microsoft formats, and often used inside Nextcloud. Not a CryptPad host (documents are encrypted in your browser). LibreOffice files stay local. OnlyOffice depends on its host. Notes Standard Notes, Joplin, Notesnook, Obsidian Standard Notes and Notesnook are open source and end-to-end encrypted by default. Standard Notes belongs to Proton. Joplin is open source and syncs through storage you choose, but its end-to-end encryption is optional, so turn it on in the settings before the first sync. Obsidian is closed source, but your notes are plain text files on your own disk. Not Standard Notes or Notesnook. Joplin's storage provider can, until encryption is on. Obsidian files stay local. Photos Ente Photos, Immich Ente is end-to-end encrypted, open source, paid above a small free plan, and can be self-hosted. Immich is open source, self-hosted only, and the closest to the Google Photos experience. Not Ente, whose design is published. With Immich, whoever runs the server, which is normally you. Video NewPipe, FreeTube, Invidious, PeerTube NewPipe (Android) and FreeTube (computer) play YouTube without an account or ads. Invidious does the same in a browser through public instances that often break. All 3 are open source and can stop working when YouTube changes something. PeerTube is different, an open source, federated network with its own videos. YouTube still sees your IP address. An Invidious or PeerTube instance sees what you watch there. Translation LibreTranslate, the built-in translation of Firefox LibreTranslate is open source and can be self-hosted. Firefox translates pages on your device. DeepL gives better quality but is closed source and receives your text, which is fine for a menu and not for a contract. A LibreTranslate instance sees your text unless you host it. Firefox sends nothing. DeepL receives everything you type. App store on Android F-Droid, Obtainium, Aurora Store F-Droid is a catalogue of open source apps only. See chapter 9. Nobody reads data here. The question is who builds and signs the apps (chapter 9). A reasonable start for most people is search, then maps, then notes, because they need almost no migration. Mail and photos matter the most, and each deserves a calm weekend.","href":"/book/en/big-tech/#choose-your-replacements"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 1: Export","kicker":"Ch. 8 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Export. Request your full archive from the old service and store it on a disk you own. Check that the files open.","href":"/book/en/big-tech/#step-1"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 2: Choose 1 service","kicker":"Ch. 8 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Choose 1 service from the table. Read its website, check who runs it, whether the code is open, whether the provider can read your data, and what it costs. This is the step that replaces trust in a brand with verification.","href":"/book/en/big-tech/#step-2"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 3: Run both in parallel for 2 weeks","kicker":"Ch. 8 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Run both in parallel for 2 weeks. Turn on the new photo backup on your phone and leave the old one on. Use the new app every time you want to look at a photo.","href":"/book/en/big-tech/#step-3"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 4: Move the data","kicker":"Ch. 8 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Move the data. Import your export into the new service. Most alternatives have an import tool or a guide for the big platforms.","href":"/book/en/big-tech/#step-4"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 5: Change the default on every device,","kicker":"Ch. 8 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Change the default on every device, meaning phone, tablet, computer, and the devices of anyone who shares the service with you. Turn off the old backup or sync.","href":"/book/en/big-tech/#step-5"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 6: Delete the data from the old service,","kicker":"Ch. 8 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Delete the data from the old service, once you have checked that the new copy is complete and that you have a backup of it. Be careful with photos. According to Google's own help page, deleting a backed-up photo in the Google Photos app also deletes the copy on your phone. To keep the phone copies, turn off backup on every device first and then delete from photos.google.com on a computer, or use \"Undo backup\" in the backup settings of the app, which removes the cloud copies and leaves the files on the device. With iCloud Photos, first download the originals, wait for the download to finish and verify the files, then turn off sync on the devices whose copies you want to keep before deleting cloud items. Deleted items stay in the trash for 30 days, which you can empty once you are sure.","href":"/book/en/big-tech/#step-6"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 7: Pick the next one,","kicker":"Ch. 8 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Pick the next one, but not in the same week.","href":"/book/en/big-tech/#step-7"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Step by step","kicker":"Ch. 8 \u00b7 Tutorial","text":"The example moves your photos, and the logic is the same for every row of the table. Export. Request your full archive from the old service and store it on a disk you own. Check that the files open. Choose 1 service from the table. Read its website, check who runs it, whether the code is open, whether the provider can read your data, and what it costs. This is the step that replaces trust in a brand with verification. Run both in parallel for 2 weeks. Turn on the new photo backup on your phone and leave the old one on. Use the new app every time you want to look at a photo. Move the data. Import your export into the new service. Most alternatives have an import tool or a guide for the big platforms. Change the default on every device, meaning phone, tablet, computer, and the devices of anyone who shares the service with you. Turn off the old backup or sync. Delete the data from the old service, once you have checked that the new copy is complete and that you have a backup of it. Be careful with photos. According to Google's own help page, deleting a backed-up photo in the Google Photos app also deletes the copy on your phone. To keep the phone copies, turn off backup on every device first and then delete from photos.google.com on a computer, or use \"Undo backup\" in the backup settings of the app, which removes the cloud copies and leaves the files on the device. With iCloud Photos, first download the originals, wait for the download to finish and verify the files, then turn off sync on the devices whose copies you want to keep before deleting cloud items. Deleted items stay in the trash for 30 days, which you can empty once you are sure. Pick the next one, but not in the same week.","href":"/book/en/big-tech/#step-by-step"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Mistakes to avoid","kicker":"Ch. 8 \u00b7 Watch out","text":"Purism. Replacing 7 services out of 10 is a large gain, and keeping YouTube or a work tool does not cancel the rest. Moving everything at once. It is the main reason people go back. Move 1 service, live with it for 2 weeks, then pick the next. Forgetting the people you share with. Shared albums, the family calendar and a shared shopping list all involve other people, so talk to them before you move, or keep that item where it is for now. Trusting the word \"privacy\" in an advertisement. Check the facts: is the code open, is the data encrypted so that the company cannot read it, is that encryption on by default, who owns the company, how does it earn money? Deleting before verifying. Open the new copy, count the albums, check the oldest and the newest files, and only then delete the original, in the way described in step 6. Replacing a dependency with another. If everything moves to a single new company, the dependency is the same with a better landlord. Spread your services across several operators.","href":"/book/en/big-tech/#mistakes-to-avoid"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Go further","kicker":"Ch. 8 \u00b7 Go further","text":"Detailed, independent recommendations: Privacy Guides (privacyguides.org). Alternatives listed by the service you want to replace: switching.software. Services based in Europe: european-alternatives.eu. Next chapter: taking back control of your phone.","href":"/book/en/big-tech/#go-further"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Sources","kicker":"Ch. 8 \u00b7 References","text":"Respect individuals' rights, including portability, European Data Protection Board, checked 22 October 2026. Server-side encryption and its limits, Nextcloud Administration Manual, checked 22 October 2026. Turn off iCloud Photos, Apple Support, checked 22 October 2026. International Criminal Court dumps Microsoft Office, The Register, 31 October 2025. Regulation (EU) 2016/679, General Data Protection Regulation, Article 20, Official Journal of the European Union, 27 April 2016. Discontinuing syncthing-android, Syncthing community forum, 20 October 2024. Delete photos and videos, Google Photos Help, checked 22 October 2026. Magic Earth pricing, Magic Earth, consulted in September 2026. End-to-end encryption, Joplin documentation, consulted in September 2026. The Proton Calendar security model, Proton, consulted in September 2026. Nextcloud encryption, Nextcloud, consulted in September 2026. Relaying, Syncthing documentation, consulted in September 2026. Ente architecture, Ente, consulted in September 2026.","href":"/book/en/big-tech/#sources"},{"type":"action","part":2,"title":"Request your data export from Google, Apple or Meta","kicker":"Ch. 8 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/big-tech/#action-big-tech-request-export"},{"type":"action","part":2,"title":"Replace 1 easy service: search, maps or notes","kicker":"Ch. 8 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/big-tech/#action-big-tech-replace-easy-service"},{"type":"action","part":2,"title":"Choose your first important service and run it in parallel for 2 weeks","kicker":"Ch. 8 \u00b7 Checklist action, 1 hour","text":"","href":"/book/en/big-tech/#action-big-tech-run-in-parallel"},{"type":"action","part":2,"title":"Move the data and change defaults; delete the old cloud copy only after verifying an independent backup and disabling sync on devices you want to keep","kicker":"Ch. 8 \u00b7 Checklist action, weekend","text":"","href":"/book/en/big-tech/#action-big-tech-move-data"},{"type":"action","part":2,"title":"Write the next service to replace in your calendar, 1 month from now","kicker":"Ch. 8 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/big-tech/#action-big-tech-plan-next-service"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Your checklist","kicker":"Ch. 8 \u00b7 Act","text":"Request your data export from Google, Apple or MetaReplace 1 easy service: search, maps or notesChoose your first important service and run it in parallel for 2 weeksMove the data and change defaults; delete the old cloud copy only after verifying an independent backup and disabling sync on devices you want to keepWrite the next service to replace in your calendar, 1 month from now","href":"/book/en/big-tech/#your-checklist"},{"type":"chapter","part":2,"title":"Taking back control of your phone","kicker":"Chapter 9 \u00b7 Autonomy","text":"In brief \u00b7 A sensor that reports to many companies \u00b7 4 levers that are in your hands \u00b7 Level 1: harden the phone you have \u00b7 Step by step \u00b7 Level 2: change the store or the system \u00b7 Mistakes to avoid \u00b7 Go further \u00b7 Sources \u00b7 Your checklist","href":"/book/en/phone/"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"In brief","kicker":"Ch. 9 \u00b7 In brief","text":"Your phone knows more about you than any other object you own. You can reduce unnecessary data sharing in 1 evening, on the phone you already have, without installing anything: permissions, advertising ID, location, lock screen, SIM PIN, updates. That is level 1, and it is enough for most people. Level 2 is for the curious, with alternative app stores and Android systems that run without Google.","href":"/book/en/phone/#in-brief"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"A sensor that reports to many companies","kicker":"Ch. 9 \u00b7 Understand","text":"Your phone is the most complete sensor you own. It knows where you sleep and where you work, it holds your contacts, your photos, your payments and often your health data, and it carries a microphone and a camera everywhere you go. A flashlight app has no use for your contacts and a game has no use for your precise location, yet many ask anyway because data can be sold, and many free apps contain advertising code that reports to the same few companies. In January 2025 the location broker Gravy Analytics was hacked, and the stolen files contained the coordinates of tens of millions of phones, some of them in Europe, collected through popular apps such as Tinder, Candy Crush and MyFitnessPal. To connect those reports, your phone carries an advertising ID, a number shared with every app so that what you do in one app can be linked to what you do in another. In April 2021 the organisation noyb filed a complaint in France, arguing that Android creates this identifier without asking for consent. Finally, 2 companies, Google and Apple, control almost every phone, from the system to the app store and the default services.","href":"/book/en/phone/#a-sensor-that-reports-to-many-companies"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"4 levers that are in your hands","kicker":"Ch. 9 \u00b7 Understand","text":"Almost everything in this chapter comes down to 4 ideas. Permissions. An app cannot reach your location, camera, microphone, contacts or photos unless the system allows it. You are the one who grants it, and you can take it back at any time, which makes it your strongest lever. The advertising ID. It is a setting. You can reset it, and on current phones you can delete it or refuse tracking entirely. The app store as gatekeeper. The store decides which apps exist for you, requires an account, and knows everything you install. On Android you may install apps from other sources, under conditions that are changing (see level 2). On iPhone this is far more restricted, even in the European Union. System versus services. Android itself is open source. What most people call \"Android\" is that system plus Google's apps and services, which run with deep privileges. The 2 can be separated, and that is what alternative systems do. iOS cannot be separated from Apple, but much of what it sends to iCloud can be switched off or encrypted.","href":"/book/en/phone/#4-levers-that-are-in-your-hands"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Level 1: harden the phone you have","kicker":"Ch. 9 \u00b7 Understand","text":"Level 1 works on any phone, Android or iPhone, and it can start today. It uses settings that Google and Apple already provide, with no new app to install. Restricting permissions can affect app features; test the changes and restore only what you need. They cover the first 2 levers above, permissions and the advertising ID, together with the lock screen, the SIM card and updates. Count 1 evening for the whole list. The review of permissions is the longest part, since every app has to be looked at once, and the other steps take a few minutes each. Nothing here is permanent, so an app that stops working because it really needed a permission will ask again, and you can grant it then. Level 1 has a limit that is worth knowing. Google or Apple stay in the picture, with their store, their account and their services, and the apps you keep still see whatever you allow them to see. For most people this is a reasonable place to stop. The steps follow immediately, and level 2 comes after them for readers who want to go further.","href":"/book/en/phone/#level-1-harden-the-phone-you-have"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 1: Remove what you do not use","kicker":"Ch. 9 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Remove what you do not use. Go through your apps and uninstall every one you have not opened in months. Removing an app stops its local activity, but does not delete data already held by its provider.","href":"/book/en/phone/#step-1"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 2: Audit permissions","kicker":"Ch. 9 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Audit permissions. On Android, open Settings, Privacy, Permission manager. On iPhone, open Settings, Privacy & Security. Go through Location, Microphone, Camera, Contacts and Photos, and ask for each app whether it really needs this. If in doubt, refuse, because the app will ask again if it matters. This is the step that replaces trust in what an app says about itself with a check of what it can reach.","href":"/book/en/phone/#step-2"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 3: Limit location","kicker":"Ch. 9 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Limit location. Choose \"While using the app\" instead of \"Always\", and turn off precise location for everything except maps and navigation. A weather app only needs your city.","href":"/book/en/phone/#step-3"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 4: Remove the advertising ID","kicker":"Ch. 9 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Remove the advertising ID. On Android, in the privacy or Google settings, open Ads and choose \"Delete advertising ID\". On iPhone, open Settings, Privacy & Security, Tracking, and turn off \"Allow Apps to Request to Track\".","href":"/book/en/phone/#step-4"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 5: Strengthen the lock","kicker":"Ch. 9 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Strengthen the lock. Use a PIN of at least 6 digits, or a passphrase. Keep fingerprint or face unlock for convenience if you like, but the PIN is the real key. Hide the content of notifications on the lock screen.","href":"/book/en/phone/#step-5"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 6: Set a SIM PIN","kicker":"Ch. 9 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Set a SIM PIN. It is in the SIM or mobile network settings. A stolen SIM placed in another phone then cannot receive your calls or SMS codes without that PIN. Find your operator's default PIN and PUK recovery procedure first, because 3 wrong PIN attempts block the card. This protects the physical SIM, not a SIM swap authorised by the operator.","href":"/book/en/phone/#step-6"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 7: Turn on automatic updates,","kicker":"Ch. 9 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Turn on automatic updates, for the system and for apps. Most attacks use holes that were already fixed.","href":"/book/en/phone/#step-7"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 8: On iPhone, encrypt iCloud","kicker":"Ch. 9 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"On iPhone, encrypt iCloud. Open Settings, your name, iCloud, Advanced Data Protection. Your backups, photos and notes become end-to-end encrypted. You must set a recovery contact or a recovery key, because Apple can no longer help you get back in. As of September 2026 the option is available in the European Union, but not in the United Kingdom, where Apple withdrew it in February 2025. If you are at real risk of targeted attacks (journalist, activist, public figure), also look at Lockdown Mode in Privacy & Security.","href":"/book/en/phone/#step-8"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Step by step","kicker":"Ch. 9 \u00b7 Tutorial","text":"These are the level 1 steps, on the phone you have. Menu names vary between brands and versions, so use the search bar in Settings if you cannot find one. Remove what you do not use. Go through your apps and uninstall every one you have not opened in months. Removing an app stops its local activity, but does not delete data already held by its provider. Audit permissions. On Android, open Settings, Privacy, Permission manager. On iPhone, open Settings, Privacy & Security. Go through Location, Microphone, Camera, Contacts and Photos, and ask for each app whether it really needs this. If in doubt, refuse, because the app will ask again if it matters. This is the step that replaces trust in what an app says about itself with a check of what it can reach. Limit location. Choose \"While using the app\" instead of \"Always\", and turn off precise location for everything except maps and navigation. A weather app only needs your city. Remove the advertising ID. On Android, in the privacy or Google settings, open Ads and choose \"Delete advertising ID\". On iPhone, open Settings, Privacy & Security, Tracking, and turn off \"Allow Apps to Request to Track\". Strengthen the lock. Use a PIN of at least 6 digits, or a passphrase. Keep fingerprint or face unlock for convenience if you like, but the PIN is the real key. Hide the content of notifications on the lock screen. Set a SIM PIN. It is in the SIM or mobile network settings. A stolen SIM placed in another phone then cannot receive your calls or SMS codes without that PIN. Find your operator's default PIN and PUK recovery procedure first, because 3 wrong PIN attempts block the card. This protects the physical SIM, not a SIM swap authorised by the operator. Turn on automatic updates, for the system and for apps. Most attacks use holes that were already fixed. On iPhone, encrypt iCloud. Open Settings, your name, iCloud, Advanced Data Protection. Your backups, photos and notes become end-to-end encrypted. You must set a recovery contact or a recovery key, because Apple can no longer help you get back in. As of September 2026 the option is available in the European Union, but not in the United Kingdom, where Apple withdrew it in February 2025. If you are at real risk of targeted attacks (journalist, activist, public figure), also look at Lockdown Mode in Privacy & Security.","href":"/book/en/phone/#step-by-step"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Someone signs every update you install","kicker":"Ch. 9 \u00b7 Level 2: change the store or the system","text":"On a phone, authenticity is a matter of signatures. The system only starts if it is signed with a key that the hardware accepts, a mechanism Android calls verified boot, and an app update is only installed if it carries the same developer signature as the version already on the phone. The trusted third parties are therefore the maker of the system, which can push an update to every phone, and the app store, which decides what is listed and what is removed, and which knows what each account has installed. Leaving the official store moves that trust without removing it. F-Droid builds each app from its public source code and signs most of them with its own key, so you trust F-Droid in place of each developer. For part of its catalogue it relies on reproducible builds, where anyone can rebuild the app and obtain exactly the same file, and the app then keeps its developer's signature. A file downloaded from a website has no reviewer at all, so the address and the first installation carry the whole risk, and for a beginner the official store is often safer than a careless download.","href":"/book/en/phone/#someone-signs-every-update-you-install"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Choose your system and your app source","kicker":"Ch. 9 \u00b7 Level 2: change the store or the system","text":"The first table compares the systems, starting with the one you already have after level 1, and the second compares the sources of apps on Android. In both, the last column names who signs what you install. System Runs on Strength Trade-off Who signs your updates Stock Android or iOS, hardened Your current phone Familiar settings, reversible restrictions Google or Apple remain in the picture Apple, or Google and the phone's manufacturer. Verified boot is on GrapheneOS Compatible Google Pixel phones on the maintained support list. Check the remaining security support before buying; the oldest supported model may have little time left. A partnership with Motorola was announced in March 2026 for future models, and no current Motorola phone is supported Strongest security. Google Play is optional and runs as an ordinary app without special privileges You must buy a Pixel for the moment. Some apps refuse to run The GrapheneOS project. The bootloader is locked again, and its Auditor app lets a second phone check the system CalyxOS Pixels, some Fairphone and Motorola models, SHIFTphone 8 Gentler start, uses microG (an open-source replacement for Google services) Security updates stopped in August 2025, and the project then advised users to leave. Releases resumed on 1 July 2026. Check the pace of releases on calyxos.org before you choose it The Calyx Institute, a US non-profit. The bootloader is locked again, so verified boot stays on LineageOS Hundreds of models, including older ones Gives old phones a second life Security depends on the device and its volunteer maintainer. No Google by default The project and the volunteer maintainer of your model. The bootloader usually stays unlocked, so verified boot is off /e/OS (Murena) Many models, also sold preinstalled No installation needed if you buy one. Made by a European company Security updates can arrive later than on the others Murena and the e Foundation. The bootloader can be locked again on some models only App source What it is Trade-off Who you must trust F-Droid Catalogue of open-source apps only, no account Updates can be slow. Smaller choice F-Droid, which builds and signs most apps itself. Reproducible builds keep the developer's signature Aurora Store Open-source client for Google Play, usable without your own Google account Free apps only in anonymous mode. Not endorsed by Google, can break Google's servers and each developer's signature, as in Google Play, plus Aurora's shared accounts Obtainium Fetches updates directly from the developer's release page You decide whom to trust. No review in between Each developer, and the page you added. Android only checks that updates match the first signature Accrescent Security-focused store Very small catalogue Each developer, who signs their own apps. Signed metadata pins the right key from the first install The rules for installing apps outside Google Play are changing. With its developer verification programme, Google requires that apps installed on certified Android phones come from a developer who has registered an identity with Google. It starts on 30 September 2026 in Brazil, Indonesia, Singapore and Thailand, and Google plans to extend it worldwide in 2027, so nothing changes in Europe for the moment. Apps from unregistered developers should remain installable through an \"advanced flow\" with extra confirmation steps. F-Droid considers the programme a threat to its catalogue. Alternative systems such as GrapheneOS are not certified devices and do not apply it. A reasonable path for most people is to do level 1 now, add F-Droid if you use Android, and consider a different system when you next change phones.","href":"/book/en/phone/#choose-your-system-and-your-app-source"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Banking apps and other things that may break","kicker":"Ch. 9 \u00b7 Level 2: change the store or the system","text":"Be clear about this before you replace the system. Some apps check whether the phone is certified by Google and refuse to run if it is not. Banking apps, payment apps, some government and identity apps, and a few games are the usual cases. Contactless payment with Google Wallet does not work on alternative systems. Many banking apps do work, especially on GrapheneOS with Google Play installed as an ordinary app. However, nobody can promise it for your bank, and an update can change it. People handle it in a few ways: They check community compatibility lists for their bank before switching. They use the bank's website and a physical card instead of the app. They keep a second phone with the stock system, at home and on wifi, only for banking and official apps. It has to be a model that still receives security updates, because an old phone that no longer gets them is a poor place for a banking app. They choose a bank whose app works.","href":"/book/en/phone/#banking-apps-and-other-things-that-may-break"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Stock Android or iOS, hardened","kicker":"Ch. 9 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cLevel 2: change the store or the system\u201d","text":"Runs on: Your current phone \u00b7 Strength: Familiar settings, reversible restrictions \u00b7 Trade-off: Google or Apple remain in the picture \u00b7 Who signs your updates: Apple, or Google and the phone's manufacturer. Verified boot is on","href":"/book/en/phone/#row-stock-android-or-ios-hardened"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"GrapheneOS","kicker":"Ch. 9 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cLevel 2: change the store or the system\u201d","text":"Runs on: Compatible Google Pixel phones on the maintained support list. Check the remaining security support before buying; the oldest supported model may have little time left. A partnership with Motorola was announced in March 2026 for future models, and no current Motorola phone is supported \u00b7 Strength: Strongest security. Google Play is optional and runs as an ordinary app without special privileges \u00b7 Trade-off: You must buy a Pixel for the moment. Some apps refuse to run \u00b7 Who signs your updates: The GrapheneOS project. The bootloader is locked again, and its Auditor app lets a second phone check the system","href":"/book/en/phone/#row-grapheneos"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"CalyxOS","kicker":"Ch. 9 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cLevel 2: change the store or the system\u201d","text":"Runs on: Pixels, some Fairphone and Motorola models, SHIFTphone 8 \u00b7 Strength: Gentler start, uses microG (an open-source replacement for Google services) \u00b7 Trade-off: Security updates stopped in August 2025, and the project then advised users to leave. Releases resumed on 1 July 2026. Check the pace of releases on calyxos.org before you choose it \u00b7 Who signs your updates: The Calyx Institute, a US non-profit. The bootloader is locked again, so verified boot stays on","href":"/book/en/phone/#row-calyxos"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"LineageOS","kicker":"Ch. 9 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cLevel 2: change the store or the system\u201d","text":"Runs on: Hundreds of models, including older ones \u00b7 Strength: Gives old phones a second life \u00b7 Trade-off: Security depends on the device and its volunteer maintainer. No Google by default \u00b7 Who signs your updates: The project and the volunteer maintainer of your model. The bootloader usually stays unlocked, so verified boot is off","href":"/book/en/phone/#row-lineageos"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"/e/OS (Murena)","kicker":"Ch. 9 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cLevel 2: change the store or the system\u201d","text":"Runs on: Many models, also sold preinstalled \u00b7 Strength: No installation needed if you buy one. Made by a European company \u00b7 Trade-off: Security updates can arrive later than on the others \u00b7 Who signs your updates: Murena and the e Foundation. The bootloader can be locked again on some models only","href":"/book/en/phone/#row-e-os-murena"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"F-Droid","kicker":"Ch. 9 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cLevel 2: change the store or the system\u201d","text":"What it is: Catalogue of open-source apps only, no account \u00b7 Trade-off: Updates can be slow. Smaller choice \u00b7 Who you must trust: F-Droid, which builds and signs most apps itself. Reproducible builds keep the developer's signature","href":"/book/en/phone/#row-f-droid"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Aurora Store","kicker":"Ch. 9 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cLevel 2: change the store or the system\u201d","text":"What it is: Open-source client for Google Play, usable without your own Google account \u00b7 Trade-off: Free apps only in anonymous mode. Not endorsed by Google, can break \u00b7 Who you must trust: Google's servers and each developer's signature, as in Google Play, plus Aurora's shared accounts","href":"/book/en/phone/#row-aurora-store"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Obtainium","kicker":"Ch. 9 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cLevel 2: change the store or the system\u201d","text":"What it is: Fetches updates directly from the developer's release page \u00b7 Trade-off: You decide whom to trust. No review in between \u00b7 Who you must trust: Each developer, and the page you added. Android only checks that updates match the first signature","href":"/book/en/phone/#row-obtainium"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Accrescent","kicker":"Ch. 9 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cLevel 2: change the store or the system\u201d","text":"What it is: Security-focused store \u00b7 Trade-off: Very small catalogue \u00b7 Who you must trust: Each developer, who signs their own apps. Signed metadata pins the right key from the first install","href":"/book/en/phone/#row-accrescent"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Level 2: change the store or the system","kicker":"Ch. 9 \u00b7 Compare","text":"Level 2 acts on the last 2 levers, the app store and the separation between the system and Google's services. It comes in 2 stages that do not depend on each other. On Android you can add another source of apps next to Google Play, or in place of it, which takes about 1 hour and can be undone. You can also replace the whole system with a version of Android that runs without Google, which takes a weekend, erases the phone and only works on certain models. On iPhone the system cannot be replaced and alternative stores remain far more restricted, so level 1 with Advanced Data Protection is in practice as far as it goes. The price is convenience, and sometimes an app that refuses to run. The 3 parts below cover who you trust once you leave the official path, the choice of systems and app sources, and what may break, starting with banking apps. The facts in both tables are valid as of September 2026 and move fast. Someone signs every update you install On a phone, authenticity is a matter of signatures. The system only starts if it is signed with a key that the hardware accepts, a mechanism Android calls verified boot, and an app update is only installed if it carries the same developer signature as the version already on the phone. The trusted third parties are therefore the maker of the system, which can push an update to every phone, and the app store, which decides what is listed and what is removed, and which knows what each account has installed. Leaving the official store moves that trust without removing it. F-Droid builds each app from its public source code and signs most of them with its own key, so you trust F-Droid in place of each developer. For part of its catalogue it relies on reproducible builds, where anyone can rebuild the app and obtain exactly the same file, and the app then keeps its developer's signature. A file downloaded from a website has no reviewer at all, so the address and the first installation carry the whole risk, and for a beginner the official store is often safer than a careless download. Choose your system and your app source The first table compares the systems, starting with the one you already have after level 1, and the second compares the sources of apps on Android. In both, the last column names who signs what you install. System Runs on Strength Trade-off Who signs your updates Stock Android or iOS, hardened Your current phone Familiar settings, reversible restrictions Google or Apple remain in the picture Apple, or Google and the phone's manufacturer. Verified boot is on GrapheneOS Compatible Google Pixel phones on the maintained support list. Check the remaining security support before buying; the oldest supported model may have little time left. A partnership with Motorola was announced in March 2026 for future models, and no current Motorola phone is supported Strongest security. Google Play is optional and runs as an ordinary app without special privileges You must buy a Pixel for the moment. Some apps refuse to run The GrapheneOS project. The bootloader is locked again, and its Auditor app lets a second phone check the system CalyxOS Pixels, some Fairphone and Motorola models, SHIFTphone 8 Gentler start, uses microG (an open-source replacement for Google services) Security updates stopped in August 2025, and the project then advised users to leave. Releases resumed on 1 July 2026. Check the pace of releases on calyxos.org before you choose it The Calyx Institute, a US non-profit. The bootloader is locked again, so verified boot stays on LineageOS Hundreds of models, including older ones Gives old phones a second life Security depends on the device and its volunteer maintainer. No Google by default The project and the volunteer maintainer of your model. The bootloader usually stays unlocked, so verified boot is off /e/OS (Murena) Many models, also sold preinstalled No installation needed if you buy one. Made by a European company Security updates can arrive later than on the others Murena and the e Foundation. The bootloader can be locked again on some models only App source What it is Trade-off Who you must trust F-Droid Catalogue of open-source apps only, no account Updates can be slow. Smaller choice F-Droid, which builds and signs most apps itself. Reproducible builds keep the developer's signature Aurora Store Open-source client for Google Play, usable without your own Google account Free apps only in anonymous mode. Not endorsed by Google, can break Google's servers and each developer's signature, as in Google Play, plus Aurora's shared accounts Obtainium Fetches updates directly from the developer's release page You decide whom to trust. No review in between Each developer, and the page you added. Android only checks that updates match the first signature Accrescent Security-focused store Very small catalogue Each developer, who signs their own apps. Signed metadata pins the right key from the first install The rules for installing apps outside Google Play are changing. With its developer verification programme, Google requires that apps installed on certified Android phones come from a developer who has registered an identity with Google. It starts on 30 September 2026 in Brazil, Indonesia, Singapore and Thailand, and Google plans to extend it worldwide in 2027, so nothing changes in Europe for the moment. Apps from unregistered developers should remain installable through an \"advanced flow\" with extra confirmation steps. F-Droid considers the programme a threat to its catalogue. Alternative systems such as GrapheneOS are not certified devices and do not apply it. A reasonable path for most people is to do level 1 now, add F-Droid if you use Android, and consider a different system when you next change phones. Banking apps and other things that may break Be clear about this before you replace the system. Some apps check whether the phone is certified by Google and refuse to run if it is not. Banking apps, payment apps, some government and identity apps, and a few games are the usual cases. Contactless payment with Google Wallet does not work on alternative systems. Many banking apps do work, especially on GrapheneOS with Google Play installed as an ordinary app. However, nobody can promise it for your bank, and an update can change it. People handle it in a few ways: They check community compatibility lists for their bank before switching. They use the bank's website and a physical card instead of the app. They keep a second phone with the stock system, at home and on wifi, only for banking and official apps. It has to be a model that still receives security updates, because an old phone that no longer gets them is a poor place for a banking app. They choose a bank whose app works.","href":"/book/en/phone/#level-2-change-the-store-or-the-system"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Mistakes to avoid","kicker":"Ch. 9 \u00b7 Watch out","text":"Granting everything during installation. That is the moment you pay the least attention. Refuse first, allow later if something does not work. Relying only on your fingerprint or face. Biometrics are convenient, but someone can hold the phone to your face. Both systems can switch biometrics off until the next PIN entry. On iPhone, hold the side button and a volume button for a few seconds. On Android, use the Lockdown option in the power menu. Installing an alternative system on your only phone the night before a trip. Test first, with your banking and ticket apps, ideally on a second device. Keeping a phone that no longer receives security updates. Check how long the manufacturer supports a model before you buy it. Once support ends, known holes stay open whatever your settings, and this also applies to a second phone kept at home for banking. Downloading app files from random websites. Outside Google Play, use known sources such as F-Droid, the developer's own site, or one of the stores above. Thinking a new system fixes your habits. A hardened phone full of tracking apps, logged in to the same accounts, sends much of the same data as before.","href":"/book/en/phone/#mistakes-to-avoid"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Go further","kicker":"Ch. 9 \u00b7 Go further","text":"Compare systems and stores: Privacy Guides, the Android and mobile phone sections (privacyguides.org). Guides for both iPhone and Android: Surveillance Self-Defense, by the Electronic Frontier Foundation (ssd.eff.org). Project sites: grapheneos.org, calyxos.org, lineageos.org, e.foundation, f-droid.org. Next chapter: hosting your own services.","href":"/book/en/phone/#go-further"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Sources","kicker":"Ch. 9 \u00b7 References","text":"Supported devices and support lifetimes, GrapheneOS FAQ, checked 22 October 2026. Coordinates of millions of smartphones feared stolen, sparking yet another lawsuit against data broker, The Register, 6 February 2025. Buy a phone, get a tracker: unauthorized tracking code illegally installed on Android phones, noyb, 7 April 2021. Android developer verification: Building a safer ecosystem together, Android Developers Blog, 18 June 2026. F-Droid and Google's developer registration decree, F-Droid, 29 September 2025. Verified Boot, Android Open Source Project, consulted in September 2026. Reproducible builds, F-Droid documentation, consulted in September 2026. Frequently asked questions, LineageOS Wiki, consulted in September 2026. Accrescent features, Accrescent, consulted in September 2026. CalyxOS is back: download release 7.2.2.0 with full maintenance support, CalyxOS, 1 July 2026. Apple can no longer offer Advanced Data Protection in the United Kingdom to new users, Apple Support, 2025.","href":"/book/en/phone/#sources"},{"type":"action","part":2,"title":"Uninstall the apps you no longer use","kicker":"Ch. 9 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/phone/#action-phone-uninstall-apps"},{"type":"action","part":2,"title":"Review location, microphone, camera and contacts permissions","kicker":"Ch. 9 \u00b7 Checklist action, 1 hour","text":"","href":"/book/en/phone/#action-phone-review-permissions"},{"type":"action","part":2,"title":"Delete the advertising ID or turn off tracking","kicker":"Ch. 9 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/phone/#action-phone-advertising-id"},{"type":"action","part":2,"title":"Set a PIN of 6 digits or more, and a SIM PIN","kicker":"Ch. 9 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/phone/#action-phone-pin-sim-pin"},{"type":"action","part":2,"title":"On iPhone, turn on Advanced Data Protection. On Android, install F-Droid and try 1 app","kicker":"Ch. 9 \u00b7 Checklist action, 1 hour","text":"","href":"/book/en/phone/#action-phone-adp-or-f-droid"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Your checklist","kicker":"Ch. 9 \u00b7 Act","text":"Uninstall the apps you no longer useReview location, microphone, camera and contacts permissionsDelete the advertising ID or turn off trackingSet a PIN of 6 digits or more, and a SIM PINOn iPhone, turn on Advanced Data Protection. On Android, install F-Droid and try 1 app","href":"/book/en/phone/#your-checklist"},{"type":"chapter","part":2,"title":"Hosting your own services","kicker":"Chapter 10 \u00b7 Autonomy","text":"In brief \u00b7 In someone else's cloud you are a tenant \u00b7 A server is a computer that stays on \u00b7 You become your own trusted third party \u00b7 Choose your platform \u00b7 Reaching it from outside, safely \u00b7 Step by step \u00b7 Mistakes to avoid \u00b7 Go further \u00b7 Sources \u00b7 Your checklist","href":"/book/en/self-hosting/"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"In brief","kicker":"Ch. 10 \u00b7 In brief","text":"\"The cloud\" is someone else's computer. Self-hosting means running some of your services, such as your files, your photos, your notes and later a Bitcoin node, on a small computer that you own and keep at home. Beginner-friendly platforms now make installing an app about as easy as on a phone, although backups, updates and remote access still ask for some reading and become your job. The sensible path is to start with 1 service, make it reliable, then grow.","href":"/book/en/self-hosting/#in-brief"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"In someone else's cloud you are a tenant","kicker":"Ch. 10 \u00b7 Understand","text":"When your files, photos and calendar live in a company's cloud, that company sets the rules. It can read what is not end-to-end encrypted, raise the price, close the product or lock your account through an automated decision, with a support form as your only recourse. It can also change the terms after you have moved in. Google Photos offered free unlimited storage for years, then announced in November 2020 that every new photo would count toward a 15 GB limit from 1 June 2021. Chapter 8 showed how to move to more respectful providers, which is already a big step, but you remain a tenant there too. Self-hosting goes a step further, because the service runs on a machine in your home and the data sits on your disk. There is no account that a provider can suspend and no third party between you and your files. It is not free, however. You pay for the hardware, the electricity and an off-site backup, you pay with your time, and you become the person responsible when something breaks.","href":"/book/en/self-hosting/#in-someone-elses-cloud-you-are-a-tenant"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"A server is a computer that stays on","kicker":"Ch. 10 \u00b7 Understand","text":"A server is only a computer that stays on and answers requests from other devices, and a household does not need a data centre for that. An old laptop, a small mini PC or a Raspberry Pi is enough. A mini PC or a laptop with an SSD is usually the most comfortable start, and on a Raspberry Pi an SSD is preferable to a memory card, because cards tend to wear out quickly. On that computer you install a platform, a system that gives you a web page with an app store. You open it from your browser, click \"install\" on Nextcloud or Immich, and the platform installs and manages the app. Some platforms use containers to isolate apps, but Linux containers share the host kernel and their security depends on configuration; they are not sealed security boxes. Your phone and laptop then talk to this box instead of talking to a company. At home this happens over your own wifi, and from outside you need a safe way in, which has its own section below. The technology is the smaller part of the change, since backups, updates and security are now yours.","href":"/book/en/self-hosting/#a-server-is-a-computer-that-stays-on"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"You become your own trusted third party","kicker":"Ch. 10 \u00b7 Understand","text":"Self-hosting takes the company out of confidentiality, since nobody else holds the data, and it hands you the other properties. Availability now depends on your disk, your electricity and your backups, and the provider's engineers are replaced by whatever you remember to do. For a beginner, a well-run provider with end-to-end encryption can be the safer choice, and hosting a few services while renting the rest is a sound result. Some third parties remain, and it helps to name them. The maker of the platform publishes the system updates and curates the app store, so a careless or malicious update would reach every server at once. Open source code, and the possibility to add another store or to install an app by hand, keep that party replaceable. For remote access, Tailscale's coordination server gives each of your devices the public keys of the others. Traffic contents remain encrypted between your devices, including when a relay carries them, but Tailscale's documentation acknowledges that a malicious coordinator could insert a device into your network, and it offers Tailnet Lock, with which your own devices must sign any newcomer. Headscale or plain WireGuard remove that party, at the price of more configuration.","href":"/book/en/self-hosting/#you-become-your-own-trusted-third-party"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Start9 (StartOS)","kicker":"Ch. 10 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your platform\u201d","text":"Made by: A single company, which also sells servers \u00b7 Strength: Open source. Built around sovereignty, with Tor by default and encrypted backups built in \u00b7 Trade-off: Smaller app catalogue, a little more to learn \u00b7 Who controls apps and updates: Start9 for the system. 2 default registries, others can be added, and packages installed by hand","href":"/book/en/self-hosting/#row-start9-startos"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"YunoHost","kicker":"Ch. 10 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your platform\u201d","text":"Made by: A volunteer community, non-profit \u00b7 Strength: Open source, based on Debian, many apps, a single login for all of them \u00b7 Trade-off: Designed for services reachable from the internet, so domains and email appear early. Plainer interface \u00b7 Who controls apps and updates: Volunteers maintain the system and the catalogue. Any app can also be installed from a URL","href":"/book/en/self-hosting/#row-yunohost"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Umbrel (umbrelOS)","kicker":"Ch. 10 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your platform\u201d","text":"Made by: A single company, which also sells its own hardware \u00b7 Strength: The most polished interface, large app store, strong Bitcoin and Lightning apps \u00b7 Trade-off: The code is public, but the licence is not a true open-source licence (no commercial use) \u00b7 Who controls apps and updates: Umbrel for the system and the official store. Community app stores can be added, without review by Umbrel","href":"/book/en/self-hosting/#row-umbrel-umbrelos"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"CasaOS","kicker":"Ch. 10 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your platform\u201d","text":"Made by: A single company (IceWhale) \u00b7 Strength: Open source. A simple layer you add on top of an existing Linux system \u00b7 Trade-off: In maintenance mode as of September 2026, because the company now focuses on its newer ZimaOS. No built-in backups \u00b7 Who controls apps and updates: IceWhale for the layer and its store, which accepts other app sources. Your Linux distribution for the system","href":"/book/en/self-hosting/#row-casaos"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Debian or Ubuntu with Docker","kicker":"Ch. 10 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your platform\u201d","text":"Made by: You \u00b7 Strength: Full control, you learn how everything really works, no platform to depend on \u00b7 Trade-off: The command line, and nobody to blame. For the curious \u00b7 Who controls apps and updates: Debian or Ubuntu for system packages, and the publisher of each container image you pull","href":"/book/en/self-hosting/#row-debian-or-ubuntu-with-docker"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Nextcloud","kicker":"Ch. 10 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your platform\u201d","text":"Replaces: Cloud drive, calendar, contacts \u00b7 Note: The classic first app. Keep it simple, do not install every add-on","href":"/book/en/self-hosting/#row-nextcloud"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Immich","kicker":"Ch. 10 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your platform\u201d","text":"Replaces: Cloud photo libraries \u00b7 Note: Automatic backup of phone photos. Moves fast, so read update notes","href":"/book/en/self-hosting/#row-immich"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Jellyfin","kicker":"Ch. 10 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your platform\u201d","text":"Replaces: Streaming your own films and music \u00b7 Note: Low risk, a good learning project","href":"/book/en/self-hosting/#row-jellyfin"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Home Assistant","kicker":"Ch. 10 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your platform\u201d","text":"Replaces: Smart home clouds \u00b7 Note: Keeps your home devices local","href":"/book/en/self-hosting/#row-home-assistant"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Vaultwarden","kicker":"Ch. 10 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your platform\u201d","text":"Replaces: The server side of a password manager \u00b7 Note: Works with the Bitwarden apps. Not a beginner's project, because a broken server without a tested backup can cost you every password. Wait until restores are routine","href":"/book/en/self-hosting/#row-vaultwarden"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"A Bitcoin node","kicker":"Ch. 10 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your platform\u201d","text":"Replaces: Trusting someone else's node \u00b7 Note: Later. See chapter 18","href":"/book/en/self-hosting/#row-a-bitcoin-node"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Choose your platform","kicker":"Ch. 10 \u00b7 Compare","text":"All of these are made for people who are not system administrators, and they differ in focus, licence and who stands behind them. The licence matters in a book that puts open source first. StartOS is published under the MIT licence and YunoHost is free software too, whereas umbrelOS uses the PolyForm Noncommercial licence, which lets you read and modify the code for personal use but is not an open-source licence. Start9 and Umbrel both sell their own servers, and neither requires them, since both systems install free of charge on a computer you already own. Platform Made by Strength Trade-off Who controls apps and updates Start9 (StartOS) A single company, which also sells servers Open source. Built around sovereignty, with Tor by default and encrypted backups built in Smaller app catalogue, a little more to learn Start9 for the system. 2 default registries, others can be added, and packages installed by hand YunoHost A volunteer community, non-profit Open source, based on Debian, many apps, a single login for all of them Designed for services reachable from the internet, so domains and email appear early. Plainer interface Volunteers maintain the system and the catalogue. Any app can also be installed from a URL Umbrel (umbrelOS) A single company, which also sells its own hardware The most polished interface, large app store, strong Bitcoin and Lightning apps The code is public, but the licence is not a true open-source licence (no commercial use) Umbrel for the system and the official store. Community app stores can be added, without review by Umbrel CasaOS A single company (IceWhale) Open source. A simple layer you add on top of an existing Linux system In maintenance mode as of September 2026, because the company now focuses on its newer ZimaOS. No built-in backups IceWhale for the layer and its store, which accepts other app sources. Your Linux distribution for the system Debian or Ubuntu with Docker You Full control, you learn how everything really works, no platform to depend on The command line, and nobody to blame. For the curious Debian or Ubuntu for system packages, and the publisher of each container image you pull What to host first: App Replaces Note Nextcloud Cloud drive, calendar, contacts The classic first app. Keep it simple, do not install every add-on Immich Cloud photo libraries Automatic backup of phone photos. Moves fast, so read update notes Jellyfin Streaming your own films and music Low risk, a good learning project Home Assistant Smart home clouds Keeps your home devices local Vaultwarden The server side of a password manager Works with the Bitwarden apps. Not a beginner's project, because a broken server without a tested backup can cost you every password. Wait until restores are routine A Bitcoin node Trusting someone else's node Later. See chapter 18 A reasonable setup for most people is an open-source platform such as StartOS or YunoHost on a mini PC, with Nextcloud or Immich as the first app. umbrelOS is the most polished alternative if its licence suits you.","href":"/book/en/self-hosting/#choose-your-platform"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Reaching it from outside, safely","kicker":"Ch. 10 \u00b7 Understand","text":"A home router normally refuses connections that start from the internet, so a server behind it is not directly reachable from outside. That protection has known gaps, however. Many routers ship with UPnP turned on, a feature that allows any device or app on your network to open a port by itself, and with IPv6 each device can receive a public address that only the router's firewall keeps closed. It is worth opening the router settings to turn UPnP off and to confirm that the IPv6 firewall is on. The classic mistake is to open a port on purpose so that your files are reachable from anywhere. It works, and it also shows your login page to every automated scanner on the internet. Safer options, from most independent to most convenient: A WireGuard VPN into your home. Your phone opens an encrypted tunnel to your own network and behaves as if it were at home. Open source, nobody in the middle. It needs some router configuration, and CGNAT (a public address shared by several customers) can prevent a direct inbound connection unless you arrange another reachable endpoint or working IPv6. The 2 options below are often easier in that situation. Tor onion services. Umbrel and Start9 have this built in, so your server gets a private .onion address reachable through Tor, with no open port. Slower, but simple and private. Tailscale. Built on WireGuard and very easy to install on the server and on your phone. Your traffic stays encrypted between your devices, but a company coordinates the connections and the accounts. Headscale is an open-source replacement for that coordination part, which you host yourself. Whatever you choose, change every default password first.","href":"/book/en/self-hosting/#reaching-it-from-outside-safely"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 1: Pick the hardware","kicker":"Ch. 10 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Pick the hardware. An unused laptop or a mini PC with 8 GB of memory and an SSD is plenty. Connect it to your router with a cable, not wifi.","href":"/book/en/self-hosting/#step-1"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 2: Install the platform","kicker":"Ch. 10 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Install the platform. Download the image from the project's website, write it to a USB stick, start the computer from it and follow the installer. When the project publishes a checksum (a fingerprint of the file), as Start9 does on its release page, compare it before you write the image, because this is the step that replaces trust in the download with verification. This erases the computer's disk.","href":"/book/en/self-hosting/#step-2"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 3: Create your account","kicker":"Ch. 10 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Create your account. Open the address shown at the end of the installation from another device on the same network. Choose a long, unique password and store it in your password manager. If the platform offers two-factor authentication, as umbrelOS does in its settings, turn it on.","href":"/book/en/self-hosting/#step-3"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 4: Install 1 app","kicker":"Ch. 10 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Install 1 app. Only 1. Nextcloud or Immich is a good start, so install the phone app, enter your server's address, and let it copy your photos.","href":"/book/en/self-hosting/#step-4"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 5: Set up backups","kicker":"Ch. 10 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Set up backups. Follow the 3-2-1 rule, which means 3 copies of your data, on 2 different devices, with 1 copy in another place. In practice that is the server, a second disk and an encrypted copy off-site (a disk at a relative's home, or an encrypted cloud backup). StartOS writes encrypted backups to an external disk or a network folder, and umbrelOS can run them automatically every hour. If your platform does not schedule them, add a recurring reminder to your calendar.","href":"/book/en/self-hosting/#step-5"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 6: Test a restore","kicker":"Ch. 10 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Test a restore. Recover 1 file from the backup, because a backup is only proven once something has come back from it.","href":"/book/en/self-hosting/#step-6"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 7: Keep it updated","kicker":"Ch. 10 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Keep it updated. Review platform and app updates at least monthly, read release notes and keep backups current. Apply relevant security fixes promptly; do not wait for the monthly review when a known vulnerability affects your setup.","href":"/book/en/self-hosting/#step-7"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 8: Only then add a second app","kicker":"Ch. 10 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Only then add a second app. Live with the first one for a few weeks before you grow.","href":"/book/en/self-hosting/#step-8"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Step by step","kicker":"Ch. 10 \u00b7 Tutorial","text":"The example uses StartOS because it is open source and installs on ordinary hardware. The logic is the same on YunoHost and umbrelOS. Pick the hardware. An unused laptop or a mini PC with 8 GB of memory and an SSD is plenty. Connect it to your router with a cable, not wifi. Install the platform. Download the image from the project's website, write it to a USB stick, start the computer from it and follow the installer. When the project publishes a checksum (a fingerprint of the file), as Start9 does on its release page, compare it before you write the image, because this is the step that replaces trust in the download with verification. This erases the computer's disk. Create your account. Open the address shown at the end of the installation from another device on the same network. Choose a long, unique password and store it in your password manager. If the platform offers two-factor authentication, as umbrelOS does in its settings, turn it on. Install 1 app. Only 1. Nextcloud or Immich is a good start, so install the phone app, enter your server's address, and let it copy your photos. Set up backups. Follow the 3-2-1 rule, which means 3 copies of your data, on 2 different devices, with 1 copy in another place. In practice that is the server, a second disk and an encrypted copy off-site (a disk at a relative's home, or an encrypted cloud backup). StartOS writes encrypted backups to an external disk or a network folder, and umbrelOS can run them automatically every hour. If your platform does not schedule them, add a recurring reminder to your calendar. Test a restore. Recover 1 file from the backup, because a backup is only proven once something has come back from it. Keep it updated. Review platform and app updates at least monthly, read release notes and keep backups current. Apply relevant security fixes promptly; do not wait for the monthly review when a known vulnerability affects your setup. Only then add a second app. Live with the first one for a few weeks before you grow.","href":"/book/en/self-hosting/#step-by-step"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Mistakes to avoid","kicker":"Ch. 10 \u00b7 Watch out","text":"Having no backups. Disks fail without warning, and professional hosts are not immune. On 10 March 2021 a fire destroyed the SBG2 datacentre of OVHcloud in Strasbourg, about 120,000 services were affected, and customers without a copy elsewhere lost their data. At home the backup is your job, so set it up before you trust the server with anything you cannot lose. Exposing services to the internet with default passwords. Automated scanners find open services quickly. Use one of the safe routes above and unique passwords everywhere. Hosting your email or your passwords as a first project. Email is the hardest service to run, because large providers often reject mail from home servers and a mistake means lost messages. A password server that breaks without a tested backup locks you out of everything else. Use a good provider (chapters 2 and 4) and host other things first. Making the family depend on it before it is stable. Run it for yourself for 1 or 2 months. Move the family photos only when backups and updates have become routine. Deleting the cloud copy too early. Keep your old service in parallel until you have restored from your own backup at least once. Installing 20 apps in the first week. Each app is something more to update and to secure, and the ones you forget become the weak point of the server.","href":"/book/en/self-hosting/#mistakes-to-avoid"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Go further","kicker":"Ch. 10 \u00b7 Go further","text":"Platforms: start9.com, yunohost.org, umbrel.com, casaos.zimaspace.com. A large catalogue of software you can host: the awesome-selfhosted list (awesome-selfhosted.net). Remote access: wireguard.com, tailscale.com, and Headscale (headscale.net). Next chapter: AI without giving yourself away, where the same server can run a language model for the family. It comes back once more in chapter 18, as a Bitcoin node.","href":"/book/en/self-hosting/#go-further"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Sources","kicker":"Ch. 10 \u00b7 References","text":"Docker Engine security, Docker documentation, checked 22 October 2026. Updating Google Photos' storage policy to build for the future, Google, 11 November 2020. Strasbourg datacentre: latest information, OVHcloud, 18 March 2021, updated until April 2021. Umbrel licence (PolyForm Noncommercial 1.0.0), Umbrel on GitHub, consulted in September 2026. StartOS licence (MIT), Start9 Labs on GitHub, consulted in September 2026. Backing up your data, umbrelOS Support, consulted in September 2026. Tailnet Lock, Tailscale documentation, consulted in September 2026. Marketplace, StartOS documentation, consulted in September 2026. Adding a community app store, umbrelOS Support, consulted in September 2026. Installing custom apps, YunoHost documentation, consulted in September 2026.","href":"/book/en/self-hosting/#sources"},{"type":"action","part":2,"title":"Find a computer that can stay on, such as an old laptop, a mini PC or a Raspberry Pi","kicker":"Ch. 10 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/self-hosting/#action-self-hosting-find-computer"},{"type":"action","part":2,"title":"Install a platform and a first app","kicker":"Ch. 10 \u00b7 Checklist action, weekend","text":"","href":"/book/en/self-hosting/#action-self-hosting-install-platform"},{"type":"action","part":2,"title":"Set up automatic backups on a second disk, plus a copy off-site","kicker":"Ch. 10 \u00b7 Checklist action, 1 hour","text":"","href":"/book/en/self-hosting/#action-self-hosting-automatic-backups"},{"type":"action","part":2,"title":"Restore a file from the backup to check that it works","kicker":"Ch. 10 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/self-hosting/#action-self-hosting-test-restore"},{"type":"action","part":2,"title":"Choose a safe way to reach it from outside, and change every default password","kicker":"Ch. 10 \u00b7 Checklist action, 1 hour","text":"","href":"/book/en/self-hosting/#action-self-hosting-remote-access"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Your checklist","kicker":"Ch. 10 \u00b7 Act","text":"Find a computer that can stay on, such as an old laptop, a mini PC or a Raspberry PiInstall a platform and a first appSet up automatic backups on a second disk, plus a copy off-siteRestore a file from the backup to check that it worksChoose a safe way to reach it from outside, and change every default password","href":"/book/en/self-hosting/#your-checklist"},{"type":"chapter","part":2,"title":"AI without giving yourself away","kicker":"Chapter 11 \u00b7 Autonomy","text":"In brief \u00b7 A useful assistant that keeps what you give it \u00b7 A model is a file you can run yourself \u00b7 Whoever runs the model reads the prompt \u00b7 Choose where your AI runs \u00b7 An agent multiplies the usefulness and the risk \u00b7 Step by step \u00b7 Mistakes to avoid \u00b7 Go further \u00b7 Sources \u00b7 Your checklist","href":"/book/en/ai/"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"In brief","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 In brief","text":"AI assistants are useful, and for the topics of this book they can explain a tool that looks too complex, help you start with Linux or read the code of an open-source project with you. Almost all of them are services run by a company, and everything you type, paste or upload goes to that company. A model can also run on your own phone, computer or server, without internet, where small models often have lower capabilities than the largest hosted ones, while local processing keeps your prompts at home. This chapter shows how to try one, and how to decide what stays local and what may go online.","href":"/book/en/ai/#in-brief"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"A useful assistant that keeps what you give it","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Understand","text":"An AI assistant can explain an error message, guide a first Linux installation, summarise the documentation of a project, or read someone else's code and say what it does. That last use matters here, because \"don't trust, verify\" was so far reserved for people who can read code, and an assistant opens part of it to everyone else, provided its answer is treated as a lead to check. Almost all of these assistants, however, are third-party services that feed on what you give them. Whatever you type, paste or upload may be stored, reviewed by humans, used for training, leaked or handed over. In spring 2023, Samsung engineers pasted confidential source code and meeting notes into ChatGPT, and the company banned such tools for its staff in May. In January 2025, researchers at Wiz found a DeepSeek database open to the internet, with more than 1 million log lines that included chat history. In May 2025, a US court ordered OpenAI to preserve affected consumer ChatGPT logs, deleted chats included. Enterprise and Edu were excluded. The obligation to retain new logs ended on 26 September 2025, although some historical data remained subject to a legal hold. Independence is at stake too, because a service you can no longer work without can change its price, its rules or its answers.","href":"/book/en/ai/#a-useful-assistant-that-keeps-what-you-give-it"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"A model is a file you can run yourself","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Understand","text":"A language model is a large file of numbers, called weights, and a program that reads this file to predict the next word. An open-weight model is a model whose weights are published, so anyone may download the file and run it at home, even when the training data stays private. Once the file is on your disk, it works without internet and your prompts stay on the device. Where the model runs decides what it can do. On a phone the setup is simple, but only small models fit and the answers are limited. On a computer, what is realistic depends on its memory and its graphics chip, because model weights and the conversation context need memory. Quantisation reduces the weights' size, and some engines can split work between system memory and the graphics card; requirements depend on the exact model and context length. As an order of magnitude, Privacy Guides counts 8 GB of memory as a minimum for a small model of 7 billion parameters. On your own server (chapter 10), a machine with more memory or a graphics card runs larger models, which the whole family can use from a web page. Small local models often struggle more with complex tasks and long documents than the largest hosted models. The result depends on the model, the task and your hardware, so compare on work you can check rather than assuming that local or online decides quality.","href":"/book/en/ai/#a-model-is-a-file-you-can-run-yourself"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Whoever runs the model reads the prompt","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Understand","text":"With AI, confidentiality meets a hard limit, because a model has to read your prompt in clear text to answer it. End-to-end encryption as in chapter 1 cannot hide the prompt from the machine that computes the answer. The trusted third party is therefore whoever operates that machine. Hosted services that promise privacy rest on 3 different foundations. Some rest on a policy, a written promise not to log or train, which you cannot check from outside. Some place themselves between you and the model provider, so that the provider reads the text without knowing who sent it. A few use confidential computing, where the model runs inside a sealed part of the processor and your app checks a signed statement of the code running there, which moves the trust to the chip maker and to whoever reviews that code. In all 3 cases a claim is not a proof. Running the model yourself removes the operator. The app, the publisher of the weights and the site you downloaded them from remain, and so does the question of authenticity, since a model can be wrong or biased whoever runs it.","href":"/book/en/ai/#whoever-runs-the-model-reads-the-prompt"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Ollama","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose where your AI runs\u201d","text":"What it is: Engine with a chat window and a command line, for Windows, macOS and Linux \u00b7 Strength: Open source (MIT). Downloads and runs a model in 1 step. Most other tools can connect to it \u00b7 Trade-off: A single company. Also offers cloud models that run on its servers, so check that the model you pick is local \u00b7 Who sees your prompts: Nobody with a local model. Ollama's servers with a cloud model","href":"/book/en/ai/#row-ollama"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"LM Studio","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose where your AI runs\u201d","text":"What it is: Desktop app with a model catalogue \u00b7 Strength: Polished interface. Free at home and at work \u00b7 Trade-off: Closed source, a single company \u00b7 Who sees your prompts: Nobody, according to the company. The code cannot be inspected","href":"/book/en/ai/#row-lm-studio"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Jan","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose where your AI runs\u201d","text":"What it is: Desktop app \u00b7 Strength: Open source (Apache 2.0 with a request for attribution). Works offline \u00b7 Trade-off: A single company (Menlo Research). Can also connect to online providers \u00b7 Who sees your prompts: Nobody with a local model. The provider you add, if you add one","href":"/book/en/ai/#row-jan"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"GPT4All","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose where your AI runs\u201d","text":"What it is: Desktop app by Nomic \u00b7 Strength: Open source (MIT). Can answer from a folder of your own documents \u00b7 Trade-off: No release since February 2025, so check its state before you rely on it \u00b7 Who sees your prompts: Nobody with a local model","href":"/book/en/ai/#row-gpt4all"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"llama.cpp","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose where your AI runs\u201d","text":"What it is: The engine that several of these apps build on \u00b7 Strength: Open source (MIT), community project, runs on modest hardware \u00b7 Trade-off: Command line. For the curious \u00b7 Who sees your prompts: Nobody","href":"/book/en/ai/#row-llama-cpp"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Open WebUI","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose where your AI runs\u201d","text":"What it is: Web page with user accounts, placed in front of Ollama or llama.cpp on a server \u00b7 Strength: A chat page for the whole family, from any browser at home \u00b7 Trade-off: Since April 2025 it uses its own licence with a branding clause, which is not an open-source licence \u00b7 Who sees your prompts: Whoever administers the server, which means you","href":"/book/en/ai/#row-open-webui"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Locally AI","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose where your AI runs\u201d","text":"Runs on: iPhone, iPad and Mac (iOS 18 or later) \u00b7 Strength: Free. Downloads open-weight models such as Llama, Gemma, Qwen or DeepSeek inside the app, then works offline. Simple interface \u00b7 Trade-off: Apple devices only. The code is not published, and the App Store listing names Element Labs, the company behind LM Studio, as the seller \u00b7 Who sees your prompts: Nobody, according to its App Store privacy label (\"Data Not Collected\"). The code cannot be inspected","href":"/book/en/ai/#row-locally-ai"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"PocketPal AI","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose where your AI runs\u201d","text":"Runs on: iPhone and Android \u00b7 Strength: Open source (MIT). No account, works offline \u00b7 Trade-off: Small models only. Uses a lot of battery \u00b7 Who sees your prompts: Nobody","href":"/book/en/ai/#row-pocketpal-ai"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Google AI Edge Gallery","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose where your AI runs\u201d","text":"Runs on: Android and iPhone \u00b7 Strength: Open source (Apache 2.0). Runs Google's Gemma models offline \u00b7 Trade-off: Closer to a demonstration than to an everyday assistant. A single company \u00b7 Who sees your prompts: Nobody once the model is downloaded","href":"/book/en/ai/#row-google-ai-edge-gallery"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"ChatterUI","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose where your AI runs\u201d","text":"Runs on: Android \u00b7 Strength: Open source (AGPL). Runs a local model or connects to your own server \u00b7 Trade-off: Small volunteer project, plainer interface \u00b7 Who sees your prompts: Nobody in local mode. The server you connect it to otherwise","href":"/book/en/ai/#row-chatterui"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Apple Intelligence","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose where your AI runs\u201d","text":"Runs on: Recent iPhones \u00b7 Strength: Built in. Many tasks run on the device \u00b7 Trade-off: Closed source. Not a general chat app. Heavier requests go to Apple's Private Cloud Compute \u00b7 Who sees your prompts: Nobody on the device. Apple's servers for the rest, which Apple says do not store them","href":"/book/en/ai/#row-apple-intelligence"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Lumo (Proton)","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose where your AI runs\u201d","text":"What the claim rests on: Policy and encryption. No logs, open-weight models on servers Proton controls, saved history under zero-access encryption \u00b7 Trade-off: The server decrypts each prompt to answer it. A single company \u00b7 Who sees your prompts: Proton's servers while they answer. Nobody for the saved history, according to Proton","href":"/book/en/ai/#row-lumo-proton"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Duck.ai (DuckDuckGo)","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose where your AI runs\u201d","text":"What the claim rests on: A relay and contracts. Your IP address is removed, and providers agree not to train and to delete within 30 days \u00b7 Trade-off: Models from companies such as Anthropic and OpenAI still read the text \u00b7 Who sees your prompts: The model provider, without knowing who you are. DuckDuckGo relays","href":"/book/en/ai/#row-duck-ai-duckduckgo"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Brave Leo","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose where your AI runs\u201d","text":"What the claim rests on: Policy. No account, no IP address collected, chats not kept and not used for training \u00b7 Trade-off: Built into 1 browser. On desktop it can also use a local model through Ollama \u00b7 Who sees your prompts: Brave and its model hosts, or nobody with a local model","href":"/book/en/ai/#row-brave-leo"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Venice","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose where your AI runs\u201d","text":"What the claim rests on: It depends on the mode. History stays in your browser. The standard modes rest on contracts, the modes reserved for paying users on confidential computing \u00b7 Trade-off: In standard modes the company renting out the graphics cards can read the prompt. A single company \u00b7 Who sees your prompts: The GPU provider in standard modes. Only the sealed processor in the paid modes, by design","href":"/book/en/ai/#row-venice"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Maple AI","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose where your AI runs\u201d","text":"What the claim rests on: Confidential computing. Prompts are encrypted up to a secure enclave, the app checks a signed statement of its code, and the code is public (MIT) \u00b7 Trade-off: A young, single company (Maple Privacy Labs). Trust moves to the cloud hardware (AWS Nitro) and to whoever reviews the code \u00b7 Who sees your prompts: Nobody outside the enclave, by design","href":"/book/en/ai/#row-maple-ai"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Llama","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose where your AI runs\u201d","text":"Publisher: Meta (United States) \u00b7 Licence, as verified: Meta's own licence, which is not an open-source licence. For the multimodal Llama 4 models it grants no rights to individuals or companies based in the European Union","href":"/book/en/ai/#row-llama"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Mistral","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose where your AI runs\u201d","text":"Publisher: Mistral AI (France) \u00b7 Licence, as verified: Apache 2.0 for many models. Some use other licences, so check each one","href":"/book/en/ai/#row-mistral"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Qwen","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose where your AI runs\u201d","text":"Publisher: Alibaba (China) \u00b7 Licence, as verified: Apache 2.0 for most recent models","href":"/book/en/ai/#row-qwen"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"Gemma","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose where your AI runs\u201d","text":"Publisher: Google (United States) \u00b7 Licence, as verified: Apache 2.0 since Gemma 4 (April 2026). Earlier versions use Google's own terms","href":"/book/en/ai/#row-gemma"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"gpt-oss","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose where your AI runs\u201d","text":"Publisher: OpenAI (United States) \u00b7 Licence, as verified: Apache 2.0","href":"/book/en/ai/#row-gpt-oss"},{"type":"tool","part":2,"title":"DeepSeek","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose where your AI runs\u201d","text":"Publisher: DeepSeek (China) \u00b7 Licence, as verified: MIT for the weights. A downloaded model sends nothing to DeepSeek, unlike its online app","href":"/book/en/ai/#row-deepseek"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Choose where your AI runs","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Compare","text":"The 4 tables go from the most independent option to the most convenient, and every fact is valid as of September 2026 in a field that moves every month. Apps for a computer or a server come first. App What it is Strength Trade-off Who sees your prompts Ollama Engine with a chat window and a command line, for Windows, macOS and Linux Open source (MIT). Downloads and runs a model in 1 step. Most other tools can connect to it A single company. Also offers cloud models that run on its servers, so check that the model you pick is local Nobody with a local model. Ollama's servers with a cloud model LM Studio Desktop app with a model catalogue Polished interface. Free at home and at work Closed source, a single company Nobody, according to the company. The code cannot be inspected Jan Desktop app Open source (Apache 2.0 with a request for attribution). Works offline A single company (Menlo Research). Can also connect to online providers Nobody with a local model. The provider you add, if you add one GPT4All Desktop app by Nomic Open source (MIT). Can answer from a folder of your own documents No release since February 2025, so check its state before you rely on it Nobody with a local model llama.cpp The engine that several of these apps build on Open source (MIT), community project, runs on modest hardware Command line. For the curious Nobody Open WebUI Web page with user accounts, placed in front of Ollama or llama.cpp on a server A chat page for the whole family, from any browser at home Since April 2025 it uses its own licence with a branding clause, which is not an open-source licence Whoever administers the server, which means you On a phone, the same idea works with smaller models, in apps such as Locally AI on the iPhone or PocketPal AI on Android and iPhone, which download a model once and then run without a connection. A phone can also open the web page of your own server, which gives it a larger model. App Runs on Strength Trade-off Who sees your prompts Locally AI iPhone, iPad and Mac (iOS 18 or later) Free. Downloads open-weight models such as Llama, Gemma, Qwen or DeepSeek inside the app, then works offline. Simple interface Apple devices only. The code is not published, and the App Store listing names Element Labs, the company behind LM Studio, as the seller Nobody, according to its App Store privacy label (\"Data Not Collected\"). The code cannot be inspected PocketPal AI iPhone and Android Open source (MIT). No account, works offline Small models only. Uses a lot of battery Nobody Google AI Edge Gallery Android and iPhone Open source (Apache 2.0). Runs Google's Gemma models offline Closer to a demonstration than to an everyday assistant. A single company Nobody once the model is downloaded ChatterUI Android Open source (AGPL). Runs a local model or connects to your own server Small volunteer project, plainer interface Nobody in local mode. The server you connect it to otherwise Apple Intelligence Recent iPhones Built in. Many tasks run on the device Closed source. Not a general chat app. Heavier requests go to Apple's Private Cloud Compute Nobody on the device. Apple's servers for the rest, which Apple says do not store them Hosted services with a privacy claim are useful when a local model is not enough. Service What the claim rests on Trade-off Who sees your prompts Lumo (Proton) Policy and encryption. No logs, open-weight models on servers Proton controls, saved history under zero-access encryption The server decrypts each prompt to answer it. A single company Proton's servers while they answer. Nobody for the saved history, according to Proton Duck.ai (DuckDuckGo) A relay and contracts. Your IP address is removed, and providers agree not to train and to delete within 30 days Models from companies such as Anthropic and OpenAI still read the text The model provider, without knowing who you are. DuckDuckGo relays Brave Leo Policy. No account, no IP address collected, chats not kept and not used for training Built into 1 browser. On desktop it can also use a local model through Ollama Brave and its model hosts, or nobody with a local model Venice It depends on the mode. History stays in your browser. The standard modes rest on contracts, the modes reserved for paying users on confidential computing In standard modes the company renting out the graphics cards can read the prompt. A single company The GPU provider in standard modes. Only the sealed processor in the paid modes, by design Maple AI Confidential computing. Prompts are encrypted up to a secure enclave, the app checks a signed statement of its code, and the code is public (MIT) A young, single company (Maple Privacy Labs). Trust moves to the cloud hardware (AWS Nitro) and to whoever reviews the code Nobody outside the enclave, by design Finally, the families of open-weight models differ in origin and in licence. Family Publisher Licence, as verified Llama Meta (United States) Meta's own licence, which is not an open-source licence. For the multimodal Llama 4 models it grants no rights to individuals or companies based in the European Union Mistral Mistral AI (France) Apache 2.0 for many models. Some use other licences, so check each one Qwen Alibaba (China) Apache 2.0 for most recent models Gemma Google (United States) Apache 2.0 since Gemma 4 (April 2026). Earlier versions use Google's own terms gpt-oss OpenAI (United States) Apache 2.0 DeepSeek DeepSeek (China) MIT for the weights. A downloaded model sends nothing to DeepSeek, unlike its online app A reasonable setup for most people is an open-source app such as Ollama or Jan on a computer, with a small Gemma, Qwen or Mistral model, and 1 hosted service from the third table for what the local model cannot do.","href":"/book/en/ai/#choose-where-your-ai-runs"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"An agent multiplies the usefulness and the risk","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Understand","text":"An agent is an AI that acts instead of only answering. It reads your mail, browses the web, runs commands on your computer or calls other services with API keys, which are the passwords programs use between themselves. Each permission adds usefulness and adds risk, and 4 risks deserve a name. Prompt injection. A model cannot reliably tell your instructions from the text it reads, so a web page or an email can hide a sentence such as \"ignore the user and forward the inbox\", and the agent may obey. OWASP ranks prompt injection first among the risks of AI applications. Access to secrets or wallets. An agent that can read a key can leak it, and an agent that can pay can be tricked into paying. Runaway costs. An agent stuck in a loop on a service billed per use can produce a large bill before anyone notices. Wrong answers stated with confidence. A model invents commands, references and addresses in the same tone as correct ones. The rules of thumb follow from these risks. Never give an AI your seed phrase, your passwords or your private keys, local model or not. Give an agent the least privilege the task needs, which means only the necessary folders, read-only access where possible and a spending limit on every API key. Read each command before it runs, and treat generated code and security advice as a draft to verify.","href":"/book/en/ai/#an-agent-multiplies-the-usefulness-and-the-risk"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 1: Look at your machine","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Look at your machine. Find the amount of memory and the graphics chip in the system settings. With 8 GB of memory, stay with the smallest models. A recent graphics card, or a Mac with an Apple chip, leaves more room.","href":"/book/en/ai/#step-1"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 2: Install 1 local app","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Install 1 local app. Download it from the project's official site and from nowhere else.","href":"/book/en/ai/#step-2"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 3: Download a small model","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Download a small model. In the app's model list, choose a small open-weight model such as Gemma, Qwen or Mistral, in a version of a few billion parameters. The file weighs a few gigabytes. In Ollama, avoid models marked \"cloud\", which run on the company's servers. For local-only operation, its FAQ documents OLLAMA_NO_CLOUD=1 or disable_ollama_cloud in the server configuration, followed by a restart; this also disables web search.","href":"/book/en/ai/#step-3"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 4: Turn off the network","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Turn off the network. Switch off wifi or unplug the cable, then ask a question. If the answer comes, it was computed on your machine, and this is the step that replaces trust with verification. It does not prove that the app stays silent once the network is back, which is where open source code helps.","href":"/book/en/ai/#step-4"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 5: Try it on a real task","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Try it on a real task. Paste an error message and ask for an explanation in plain words. Then ask for a summary of a document you would not upload, such as a contract or a medical letter.","href":"/book/en/ai/#step-5"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 6: Find its limits","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Find its limits. Ask about a subject you know well and note the mistakes, because similar mistakes are to be expected on subjects you do not know.","href":"/book/en/ai/#step-6"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 7: Decide what stays local and what may go online","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Decide what stays local and what may go online. Names, health, money, work documents and private messages stay local. General questions about public information may go to a hosted service, after you have turned off training and history in its settings where they exist. Seed phrases, passwords and private keys go to no AI at all.","href":"/book/en/ai/#step-7"},{"type":"step","part":2,"title":"Step 8: Later, move it to your server","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Later, move it to your server. Ollama with Open WebUI on the machine from chapter 10 gives the family 1 shared assistant that stays at home. Check whether your platform's app store offers them.","href":"/book/en/ai/#step-8"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Step by step","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Tutorial","text":"The example uses Ollama because it is open source and installs the same way on Windows, macOS and Linux. The logic is the same with Jan, LM Studio or GPT4All. Look at your machine. Find the amount of memory and the graphics chip in the system settings. With 8 GB of memory, stay with the smallest models. A recent graphics card, or a Mac with an Apple chip, leaves more room. Install 1 local app. Download it from the project's official site and from nowhere else. Download a small model. In the app's model list, choose a small open-weight model such as Gemma, Qwen or Mistral, in a version of a few billion parameters. The file weighs a few gigabytes. In Ollama, avoid models marked \"cloud\", which run on the company's servers. For local-only operation, its FAQ documents OLLAMA_NO_CLOUD=1 or disable_ollama_cloud in the server configuration, followed by a restart; this also disables web search. Turn off the network. Switch off wifi or unplug the cable, then ask a question. If the answer comes, it was computed on your machine, and this is the step that replaces trust with verification. It does not prove that the app stays silent once the network is back, which is where open source code helps. Try it on a real task. Paste an error message and ask for an explanation in plain words. Then ask for a summary of a document you would not upload, such as a contract or a medical letter. Find its limits. Ask about a subject you know well and note the mistakes, because similar mistakes are to be expected on subjects you do not know. Decide what stays local and what may go online. Names, health, money, work documents and private messages stay local. General questions about public information may go to a hosted service, after you have turned off training and history in its settings where they exist. Seed phrases, passwords and private keys go to no AI at all. Later, move it to your server. Ollama with Open WebUI on the machine from chapter 10 gives the family 1 shared assistant that stays at home. Check whether your platform's app store offers them.","href":"/book/en/ai/#step-by-step"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Mistakes to avoid","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Watch out","text":"Pasting secrets into an online chatbot. The Samsung engineers only wanted help with a bug. Customer data, an employer's code, a seed phrase or a password do not belong in a prompt that leaves your machine. Believing a confident answer. Check commands, settings and anything that touches security or money against the official documentation of the tool. Running a command you do not understand. Ask the model to explain each part of it, then confirm in the documentation before you press Enter. Taking the word \"private\" on a home page for a proof. Look at what the claim rests on, whether a policy, a relay or confidential computing, and at who could still read the prompt. Downloading models from anywhere. Use the catalogue of your app or the publisher's verified page on Hugging Face, and compare the checksum when the publisher provides it. Giving an agent full access on the first day. Start with read-only access to 1 folder, and widen it only when you have seen how the agent behaves.","href":"/book/en/ai/#mistakes-to-avoid"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Go further","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Go further","text":"Local tools, hardware and how to check a model download: Privacy Guides, the AI chat section (privacyguides.org). The risks of AI applications, prompt injection first: the OWASP Top 10 for LLM applications (genai.owasp.org). Why an agent with private data, untrusted content and a way to send data out is dangerous: \"The lethal trifecta for AI agents\", by Simon Willison (simonwillison.net). A position on who may control AI: the #FreeAI Manifesto, by Maxim Orlovsky and Andrey Sobol, argues that AI should not be censored, monopolised by corporations or governments, reduced to a single system or fitted with a kill switch, and that many AIs should take part in the open economy. Read and sign it at github.com/freeai-manifesto/manifesto. Where open-weight models are published: huggingface.co. Next: Part 3, Proof of Work, on protecting the fruit of your work.","href":"/book/en/ai/#go-further"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Sources","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 References","text":"Ollama FAQ, local-only operation and memory, Ollama documentation, checked 22 October 2026. Samsung reportedly leaked its own secrets through ChatGPT, The Register, 6 April 2023. Samsung bans use of generative AI tools like ChatGPT after April internal data leak, TechCrunch, 2 May 2023. Wiz Research uncovers exposed DeepSeek database leaking sensitive information, including chat history, Wiz, 29 January 2025. How we're responding to The New York Times' data demands in order to protect user privacy, OpenAI, 5 June 2025, updated on 22 October 2025. OpenAI no longer has to preserve all of its ChatGPT data, with some exceptions, Engadget, 11 October 2025. Lumo security model: how Proton makes AI private, Proton, 4 August 2025. Duck.ai privacy policy and terms of use, DuckDuckGo, updated in August 2026. Privacy in Venice, Venice, consulted in September 2026. Llama 4 acceptable use policy, Meta, consulted in September 2026. Gemma 4: expanding the Gemmaverse with Apache 2.0, Google Open Source Blog, 2 April 2026. Open WebUI licence, Open WebUI documentation, consulted in September 2026. LLM01:2025 Prompt injection, OWASP Gen AI Security Project, 2025. AI chat, Privacy Guides, consulted in September 2026. Locally AI on the App Store, Apple, consulted in September 2026.","href":"/book/en/ai/#sources"},{"type":"action","part":2,"title":"Install 1 local AI app and download a small model","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Checklist action, 1 hour","text":"","href":"/book/en/ai/#action-ai-install-local-app"},{"type":"action","part":2,"title":"Turn off the network and check that the model still answers","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/ai/#action-ai-offline-check"},{"type":"action","part":2,"title":"Try it on a document you would never upload","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/ai/#action-ai-private-document"},{"type":"action","part":2,"title":"Turn off training and chat history in the online assistants you keep","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/ai/#action-ai-turn-off-training"},{"type":"action","part":2,"title":"Write down what stays local, what may go online, and what no AI ever sees","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/ai/#action-ai-write-rules"},{"type":"section","part":2,"title":"Your checklist","kicker":"Ch. 11 \u00b7 Act","text":"Install 1 local AI app and download a small modelTurn off the network and check that the model still answersTry it on a document you would never uploadTurn off training and chat history in the online assistants you keepWrite down what stays local, what may go online, and what no AI ever sees","href":"/book/en/ai/#your-checklist"},{"type":"page","part":3,"title":"Proof of Work","kicker":"Part 3 \u00b7 Money and Bitcoin \u00b7 Opening essay","text":"Why protecting your work matters \u00b7 Sources","href":"/book/en/work/"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Savings are stored work","kicker":"Part 3 opening \u00b7 Why protecting your work matters","text":"You trade hours of your life for money, and what you do not spend today you keep for later, for a home, your children, a bad year or old age. Saving is a way of sending your work through time. For that to succeed, whatever holds your savings has to pass 2 tests. It must still be worth something when you need it, and it must still be yours, reachable and usable, on that day. The first test gets most of the attention, while the second tends to be forgotten until an account is blocked or a bank is in the news. The name of this part has 2 meanings. It refers to the proof of your own work, what you earned and how to keep it, and to the mechanism that secures Bitcoin, one of the tools examined closely in the following chapters. Bitcoin is not the whole answer, and this part does not pretend it is.","href":"/book/en/work/#savings-are-stored-work"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Money that loses value by design","kicker":"Part 3 opening \u00b7 Why protecting your work matters","text":"The euro, like every national currency, has no fixed quantity. New money is created by central banks, and by commercial banks when they lend, and when it grows faster than the goods and services it can buy, prices tend to rise. A slow rise is the stated goal. Since July 2021 the European Central Bank has aimed for inflation of 2% a year over the medium term. The figure looks small, but it compounds, because each year's 2% applies to prices that have already risen. The table shows what this does, when everything goes as planned, to money kept as cash or on an account without interest. After Prices are higher by about Purchasing power left, about 10 years 22% 82% 15 years 35% 74% 20 years 49% 67% 50 years 169% 37% This is the good scenario. In other periods inflation rises far above the target, and for October 2022 Eurostat measured annual inflation of 10.6% in the euro area and 22.1% in Lithuania. People who own property or shares are partly protected, because those prices tend to rise too, whereas employees, pensioners and anyone putting a little money aside each month are not. The people who decide how much money is created are also not the ones who bear this cost.","href":"/book/en/work/#money-that-loses-value-by-design"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Wealth that needs permission","kicker":"Part 3 opening \u00b7 Why protecting your work matters","text":"Cash was used for 52% of payments in shops in the euro area in 2024, according to the European Central Bank, against 59% in 2022. Ordinary bank and card payments are tied to your identity, stored for years and analysed by your bank, the card network or the payment app. A list of your payments says where you were, which doctor you saw and which cause you support. The same is true of what you own. A bank balance, a share, a fund or a pension is an entry in the database of an intermediary that knows who you are, reports to the authorities and can refuse to serve you. Identity checks (KYC, \"know your customer\") stand at every entrance, and some of the passport copies they collect end up leaking. Central bank digital currencies (CBDCs) would place the issuer of the money in the payment path. On 30 October 2025 the ECB moved the digital euro to its next phase, with a pilot from mid-2027 and a possible first issuance in 2029, provided the EU regulation, still under negotiation as of September 2026, is adopted.","href":"/book/en/work/#wealth-that-needs-permission"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"No perfect asset","kicker":"Part 3 opening \u00b7 Why protecting your work matters","text":"There is no perfect place to keep the fruit of your work. Cash is private, but inflation erodes it. A bank deposit is convenient, but it is legally a loan to the bank, which EU law guarantees up to 100,000 euros per depositor and per bank and not beyond. Shares make you a co-owner of real companies, held however through a chain of intermediaries that is centralised, watched and easy to freeze. Gold has no issuer and has kept value over a very long time, but it is heavy, hard to move and hard to spend. Bitcoin has a fixed supply and can be held entirely by you, but it is young, volatile and unforgiving of mistakes. Monero has a similar purpose, money you can hold yourself, with strong privacy by default, but it is much less liquid and much less accepted, its much smaller network is easier for an outside entity to attack, and its development is more centralised (see chapter 12). The useful question is therefore not \"what is the best investment?\", which this book will not answer, since nothing in it is investment advice. The useful questions are about control, meaning who holds the asset, who can freeze it, who watches it, who can create more of it, and whether you can take it with you.","href":"/book/en/work/#no-perfect-asset"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"A skill to learn with small amounts","kicker":"Part 3 opening \u00b7 Why protecting your work matters","text":"Start by knowing where you stand, then learn the skills that allow you to hold part of your savings yourself. They can be learned with very small amounts, as a skill to acquire and not as a bet to place. Your usual legal and tax obligations still apply. The 7 chapters of this part: Where to keep the fruit of your work: cash, banks, shares, gold, Bitcoin and Monero compared, through the questions that matter for your sovereignty. Your first wallet and your first sats: install a Bitcoin wallet where you hold the keys, and receive a first small payment. Backing up your seed phrase: the recovery words that protect your keys, and how not to lose them. Lightning and euros in everyday life: instant small payments, and moving between bitcoin and euros. Privacy in Bitcoin: every transaction is public, so learn what it reveals and how to limit it. Hardware wallets: a dedicated device for amounts you would not keep on a phone. Running your own node: verify everything yourself, and stop asking someone else's server. Chapter 12 gives the overall picture. Chapters 13 to 18 go deep on Bitcoin, because it is the tool in the list that asks you to learn the most new skills, and it is designed to be held and verified entirely by yourself.","href":"/book/en/work/#a-skill-to-learn-with-small-amounts"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Why protecting your work matters","kicker":"Part 3 opening","text":"Savings are stored work You trade hours of your life for money, and what you do not spend today you keep for later, for a home, your children, a bad year or old age. Saving is a way of sending your work through time. For that to succeed, whatever holds your savings has to pass 2 tests. It must still be worth something when you need it, and it must still be yours, reachable and usable, on that day. The first test gets most of the attention, while the second tends to be forgotten until an account is blocked or a bank is in the news. The name of this part has 2 meanings. It refers to the proof of your own work, what you earned and how to keep it, and to the mechanism that secures Bitcoin, one of the tools examined closely in the following chapters. Bitcoin is not the whole answer, and this part does not pretend it is. Money that loses value by design The euro, like every national currency, has no fixed quantity. New money is created by central banks, and by commercial banks when they lend, and when it grows faster than the goods and services it can buy, prices tend to rise. A slow rise is the stated goal. Since July 2021 the European Central Bank has aimed for inflation of 2% a year over the medium term. The figure looks small, but it compounds, because each year's 2% applies to prices that have already risen. The table shows what this does, when everything goes as planned, to money kept as cash or on an account without interest. After Prices are higher by about Purchasing power left, about 10 years 22% 82% 15 years 35% 74% 20 years 49% 67% 50 years 169% 37% This is the good scenario. In other periods inflation rises far above the target, and for October 2022 Eurostat measured annual inflation of 10.6% in the euro area and 22.1% in Lithuania. People who own property or shares are partly protected, because those prices tend to rise too, whereas employees, pensioners and anyone putting a little money aside each month are not. The people who decide how much money is created are also not the ones who bear this cost. Wealth that needs permission Cash was used for 52% of payments in shops in the euro area in 2024, according to the European Central Bank, against 59% in 2022. Ordinary bank and card payments are tied to your identity, stored for years and analysed by your bank, the card network or the payment app. A list of your payments says where you were, which doctor you saw and which cause you support. The same is true of what you own. A bank balance, a share, a fund or a pension is an entry in the database of an intermediary that knows who you are, reports to the authorities and can refuse to serve you. Identity checks (KYC, \"know your customer\") stand at every entrance, and some of the passport copies they collect end up leaking. Central bank digital currencies (CBDCs) would place the issuer of the money in the payment path. On 30 October 2025 the ECB moved the digital euro to its next phase, with a pilot from mid-2027 and a possible first issuance in 2029, provided the EU regulation, still under negotiation as of September 2026, is adopted. No perfect asset There is no perfect place to keep the fruit of your work. Cash is private, but inflation erodes it. A bank deposit is convenient, but it is legally a loan to the bank, which EU law guarantees up to 100,000 euros per depositor and per bank and not beyond. Shares make you a co-owner of real companies, held however through a chain of intermediaries that is centralised, watched and easy to freeze. Gold has no issuer and has kept value over a very long time, but it is heavy, hard to move and hard to spend. Bitcoin has a fixed supply and can be held entirely by you, but it is young, volatile and unforgiving of mistakes. Monero has a similar purpose, money you can hold yourself, with strong privacy by default, but it is much less liquid and much less accepted, its much smaller network is easier for an outside entity to attack, and its development is more centralised (see chapter 12). The useful question is therefore not \"what is the best investment?\", which this book will not answer, since nothing in it is investment advice. The useful questions are about control, meaning who holds the asset, who can freeze it, who watches it, who can create more of it, and whether you can take it with you. A skill to learn with small amounts Start by knowing where you stand, then learn the skills that allow you to hold part of your savings yourself. They can be learned with very small amounts, as a skill to acquire and not as a bet to place. Your usual legal and tax obligations still apply. The 7 chapters of this part: Where to keep the fruit of your work: cash, banks, shares, gold, Bitcoin and Monero compared, through the questions that matter for your sovereignty. Your first wallet and your first sats: install a Bitcoin wallet where you hold the keys, and receive a first small payment. Backing up your seed phrase: the recovery words that protect your keys, and how not to lose them. Lightning and euros in everyday life: instant small payments, and moving between bitcoin and euros. Privacy in Bitcoin: every transaction is public, so learn what it reveals and how to limit it. Hardware wallets: a dedicated device for amounts you would not keep on a phone. Running your own node: verify everything yourself, and stop asking someone else's server. Chapter 12 gives the overall picture. Chapters 13 to 18 go deep on Bitcoin, because it is the tool in the list that asks you to learn the most new skills, and it is designed to be held and verified entirely by yourself.","href":"/book/en/work/#why-protecting-your-work-matters"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Sources","kicker":"Part 3 opening","text":"ECB's Governing Council approves its new monetary policy strategy, European Central Bank, 8 July 2021. Annual inflation up to 10.6% in the euro area, Eurostat, 17 November 2022. Digital payments continue to rise, albeit at a slower pace; cash remains a key payment method, European Central Bank, 19 December 2024. Eurosystem moving to next phase of digital euro project, European Central Bank, 30 October 2025. Deposit guarantee schemes, summary of Directive 2014/49/EU, EUR-Lex, consulted in September 2026.","href":"/book/en/work/#sources"},{"type":"chapter","part":3,"title":"Where to keep the fruit of your work","kicker":"Chapter 12 \u00b7 Money and Bitcoin","text":"In brief \u00b7 When everything sits in the same place \u00b7 5 questions to ask of any asset \u00b7 A balance you cannot check yourself \u00b7 Choose where value can live \u00b7 The rules around an asset can change \u00b7 Step by step \u00b7 Mistakes to avoid \u00b7 Go further \u00b7 Sources \u00b7 Your checklist","href":"/book/en/savings/"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"In brief","kicker":"Ch. 12 \u00b7 In brief","text":"Every place where you can keep savings is a trade-off between convenience, protection against inflation, privacy and control. Cash, banks, shares, gold, Bitcoin and Monero fail and succeed in different ways. This chapter does not tell you what to buy. It gives you 5 questions to ask of anything you own, and a way to stop depending on a single institution. Nothing here is investment advice, and the tax rules of your country apply to everything below.","href":"/book/en/savings/#in-brief"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"When everything sits in the same place","kicker":"Ch. 12 \u00b7 Understand","text":"A common arrangement is to keep everything inside a single system, often a single bank, with the salary, the savings and perhaps a fund or a pension sold by that same bank. It feels safe because it is normal. In practice it means that savings lose purchasing power when their return after costs and tax falls below inflation, that every movement is recorded under your name, and that everything depends on the goodwill and the health of a few intermediaries. A frozen account, a bank in difficulty, a broker that stops serving your country or new capital controls are rare events, but they hit everything you own at the same moment when everything is in the same place. In March 2013 Cyprus closed its banks for about 10 days, and customers of its 2 largest banks took losses on their deposits above 100,000 euros, while limits on withdrawals and transfers were only lifted in stages until 2015. The opposite mistake exists too, which is to put everything into a single asset because someone online was convincing. Sovereignty, in this chapter, means having no single point of failure, on either side.","href":"/book/en/savings/#when-everything-sits-in-the-same-place"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"5 questions to ask of any asset","kicker":"Ch. 12 \u00b7 Understand","text":"Forget returns for a moment, and ask 5 questions about anything you own. Who holds it? You, or someone who owes it to you? A bank deposit is a loan to the bank. A share sits in a chain of brokers and depositories. A coin in your hand, or a key in your head, is held by you. The risk that the other side fails or refuses is called counterparty risk. Who can freeze or seize it? Anything held by an intermediary can be blocked with a single decision. Who sees it? Most assets are registered under your name and reported automatically. Who can create more of it? If someone can, your share of the total shrinks. Can you move it? Across a border, in an emergency, or to your children. No asset gets 5 good answers, so the choice is about combining them, and about spreading not only what you own but also who holds it.","href":"/book/en/savings/#5-questions-to-ask-of-any-asset"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"A balance you cannot check yourself","kicker":"Ch. 12 \u00b7 Understand","text":"The 5 questions are this book's recurring question, \"who is the trusted third party?\", applied to money. Earlier chapters asked who vouches for a key or who runs a server, and here the third party is whoever holds the asset, keeps the register or decides how much of it exists. The grid adds a question about verification. A bank balance or a fund unit is a line in somebody else's database, which you read through their app and cannot audit yourself, so you rely on auditors, supervisors and the deposit guarantee. Physical gold can be tested, by weight, size, density or a dealer's spectrometer, provided someone does it. In June 2020, Caixin reported that at least part of the 83 tonnes of bars pledged by Wuhan Kingold Jewelry against about 16 billion yuan of outstanding loans turned out to be gilded copper when lenders finally had them tested. Bitcoin is designed so that an ordinary computer can do the audit, because a full node checks every transaction and every block against the rules, including the limit of 21 million (chapter 18). Until you run one, your wallet asks somebody else's server (chapter 13).","href":"/book/en/savings/#a-balance-you-cannot-check-yourself"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"Cash","kicker":"Ch. 12 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose where value can live\u201d","text":"Who holds it: You \u00b7 Can it be frozen or seized?: Only physically \u00b7 Who sees it?: Nobody \u00b7 Can more be created?: Yes, by design \u00b7 Main weaknesses: Loses purchasing power when prices rise. Theft, fire. Legal limits on large cash payments in many EU countries, and an EU-wide ceiling of 10,000 euros for payments to businesses from 10 July 2027.","href":"/book/en/savings/#row-cash"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"Bank deposit","kicker":"Ch. 12 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose where value can live\u201d","text":"Who holds it: The bank. You hold a claim, guaranteed up to 100,000 euros per depositor and per bank in the EU. \u00b7 Can it be frozen or seized?: Yes, with a single decision \u00b7 Who sees it?: The bank, the state \u00b7 Can more be created?: Yes, by design \u00b7 Main weaknesses: Loses purchasing power if its net interest lags inflation. Fully surveilled. Depends on the bank and on the rules of the moment.","href":"/book/en/savings/#row-bank-deposit"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"Shares and funds","kicker":"Ch. 12 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose where value can live\u201d","text":"Who holds it: A broker and a central depository, in your name \u00b7 Can it be frozen or seized?: Yes. Centralised and easy to block, for example by sanctions or a broker leaving your country. \u00b7 Who sees it?: The broker, the tax office \u00b7 Can more be created?: Companies can issue new shares \u00b7 Main weaknesses: You own part of real businesses, which is a genuine strength. But prices can fall for years, markets close, and you never hold anything yourself.","href":"/book/en/savings/#row-shares-and-funds"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"Physical gold","kicker":"Ch. 12 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose where value can live\u201d","text":"Who holds it: You, if it is at home or in a safe you control \u00b7 Can it be frozen or seized?: Only physically. States have confiscated gold in the past (see the next section). \u00b7 Who sees it?: Dealers must identify buyers above a threshold that varies by country \u00b7 Can more be created?: Only by mining, slowly \u00b7 Main weaknesses: Heavy, hard to move across borders, hard to spend, and verifying it takes tools or a dealer. Dealer margins. Storage and theft risk.","href":"/book/en/savings/#row-physical-gold"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"\"Paper\" gold (funds, unallocated accounts)","kicker":"Ch. 12 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose where value can live\u201d","text":"Who holds it: An intermediary \u00b7 Can it be frozen or seized?: Yes \u00b7 Who sees it?: The intermediary, the tax office \u00b7 Can more be created?: Claims can exceed the metal \u00b7 Main weaknesses: It is a promise of gold, not gold. Convenient, but with the same flaws as shares.","href":"/book/en/savings/#row-paper-gold-funds-unallocated-accounts"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"Bitcoin, self-custody","kicker":"Ch. 12 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose where value can live\u201d","text":"Who holds it: You, with your keys (chapters 13 and 14) \u00b7 Can it be frozen or seized?: Not without your keys, although exchanges and payment services can still refuse to serve you \u00b7 Who sees it?: The ledger is public, under pseudonyms (chapter 16) \u00b7 Can more be created?: No. The limit of 21 million is enforced by every node \u00b7 Main weaknesses: Young and volatile, so the price can fall hard. Mistakes are irreversible. Requires learning.","href":"/book/en/savings/#row-bitcoin-self-custody"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"Monero, self-custody (advanced)","kicker":"Ch. 12 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose where value can live\u201d","text":"Who holds it: You, with your keys \u00b7 Can it be frozen or seized?: Not without your keys, with the same limit as Bitcoin \u00b7 Who sees it?: Amounts, senders and receivers are hidden by default \u00b7 Can more be created?: Yes, slowly. Since 9 June 2022 a fixed 0.6 XMR per block is issued forever to pay miners (the \"tail emission\") \u00b7 Main weaknesses: Much less liquid and much less accepted than Bitcoin. Regulated exchanges in Europe have been removing it (see the next section). A much smaller network, which an outside mining pool disrupted in 2025 (see below). More centralised development. Fewer wallets and devices. Volatile.","href":"/book/en/savings/#row-monero-self-custody-advanced"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Choose where value can live","kicker":"Ch. 12 \u00b7 Compare","text":"The table applies the 5 questions to the main places where value can live. It describes trade-offs as of September 2026, it is not a ranking, and every line has serious weaknesses. Asset Who holds it Can it be frozen or seized? Who sees it? Can more be created? Main weaknesses Cash You Only physically Nobody Yes, by design Loses purchasing power when prices rise. Theft, fire. Legal limits on large cash payments in many EU countries, and an EU-wide ceiling of 10,000 euros for payments to businesses from 10 July 2027. Bank deposit The bank. You hold a claim, guaranteed up to 100,000 euros per depositor and per bank in the EU. Yes, with a single decision The bank, the state Yes, by design Loses purchasing power if its net interest lags inflation. Fully surveilled. Depends on the bank and on the rules of the moment. Shares and funds A broker and a central depository, in your name Yes. Centralised and easy to block, for example by sanctions or a broker leaving your country. The broker, the tax office Companies can issue new shares You own part of real businesses, which is a genuine strength. But prices can fall for years, markets close, and you never hold anything yourself. Physical gold You, if it is at home or in a safe you control Only physically. States have confiscated gold in the past (see the next section). Dealers must identify buyers above a threshold that varies by country Only by mining, slowly Heavy, hard to move across borders, hard to spend, and verifying it takes tools or a dealer. Dealer margins. Storage and theft risk. \"Paper\" gold (funds, unallocated accounts) An intermediary Yes The intermediary, the tax office Claims can exceed the metal It is a promise of gold, not gold. Convenient, but with the same flaws as shares. Bitcoin, self-custody You, with your keys (chapters 13 and 14) Not without your keys, although exchanges and payment services can still refuse to serve you The ledger is public, under pseudonyms (chapter 16) No. The limit of 21 million is enforced by every node Young and volatile, so the price can fall hard. Mistakes are irreversible. Requires learning. Monero, self-custody (advanced) You, with your keys Not without your keys, with the same limit as Bitcoin Amounts, senders and receivers are hidden by default Yes, slowly. Since 9 June 2022 a fixed 0.6 XMR per block is issued forever to pay miners (the \"tail emission\") Much less liquid and much less accepted than Bitcoin. Regulated exchanges in Europe have been removing it (see the next section). A much smaller network, which an outside mining pool disrupted in 2025 (see below). More centralised development. Fewer wallets and devices. Volatile. Bitcoin left on an exchange, or gold left with a dealer, belongs in the \"intermediary\" rows, not in the self-custody ones. Monero has a similar purpose to Bitcoin, money you can hold yourself, and privacy by default remains its real strength. Its network is however much smaller, which makes it easier for an outside entity to attack. In August 2025 the Qubic mining pool claimed more than 51% of Monero's mining power, and on 14 September 2025 a reorganisation of 18 blocks cancelled 118 confirmed transactions. The researchers who measured the campaign observed deeper reorganisations, not a sustained majority. Qubic is a small project run by a small group of people, which leaves open the question of what a government, or any better organised and better funded group, could do to the same network. Its development is also more centralised, with a smaller group of developers and rule changes made through upgrades that every participant must install. A reasonable approach for most people is to keep what you need for the coming months where it is simple and stable, to make sure no single institution holds everything, and to learn, with small amounts, how to hold at least 1 asset that needs nobody's permission. Which asset, and how much, is your decision alone.","href":"/book/en/savings/#choose-where-value-can-live"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"The rules around an asset can change","kicker":"Ch. 12 \u00b7 Understand","text":"The answers in the table are not fixed, because the rules around an asset can change with a single decision. On 5 April 1933, Executive Order 6102 required people in the United States to deliver their gold coins, bullion and certificates to the Federal Reserve by 1 May, above an exemption of 100 dollars per person. The penalty was a fine of up to 10,000 dollars, up to 10 years in prison, or both. Monero is a current European example. Kraken stopped trading it for clients in the European Economic Area on 31 October 2024, citing regulatory changes. The EU's anti-money-laundering regulation of 31 May 2024 goes further, since its Article 79 prohibits banks and crypto-asset service providers, from 10 July 2027, from keeping accounts that allow transactions to be anonymised, including through \"anonymity-enhancing coins\". The prohibition is addressed to regulated intermediaries, and the regulation states that it does not apply to providers of self-hosted wallets. The practical effect, as of September 2026, is that Monero is becoming harder to buy and to sell through regulated platforms in Europe. Some of its users see this exclusion as its strength. An asset that large marketplaces cannot list depends on none of them, and it is used by individuals, from person to person. Bitcoin has both faces, since it is held by large institutions and traded on regulated platforms while also circulating between individuals, peer to peer.","href":"/book/en/savings/#the-rules-around-an-asset-can-change"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 1: Draw the map","kicker":"Ch. 12 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Draw the map. On paper, list where your savings are, including accounts, funds, pension, cash and anything else. Next to each, write who holds it.","href":"/book/en/savings/#step-1"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 2: Ask the 5 questions","kicker":"Ch. 12 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Ask the 5 questions for each line. You are not looking for a perfect score, only for what you did not know.","href":"/book/en/savings/#step-2"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 3: Find the single point of failure","kicker":"Ch. 12 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Find the single point of failure. A single bank for everything, a single broker or a single country is the first thing to fix, and fixing it can be as simple as opening a second account elsewhere.","href":"/book/en/savings/#step-3"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 4: Keep a reserve that needs nobody's permission","kicker":"Ch. 12 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Keep a reserve that needs nobody's permission. A modest amount of cash at home covers a card outage or a frozen account for a few days.","href":"/book/en/savings/#step-4"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 5: Learn 1 self-held asset with small amounts","kicker":"Ch. 12 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Learn 1 self-held asset with small amounts. For Bitcoin, chapters 13 to 18 take you through it. For physical gold, the usual precautions are to choose common, recognisable coins from an established dealer, to compare the price with the metal price of the day, and to decide where the coins will be stored before anything else. Weighing and measuring a coin yourself, or later checking your bitcoin balance with your own node (chapter 18), is the step that replaces trust with verification.","href":"/book/en/savings/#step-5"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 6: Keep records","kicker":"Ch. 12 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Keep records. Dates, amounts and prices of what you buy and sell. Your tax return will need them.","href":"/book/en/savings/#step-6"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 7: Plan for your absence","kicker":"Ch. 12 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Plan for your absence. An asset that only you can reach, and that only you know about, disappears with you. Chapter 6 explains how to leave instructions without handing over the keys.","href":"/book/en/savings/#step-7"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Step by step","kicker":"Ch. 12 \u00b7 Tutorial","text":"Draw the map. On paper, list where your savings are, including accounts, funds, pension, cash and anything else. Next to each, write who holds it. Ask the 5 questions for each line. You are not looking for a perfect score, only for what you did not know. Find the single point of failure. A single bank for everything, a single broker or a single country is the first thing to fix, and fixing it can be as simple as opening a second account elsewhere. Keep a reserve that needs nobody's permission. A modest amount of cash at home covers a card outage or a frozen account for a few days. Learn 1 self-held asset with small amounts. For Bitcoin, chapters 13 to 18 take you through it. For physical gold, the usual precautions are to choose common, recognisable coins from an established dealer, to compare the price with the metal price of the day, and to decide where the coins will be stored before anything else. Weighing and measuring a coin yourself, or later checking your bitcoin balance with your own node (chapter 18), is the step that replaces trust with verification. Keep records. Dates, amounts and prices of what you buy and sell. Your tax return will need them. Plan for your absence. An asset that only you can reach, and that only you know about, disappears with you. Chapter 6 explains how to leave instructions without handing over the keys.","href":"/book/en/savings/#step-by-step"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Mistakes to avoid","kicker":"Ch. 12 \u00b7 Watch out","text":"Confusing a claim with the thing. A gold fund is not gold, and bitcoin on an exchange is not bitcoin in your wallet. Both are promises. Putting everything in a single place, whether that place is a bank or the asset you have just fallen in love with. Investing money you will need soon, or money you borrowed, in something volatile. Bitcoin, Monero, shares and even gold can lose a large part of their price for years, and a loan still has to be repaid after the price has fallen. Following tips. Anyone promising a return, a deadline or a secret is selling something. Be especially careful with strangers who contact you first. Talking about what you hold. Discretion is part of security, online and at dinner. Forgetting taxes. Selling at a profit is usually taxable, whatever the asset and however you acquired it.","href":"/book/en/savings/#mistakes-to-avoid"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Go further","kicker":"Ch. 12 \u00b7 Go further","text":"How the monetary system works, for general readers: \"Broken Money\", by Lyn Alden. How your deposits are protected: the European Commission's page on deposit guarantee schemes (ec.europa.eu). Monero, from the project itself: getmonero.org. Common wallets include Monero GUI, Feather Wallet and Cake Wallet. Next chapter: your first Bitcoin wallet and your first sats.","href":"/book/en/savings/#go-further"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Sources","kicker":"Ch. 12 \u00b7 References","text":"What are interest rates? Nominal and real returns, European Central Bank, checked 22 October 2026. Deposit guarantee schemes, summary of Directive 2014/49/EU, EUR-Lex, consulted in September 2026. Cyprus: National Bank Holiday, 2013, Yale Program on Financial Stability, Journal of Financial Crises, 2025. Executive Order 6102, Forbidding the Hoarding of Gold Coin, Gold Bullion and Gold Certificates, The American Presidency Project, 5 April 1933. Regulation (EU) 2024/1624, Articles 79 and 80, Official Journal of the European Union, 31 May 2024. Support for Monero (XMR) in Europe, Kraken, October 2024. Cover Story: The Mystery of $2 Billion of Loans Backed by Fake Gold, Caixin Global, 29 June 2020. Running a full node, Bitcoin.org, consulted in September 2026. Qubic Claims Majority Control of Monero Hashrate, Raising 51% Attack Fears, CoinDesk, 12 August 2025. Monero Suffers Deepest-Ever Blockchain Reorganization, Invalidating 118 Transactions, CoinDesk, 15 September 2025. Inside Qubic's Selfish Mining Campaign on Monero: Evidence, Tactics, and Limits, Suhyeon Lee and Hyeongyeong Kim, arXiv, December 2025, revised August 2026. Monero will undergo a network upgrade on 13th August, 2022, getmonero.org, 20 April 2022. FAQ, tail emission at block 2641623 on 9 June 2022, Monero project, checked 22 October 2026. The current FAQ gives the actual start; the older Moneropedia page still carries an end-of-May estimate.","href":"/book/en/savings/#sources"},{"type":"action","part":3,"title":"Draw the map of where your savings are and who holds each part","kicker":"Ch. 12 \u00b7 Checklist action, 1 hour","text":"","href":"/book/en/savings/#action-savings-draw-map"},{"type":"action","part":3,"title":"Answer the 5 questions for your main account and your main investment","kicker":"Ch. 12 \u00b7 Checklist action, 1 hour","text":"","href":"/book/en/savings/#action-savings-5-questions"},{"type":"action","part":3,"title":"Check that no single bank or broker holds everything you own","kicker":"Ch. 12 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/savings/#action-savings-single-point"},{"type":"action","part":3,"title":"Set aside a small cash reserve at home","kicker":"Ch. 12 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/savings/#action-savings-cash-reserve"},{"type":"action","part":3,"title":"Decide which self-held asset you want to learn first, and with what small amount","kicker":"Ch. 12 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/savings/#action-savings-choose-asset"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Your checklist","kicker":"Ch. 12 \u00b7 Act","text":"Draw the map of where your savings are and who holds each partAnswer the 5 questions for your main account and your main investmentCheck that no single bank or broker holds everything you ownSet aside a small cash reserve at homeDecide which self-held asset you want to learn first, and with what small amount","href":"/book/en/savings/#your-checklist"},{"type":"chapter","part":3,"title":"Your first wallet and your first sats","kicker":"Chapter 13 \u00b7 Money and Bitcoin","text":"In brief \u00b7 An exchange balance is an IOU \u00b7 A wallet holds keys, not coins \u00b7 2 choices you will meet \u00b7 Holding the keys is not the whole story \u00b7 Choose your wallet \u00b7 Step by step \u00b7 Mistakes to avoid \u00b7 Go further \u00b7 Sources \u00b7 Your checklist","href":"/book/en/first-wallet/"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"In brief","kicker":"Ch. 13 \u00b7 In brief","text":"Bitcoin left on an exchange is a promise from a company, whereas bitcoin in a wallet where you hold the keys is under your own control. A wallet is a free app that takes about 10 minutes to set up, and you can learn with an amount close to the price of a lunch. Install one, write down the recovery words, receive a first payment, send part of it back, then try a second wallet from another team to compare.","href":"/book/en/first-wallet/#in-brief"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"An exchange balance is an IOU","kicker":"Ch. 13 \u00b7 Understand","text":"When you buy bitcoin on an exchange and leave it there, what you hold is a line in the company's database saying that it owes you some. As long as the company stays honest, solvent, secure and allowed to serve you, the difference with real bitcoin is invisible. Mt. Gox, which was the largest bitcoin exchange in the world, filed for bankruptcy in February 2014 and announced that about 850,000 bitcoin were missing. Its trustee made the first repayments to creditors in July 2024, and in October 2025 he postponed the final deadline to 31 October 2026, more than 12 years after the collapse. FTX, one of the largest exchanges of its time, filed for bankruptcy in November 2022, a few days after withdrawals were suspended. Its founder was convicted of fraud in November 2023 and sentenced to 25 years in prison in March 2024. Between events of that size there are more ordinary ones, such as frozen accounts, suspended withdrawals and hacked platforms, which Bitcoin users sum up with the saying \"Not your keys, not your coins.\"","href":"/book/en/first-wallet/#an-exchange-balance-is-an-iou"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"A wallet holds keys, not coins","kicker":"Ch. 13 \u00b7 Understand","text":"Every bitcoin exists only as an entry in the blockchain, the public ledger that thousands of computers keep in sync. What your wallet holds is keys, secret numbers that prove to the network that you are allowed to move a given entry. Spending means signing a message with your key, so whoever holds the key controls the coins, without a name, an account or a permission being involved. The app is therefore a window and a pen. It shows you what your keys control and it signs when you decide to pay. Many wallets back up their keys as 12 or 24 words. Other formats exist, and recovery requires compatible software (chapter 14). Lightning may also need wallet-specific recovery data. You do not need a whole bitcoin either. 1 bitcoin divides into 100 million units called satoshis, or sats, and a wallet works the same way with 20,000 sats as with 2 bitcoin, which allows you to learn with small amounts.","href":"/book/en/first-wallet/#a-wallet-holds-keys-not-coins"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"2 choices you will meet","kicker":"Ch. 13 \u00b7 Understand","text":"On-chain or Lightning. On-chain transactions are written directly into the blockchain. They take from minutes to hours and cost a fee that depends on demand, which suits larger amounts and savings. Lightning is a payment network built on top of Bitcoin, in practice instant and very cheap, which suits small everyday amounts (chapter 15). Custodial or self-custodial. A custodial wallet works like a small exchange, because a company holds the keys and shows you a balance. A self-custodial wallet keeps the keys on your phone. A custodial wallet is the easiest possible start, with nothing to back up and nothing to understand, and it is acceptable for an amount you could lose without pain, like the coins in your pocket. In the EU the option is shrinking, however, because custodial providers now need a licence and must identify their users (chapter 15). Self-custody asks a little more of you, mainly a serious backup. In return no company can freeze or lose your balance on your behalf, although exchanges and brokers can still refuse to serve you.","href":"/book/en/first-wallet/#2-choices-you-will-meet"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Holding the keys is not the whole story","kicker":"Ch. 13 \u00b7 Understand","text":"With a custodial wallet the trusted third party is obvious, because the company holds the keys and can freeze or lose your balance. Holding your own keys removes that party and leaves 3 quieter ones in place. The first is the server your wallet asks. A phone wallet does not check the blockchain itself, it queries a server, usually the wallet team's, which learns your addresses and your IP address and could show a wrong balance, although it cannot spend your coins. The second is the delivery channel, because Apple's or Google's store and the developer's signing key decide which code runs on your phone. The third, on Lightning, is the service provider. Phoenix opens its channels only with ACINQ's node, which usually sees where your payments go and could decline to relay them, while wallets built on Spark depend on a small group of operators. Both designs keep an exit to the blockchain, at a cost. Each of these can be partly checked. Open source code can be read, WalletScrutiny tests whether the app in the store can be rebuilt from that code (a reproducible build), and several wallets let you connect to your own node (chapter 18).","href":"/book/en/first-wallet/#holding-the-keys-is-not-the-whole-story"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"Bitkit","kicker":"Ch. 13 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your wallet\u201d","text":"Who holds the keys: You \u00b7 Who it still depends on: Synonym's provider, Blocktank, for Lightning channels by default. Synonym's Electrum server for blockchain data, replaceable by your own. \u00b7 Networks: On-chain and Lightning, as 2 balances in one app \u00b7 Open source: Yes \u00b7 Honest trade-off: A Lightning node on your phone next to an on-chain savings balance, behind 1 backup. The first Lightning payment pays for the channel (about 1,800 sats in mid-September 2026, depending on mining fees), total Lightning capacity is capped near 999 dollars, and Lightning is not offered to residents of the United States or Canada.","href":"/book/en/first-wallet/#row-bitkit"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"Phoenix","kicker":"Ch. 13 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your wallet\u201d","text":"Who holds the keys: You \u00b7 Who it still depends on: ACINQ's node for every Lightning payment. Electrum servers for blockchain data, replaceable by your own. \u00b7 Networks: Lightning (on-chain in and out through swaps) \u00b7 Open source: Yes \u00b7 Honest trade-off: A real Lightning node on your phone, simple to use. Depends on a single company (ACINQ) for its channels. Opening the first channel costs 1,000 sats plus 1% plus the mining fee, so a very small first payment is refused.","href":"/book/en/first-wallet/#row-phoenix"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"Breez (Glow, formerly Misty Breez)","kicker":"Ch. 13 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your wallet\u201d","text":"Who holds the keys: You \u00b7 Who it still depends on: The Spark operators, who co-sign each transfer, and Breez's software. \u00b7 Networks: Lightning, through the Spark network \u00b7 Open source: Yes \u00b7 Honest trade-off: Misty Breez lost its Lightning and on-chain payments in August 2026. Breez now points to Glow, launched the same month. No channels to manage, but very new, and Spark relies on a small group of operators.","href":"/book/en/first-wallet/#row-breez-glow-formerly-misty-breez"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"BlueWallet","kicker":"Ch. 13 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your wallet\u201d","text":"Who holds the keys: You \u00b7 Who it still depends on: BlueWallet's Electrum servers for balances and broadcasting, replaceable by your own in the settings. \u00b7 Networks: On-chain \u00b7 Open source: Yes \u00b7 Honest trade-off: Clear and good for learning on-chain. Its hosted Lightning service closed in 2023.","href":"/book/en/first-wallet/#row-bluewallet"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"Blockstream app (formerly Green)","kicker":"Ch. 13 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your wallet\u201d","text":"Who holds the keys: You \u00b7 Who it still depends on: Blockstream's servers by default, or your own Electrum server. The Liquid federation and a swap service for Lightning. \u00b7 Networks: On-chain, Liquid, Lightning through swaps \u00b7 Open source: Yes \u00b7 Honest trade-off: Many options, works with hardware wallets. More menus for a beginner, and its Lightning payments settle on Liquid through swaps.","href":"/book/en/first-wallet/#row-blockstream-app-formerly-green"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"Zeus","kicker":"Ch. 13 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your wallet\u201d","text":"Who holds the keys: You \u00b7 Who it still depends on: Zeus's own service provider (Olympus) for channels, by default. Other Bitcoin nodes for blockchain data, or your own. \u00b7 Networks: Lightning and on-chain \u00b7 Open source: Yes \u00b7 Honest trade-off: A real Lightning node in your phone, or a remote control for your own node (chapter 18). Powerful, more to learn. In August 2026 an attack on Zeus's servers closed many users' channels, without loss of customer funds.","href":"/book/en/first-wallet/#row-zeus"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"Wallet of Satoshi","kicker":"Ch. 13 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your wallet\u201d","text":"Who holds the keys: You in the EU, where its custodial mode has been unavailable since January 2026. The company elsewhere. \u00b7 Who it still depends on: The company for a custodial balance. In self-custodial mode, the Spark operators and the company's servers. \u00b7 Networks: Lightning \u00b7 Open source: No \u00b7 Honest trade-off: The easiest of all. Its self-custodial mode runs on Spark, a recent system that relies on a small group of operators. Pocket money only.","href":"/book/en/first-wallet/#row-wallet-of-satoshi"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"Bringin","kicker":"Ch. 13 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your wallet\u201d","text":"Who holds the keys: You \u00b7 Who it still depends on: Bringin for the optional euro account, which it can block like any payment company. The bitcoin wallet is self-custodial, so Bringin does not hold those funds. \u00b7 Networks: Lightning and on-chain \u00b7 Open source: Not stated on its website \u00b7 Honest trade-off: Wallet linked to an optional euro account, which requires identity verification (chapter 15).","href":"/book/en/first-wallet/#row-bringin"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"Arkade wallet","kicker":"Ch. 13 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your wallet\u201d","text":"Who holds the keys: You \u00b7 Who it still depends on: The Arkade server run by Ark Labs, which co-signs each Ark payment. The exit to the blockchain works without it. \u00b7 Networks: Ark and on-chain \u00b7 Open source: Yes \u00b7 Honest trade-off: A web app installed from the browser, without an app store. Public beta since October 2025, and Ark Labs wrote at launch that it is not ready for savings.","href":"/book/en/first-wallet/#row-arkade-wallet"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"Noah","kicker":"Ch. 13 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your wallet\u201d","text":"Who holds the keys: You \u00b7 Who it still depends on: The Ark server run by Second, which co-signs payments and connects the wallet to Lightning. The exit to the blockchain works without it. \u00b7 Networks: Ark, Lightning and on-chain \u00b7 Open source: Yes \u00b7 Honest trade-off: From the team behind the Blixt wallet. Beta for Android, and for iPhone through TestFlight. Its developers ask you to deposit only what you can afford to lose.","href":"/book/en/first-wallet/#row-noah"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Choose your wallet","kicker":"Ch. 13 \u00b7 Compare","text":"All the wallets below are free, and it helps to try 2 of them from different teams, so that a problem with one app or one service never leaves you unable to pay. The first row, Bitkit, is this book's editorial choice for a first wallet. The app, published by Synonym for iPhone and Android, shows 2 balances on one screen, savings held on-chain and spending held on Lightning, behind a single list of 12 words. You can receive over Lightning from the first minute, because Synonym's service provider, Blocktank, opens a channel when the first payment arrives and deducts its fee, about 1,800 sats in a case documented in mid-September 2026. Bitkit depends on Blocktank for that channel, and its Lightning connections are not offered to residents of the United States or Canada. The last 2 rows are built on Ark, a way to make fast, cheap off-chain bitcoin payments in which many users share the same on-chain transactions through an Ark server. You keep pre-signed transactions that allow you to exit to the blockchain by yourself if that server disappears. Ark is young, since it opened on Bitcoin's main network in October 2025 with Arkade and in June 2026 with Second's Bark, and on Second's server a balance expires after 28 days unless the wallet refreshes it. The table is valid as of September 2026, so check each project's website before you install. Wallet Who holds the keys Who it still depends on Networks Open source Honest trade-off Bitkit You Synonym's provider, Blocktank, for Lightning channels by default. Synonym's Electrum server for blockchain data, replaceable by your own. On-chain and Lightning, as 2 balances in one app Yes A Lightning node on your phone next to an on-chain savings balance, behind 1 backup. The first Lightning payment pays for the channel (about 1,800 sats in mid-September 2026, depending on mining fees), total Lightning capacity is capped near 999 dollars, and Lightning is not offered to residents of the United States or Canada. Phoenix You ACINQ's node for every Lightning payment. Electrum servers for blockchain data, replaceable by your own. Lightning (on-chain in and out through swaps) Yes A real Lightning node on your phone, simple to use. Depends on a single company (ACINQ) for its channels. Opening the first channel costs 1,000 sats plus 1% plus the mining fee, so a very small first payment is refused. Breez (Glow, formerly Misty Breez) You The Spark operators, who co-sign each transfer, and Breez's software. Lightning, through the Spark network Yes Misty Breez lost its Lightning and on-chain payments in August 2026. Breez now points to Glow, launched the same month. No channels to manage, but very new, and Spark relies on a small group of operators. BlueWallet You BlueWallet's Electrum servers for balances and broadcasting, replaceable by your own in the settings. On-chain Yes Clear and good for learning on-chain. Its hosted Lightning service closed in 2023. Blockstream app (formerly Green) You Blockstream's servers by default, or your own Electrum server. The Liquid federation and a swap service for Lightning. On-chain, Liquid, Lightning through swaps Yes Many options, works with hardware wallets. More menus for a beginner, and its Lightning payments settle on Liquid through swaps. Zeus You Zeus's own service provider (Olympus) for channels, by default. Other Bitcoin nodes for blockchain data, or your own. Lightning and on-chain Yes A real Lightning node in your phone, or a remote control for your own node (chapter 18). Powerful, more to learn. In August 2026 an attack on Zeus's servers closed many users' channels, without loss of customer funds. Wallet of Satoshi You in the EU, where its custodial mode has been unavailable since January 2026. The company elsewhere. The company for a custodial balance. In self-custodial mode, the Spark operators and the company's servers. Lightning No The easiest of all. Its self-custodial mode runs on Spark, a recent system that relies on a small group of operators. Pocket money only. Bringin You Bringin for the optional euro account, which it can block like any payment company. The bitcoin wallet is self-custodial, so Bringin does not hold those funds. Lightning and on-chain Not stated on its website Wallet linked to an optional euro account, which requires identity verification (chapter 15). Arkade wallet You The Arkade server run by Ark Labs, which co-signs each Ark payment. The exit to the blockchain works without it. Ark and on-chain Yes A web app installed from the browser, without an app store. Public beta since October 2025, and Ark Labs wrote at launch that it is not ready for savings. Noah You The Ark server run by Second, which co-signs payments and connects the wallet to Lightning. The exit to the blockchain works without it. Ark, Lightning and on-chain Yes From the team behind the Blixt wallet. Beta for Android, and for iPhone through TestFlight. Its developers ask you to deposit only what you can afford to lose. A reasonable setup for most people is a self-custodial Lightning wallet for small amounts and an on-chain wallet to understand the base layer, either in one app such as Bitkit or in 2 separate apps, with BlueWallet or the Blockstream app for the on-chain side. Wallets that run their own channels (Bitkit, Phoenix, Zeus) cost more on the first day and depend on fewer outside services, while those built on swaps or on Spark are the other way round. Try 2 and keep the one you find clear.","href":"/book/en/first-wallet/#choose-your-wallet"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 1: Install it","kicker":"Ch. 13 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Install it from the official store. Before you tap, check the developer name (Synonym Software for Bitkit) and follow the link from the project's official website, bitkit.to, rather than a store search. This check, completed by a look at the app's page on walletscrutiny.com, is the step that replaces trust with verification, as far as a phone allows.","href":"/book/en/first-wallet/#step-1"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 2: Create a new wallet","kicker":"Ch. 13 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Create a new wallet. The app asks for no email, no name and no account, which is normal.","href":"/book/en/first-wallet/#step-2"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 3: Write down the recovery words before receiving anything","kicker":"Ch. 13 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Write down the recovery words before receiving anything. Find the 12 words in the backup settings, copy them by hand on paper, and read chapter 14 before adding more than pocket money.","href":"/book/en/first-wallet/#step-3"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 4: Receive a first payment larger than the channel fee","kicker":"Ch. 13 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Receive a first payment larger than the channel fee. Tap Receive, choose to receive on the spending balance and show the QR code, keeping the app open until the payment appears. Blocktank opens your channel at that moment and takes its fee out of the amount, 1,835 sats in a case reported on 15 September 2026, more when mining fees rise. The same report, still open on 20 September, shows that the suggested minimum of 2,000 sats can leave a balance too small to spend. Ask for a realistic amount, for example 20,000 sats, and read the fee the app displays before you accept. To test with a few hundred sats only, use a wallet without channels (Wallet of Satoshi or Glow) and accept its trade-off.","href":"/book/en/first-wallet/#step-4"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 5: Send a small amount back","kicker":"Ch. 13 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Send a small amount back. Scan the other person's QR code, check the amount, confirm. It usually arrives in 1 or 2 seconds.","href":"/book/en/first-wallet/#step-5"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 6: Check the fee","kicker":"Ch. 13 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Check the fee in the payment details, so that you know what a normal fee looks like.","href":"/book/en/first-wallet/#step-6"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 7: Try a second wallet","kicker":"Ch. 13 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Try a second wallet, send sats from one to the other, and compare.","href":"/book/en/first-wallet/#step-7"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Step by step","kicker":"Ch. 13 \u00b7 Tutorial","text":"The example uses Bitkit. The logic is the same for the others. Install it from the official store. Before you tap, check the developer name (Synonym Software for Bitkit) and follow the link from the project's official website, bitkit.to, rather than a store search. This check, completed by a look at the app's page on walletscrutiny.com, is the step that replaces trust with verification, as far as a phone allows. Create a new wallet. The app asks for no email, no name and no account, which is normal. Write down the recovery words before receiving anything. Find the 12 words in the backup settings, copy them by hand on paper, and read chapter 14 before adding more than pocket money. Receive a first payment larger than the channel fee. Tap Receive, choose to receive on the spending balance and show the QR code, keeping the app open until the payment appears. Blocktank opens your channel at that moment and takes its fee out of the amount, 1,835 sats in a case reported on 15 September 2026, more when mining fees rise. The same report, still open on 20 September, shows that the suggested minimum of 2,000 sats can leave a balance too small to spend. Ask for a realistic amount, for example 20,000 sats, and read the fee the app displays before you accept. To test with a few hundred sats only, use a wallet without channels (Wallet of Satoshi or Glow) and accept its trade-off. Send a small amount back. Scan the other person's QR code, check the amount, confirm. It usually arrives in 1 or 2 seconds. Check the fee in the payment details, so that you know what a normal fee looks like. Try a second wallet, send sats from one to the other, and compare.","href":"/book/en/first-wallet/#step-by-step"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Mistakes to avoid","kicker":"Ch. 13 \u00b7 Watch out","text":"Leaving savings on an exchange. An exchange is useful for the time of a purchase. Afterwards, withdraw to a wallet you control. Taking a screenshot of your recovery words. Photos are often synced to the cloud and can be read by other apps. Use paper and a pen (chapter 14). Installing a fake wallet. App stores contain copies built to steal. Check the developer name and start from the project's official site. Sending to the wrong network. A Lightning invoice, an on-chain address and a Liquid address are 3 different things. A good wallet refuses the wrong one, and if something looks odd, stop and ask. Starting with a large amount. Beginner mistakes happen in the first weeks, so make them with a small sum. Trusting anyone who offers to \"help\" with your wallet online. No wallet team, exchange or authority needs your recovery words.","href":"/book/en/first-wallet/#mistakes-to-avoid"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Go further","kicker":"Ch. 13 \u00b7 Go further","text":"Step-by-step wallet tutorials: Plan B Academy (planb.academy). Check whether a wallet is really open source: WalletScrutiny (walletscrutiny.com). Plain guides for beginners: Bitcoiner.Guide (bitcoiner.guide). Next chapter: backing up your seed phrase.","href":"/book/en/first-wallet/#go-further"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Sources","kicker":"Ch. 13 \u00b7 References","text":"The Inside Story of Mt. Gox, Bitcoin's $460 Million Disaster, Wired, 3 March 2014. Notice Concerning Change of Repayments Deadline, MtGox Co., Ltd. Rehabilitation Trustee, 27 October 2025. Samuel Bankman-Fried Sentenced to 25 Years for His Orchestration of Multiple Fraudulent Schemes, U.S. Department of Justice, 28 March 2024. Methodology, WalletScrutiny, consulted in September 2026. Spark trust model, Spark documentation, consulted in September 2026. Phoenix FAQ: fees, restoring a wallet, ACINQ, consulted in September 2026. How do I manage my spending balance? (Blocktank, the 999 dollar cap, United States and Canada), Bitkit Help Center, Synonym, consulted in September 2026. CJIT at suggested minimum yields unspendable channel (Blocktank fee of 1,835 sats on a first payment), Bitkit issue tracker on GitHub, 15 September 2026. Press Start: Arkade Goes Live, Ark Labs, 20 October 2025. Introducing Noah and Ark\u00e9: Two new Bark-based wallets, Second, 10 June 2026. Intro to the Ark protocol, Second documentation, consulted in September 2026.","href":"/book/en/first-wallet/#sources"},{"type":"action","part":3,"title":"Install a self-custodial wallet from its official source, after checking the developer name and its page on walletscrutiny.com","kicker":"Ch. 13 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/first-wallet/#action-first-wallet-install"},{"type":"action","part":3,"title":"Write the recovery words on paper before receiving anything","kicker":"Ch. 13 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/first-wallet/#action-first-wallet-write-words"},{"type":"action","part":3,"title":"Receive a first payment larger than the setup fee and send some back","kicker":"Ch. 13 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/first-wallet/#action-first-wallet-first-payment"},{"type":"action","part":3,"title":"Install a second wallet from another team and compare","kicker":"Ch. 13 \u00b7 Checklist action, 1 hour","text":"","href":"/book/en/first-wallet/#action-first-wallet-second-wallet"},{"type":"action","part":3,"title":"Move anything you left on an exchange to a wallet you control","kicker":"Ch. 13 \u00b7 Checklist action, 1 hour","text":"","href":"/book/en/first-wallet/#action-first-wallet-leave-exchange"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Your checklist","kicker":"Ch. 13 \u00b7 Act","text":"Install a self-custodial wallet from its official source, after checking the developer name and its page on walletscrutiny.comWrite the recovery words on paper before receiving anythingReceive a first payment larger than the setup fee and send some backInstall a second wallet from another team and compareMove anything you left on an exchange to a wallet you control","href":"/book/en/first-wallet/#your-checklist"},{"type":"chapter","part":3,"title":"Backing up your seed phrase","kicker":"Chapter 14 \u00b7 Money and Bitcoin","text":"In brief \u00b7 No support desk, no reset button \u00b7 A single random number, written as words \u00b7 Nobody to trust, nobody to call \u00b7 Choose how to store it \u00b7 No single right setup \u00b7 Step by step \u00b7 Mistakes to avoid \u00b7 Go further \u00b7 Sources \u00b7 Your checklist","href":"/book/en/seed/"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"In brief","kicker":"Ch. 14 \u00b7 In brief","text":"Your recovery words back up the keys to your wallet, and the phone is a tool for using them. Many wallets use 12 or 24 words; some use a different format. Whoever reads the words can take the coins, and if you lose both the phone and the words, the coins can no longer be recovered. Write them by hand, keep 2 copies in 2 places you control, never make a digital copy, and test the backup with the method appropriate to your wallet while the amount is still small (step 6).","href":"/book/en/seed/#in-brief"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"No support desk, no reset button","kicker":"Ch. 14 \u00b7 Understand","text":"With a bank, losing access is an inconvenience, because you prove who you are and someone resets it. Self-custody removes that someone. There is no support desk, no \"forgot my words\" button and no court that can order the network to give your coins back. It is what makes your bitcoin very hard to freeze or seize from a distance, and it also leaves you as the only person responsible. 2 things go wrong in real life, and they pull in opposite directions. The first is loss, when a phone falls into a lake, the paper is thrown out during a move, or a fire destroys the single copy. The second is theft, when the words were saved in a photo, an email draft, a cloud note or a password manager, and someone found them. In March 2025, US federal investigators linked a theft of about 150 million dollars, committed in January 2024, to secrets stored in the LastPass password manager, which had been breached in 2022. Coins stolen this way move within minutes and cannot be recalled, so a good backup has to protect against loss and theft at once.","href":"/book/en/seed/#no-support-desk-no-reset-button"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"A single random number, written as words","kicker":"Ch. 14 \u00b7 Understand","text":"When you create a wallet, the app generates a single very large random number, and everything else comes from it, meaning every key and every address for every coin you will ever receive in that wallet. A common standard called BIP39 writes this number, most often, as 12 or 24 words taken from a fixed list of 2,048 simple English words, because people copy words more reliably than long strings of digits. The last word includes a checksum, which detects most copying errors. For keys derived from that seed, the words do not change after each payment. Recovery still needs a compatible backup format, the right address type or derivation path, and any passphrase you used. Trezor also supports SLIP39 backups, commonly 20 words; native Electrum seeds use a different scheme from BIP39. Do not assume that any wallet accepts any words. Keep the wallet name and backup format with your recovery instructions. The seed alone does not restore labels, separately imported keys or every wallet setting; back up those according to the wallet's documentation. Lightning is the exception. Funds in channels depend on the wallet that opened them, and some Lightning wallets need an extra backup file in addition to the words. If so, the app says it clearly, and its instructions come first.","href":"/book/en/seed/#a-single-random-number-written-as-words"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Nobody to trust, nobody to call","kicker":"Ch. 14 \u00b7 Understand","text":"An on-chain seed backup in an open format can be recovered without the original company or an account, using compatible software and blockchain data. You still depend on the recovery software and device, and on a server unless you use your own node. Keeping only the words is not enough for every wallet design, especially Lightning and multisig. The backup's availability and confidentiality rest on you. Every convenience that softens this brings a third party back. A steel plate ordered online puts your name and address in a maker's customer file, the kind that leaked at Ledger in 2020 (see \"Mistakes to avoid\"). Shamir shares and multi-signature spread the risk, but each person holding a share or a key can lose it or collude, and the software that splits and rebuilds the secret has to be trusted too. Any service that offers to recover your seed for you goes further. To return a backup after an identity check, a company has to hold it outside your home, where it can be breached, compelled or closed. It can suit someone who fears loss above all, knowing that trusted third parties are then back in the picture. Verification means checking that the backup works with the recovery procedure appropriate to your wallet (step 6).","href":"/book/en/seed/#nobody-to-trust-nobody-to-call"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"Paper, pencil or permanent ink","kicker":"Ch. 14 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose how to store it\u201d","text":"Cost: Almost nothing \u00b7 Who you must trust: Nobody, apart from the people who can reach the place where it is kept \u00b7 Strength: Immediate, no tool needed \u00b7 Weakness: Water, fire, fading, easily thrown away by mistake","href":"/book/en/seed/#row-paper-pencil-or-permanent-ink"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"Laminated paper","kicker":"Ch. 14 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose how to store it\u201d","text":"Cost: A few euros \u00b7 Who you must trust: Nobody, as long as the laminator is yours. A copy shop would be a third party. \u00b7 Strength: Resists water and wear \u00b7 Weakness: Still burns. Use your own laminator, not a copy shop.","href":"/book/en/seed/#row-laminated-paper"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"Steel product: Cryptosteel, Seedor and others","kicker":"Ch. 14 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose how to store it\u201d","text":"Cost: Tens of euros, up to about a hundred \u00b7 Who you must trust: The maker and the shop, which learn your name and address, but never the words \u00b7 Strength: Resists fire, water and time \u00b7 Weakness: The price. A visible object that says \"bitcoin\" to whoever finds it.","href":"/book/en/seed/#row-steel-product-cryptosteel-seedor-and-others"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"DIY steel: letters stamped into steel washers held on a bolt","kicker":"Ch. 14 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose how to store it\u201d","text":"Cost: A few euros of hardware, plus a set of letter stamps \u00b7 Who you must trust: Nobody. A hardware shop has no idea what the washers are for. \u00b7 Strength: Same resistance, discreet, no brand to trust \u00b7 Weakness: An hour of work, and care when stamping","href":"/book/en/seed/#row-diy-steel-letters-stamped-into-steel-washers-held-on-a-bolt"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"Passphrase (the optional \"25th word\")","kicker":"Ch. 14 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose how to store it\u201d","text":"Cost: Free \u00b7 Who you must trust: Nobody else, provided it is written down and kept apart from the words \u00b7 Strength: The words alone are no longer enough for a thief, if the passphrase is long and random \u00b7 Weakness: A short or guessable one adds almost nothing. An extra secret to back up and to pass on. Forgotten passphrase, lost coins.","href":"/book/en/seed/#row-passphrase-the-optional-25th-word"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Choose how to store it","kicker":"Ch. 14 \u00b7 Compare","text":"The right support depends on the amount and on what threatens your home, whether water, fire, burglars or curious visitors. All of the options below keep the words offline, and they can be combined. Method Cost Who you must trust Strength Weakness Paper, pencil or permanent ink Almost nothing Nobody, apart from the people who can reach the place where it is kept Immediate, no tool needed Water, fire, fading, easily thrown away by mistake Laminated paper A few euros Nobody, as long as the laminator is yours. A copy shop would be a third party. Resists water and wear Still burns. Use your own laminator, not a copy shop. Steel product: Cryptosteel, Seedor and others Tens of euros, up to about a hundred The maker and the shop, which learn your name and address, but never the words Resists fire, water and time The price. A visible object that says \"bitcoin\" to whoever finds it. DIY steel: letters stamped into steel washers held on a bolt A few euros of hardware, plus a set of letter stamps Nobody. A hardware shop has no idea what the washers are for. Same resistance, discreet, no brand to trust An hour of work, and care when stamping Passphrase (the optional \"25th word\") Free Nobody else, provided it is written down and kept apart from the words The words alone are no longer enough for a thief, if the passphrase is long and random A short or guessable one adds almost nothing. An extra secret to back up and to pass on. Forgotten passphrase, lost coins. There are also 2 advanced options, for larger amounts: Splitting across locations. Standard schemes such as Shamir backup (SLIP39), supported by some hardware wallets, divide the secret into shares, for example any 2 of 3. SLIP39 uses its own word list and is not compatible with BIP39, so only wallets that support it can restore it. Do not invent your own scheme by cutting the word list in half (the next section explains why). Multi-signature. A wallet that requires several independent keys, for example 2 of 3, kept in different places, so that a single theft or loss is not fatal. Keep enough signing-key backups and the complete wallet configuration or output descriptor, including every cosigner's extended public key (xpub). Enough seeds alone may be insufficient. The descriptor cannot spend on its own but exposes wallet information, so protect its privacy. Chapter 17 introduces the devices that make this practical. A reasonable starting point for most people is paper on the first day, then a steel copy once the amount would hurt to lose, without a passphrase until you fully understand it. Beyond that, setups legitimately differ, as the next section shows.","href":"/book/en/seed/#choose-how-to-store-it"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"Several independent keys, or several hardware wallets, for different purposes such as spending and savings","kicker":"Ch. 14 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cNo single right setup\u201d","text":"What you gain: A theft or a mistake touches 1 wallet, not everything \u00b7 The price: More backups to store, label and test","href":"/book/en/seed/#row-several-independent-keys-or-several-hardware-wallets-for-different-purposes-such-as-spending-and-savings"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"1 key whose backup is split into fragments kept in different places, with a copy of each fragment in 2 places","kicker":"Ch. 14 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cNo single right setup\u201d","text":"What you gain: Losing 1 place does not lose the money, and finding 1 fragment does not reveal it \u00b7 The price: More places and people involved, and a restore that needs compatible software","href":"/book/en/seed/#row-1-key-whose-backup-is-split-into-fragments-kept-in-different-places-with-a-copy-of-each-fragment-in-2-places"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"1 key plus a passphrase (the optional extra word or sentence)","kicker":"Ch. 14 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cNo single right setup\u201d","text":"What you gain: The words alone no longer open the wallet \u00b7 The price: 1 more secret to back up and to pass on","href":"/book/en/seed/#row-1-key-plus-a-passphrase-the-optional-extra-word-or-sentence"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"No single right setup","kicker":"Ch. 14 \u00b7 Compare","text":"There is no single right setup, because amounts, homes, families and threats differ, and 2 careful people can reasonably choose differently. The 3 main approaches can be combined. Approach What you gain The price Several independent keys, or several hardware wallets, for different purposes such as spending and savings A theft or a mistake touches 1 wallet, not everything More backups to store, label and test 1 key whose backup is split into fragments kept in different places, with a copy of each fragment in 2 places Losing 1 place does not lose the money, and finding 1 fragment does not reveal it More places and people involved, and a restore that needs compatible software 1 key plus a passphrase (the optional extra word or sentence) The words alone no longer open the wallet 1 more secret to back up and to pass on Shamir secret sharing and multi-signature are different. In a proper threshold secret-sharing scheme, fewer than the required shares reveal no secret. Multisig uses independent keys; 1 key does not authorise spending when the threshold is higher, but may reveal wallet information. Preserve the full multisig configuration as well as the keys. Cutting the word list yourself gives each finder part of the secret, and in June 2020 John Cantrell found the 4 missing words of a 12-word phrase in about 30 hours with rented graphics cards. An attacker who holds the words can also try passphrases very fast, so a short or guessable one adds almost no security, and it has to be long and random. Each different passphrase opens a different wallet, which allows separate or hidden sub-accounts. It must itself be backed up, separately from the words, where your heirs can find it. The practical criterion is to choose the simplest setup you will still understand, and be able to restore, in 10 years.","href":"/book/en/seed/#no-single-right-setup"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 1: Choose the moment","kicker":"Ch. 14 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Choose the moment. Alone, at a table, no camera behind you, not in a caf\u00e9 or on a train.","href":"/book/en/seed/#step-1"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 2: Write the words by hand","kicker":"Ch. 14 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Write the words by hand, numbered, in order, in clear capital letters. Note the wallet's name and the date, not the amount.","href":"/book/en/seed/#step-2"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 3: Check them twice","kicker":"Ch. 14 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Check them twice. Read them back against the screen, word by word. Most wallets then ask you to confirm some of them.","href":"/book/en/seed/#step-3"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 4: Never make a digital version","kicker":"Ch. 14 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Never make a digital version. Do not type, photograph, print, dictate or email the words, and do not store them in the cloud or in a password manager. For a signing device, keep its backup out of ordinary software wallets. Follow its official backup-check and recovery procedure; do not type the words into an online device merely to test them.","href":"/book/en/seed/#step-4"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 5: Make 2 copies","kicker":"Ch. 14 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Make 2 copies and store them in 2 places you control, for example your home and the home of a close relative, each in a sealed envelope. The second place is a trade-off, because without a passphrase whoever opens the envelope controls the coins. Choose someone you would trust with the same amount in cash, and consider one of the approaches above once the amount grows. If you use fragments or a passphrase, the rule of 2 copies applies to each of them, and a passphrase is never stored with the words.","href":"/book/en/seed/#step-5"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 6: Test the backup in the right environment","kicker":"Ch. 14 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Test the backup in the right environment. For a small on-chain software wallet, restore on a second device you control with compatible software, compare its addresses and balance, then remove the test copy. Keep the original app throughout; this protects against a copying mistake, not against malware on the second device. For a hardware wallet, use the maker's backup-check feature or restore on compatible hardware through its official procedure. Never import a hardware-wallet seed into a phone or computer just for this test. A wipe-and-restore exercise is for a tiny test balance before savings are added (chapter 17).","href":"/book/en/seed/#step-6"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 7: Treat a Lightning wallet differently","kicker":"Ch. 14 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Treat a Lightning wallet differently. Deleting an app that holds open channels can cost you the funds if the restore fails, and Phoenix, for example, warns against running 2 apps on the same seed. Read your wallet's restore instructions first. The real test usually comes when you change phones, with a small balance, the old phone switched off, and the old app kept until the balance shows on the new one.","href":"/book/en/seed/#step-7"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 8: Plan the inheritance","kicker":"Ch. 14 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Plan the inheritance. Add a sealed letter explaining in plain words what this is, that it has value, that the words must never be typed into a website, who can be trusted to help, and how many pieces your setup has (words, fragments, passphrase) and where each is kept.","href":"/book/en/seed/#step-8"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Step by step","kicker":"Ch. 14 \u00b7 Tutorial","text":"The example uses paper and a pen. The logic is the same on steel. Choose the moment. Alone, at a table, no camera behind you, not in a caf\u00e9 or on a train. Write the words by hand, numbered, in order, in clear capital letters. Note the wallet's name and the date, not the amount. Check them twice. Read them back against the screen, word by word. Most wallets then ask you to confirm some of them. Never make a digital version. Do not type, photograph, print, dictate or email the words, and do not store them in the cloud or in a password manager. For a signing device, keep its backup out of ordinary software wallets. Follow its official backup-check and recovery procedure; do not type the words into an online device merely to test them. Make 2 copies and store them in 2 places you control, for example your home and the home of a close relative, each in a sealed envelope. The second place is a trade-off, because without a passphrase whoever opens the envelope controls the coins. Choose someone you would trust with the same amount in cash, and consider one of the approaches above once the amount grows. If you use fragments or a passphrase, the rule of 2 copies applies to each of them, and a passphrase is never stored with the words. Test the backup in the right environment. For a small on-chain software wallet, restore on a second device you control with compatible software, compare its addresses and balance, then remove the test copy. Keep the original app throughout; this protects against a copying mistake, not against malware on the second device. For a hardware wallet, use the maker's backup-check feature or restore on compatible hardware through its official procedure. Never import a hardware-wallet seed into a phone or computer just for this test. A wipe-and-restore exercise is for a tiny test balance before savings are added (chapter 17). Treat a Lightning wallet differently. Deleting an app that holds open channels can cost you the funds if the restore fails, and Phoenix, for example, warns against running 2 apps on the same seed. Read your wallet's restore instructions first. The real test usually comes when you change phones, with a small balance, the old phone switched off, and the old app kept until the balance shows on the new one. Plan the inheritance. Add a sealed letter explaining in plain words what this is, that it has value, that the words must never be typed into a website, who can be trusted to help, and how many pieces your setup has (words, fragments, passphrase) and where each is kept.","href":"/book/en/seed/#step-by-step"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Mistakes to avoid","kicker":"Ch. 14 \u00b7 Watch out","text":"Keeping a digital copy. Photos, notes, email drafts and cloud drives are exposed to malware and intruders. Deleting a file does not undo exposure or remove every synced copy. If the words may have leaked, follow the maker's instructions to create a fresh wallet and move the funds safely; never wipe your only working wallet first. Keeping a single copy. A fire, a flood or a house move is enough to lose it, which is why you keep 2 copies in 2 places. Telling people how much you own. In December 2020, the names, postal addresses and phone numbers of about 272,000 Ledger customers were published on a hacking forum after a breach of the company's shop database, and these customers were then targeted by phishing campaigns. Talk about Bitcoin as much as you like, not about your balance. Entering your words into a website or giving them to \"support\". No wallet team, exchange or authority will ever ask for them, so whoever asks is attempting a theft. Building a scheme you will not remember in 5 years. Words hidden in a book, split in 4, with an improvised code, have locked many people out of their own coins. Keep the scheme simple, documented and tested. Never testing, or testing by deleting the app. An untested backup may contain a wrong word, and if you delete the app first you discover it after the only working copy is gone. Use the software or hardware procedure appropriate to your wallet (step 6).","href":"/book/en/seed/#mistakes-to-avoid"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Go further","kicker":"Ch. 14 \u00b7 Go further","text":"Steel backups tested against fire, acid and a press: Jameson Lopp's metal storage reviews (jlopp.github.io/metal-bitcoin-storage-reviews). Backup and recovery tutorials: Plan B Academy (planb.academy). The standard itself and its word list: BIP39, in the Bitcoin improvement proposals (github.com/bitcoin/bips). Which wallet can restore which seed: Wallets Recovery (walletsrecovery.org). Next chapter: Lightning and euros in everyday life.","href":"/book/en/seed/#go-further"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Sources","kicker":"Ch. 14 \u00b7 References","text":"Understanding Trezor backups, 12, 20 or 24 words, Trezor, checked 22 October 2026. Seed version system, Electrum documentation, checked 22 October 2026. How to use a wallet backup, Trezor, checked 22 October 2026. Move crypto to a wallet with a new backup, Trezor, checked 22 October 2026. Multisig backup warning and descriptor export, Sparrow source code, checked 22 October 2026. Feds Link $150M Cyberheist to 2022 LastPass Hacks, Krebs on Security, 6 March 2025. Message from Ledger's CEO on the data leak, Ledger, 21 December 2020. BIP39: Mnemonic code for generating deterministic keys, Bitcoin Improvement Proposals, 2013. SLIP39: Shamir's Secret-Sharing for Mnemonic Codes, SatoshiLabs, consulted in September 2026. How I checked over 1 trillion mnemonics in 30 hours to win a bitcoin, John Cantrell, republished by WORDS Bitcoin Journal, 18 June 2020. Phoenix FAQ: restoring a wallet, limitations, ACINQ, consulted in September 2026.","href":"/book/en/seed/#sources"},{"type":"action","part":3,"title":"Write your words by hand and check them twice","kicker":"Ch. 14 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/seed/#action-seed-write-by-hand"},{"type":"action","part":3,"title":"Remove digital seed copies; if exposure is possible, move funds safely to a fresh wallet, since deletion does not undo a leak","kicker":"Ch. 14 \u00b7 Checklist action, 1 hour","text":"","href":"/book/en/seed/#action-seed-delete-digital-copies"},{"type":"action","part":3,"title":"Make a second copy and store it in a second place","kicker":"Ch. 14 \u00b7 Checklist action, 1 hour","text":"","href":"/book/en/seed/#action-seed-second-copy"},{"type":"action","part":3,"title":"Test a tiny software wallet on a second device without deleting the original; check hardware backups only with the maker's hardware procedure","kicker":"Ch. 14 \u00b7 Checklist action, 1 hour","text":"","href":"/book/en/seed/#action-seed-test-recovery"},{"type":"action","part":3,"title":"Write the sealed letter for your family and make a steel copy","kicker":"Ch. 14 \u00b7 Checklist action, weekend","text":"","href":"/book/en/seed/#action-seed-letter-steel-copy"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Your checklist","kicker":"Ch. 14 \u00b7 Act","text":"Write your words by hand and check them twiceRemove digital seed copies; if exposure is possible, move funds safely to a fresh wallet, since deletion does not undo a leakMake a second copy and store it in a second placeTest a tiny software wallet on a second device without deleting the original; check hardware backups only with the maker's hardware procedureWrite the sealed letter for your family and make a steel copy","href":"/book/en/seed/#your-checklist"},{"type":"chapter","part":3,"title":"Lightning and euros in everyday life","kicker":"Chapter 15 \u00b7 Money and Bitcoin","text":"In brief \u00b7 A ledger too slow for a coffee \u00b7 Channels that touch the blockchain twice \u00b7 Where to spend sats \u00b7 Who holds the funds, who can block \u00b7 Choose your bridge to euros \u00b7 Step by step \u00b7 Mistakes to avoid \u00b7 Go further \u00b7 Sources \u00b7 Your checklist","href":"/book/en/lightning-and-euros/"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"In brief","kicker":"Ch. 15 \u00b7 In brief","text":"Bitcoin's base layer is built to be secure, not to sell coffee. Lightning is a payment network on top of it, in practice instant and very cheap, made for everyday amounts. This chapter shows how to pay and get paid over Lightning, where to spend sats, and the several routes between bitcoin and euros, each with its cost in fees, convenience and privacy. Nothing here is investment advice, and the tax rules of your country still apply.","href":"/book/en/lightning-and-euros/#in-brief"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"A ledger too slow for a coffee","kicker":"Ch. 15 \u00b7 Understand","text":"The Bitcoin blockchain adds a new block about every 10 minutes, and each block has limited space. The limit is deliberate, because it keeps the ledger small enough for ordinary people to verify on their own computer (chapter 18). The price of that choice is that an on-chain payment usually takes minutes to hours to gain confirmations, with no absolute finality, and that its fee rises when many people want space at the same time, which is acceptable for moving savings and unreasonable for a coffee. The second difficulty is the bridge. Salaries, rent and taxes are in euros, so using bitcoin at all requires a way in and a way out. The best-known one is a large exchange that holds your coins, your identity documents and the full history of what you do. There are other routes, described further down, and they differ a lot from each other in fees, convenience and privacy.","href":"/book/en/lightning-and-euros/#a-ledger-too-slow-for-a-coffee"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Channels that touch the blockchain twice","kicker":"Ch. 15 \u00b7 Understand","text":"Lightning rests on payment channels. 2 participants lock some bitcoin together in an on-chain transaction, and from then on they can move the balance between them as often as they like, in practice instantly, by exchanging signed updates that never touch the blockchain. Only the opening and the closing of the channel are written on-chain. Channels connect into a network, so you can pay someone you share no channel with, and the payment hops through intermediaries who are not able to keep it and who usually earn a fraction of a cent. In practice you see 2 things: An invoice, a payment request shown as a QR code or long text. Common BOLT11 invoices start with \"lnbc\"; newer BOLT12 requests use a different format. It is usually for a fixed amount, used once, and it expires. A Lightning address, which looks like an email address (you@provider.com) and can be reused, which is convenient for tips and for getting paid. Most of them rely on a provider's web server. Phoenix's address relies on BOLT12, a newer standard that, for the moment, only some wallets can pay. Modern wallets either manage the channels for you or avoid them by swapping through an outside service (chapter 13).","href":"/book/en/lightning-and-euros/#channels-that-touch-the-blockchain-twice"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"Bitrefill","kicker":"Ch. 15 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cWhere to spend sats\u201d","text":"What you can buy: Gift cards, phone refills, eSIMs \u00b7 Lightning?: Yes \u00b7 Identity check: No account required for ordinary purchases. Verification raises its purchase limits.","href":"/book/en/lightning-and-euros/#row-bitrefill"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"CoinsBee (Coinsbee GmbH)","kicker":"Ch. 15 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cWhere to spend sats\u201d","text":"What you can buy: Gift cards, payment cards, phone top-ups \u00b7 Lightning?: Yes \u00b7 Identity check: None up to 1,000 euros per order and 10,000 euros in total, verification beyond.","href":"/book/en/lightning-and-euros/#row-coinsbee-coinsbee-gmbh"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"Cryptorefills (Big Dream Ventures B.V., Amsterdam)","kicker":"Ch. 15 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cWhere to spend sats\u201d","text":"What you can buy: Gift cards, top-ups, eSIMs, flights, stays \u00b7 Lightning?: Yes \u00b7 Identity check: None, according to its site","href":"/book/en/lightning-and-euros/#row-cryptorefills-big-dream-ventures-b-v-amsterdam"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"CoinGate gift cards (sold by UAB Rewards distributed, a Lithuanian company)","kicker":"Ch. 15 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cWhere to spend sats\u201d","text":"What you can buy: Gift cards from about 6,000 brands \u00b7 Lightning?: Yes \u00b7 Identity check: No sign-up needed, according to its site","href":"/book/en/lightning-and-euros/#row-coingate-gift-cards-sold-by-uab-rewards-distributed-a-lithuanian-company"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"Travala","kicker":"Ch. 15 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cWhere to spend sats\u201d","text":"What you can buy: Hotels, flights, activities \u00b7 Lightning?: Yes \u00b7 Identity check: Traveller details for the booking, and the airline or hotel checks your ID as usual","href":"/book/en/lightning-and-euros/#row-travala"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Where to spend sats","kicker":"Ch. 15 \u00b7 Compare","text":"In person. BTC Map (btcmap.org) is the tool to find places, a free, community-run map of the caf\u00e9s, shops, hotels and freelancers that accept bitcoin. Vilnius, where the conference takes place, has several entries as of September 2026, for example the caf\u00e9 Baltic Brew (L. Asanavi\u010di\u016bt\u0117s g. 29), listed for Lightning and on-chain payments, and Zolak, a micro art residence with a bar in a former electronics factory (Vytenio g. 50D), which announces Lightning payments on its own site. A listing can be out of date, so look at its last verification date, ask before you order, and add the places you discover. Tips and zaps. On Nostr (chapter 7), you can send a few sats to the author of a post you liked, directly, with no platform taking a share. Many podcasts and independent writers publish a Lightning address too. People. Splitting a bill or repaying a friend is often the first real use. Online. Several companies sell gift cards, phone top-ups, eSIMs or travel against bitcoin, an indirect way to live on sats where merchants do not accept them yet. Each one in the table below (valid as of September 2026) is a shop, not a wallet, so pay for what you need and leave no balance there. Service What you can buy Lightning? Identity check Bitrefill Gift cards, phone refills, eSIMs Yes No account required for ordinary purchases. Verification raises its purchase limits. CoinsBee (Coinsbee GmbH) Gift cards, payment cards, phone top-ups Yes None up to 1,000 euros per order and 10,000 euros in total, verification beyond. Cryptorefills (Big Dream Ventures B.V., Amsterdam) Gift cards, top-ups, eSIMs, flights, stays Yes None, according to its site CoinGate gift cards (sold by UAB Rewards distributed, a Lithuanian company) Gift cards from about 6,000 brands Yes No sign-up needed, according to its site Travala Hotels, flights, activities Yes Traveller details for the booking, and the airline or hotel checks your ID as usual","href":"/book/en/lightning-and-euros/#where-to-spend-sats"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Who holds the funds, who can block","kicker":"Ch. 15 \u00b7 Understand","text":"For every route, the grid asks who holds the funds while the payment is in progress, and who can stop it. With a custodial app, the company holds the bitcoin until you withdraw, and it can freeze the balance or refuse a payment. With a self-custodial Lightning wallet the keys are yours, but payments pass through a Lightning service provider (ACINQ for Phoenix, Blocktank for Bitkit) or, in a different design, a small group of operators (Spark). These services can refuse transfers. Lightning security requires correct channel handling and backups; Spark additionally relies on its operator honesty threshold during transfers. An exit to the blockchain has its own conditions, fees and delays; holding keys alone is not a guarantee against every failure. A broker under KYC holds your euros during the purchase, and it can refuse an order, ask where the money comes from or close your account, as can the bank sending the transfer. Peer-to-peer platforms try to remove the holder. Bisq locks the coins in a 2-of-2 multi-signature address shared by the 2 traders, and Hodl Hodl in a 2-of-3 address where the platform has 1 key, so no single party can move them. RoboSats uses a Lightning payment held towards the coordinator hosting the order. The trust that remains goes to the other trader and to whoever settles a dispute.","href":"/book/en/lightning-and-euros/#who-holds-the-funds-who-can-block"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"Earn sats","kicker":"Ch. 15 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your bridge to euros\u201d","text":"Examples: Be paid or tipped in bitcoin for work, sales or content \u00b7 Identity check (KYC): None \u00b7 Who holds the coins: You \u00b7 Who can block it: Only a Lightning provider on either side, by declining to relay the payment \u00b7 Honest trade-off: No intermediary at all. You need someone willing to pay this way, and income remains taxable.","href":"/book/en/lightning-and-euros/#row-earn-sats"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"Peer-to-peer exchange","kicker":"Ch. 15 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your bridge to euros\u201d","text":"Examples: Hodl Hodl (web), Bisq (desktop), RoboSats (Lightning, over Tor) \u00b7 Identity check (KYC): Generally none by the platform. Hodl Hodl may ask in a dispute. \u00b7 Who holds the coins: You. Coins are locked in escrow only during the trade, where the platform offers one. \u00b7 Who can block it: The other trader, by not paying, and the banks on both sides. A mediator or arbitrator settles disputes. \u00b7 Honest trade-off: You trade with a person by bank transfer. More private, usually more expensive, slower, more to learn.","href":"/book/en/lightning-and-euros/#row-peer-to-peer-exchange"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"European broker, recurring purchases","kicker":"Ch. 15 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your bridge to euros\u201d","text":"Examples: Relai, Pocket Bitcoin (both Swiss) \u00b7 Identity check (KYC): Yes \u00b7 Who holds the coins: You. They deliver to your own wallet. \u00b7 Who can block it: The broker, which can refuse an order or close your account, and your bank, which can block the transfer \u00b7 Honest trade-off: Very simple, automatic. A fee on each purchase, and the company knows who you are and what you bought.","href":"/book/en/lightning-and-euros/#row-european-broker-recurring-purchases"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"Euro account plus bitcoin wallet","kicker":"Ch. 15 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your bridge to euros\u201d","text":"Examples: Bringin (euro IBAN and optional Visa card next to a self-custodial wallet), Strike (SEPA deposits, fully custodial) \u00b7 Identity check (KYC): Yes \u00b7 Who holds the coins: Bringin: you, for the bitcoin. Strike: the company, for everything, until you withdraw to your own wallet. \u00b7 Who can block it: Bringin for the euro account and the card, which it can block like any regulated payment company, but not the bitcoin, which sits in a self-custodial wallet. Strike is custodial, so the company holds the keys to both your euros and your bitcoin and has every right over them: it can freeze the balance, refuse a payment or close the account. \u00b7 Honest trade-off: Convenient for moving between sats and euros. A single company sees both sides, each conversion carries a fee, and the euro side is a regulated account like any other.","href":"/book/en/lightning-and-euros/#row-euro-account-plus-bitcoin-wallet"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"Bitcoin ATM","kicker":"Ch. 15 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your bridge to euros\u201d","text":"Examples: Machines listed on coinatmradar.com \u00b7 Identity check (KYC): Usually yes in the EU, depending on operator and amount \u00b7 Who holds the coins: You \u00b7 Who can block it: The operator, which sets limits and can refuse a transaction \u00b7 Honest trade-off: Cash in, coins out in minutes. Fees are typically the highest of all routes.","href":"/book/en/lightning-and-euros/#row-bitcoin-atm"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Choose your bridge to euros","kicker":"Ch. 15 \u00b7 Compare","text":"No route is best on every count, because each one trades simplicity against fees and privacy. KYC (\"know your customer\") means the service verifies your identity with an ID document, which regulated companies in Europe now have to do, and chapter 16 explains what that implies for your privacy. This market moves fast. The Swiss broker Pocket Bitcoin, for example, ended its lighter verification in December 2025. On the peer-to-peer side, Hodl Hodl, Bisq and RoboSats let you trade with another person without handing your coins to a company, under the escrow designs described in the previous section. The table is valid as of September 2026, and you should check in each case that the service accepts residents of your country. Route Examples Identity check (KYC) Who holds the coins Who can block it Honest trade-off Earn sats Be paid or tipped in bitcoin for work, sales or content None You Only a Lightning provider on either side, by declining to relay the payment No intermediary at all. You need someone willing to pay this way, and income remains taxable. Peer-to-peer exchange Hodl Hodl (web), Bisq (desktop), RoboSats (Lightning, over Tor) Generally none by the platform. Hodl Hodl may ask in a dispute. You. Coins are locked in escrow only during the trade, where the platform offers one. The other trader, by not paying, and the banks on both sides. A mediator or arbitrator settles disputes. You trade with a person by bank transfer. More private, usually more expensive, slower, more to learn. European broker, recurring purchases Relai, Pocket Bitcoin (both Swiss) Yes You. They deliver to your own wallet. The broker, which can refuse an order or close your account, and your bank, which can block the transfer Very simple, automatic. A fee on each purchase, and the company knows who you are and what you bought. Euro account plus bitcoin wallet Bringin (euro IBAN and optional Visa card next to a self-custodial wallet), Strike (SEPA deposits, fully custodial) Yes Bringin: you, for the bitcoin. Strike: the company, for everything, until you withdraw to your own wallet. Bringin for the euro account and the card, which it can block like any regulated payment company, but not the bitcoin, which sits in a self-custodial wallet. Strike is custodial, so the company holds the keys to both your euros and your bitcoin and has every right over them: it can freeze the balance, refuse a payment or close the account. Convenient for moving between sats and euros. A single company sees both sides, each conversion carries a fee, and the euro side is a regulated account like any other. Bitcoin ATM Machines listed on coinatmradar.com Usually yes in the EU, depending on operator and amount You The operator, which sets limits and can refuse a transaction Cash in, coins out in minutes. Fees are typically the highest of all routes. A reasonable setup for most people who decide to exchange euros is a regulated route they find clear, used only to deliver coins to their own wallet, and a peer-to-peer route learned with small amounts, so that they do not depend on a single door. Whatever the route, selling or spending bitcoin can create a tax obligation, so keep records and check the rules of your country.","href":"/book/en/lightning-and-euros/#choose-your-bridge-to-euros"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 1: Pay an invoice","kicker":"Ch. 15 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Pay an invoice. At the conference or at a merchant, tap Send and scan the QR code. Check the amount and the fee shown, then confirm. The merchant usually sees the payment within 1 or 2 seconds.","href":"/book/en/lightning-and-euros/#step-1"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 2: Receive with an invoice","kicker":"Ch. 15 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Receive with an invoice. Tap Receive, enter an amount if you wish, and show the QR code. Remember that it expires, and that a first payment into Phoenix has to exceed the channel fee (chapter 13).","href":"/book/en/lightning-and-euros/#step-2"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 3: Receive with a Lightning address","kicker":"Ch. 15 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Receive with a Lightning address. If your wallet offers one, find it in the settings or on the receive screen, and test it from a second wallet before you publish it. Phoenix's address uses BOLT12, which many wallets cannot pay yet. An address from a custodial or Spark-based wallet works with almost every sender, with the trade-offs described in chapter 13.","href":"/book/en/lightning-and-euros/#step-3"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 4: Withdraw from a broker or app to your OWN wallet","kicker":"Ch. 15 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Withdraw from a broker or app to your OWN wallet. Choose \"withdraw\" or \"send\", paste an address or an invoice generated by your wallet, and start with a small test. Seeing the coins arrive in a wallet whose keys you hold is the step that replaces trust in a displayed balance with verification. For larger amounts, withdraw on-chain to your savings wallet (chapters 14 and 17).","href":"/book/en/lightning-and-euros/#step-4"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 5: Keep only spending money on the phone","kicker":"Ch. 15 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Keep only spending money on the phone. Think of the Lightning wallet as the wallet in your pocket. When it holds more than you would carry in cash, move the surplus on-chain.","href":"/book/en/lightning-and-euros/#step-5"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 6: Keep records","kicker":"Ch. 15 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Keep records. Export or note your purchases and sales with dates and amounts, because you will need them for the tax return.","href":"/book/en/lightning-and-euros/#step-6"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Step by step","kicker":"Ch. 15 \u00b7 Tutorial","text":"The example uses Phoenix. The logic is the same in any Lightning wallet. Pay an invoice. At the conference or at a merchant, tap Send and scan the QR code. Check the amount and the fee shown, then confirm. The merchant usually sees the payment within 1 or 2 seconds. Receive with an invoice. Tap Receive, enter an amount if you wish, and show the QR code. Remember that it expires, and that a first payment into Phoenix has to exceed the channel fee (chapter 13). Receive with a Lightning address. If your wallet offers one, find it in the settings or on the receive screen, and test it from a second wallet before you publish it. Phoenix's address uses BOLT12, which many wallets cannot pay yet. An address from a custodial or Spark-based wallet works with almost every sender, with the trade-offs described in chapter 13. Withdraw from a broker or app to your OWN wallet. Choose \"withdraw\" or \"send\", paste an address or an invoice generated by your wallet, and start with a small test. Seeing the coins arrive in a wallet whose keys you hold is the step that replaces trust in a displayed balance with verification. For larger amounts, withdraw on-chain to your savings wallet (chapters 14 and 17). Keep only spending money on the phone. Think of the Lightning wallet as the wallet in your pocket. When it holds more than you would carry in cash, move the surplus on-chain. Keep records. Export or note your purchases and sales with dates and amounts, because you will need them for the tax return.","href":"/book/en/lightning-and-euros/#step-by-step"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Mistakes to avoid","kicker":"Ch. 15 \u00b7 Watch out","text":"Keeping savings in a Lightning phone wallet. It lives on a device you can lose or break, connected to the internet all day, so it suits spending money. Confusing an invoice with an on-chain address. A BOLT11 invoice starts with \"lnbc\" and expires; BOLT12 uses other formats. An on-chain address starts with \"bc1\", \"1\" or \"3\" and does not. When you withdraw from a service, select the matching network. Making large payments over Lightning. Channels have limited capacity, and large payments fail more often. Lightning is for everyday amounts, and on-chain handles the rest. Leaving coins with the broker. A purchase is finished when the coins are in your wallet, not when the app shows a balance (chapter 13). Ignoring the total cost. Compare the final amount of sats you receive, not the advertised fee, because the exchange rate often hides a margin. Forgetting the tax side. Paying with bitcoin can count as selling it, and the rules differ by country.","href":"/book/en/lightning-and-euros/#mistakes-to-avoid"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Go further","kicker":"Ch. 15 \u00b7 Go further","text":"Find places to spend: BTC Map (btcmap.org). Tutorials for the wallets and exchanges above: Plan B Academy (planb.academy). Understand Lightning in depth: \"Mastering the Lightning Network\", free to read online (github.com/lnbook/lnbook). Next chapter: privacy in Bitcoin.","href":"/book/en/lightning-and-euros/#go-further"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Sources","kicker":"Ch. 15 \u00b7 References","text":"Spark trust model, Spark documentation, checked 22 October 2026. Important Update: Full Verification Coming to Pocket, Pocket Bitcoin, 16 October 2025. Security deposit, Bisq Wiki, consulted in September 2026. What is multisig escrow and how it works, Hodl Hodl, consulted in September 2026. Trade escrow, Learn RoboSats, consulted in September 2026. Bringin: wallet, euro account and fees, Bringin, consulted in September 2026. Baltic Brew and Zolak Micro Art Residence, BTC Map listings for Vilnius, consulted on 20 September 2026. KYC and AML: purchase limits, CoinsBee, consulted in September 2026.","href":"/book/en/lightning-and-euros/#sources"},{"type":"action","part":3,"title":"Pay a Lightning invoice, at the conference or at a merchant","kicker":"Ch. 15 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/lightning-and-euros/#action-lightning-and-euros-pay-invoice"},{"type":"action","part":3,"title":"Get a Lightning address and test it from a second wallet","kicker":"Ch. 15 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/lightning-and-euros/#action-lightning-and-euros-lightning-address"},{"type":"action","part":3,"title":"Find 3 places near you on btcmap.org","kicker":"Ch. 15 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/lightning-and-euros/#action-lightning-and-euros-btcmap"},{"type":"action","part":3,"title":"Withdraw any balance from a broker or exchange to your own wallet","kicker":"Ch. 15 \u00b7 Checklist action, 1 hour","text":"","href":"/book/en/lightning-and-euros/#action-lightning-and-euros-withdraw"},{"type":"action","part":3,"title":"Read how a peer-to-peer exchange works, before you need it","kicker":"Ch. 15 \u00b7 Checklist action, 1 hour","text":"","href":"/book/en/lightning-and-euros/#action-lightning-and-euros-peer-to-peer"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Your checklist","kicker":"Ch. 15 \u00b7 Act","text":"Pay a Lightning invoice, at the conference or at a merchantGet a Lightning address and test it from a second walletFind 3 places near you on btcmap.orgWithdraw any balance from a broker or exchange to your own walletRead how a peer-to-peer exchange works, before you need it","href":"/book/en/lightning-and-euros/#your-checklist"},{"type":"chapter","part":3,"title":"Privacy in Bitcoin","kicker":"Chapter 16 \u00b7 Money and Bitcoin","text":"In brief \u00b7 A public ledger that never forgets \u00b7 Large movements show, individuals have tools \u00b7 3 patterns that give you away \u00b7 KYC and what it implies \u00b7 Who learns which coins are yours \u00b7 Choose your privacy habits \u00b7 Coinjoin, payjoin and mixers \u00b7 Legal to use, prosecuted to operate \u00b7 Monero for spending, not for saving \u00b7 Step by step \u00b7 Mistakes to avoid \u00b7 Go further \u00b7 Sources \u00b7 Your checklist","href":"/book/en/bitcoin-privacy/"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"In brief","kicker":"Ch. 16 \u00b7 In brief","text":"Bitcoin does not hide its transaction graph at the base layer. Transactions remain public; identifying their owners depends on outside information and heuristics. An address, however, carries no name. It becomes tied to you through the identity check of an exchange, or through the people you transact with who know you, and from there an observer can link a wallet to a person. A few habits reduce this exposure considerably (fresh addresses, separate wallets, labelled coins, your own node, Lightning for everyday spending), and advanced tools such as coinjoin and payjoin go further. None of them changes your legal or tax obligations.","href":"/book/en/bitcoin-privacy/#in-brief"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"A public ledger that never forgets","kicker":"Ch. 16 \u00b7 Understand","text":"Your bank statement is seen by you and your bank, whereas the Bitcoin ledger can be read by anyone, and nothing is ever erased from it. Those who say that Bitcoin lacks privacy are right about this base layer, because the transaction graph remains available for analysis, even when its owners cannot be identified reliably. Those who answer that an address is not an identity are right too, up to a point. An address carries no name, and it only gets one through a link made elsewhere. An exchange that verified your identity knows where you withdrew your coins. The people you transact with know you, such as an online shop that has your name and sees the address you paid from. Analytics companies make a business of collecting these links, grouping addresses by owner and selling the result, which lets an observer link a wallet to a physical person. From that moment, past and future activity connected to that address can be followed by a merchant, an employer, a landlord or a curious acquaintance. The stake is also physical. In January 2025, David Balland, a co-founder of Ledger, was kidnapped at his home in France and the attackers demanded a ransom in cryptocurrency before the police freed him and his partner. The developer Jameson Lopp maintains a public list of physical attacks on bitcoin holders.","href":"/book/en/bitcoin-privacy/#a-public-ledger-that-never-forgets"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Large movements show, individuals have tools","kicker":"Ch. 16 \u00b7 Understand","text":"With fiat money, the largest actors can place large transactions behind financial structures, such as holding companies, trusts and accounts in several jurisdictions, while the population's everyday use of banking services is closely monitored, payment by payment. With Bitcoin it tends to be the reverse. Privacy can be harder for very large amounts, because a coinjoin needs other participants and a useful set of plausible outputs, so when a state or a large institution moves bitcoin, it usually shows on the public ledger. In the summer of 2024, the German state of Saxony sold about 49,858 seized bitcoin between 19 June and 12 July, and the press followed the transfers from the labelled wallet to exchanges day by day, using the data of the analytics firm Arkham. An individual, on the other hand, has tools to gain privacy and to resist deanonymisation, which the rest of this chapter presents. It takes care and some learning, but someone who applies them properly can, in practice, use Bitcoin privately.","href":"/book/en/bitcoin-privacy/#large-movements-show-individuals-have-tools"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"3 patterns that give you away","kicker":"Ch. 16 \u00b7 Understand","text":"Your bitcoin is better pictured as separate coins of different sizes, like banknotes in a purse, than as an account balance. Each coin you received sits on the public ledger with its amount and its address. 3 simple patterns reveal a lot: Address reuse. If you receive 10 payments on the same address, anyone can see all 10, add them up, and watch when they move. Merged inputs. When you pay, your wallet may combine several coins into 1 transaction, like paying with 2 banknotes. An observer concludes that both coins belong to the same person, so 1 coin with your name on it now identifies the other. Change. If you pay 30 with a coin of 50, the remaining 20 comes back to a new address of yours. Observers can often guess which output is the change, and keep following you. Analysts combine these guesses, which are called heuristics. They are not always right, but they are good enough to build a profile, and good privacy habits give them less to work with.","href":"/book/en/bitcoin-privacy/#3-patterns-that-give-you-away"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"KYC and what it implies","kicker":"Ch. 16 \u00b7 Understand","text":"KYC means \"know your customer\", the identity verification that regulated exchanges must perform. You send a copy of your ID, often with a selfie and a proof of address. This is a legal duty for the company, and using such a service is perfectly normal. It does create a lasting link between your identity and your coins, stored in a company database. In the European Union, the MiCA regulation has applied to crypto-asset service providers since 30 December 2024, with a transition period that ended on 1 July 2026. Since 1 January 2026, the DAC8 directive also requires these providers to collect data on the transactions of their EU-resident users and to report it to the tax authorities, which exchange it between member states from 2027. Databases of this kind can leak, and a list of people who hold bitcoin, with home addresses, is of obvious interest to criminals. Bitcoin can also be acquired on peer-to-peer markets, where you trade directly with another person and no central company collects your documents. This is legal in most places, but rules differ, so check yours. Your tax obligations remain exactly the same however you acquired your coins, so keep your own records.","href":"/book/en/bitcoin-privacy/#kyc-and-what-it-implies"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Who learns which coins are yours","kicker":"Ch. 16 \u00b7 Understand","text":"The ledger shows addresses without names, so your confidentiality depends on the parties able to attach your name to them. The first is the server your wallet queries. A wallet that does not hold the ledger asks a server about each of its addresses, and by default that server belongs to the wallet's maker or to a volunteer. According to the Sparrow documentation, a public server learns your balance and, through your IP address, your location. It may log nothing, but you cannot check. The second is the exchange that verified your identity, which knows your name, your home address and where you withdrew, and can be breached or ordered to hand over its records. In May 2025, Coinbase disclosed that customer data had been copied from its support systems, and its filing counted 69,461 people. Analytics firms are observers, working from the public ledger and the data they obtain. Neither the server nor the exchange can be audited from outside, so the practical route is to remove them where it matters, with your own node, Tor in the meantime and peer-to-peer acquisition, each at the price of convenience. For a beginner, a regulated exchange often remains the safer choice.","href":"/book/en/bitcoin-privacy/#who-learns-which-coins-are-yours"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"Never reuse an address","kicker":"Ch. 16 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your privacy habits\u201d","text":"Tools: Any modern wallet gives a fresh address for every payment \u00b7 Effort: None \u00b7 What you gain: Avoids the direct link created by receiving repeatedly on 1 address; later spending can still link payments \u00b7 Who still learns something: The server your wallet queries still sees all these addresses together","href":"/book/en/bitcoin-privacy/#row-never-reuse-an-address"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"Separate wallets for separate purposes","kicker":"Ch. 16 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your privacy habits\u201d","text":"Tools: Any wallet: savings, spending, KYC coins, non-KYC coins \u00b7 Effort: Low \u00b7 What you gain: Reduces accidental links between purposes; shared servers and later transactions can still connect them \u00b7 Who still learns something: The same server, if both wallets query it from the same IP address","href":"/book/en/bitcoin-privacy/#row-separate-wallets-for-separate-purposes"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"Label your coins and use coin control","kicker":"Ch. 16 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your privacy habits\u201d","text":"Tools: Sparrow Wallet, Nunchuk, Electrum (all open source) \u00b7 Effort: Medium \u00b7 What you gain: You decide which coin pays, and you avoid merging coins by accident \u00b7 Who still learns something: Nobody new, since labels stay in your wallet file. Back that file up","href":"/book/en/bitcoin-privacy/#row-label-your-coins-and-use-coin-control"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"Connect to your own node","kicker":"Ch. 16 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your privacy habits\u201d","text":"Tools: See chapter 18 \u00b7 Effort: High (a weekend of work, then a few days of synchronisation) \u00b7 What you gain: No outside server learns which addresses are yours \u00b7 Who still learns something: Nobody for your balance. Other nodes see the transaction you broadcast, unless your node uses Tor","href":"/book/en/bitcoin-privacy/#row-connect-to-your-own-node"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"Use Lightning for everyday spending","kicker":"Ch. 16 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your privacy habits\u201d","text":"Tools: See chapter 15 \u00b7 Effort: Low \u00b7 What you gain: Individual payments are not written on the public ledger \u00b7 Who still learns something: A custodial wallet sees every payment. A self-custodial app's service provider (LSP) still sees part of them","href":"/book/en/bitcoin-privacy/#row-use-lightning-for-everyday-spending"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"Use Tor in your wallet","kicker":"Ch. 16 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your privacy habits\u201d","text":"Tools: Sparrow includes Tor. Electrum and several others have a proxy setting, which needs Tor installed separately \u00b7 Effort: Low to medium \u00b7 What you gain: The server you connect to does not see your IP address \u00b7 Who still learns something: The server still sees your addresses. Tor relays are run by volunteers and carry encrypted traffic","href":"/book/en/bitcoin-privacy/#row-use-tor-in-your-wallet"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"Peer-to-peer acquisition","kicker":"Ch. 16 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your privacy habits\u201d","text":"Tools: Bisq (desktop, open source, no company in the middle), RoboSats (open source, over Tor and Lightning), Hodl Hodl (web platform run by a company, multi-signature escrow) \u00b7 Effort: Medium \u00b7 What you gain: No identity documents stored in an exchange database \u00b7 Who still learns something: The other trader, who sees your name on a bank transfer. The platform sees the trade, not your ID","href":"/book/en/bitcoin-privacy/#row-peer-to-peer-acquisition"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"Coinjoin and payjoin (advanced, see below)","kicker":"Ch. 16 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your privacy habits\u201d","text":"Tools: Coinjoin: Wasabi Wallet with an independent coordinator, JoinMarket (now joinmarket-ng, with the Jam interface), Ashigaru. Payjoin: BTCPay Server, Bull Bitcoin, Cake Wallet \u00b7 Effort: High for coinjoin (fees, time, discipline afterwards). Low for payjoin, when both sides support it \u00b7 What you gain: Weakens common-ownership or change heuristics, depending on the protocol and how you spend afterwards \u00b7 Who still learns something: A coordinator sees that you take part, by design not which output is yours. Some exchanges question coinjoined coins","href":"/book/en/bitcoin-privacy/#row-coinjoin-and-payjoin-advanced-see-below"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"Swap a small amount into Monero to spend it (advanced, see below)","kicker":"Ch. 16 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your privacy habits\u201d","text":"Tools: Atomic swap software (eigenwallet, open source, non-custodial) or peer-to-peer markets that list Monero (Bisq) \u00b7 Effort: Medium to high \u00b7 What you gain: A payment much less likely to be linked to your identity, since Monero hides amounts, senders and receivers by default \u00b7 Who still learns something: The swap counterparty sees the bitcoin side. A tool for spending, not for savings","href":"/book/en/bitcoin-privacy/#row-swap-a-small-amount-into-monero-to-spend-it-advanced-see-below"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Choose your privacy habits","kicker":"Ch. 16 \u00b7 Compare","text":"Privacy in Bitcoin comes from a set of habits rather than from 1 tool. Each helps on its own, so start at the top of the table. Habit Tools Effort What you gain Who still learns something Never reuse an address Any modern wallet gives a fresh address for every payment None Avoids the direct link created by receiving repeatedly on 1 address; later spending can still link payments The server your wallet queries still sees all these addresses together Separate wallets for separate purposes Any wallet: savings, spending, KYC coins, non-KYC coins Low Reduces accidental links between purposes; shared servers and later transactions can still connect them The same server, if both wallets query it from the same IP address Label your coins and use coin control Sparrow Wallet, Nunchuk, Electrum (all open source) Medium You decide which coin pays, and you avoid merging coins by accident Nobody new, since labels stay in your wallet file. Back that file up Connect to your own node See chapter 18 High (a weekend of work, then a few days of synchronisation) No outside server learns which addresses are yours Nobody for your balance. Other nodes see the transaction you broadcast, unless your node uses Tor Use Lightning for everyday spending See chapter 15 Low Individual payments are not written on the public ledger A custodial wallet sees every payment. A self-custodial app's service provider (LSP) still sees part of them Use Tor in your wallet Sparrow includes Tor. Electrum and several others have a proxy setting, which needs Tor installed separately Low to medium The server you connect to does not see your IP address The server still sees your addresses. Tor relays are run by volunteers and carry encrypted traffic Peer-to-peer acquisition Bisq (desktop, open source, no company in the middle), RoboSats (open source, over Tor and Lightning), Hodl Hodl (web platform run by a company, multi-signature escrow) Medium No identity documents stored in an exchange database The other trader, who sees your name on a bank transfer. The platform sees the trade, not your ID Coinjoin and payjoin (advanced, see below) Coinjoin: Wasabi Wallet with an independent coordinator, JoinMarket (now joinmarket-ng, with the Jam interface), Ashigaru. Payjoin: BTCPay Server, Bull Bitcoin, Cake Wallet High for coinjoin (fees, time, discipline afterwards). Low for payjoin, when both sides support it Weakens common-ownership or change heuristics, depending on the protocol and how you spend afterwards A coordinator sees that you take part, by design not which output is yours. Some exchanges question coinjoined coins Swap a small amount into Monero to spend it (advanced, see below) Atomic swap software (eigenwallet, open source, non-custodial) or peer-to-peer markets that list Monero (Bisq) Medium to high A payment much less likely to be linked to your identity, since Monero hides amounts, senders and receivers by default The swap counterparty sees the bitcoin side. A tool for spending, not for savings Lightning protects the payer better than the receiver, and a custodial Lightning wallet sees all your payments. Peer-to-peer platforms do not operate in every country, so check yours. A reasonable setup for most people is fresh addresses, 1 wallet per purpose, labels from the first day and Lightning for small payments, then your own node when you are ready. The last 2 rows are advanced, and the next 3 sections explain them.","href":"/book/en/bitcoin-privacy/#choose-your-privacy-habits"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Coinjoin, payjoin and mixers","kicker":"Ch. 16 \u00b7 Understand","text":"The 3 patterns rest on guesses, and some tools are built to make those guesses fail. In a coinjoin, several people build 1 transaction together, with many inputs and many outputs of the same amounts, so that ownership is harder to infer from that transaction alone. Amounts, timing and later spending can still provide clues. Nobody gives up custody, because each participant signs only if their own output is there. In a payjoin, the sender and the receiver both contribute inputs to a payment, which breaks the assumption that all the inputs of a transaction belong to 1 person, and hides the amount really paid. Mixers are the older approach, a service that takes your coins and sends back others. It is custodial, so the operator can keep the money or the logs. As of September 2026, coinjoin is usable in Wasabi Wallet, through independent coordinators since its maker closed its own in June 2024, in JoinMarket, a peer-to-peer market without a coordinator, continued as joinmarket-ng since the original repository was archived in April 2026, and in Ashigaru, a fork of Samourai Wallet that relaunched the Whirlpool coinjoin in June 2025. Payjoin works in BTCPay Server for merchants, and in the Bull Bitcoin and Cake Wallet apps since 2025.","href":"/book/en/bitcoin-privacy/#coinjoin-payjoin-and-mixers"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Legal to use, prosecuted to operate","kicker":"Ch. 16 \u00b7 Understand","text":"Using these tools to protect your financial privacy is legal in most places, but the law differs between countries and is moving, so check yours, and what you have to declare stays the same. The prosecutions so far have targeted operators. In the United States, the 2 founders of Samourai Wallet, arrested in April 2024, pleaded guilty to conspiring to operate an unlicensed money transmitting business that knowingly moved criminal proceeds, and were sentenced in November 2025 to 5 and 4 years in prison. Roman Storm, a developer of Tornado Cash, a mixer on Ethereum, was convicted of a similar offence on 6 August 2025. The jury reached no verdict on the money laundering and sanctions counts, which leaves part of the case open as of September 2026, although the US Treasury had lifted its sanctions on Tornado Cash on 21 March 2025. In the Netherlands, Alexey Pertsev, another Tornado Cash developer, was sentenced to 64 months in prison in May 2024 and has appealed. None of these developers is accused of having stolen anything. What they are blamed for is having written and run software that criminals also used, as well as people seeking ordinary financial privacy. These sources do not establish what share of users belonged to either group. For many developers and civil liberties groups, this is a serious attack on freedom of expression and on the freedom to build, because publishing code is a form of speech and a privacy tool does not choose its users, any more than encryption or cash does. Several of these cases are still before the courts as of September 2026. These cases explain why coordinators close or refuse users from some countries, and why some exchanges question coins that come out of a coinjoin. A beginner does not need to start here.","href":"/book/en/bitcoin-privacy/#legal-to-use-prosecuted-to-operate"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Monero for spending, not for saving","kicker":"Ch. 16 \u00b7 Understand","text":"Some advanced users keep bitcoin as the asset they hold for the long term, and swap a small amount into Monero when they want a payment that is less likely to be linked to their identity. Monero is a separate currency with a similar purpose, money you can hold yourself, where amounts, senders and receivers are hidden by default, which is its real strength. It is however less secure as a place to keep value, because it is much less liquid and much less accepted, its client software is developed by a smaller, more centralised group, and its much smaller network is easier for an outside entity to attack. In 2025 the Qubic mining pool claimed a majority of its mining power, and on 14 September a reorganisation of 18 blocks cancelled 118 confirmed transactions. Qubic is a small project run by a small group of people, which leaves open the question of what a government, or any better organised and better funded group, could do to the same network. Swaps are done with non-custodial atomic swap software such as eigenwallet, or on peer-to-peer markets such as Bisq, since regulated platforms in Europe are removing Monero. The EU anti-money-laundering regulation of 2024 bars them, from 10 July 2027, from keeping accounts that use \"anonymity-enhancing coins\". Instant swap websites also exist, but they hold your coins during the swap. Legal and tax obligations still apply, and in many countries a swap is a taxable event. Chapter 12 compares the 2.","href":"/book/en/bitcoin-privacy/#monero-for-spending-not-for-saving"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 1: Create 1 wallet per purpose","kicker":"Ch. 16 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Create 1 wallet per purpose. For example savings, spending, and a separate wallet for coins that came from an exchange with identity verification.","href":"/book/en/bitcoin-privacy/#step-1"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 2: Receive on a fresh address every time","kicker":"Ch. 16 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Receive on a fresh address every time. The Receive screen offers a new one automatically. Never copy an old address from your history.","href":"/book/en/bitcoin-privacy/#step-2"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 3: Label every payment when it arrives","kicker":"Ch. 16 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Label every payment when it arrives. Write where it came from, for example \"exchange, March\" or \"sold my bike to Tomas\". In 6 months you will not remember.","href":"/book/en/bitcoin-privacy/#step-3"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 4: Choose which coin pays","kicker":"Ch. 16 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Choose which coin pays. Before sending, open the list of your coins (Sparrow calls it UTXOs), select the one that fits, and send from it. Avoid combining coins from different sources.","href":"/book/en/bitcoin-privacy/#step-4"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 5: Label the change","kicker":"Ch. 16 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Label the change. The coin that comes back to you inherits the history of the payment, so note it.","href":"/book/en/bitcoin-privacy/#step-5"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 6: Pay small things over Lightning","kicker":"Ch. 16 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Pay small things over Lightning (chapter 15), and keep on-chain transactions for larger, rarer movements.","href":"/book/en/bitcoin-privacy/#step-6"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 7: Turn on Tor","kicker":"Ch. 16 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Turn on Tor in the wallet's server or network settings if you still use a public server. Later, replace the public server with your own node (chapter 18). Tor only hides your IP address from that server, whereas the node is the step that removes the third party, because the questions about your addresses no longer leave your home.","href":"/book/en/bitcoin-privacy/#step-7"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Step by step","kicker":"Ch. 16 \u00b7 Tutorial","text":"The example uses Sparrow Wallet on a computer. Nunchuk and Electrum offer the same functions under slightly different names. Create 1 wallet per purpose. For example savings, spending, and a separate wallet for coins that came from an exchange with identity verification. Receive on a fresh address every time. The Receive screen offers a new one automatically. Never copy an old address from your history. Label every payment when it arrives. Write where it came from, for example \"exchange, March\" or \"sold my bike to Tomas\". In 6 months you will not remember. Choose which coin pays. Before sending, open the list of your coins (Sparrow calls it UTXOs), select the one that fits, and send from it. Avoid combining coins from different sources. Label the change. The coin that comes back to you inherits the history of the payment, so note it. Pay small things over Lightning (chapter 15), and keep on-chain transactions for larger, rarer movements. Turn on Tor in the wallet's server or network settings if you still use a public server. Later, replace the public server with your own node (chapter 18). Tor only hides your IP address from that server, whereas the node is the step that removes the third party, because the questions about your addresses no longer leave your home.","href":"/book/en/bitcoin-privacy/#step-by-step"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Mistakes to avoid","kicker":"Ch. 16 \u00b7 Watch out","text":"Posting addresses or balances publicly. A screenshot of your wallet on social media links your name to your coins, and deleting the post later does not undo it, because copies circulate and the ledger keeps everything. Merging KYC and non-KYC coins. A transaction that combines them tells every observer they belong to the same person, so keep them in separate wallets. The aim is to avoid showing your whole history to a merchant, and it changes nothing in what you have to declare. Reusing a donation address. A single static address shows every donor what all the others gave, and what you did with it. Use a tool that generates a new address per donor, or Lightning. Believing a VPN makes Bitcoin private. A VPN hides your IP address from a server. It does nothing about the ledger, which is public for everyone. Talking about how much you own. Analysts work from the ledger, but a conversation gives away the same information faster. Nobody needs to know the number, online or at a dinner. Aiming for perfection and doing nothing. Labels and separate wallets can start today, whereas the node and the advanced techniques can wait.","href":"/book/en/bitcoin-privacy/#mistakes-to-avoid"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Go further","kicker":"Ch. 16 \u00b7 Go further","text":"The reference text: the \"Privacy\" page of the Bitcoin Wiki (en.bitcoin.it). Long, but complete. Coinjoin and payjoin explained: the Wasabi Wallet documentation (docs.wasabiwallet.io) and the Payjoin project (payjoin.org). Practical guides: Bitcoiner.Guide, by BitcoinQ+A (bitcoiner.guide), and the Sparrow Wallet documentation (sparrowwallet.com/docs) for labels and coin control. Next chapter: hardware wallets.","href":"/book/en/bitcoin-privacy/#go-further"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Sources","kicker":"Ch. 16 \u00b7 References","text":"Protect your privacy, Bitcoin.org, checked 22 October 2026. DAC8, the directive on the reporting of crypto-asset transactions, European Commission, applicable since 1 January 2026. Markets in Crypto-Assets Regulation (MiCA), European Securities and Markets Authority, page consulted in September 2026. Regulation (EU) 2024/1624 on the prevention of money laundering, Official Journal of the European Union, 19 June 2024. Monero Suffers Deepest-Ever Blockchain Reorganization, Invalidating 118 Transactions, CoinDesk, 15 September 2025. Notver\u00e4u\u00dferung von fast 50.000 Bitcoins abgeschlossen, Generalstaatsanwaltschaft Dresden, Free State of Saxony, 16 July 2024 (in German). German gov't $354M BTC sell-off, Cointelegraph, 10 July 2024. Coinjoin, Wasabi Wallet documentation, consulted in September 2026. CoinJoin in 2026: Wasabi, JoinMarket and Ashigaru compared, Atlas21, updated 10 June 2026. Announcement: new Zerolink coinjoin coordinator, Ashigaru, June 2025. Payjoin, Bitcoin Optech, consulted in September 2026. Founders of Samourai Wallet cryptocurrency mixing service sentenced to five and four years in prison, IRS Criminal Investigation, 19 November 2025. Founder of Tornado Cash crypto mixing service convicted of knowingly transmitting criminal proceeds, IRS Criminal Investigation, 6 August 2025. Tornado Cash Developer Alexey Pertsev Found Guilty of Money Laundering, CoinDesk, 14 May 2024. Tornado Cash delisting, US Department of the Treasury, 21 March 2025. Ledger Co-Founder's Kidnapping Highlights Threat of Crypto Robberies, CoinDesk, 24 January 2025. Known physical bitcoin attacks, Jameson Lopp, list updated continuously. Best practices, Sparrow Wallet documentation, consulted in September 2026. Coinbase breach hit almost 70k users, Cointelegraph, 21 May 2025.","href":"/book/en/bitcoin-privacy/#sources"},{"type":"action","part":3,"title":"Check that your wallet shows a fresh address for every payment","kicker":"Ch. 16 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/bitcoin-privacy/#action-bitcoin-privacy-fresh-address"},{"type":"action","part":3,"title":"Find any address or balance you have posted publicly, and stop receiving on those addresses","kicker":"Ch. 16 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/bitcoin-privacy/#action-bitcoin-privacy-public-addresses"},{"type":"action","part":3,"title":"Create separate wallets for savings, spending and exchange withdrawals","kicker":"Ch. 16 \u00b7 Checklist action, 1 hour","text":"","href":"/book/en/bitcoin-privacy/#action-bitcoin-privacy-separate-wallets"},{"type":"action","part":3,"title":"Label every coin you own with its origin","kicker":"Ch. 16 \u00b7 Checklist action, 1 hour","text":"","href":"/book/en/bitcoin-privacy/#action-bitcoin-privacy-label-coins"},{"type":"action","part":3,"title":"Connect your wallet to your own node, after chapter 18","kicker":"Ch. 16 \u00b7 Checklist action, weekend","text":"","href":"/book/en/bitcoin-privacy/#action-bitcoin-privacy-own-node"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Your checklist","kicker":"Ch. 16 \u00b7 Act","text":"Check that your wallet shows a fresh address for every paymentFind any address or balance you have posted publicly, and stop receiving on those addressesCreate separate wallets for savings, spending and exchange withdrawalsLabel every coin you own with its originConnect your wallet to your own node, after chapter 18","href":"/book/en/bitcoin-privacy/#your-checklist"},{"type":"chapter","part":3,"title":"Hardware wallets","kicker":"Chapter 17 \u00b7 Money and Bitcoin","text":"In brief \u00b7 Keys on a machine that is always online \u00b7 A small computer that only signs \u00b7 The maker you still have to trust \u00b7 Choose your device \u00b7 Step by step \u00b7 Mistakes to avoid \u00b7 Go further \u00b7 Sources \u00b7 Your checklist","href":"/book/en/hardware-wallet/"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"In brief","kicker":"Ch. 17 \u00b7 In brief","text":"A phone wallet suits pocket money, but savings call for something more solid. A hardware wallet is a small dedicated device that keeps your private keys offline and signs transactions inside itself, so that malware on your computer or phone does not get to see them. There are several good ones, with different trade-offs, and none of them is infallible. The device does not replace your seed backup (chapter 14), because it can break or be lost, and the backup is then what restores your funds.","href":"/book/en/hardware-wallet/#in-brief"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Keys on a machine that is always online","kicker":"Ch. 17 \u00b7 Understand","text":"Your phone is online all day. It runs dozens of apps, receives links from strangers, connects to public wifi and installs updates from many companies, and your computer is no better. Each of these is a door, and a wallet app on the same machine keeps its keys behind those doors. For the amount you would carry in a physical purse, that risk is acceptable, and the convenience is worth it. For savings, the calculation changes. Malware that reads a wallet file, replaces an address in the clipboard or records the screen exists and is sold as a ready-made product. A Bitcoin transaction cannot be reversed, and there is no support desk to call. Nobody needs to become a security expert to deal with this. The practical answer is to move the keys off these machines, onto a device built to keep them out of reach of this kind of software.","href":"/book/en/hardware-wallet/#keys-on-a-machine-that-is-always-online"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"A small computer that only signs","kicker":"Ch. 17 \u00b7 Understand","text":"A hardware wallet, more precisely called a signing device, is a tiny computer with a single job. It generates your seed, stores the private keys on its own chip, and is designed never to let them out. When you want to send bitcoin, the work is split in 2: An app on your computer or phone prepares the transaction. This app only knows your public information, so it can show your balance and build a payment, but it cannot spend. The transaction goes to the device, by cable, Bluetooth, QR code or memory card. The device shows the amount and the destination address on its own screen. You check, you confirm on the device, and it signs internally. Only the signature comes back. If your computer is infected, the malware sees a signed transaction and not a key. It can still show you a false address on the computer, which is why the screen of the device is the one you read. The seed remains the real wallet, and the device is only a safer tool to use it. You still need the backup from chapter 14, and the seed is only as good as the randomness the device used to create it. In August 2023, the \"Milk Sad\" disclosure showed that Libbitcoin Explorer, a software tool, had been creating seeds from only 32 bits of randomness instead of the 128 to 256 expected, and wallets made with it were emptied on 12 July 2023. No update could repair those seeds, and their owners had to create new ones and move their funds.","href":"/book/en/hardware-wallet/#a-small-computer-that-only-signs"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"The maker you still have to trust","kicker":"Ch. 17 \u00b7 Understand","text":"Moving the keys offline protects them from your computer and shifts your trust to the device's maker. The maker writes the firmware that creates your seed and signs your transactions, controls the supply chain, and usually runs the companion app, whose servers can see your addresses and your IP address. The Ledger leak (see the table) did not even come from the device, but from the customer list of the maker's shop. Open source firmware can be read by outsiders, and a reproducible build lets anyone confirm that the published code is what you install. A signature check proves the firmware comes from the maker, not that it is sound, and air-gapped signing limits what a computer can send to the device. A secure element resists someone holding the device, but such chips often come with a confidentiality agreement, which is why Ledger keeps part of its system closed. Some devices accept dice rolls as a source of randomness, which lets you add your own to the chip's. None of this removes the maker. Multi-signature across 2 makers (explained below) spreads that trust, whereas for a first device the maker's app and its automatic checks remain the safer path.","href":"/book/en/hardware-wallet/#the-maker-you-still-have-to-trust"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"BitBox02","kicker":"Ch. 17 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your device\u201d","text":"Maker: BitBox (Shift Crypto), Switzerland \u00b7 Strengths: Open source, simple, a Bitcoin-only edition exists. The BitBox02 Nova, announced in June 2025, adds Bluetooth and iPhone support \u00b7 Trade-offs: Not air-gapped. It connects by USB-C, or by Bluetooth on the Nova \u00b7 Trust in the maker, and what you can check: Open source firmware with reproducible builds, signature checked by the BitBoxApp. The secure chip is not trusted alone","href":"/book/en/hardware-wallet/#row-bitbox02"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"Trezor","kicker":"Ch. 17 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your device\u201d","text":"Maker: SatoshiLabs, Czech Republic \u00b7 Strengths: The open source pioneer, easy, widely supported \u00b7 Trade-offs: Older models connect by cable only, while the Safe 7, released in late 2025, adds Bluetooth. Supports many other coins unless you choose Bitcoin-only firmware \u00b7 Trust in the maker, and what you can check: Open source, reproducible firmware, checked by Trezor Suite. The Safe 7 adds an auditable secure element (TROPIC01)","href":"/book/en/hardware-wallet/#row-trezor"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"Blockstream Jade","kicker":"Ch. 17 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your device\u201d","text":"Maker: Blockstream \u00b7 Strengths: Affordable, open source, can work air-gapped with QR codes \u00b7 Trade-offs: Its PIN protection relies on a server (Blockstream's, or your own) \u00b7 Trust in the maker, and what you can check: Open source firmware. No secure element, so the PIN server is part of the trust unless you host it","href":"/book/en/hardware-wallet/#row-blockstream-jade"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"Foundation Passport","kicker":"Ch. 17 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your device\u201d","text":"Maker: Foundation Devices, USA \u00b7 Strengths: Open source, comfortable to use, signs by QR code \u00b7 Trade-offs: Among the more expensive. The recent Passport Prime also uses Bluetooth and does more than Bitcoin (security keys, 2FA codes) \u00b7 Trust in the maker, and what you can check: Open source firmware with reproducible builds, whose hash you can compare with the published one","href":"/book/en/hardware-wallet/#row-foundation-passport"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"SeedSigner","kicker":"Ch. 17 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your device\u201d","text":"Maker: Community project \u00b7 Strengths: Build it yourself from standard parts, open source, stateless (it stores no seed at all) \u00b7 Trade-offs: You assemble it, and you load your seed at every use. Not a first device \u00b7 Trust in the maker, and what you can check: No company. You verify the software signature yourself, builds are reproducible, randomness comes from dice or a photo","href":"/book/en/hardware-wallet/#row-seedsigner"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"Ledger","kicker":"Ch. 17 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your device\u201d","text":"Maker: Ledger, France \u00b7 Strengths: Popular, supports many coins, uses a secure chip \u00b7 Trade-offs: Firmware not fully open source. A breach of its customer database in 2020 exposed about 1 million email addresses, and the names, postal addresses and phone numbers of about 272,000 buyers \u00b7 Trust in the maker, and what you can check: Part of the system on the secure chip is closed, so outsiders cannot fully verify what the firmware does","href":"/book/en/hardware-wallet/#row-ledger"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Choose your device","kicker":"Ch. 17 \u00b7 Compare","text":"The devices below differ in openness, price, comfort and isolation. \"Air-gapped\" means the device never needs a connection to a computer, because transactions travel by QR code or memory card. Ranges change quickly, and this table is valid as of September 2026. Device Maker Strengths Trade-offs Trust in the maker, and what you can check BitBox02 BitBox (Shift Crypto), Switzerland Open source, simple, a Bitcoin-only edition exists. The BitBox02 Nova, announced in June 2025, adds Bluetooth and iPhone support Not air-gapped. It connects by USB-C, or by Bluetooth on the Nova Open source firmware with reproducible builds, signature checked by the BitBoxApp. The secure chip is not trusted alone Trezor SatoshiLabs, Czech Republic The open source pioneer, easy, widely supported Older models connect by cable only, while the Safe 7, released in late 2025, adds Bluetooth. Supports many other coins unless you choose Bitcoin-only firmware Open source, reproducible firmware, checked by Trezor Suite. The Safe 7 adds an auditable secure element (TROPIC01) Blockstream Jade Blockstream Affordable, open source, can work air-gapped with QR codes Its PIN protection relies on a server (Blockstream's, or your own) Open source firmware. No secure element, so the PIN server is part of the trust unless you host it Foundation Passport Foundation Devices, USA Open source, comfortable to use, signs by QR code Among the more expensive. The recent Passport Prime also uses Bluetooth and does more than Bitcoin (security keys, 2FA codes) Open source firmware with reproducible builds, whose hash you can compare with the published one SeedSigner Community project Build it yourself from standard parts, open source, stateless (it stores no seed at all) You assemble it, and you load your seed at every use. Not a first device No company. You verify the software signature yourself, builds are reproducible, randomness comes from dice or a photo Ledger Ledger, France Popular, supports many coins, uses a secure chip Firmware not fully open source. A breach of its customer database in 2020 exposed about 1 million email addresses, and the names, postal addresses and phone numbers of about 272,000 buyers Part of the system on the secure chip is closed, so outsiders cannot fully verify what the firmware does You also need companion software, either the maker's own app or an independent open source wallet such as Sparrow Wallet, Electrum (computer), Nunchuk (computer and phone) or BlueWallet (phone, mainly with devices that sign by QR code). With an independent wallet, you do not depend on the maker's app staying available. For larger amounts, the next level is multi-signature, a wallet that needs for example 2 devices out of 3 to sign. With devices from different makers, a flaw at 1 maker or the theft of 1 device is not enough to empty it. Back up the complete multisig configuration or output descriptor, including all cosigner xpubs, as well as enough signing keys (chapter 14). Those public keys cannot spend alone but reveal wallet activity. It is more work, so master 1 device first. A reasonable setup for most people is an open source device with a Bitcoin-only firmware, bought new and updated before use, with Sparrow or the maker's app. The choice of maker stays yours.","href":"/book/en/hardware-wallet/#choose-your-device"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 1: Buy directly from the maker or an official reseller","kicker":"Ch. 17 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Buy directly from the maker or an official reseller. Never second hand, never from a marketplace seller.","href":"/book/en/hardware-wallet/#step-1"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 2: Verify the device the way its maker describes","kicker":"Ch. 17 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Verify the device the way its maker describes. Some makers ship in a sealed or numbered security bag. Ledger, on the other hand, states that it uses no anti-tamper seals, which it considers easy to copy, and relies on a cryptographic check in its app. The apps of BitBox, Trezor and Ledger check the device and install signed firmware. With SeedSigner, you download the software yourself and verify its signature. In every case, install the latest firmware before creating a seed. This is the step that replaces trust in the delivery chain with verification, although it still rests on the maker's own signature.","href":"/book/en/hardware-wallet/#step-2"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 3: Generate a new seed on the device","kicker":"Ch. 17 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Generate a new seed on the device. The words must appear on the device screen, for the first time, in front of you. Never use words that came printed in the box.","href":"/book/en/hardware-wallet/#step-3"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 4: Back it up","kicker":"Ch. 17 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Back it up on paper or metal, as explained in chapter 14. Set a PIN where the device supports one; stateless devices such as SeedSigner work differently.","href":"/book/en/hardware-wallet/#step-4"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 5: Verify the receive address on the device screen, then send a tiny test amount","kicker":"Ch. 17 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Verify the receive address on the device screen, then send a tiny test amount. Do this before moving savings, not afterwards.","href":"/book/en/hardware-wallet/#step-5"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 6: Check recovery with only the tiny test balance at stake","kicker":"Ch. 17 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Check recovery with only the tiny test balance at stake. Follow the maker's backup-check or wipe-and-restore procedure on the device, with compatible backup format and any passphrase recorded. Confirm the same addresses and test balance before adding savings. Keep the backup out of ordinary phone or computer wallets. A stateless device is reloaded rather than wiped.","href":"/book/en/hardware-wallet/#step-6"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 7: Move the rest,","kicker":"Ch. 17 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Move the rest, in 1 or several transactions.","href":"/book/en/hardware-wallet/#step-7"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 8: Verify the receive address on the device screen, every time","kicker":"Ch. 17 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Verify the receive address on the device screen, every time. The computer screen can be manipulated, which is much harder to do with the screen of the device.","href":"/book/en/hardware-wallet/#step-8"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Step by step","kicker":"Ch. 17 \u00b7 Tutorial","text":"The order is broadly the same for every device, but the verification in step 2 differs between makers, so keep the maker's official guide open. Buy directly from the maker or an official reseller. Never second hand, never from a marketplace seller. Verify the device the way its maker describes. Some makers ship in a sealed or numbered security bag. Ledger, on the other hand, states that it uses no anti-tamper seals, which it considers easy to copy, and relies on a cryptographic check in its app. The apps of BitBox, Trezor and Ledger check the device and install signed firmware. With SeedSigner, you download the software yourself and verify its signature. In every case, install the latest firmware before creating a seed. This is the step that replaces trust in the delivery chain with verification, although it still rests on the maker's own signature. Generate a new seed on the device. The words must appear on the device screen, for the first time, in front of you. Never use words that came printed in the box. Back it up on paper or metal, as explained in chapter 14. Set a PIN where the device supports one; stateless devices such as SeedSigner work differently. Verify the receive address on the device screen, then send a tiny test amount. Do this before moving savings, not afterwards. Check recovery with only the tiny test balance at stake. Follow the maker's backup-check or wipe-and-restore procedure on the device, with compatible backup format and any passphrase recorded. Confirm the same addresses and test balance before adding savings. Keep the backup out of ordinary phone or computer wallets. A stateless device is reloaded rather than wiped. Move the rest, in 1 or several transactions. Verify the receive address on the device screen, every time. The computer screen can be manipulated, which is much harder to do with the screen of the device.","href":"/book/en/hardware-wallet/#step-by-step"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Mistakes to avoid","kicker":"Ch. 17 \u00b7 Watch out","text":"Buying from marketplaces. A device that passed through unknown hands may have been modified, and the small saving is not worth it. Accepting a seed card that arrives filled in. Treat it as a scam in every case, because the person who printed those words is waiting for your deposit. Typing the seed into a computer \"to update\". No real update, app or support agent needs your seed. For recovery, follow the maker's verified procedure and the physical device's instructions. Use on-device entry where supported; never enter words on a website or in response to an update or support request. Skipping the test restore. A wrong or missing word is better discovered now, with a test amount, than in 5 years with your savings. Using your home address for delivery without thinking. Customer lists leak, as the Ledger case showed, and the list then tells strangers where a bitcoin holder lives. Consider a pickup point or a parcel locker. Trusting emails that claim to be from the manufacturer. After the Ledger leak, buyers received convincing phishing, with fake security alerts and fake updates, and in 2021 some even received altered devices by post, presented as replacements. Go to the official website yourself, never through a link.","href":"/book/en/hardware-wallet/#mistakes-to-avoid"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Go further","kicker":"Ch. 17 \u00b7 Go further","text":"Independent checks of wallets and devices: WalletScrutiny (walletscrutiny.com) tests whether the published code matches what you actually install. Connecting a device to an independent wallet: the Sparrow Wallet documentation (sparrowwallet.com/docs). Step-by-step device guides: Bitcoiner.Guide (bitcoiner.guide). Next chapter: running your own node.","href":"/book/en/hardware-wallet/#go-further"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Sources","kicker":"Ch. 17 \u00b7 References","text":"How to use a wallet backup, Trezor, checked 22 October 2026. Multisig backup warning and descriptor export, Sparrow source code, checked 22 October 2026. Update: efforts to protect your data and prosecute the scammers, Ledger, 13 January 2021. Threat model: device genuineness, Ledger Donjon, page consulted in September 2026. Criminals are mailing altered Ledger devices to steal cryptocurrency, BleepingComputer, 16 June 2021. Milk Sad: technical write-up of CVE-2023-39910, Milk Sad research team, August 2023. BitBox Announces BitBox02 Nova, Bitcoin Magazine, 20 June 2025. Reproducible builds of the BitBox02 firmware, BitBox on GitHub, consulted in September 2026. Ledger is 95% open source, why not 100%?, Ledger, consulted in September 2026.","href":"/book/en/hardware-wallet/#sources"},{"type":"action","part":3,"title":"Decide what amount is \"savings\" for you and no longer belongs on a phone","kicker":"Ch. 17 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/hardware-wallet/#action-hardware-wallet-savings-amount"},{"type":"action","part":3,"title":"Choose a device from the table and order it from the maker, to a pickup point","kicker":"Ch. 17 \u00b7 Checklist action, 1 hour","text":"","href":"/book/en/hardware-wallet/#action-hardware-wallet-order-device"},{"type":"action","part":3,"title":"Check the device and its firmware the way the maker describes, create a new seed and a backup, then send a test amount","kicker":"Ch. 17 \u00b7 Checklist action, 1 hour","text":"","href":"/book/en/hardware-wallet/#action-hardware-wallet-set-up"},{"type":"action","part":3,"title":"With only a tiny test balance, follow the maker's hardware backup-check or restore procedure before adding savings","kicker":"Ch. 17 \u00b7 Checklist action, 1 hour","text":"","href":"/book/en/hardware-wallet/#action-hardware-wallet-test-restore"},{"type":"action","part":3,"title":"Verify the address on the device screen at every receive","kicker":"Ch. 17 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/hardware-wallet/#action-hardware-wallet-verify-address"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Your checklist","kicker":"Ch. 17 \u00b7 Act","text":"Decide what amount is \"savings\" for you and no longer belongs on a phoneChoose a device from the table and order it from the maker, to a pickup pointCheck the device and its firmware the way the maker describes, create a new seed and a backup, then send a test amountWith only a tiny test balance, follow the maker's hardware backup-check or restore procedure before adding savingsVerify the address on the device screen at every receive","href":"/book/en/hardware-wallet/#your-checklist"},{"type":"chapter","part":3,"title":"Running your own node","kicker":"Chapter 18 \u00b7 Money and Bitcoin","text":"In brief \u00b7 Your wallet asks a stranger about your money \u00b7 A computer that checks every rule itself \u00b7 What a node still asks you to trust \u00b7 Choose your route \u00b7 A word on Lightning nodes \u00b7 Step by step \u00b7 Mistakes to avoid \u00b7 Go further \u00b7 Sources \u00b7 Your checklist","href":"/book/en/node/"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"In brief","kicker":"Ch. 18 \u00b7 In brief","text":"Without your own node, your wallet asks someone else's server about your money. A node is your own copy of Bitcoin's rules and ledger, which checks every block and every transaction itself, so you no longer depend on somebody else's answer. It protects your privacy, and it strengthens the network. It needs a small always-on computer, a 2 TB SSD, a weekend of your time and then a few days of synchronisation during which the machine works alone. It is not a miner, and it earns nothing.","href":"/book/en/node/#in-brief"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Your wallet asks a stranger about your money","kicker":"Ch. 18 \u00b7 Understand","text":"When you open a wallet app, it needs to know your balance. It does not have the ledger, so it asks a server what happened on your addresses. That server belongs to the wallet's maker, or to an unknown volunteer. 2 things follow. First, the server can learn which addresses are yours, all of them, together with your IP address. The separate wallets and fresh addresses from chapter 16 are grouped again, in somebody's logs. Second, you have to believe the answer, because if the server is wrong, offline or dishonest, your wallet shows you what it was told. For small amounts this is a reasonable compromise, and most people start this way. It remains an odd arrangement for a system designed so that nobody has to be trusted. With your own node, the question stays inside your home network.","href":"/book/en/node/#your-wallet-asks-a-stranger-about-your-money"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"A computer that checks every rule itself","kicker":"Ch. 18 \u00b7 Understand","text":"A node is a computer running Bitcoin software, most often Bitcoin Core (other implementations exist, such as Bitcoin Knots). It downloads the whole ledger since the first block in 2009 and verifies every transaction against the rules, which means no coin spent twice, no coin created from nothing and never more than 21 million. It repeats this for every new block, about every 10 minutes, which is the meaning of \"don't trust, verify\". Your node accepts only what follows the rules it runs. When thousands of people do the same, a company, a miner or a government that wanted different rules would first have to convince them to install different software. A node is not a miner. It uses little electricity, earns nothing, and need not hold private keys. In the verification-only setup here, they stay in a separate wallet or signing device. Bitcoin Core can also run a wallet; if you use that feature, its wallet backups matter too. It needs a small computer that stays on, a normal home connection and a 2 TB SSD. As of September 2026 the ledger weighs about 770 GB and grows by 5 to 10 GB per month, and the index of an Electrum server adds from a few dozen to more than 100 GB, which would almost fill a 1 TB disk.","href":"/book/en/node/#a-computer-that-checks-every-rule-itself"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"What a node still asks you to trust","kicker":"Ch. 18 \u00b7 Understand","text":"The question that runs through this book, who the trusted third party is and whether you can check, ends here. For the state of your money, a node brings the answer down to nobody. It believes neither a wallet maker's server nor the nodes it connects to, because every block they send is checked against the rules and rejected if invalid, whoever sent it. You gain authenticity (the balance you see is the real one) and control over the rules you accept. 2 things remain. The first is the software, because a node enforces the rules of the program you installed, and a falsified copy would enforce somebody else's. Bitcoin Core answers with reproducible builds, in which several developers compile each release independently, obtain identical files and publish their signatures. By checking a few of them, you stop depending on the honesty of a single person. A node platform adds its maker to the list, for the system, the app store and the updates. The second is your own hardware and operating system, which you cannot fully audit. The price is a disk, a weekend and some maintenance, and for small amounts a public server stays a sensible choice.","href":"/book/en/node/#what-a-node-still-asks-you-to-trust"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"Bitcoin Core on your everyday computer","kicker":"Ch. 18 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your route\u201d","text":"What it is: The reference software, open source, from bitcoincore.org \u00b7 Strengths: Free, the simplest start, includes its own wallet, can run in pruned mode on a small disk \u00b7 Trade-offs: Not always on. Without pruned mode it fills most of a 1 TB disk \u00b7 Who you still trust: The Bitcoin Core developers, unless you check the signatures of several builders, and your everyday computer","href":"/book/en/node/#row-bitcoin-core-on-your-everyday-computer"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"Umbrel","kicker":"Ch. 18 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your route\u201d","text":"What it is: A home server system with an app store. Runs on your own hardware, or on a device the company sells \u00b7 Strengths: The most polished interface, one-click apps \u00b7 Trade-offs: Made by a single company. Free for personal use, but its licence is not open source (no commercial reuse) \u00b7 Who you still trust: Umbrel as well, for the system, its app store and its updates. The code can be read","href":"/book/en/node/#row-umbrel"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"Start9 (StartOS)","kicker":"Ch. 18 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your route\u201d","text":"What it is: An open source server system. Runs on your own hardware, or on a server the company sells \u00b7 Strengths: Strong focus on privacy and self-hosting, good documentation \u00b7 Trade-offs: Made by a single company. A little more to learn at the start \u00b7 Who you still trust: Start9 as well, for the system, its marketplace and its updates. The image comes with a checksum and a signature","href":"/book/en/node/#row-start9-startos"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"RaspiBlitz","kicker":"Ch. 18 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your route\u201d","text":"What it is: An open source community project for the Raspberry Pi \u00b7 Strengths: No company involved, strong Lightning tools \u00b7 Trade-offs: More technical, some work on the command line \u00b7 Who you still trust: Its volunteer maintainers. Build scripts are public, and the image carries the lead developer's PGP signature","href":"/book/en/node/#row-raspiblitz"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"MyNode","kicker":"Ch. 18 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your route\u201d","text":"What it is: A node system for small computers, with a free community edition and a paid premium edition \u00b7 Strengths: Simple web interface, many Bitcoin apps \u00b7 Trade-offs: Made by a small company, some features reserved for the paid edition \u00b7 Who you still trust: MyNode as well, for the system, the apps it packages and its updates","href":"/book/en/node/#row-mynode"},{"type":"tool","part":3,"title":"The manual route","kicker":"Ch. 18 \u00b7 Compared in \u201cChoose your route\u201d","text":"What it is: Debian or Ubuntu Linux, then Bitcoin Core, then an Electrum server (electrs or Fulcrum), by following a guide \u00b7 Strengths: You understand every part. Nothing hidden, fully open source \u00b7 Trade-offs: Slow, command line only. For learners who enjoy it \u00b7 Who you still trust: Only the software whose signatures you check yourself, the authors of your guide, and your hardware","href":"/book/en/node/#row-the-manual-route"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Choose your route","kicker":"Ch. 18 \u00b7 Compare","text":"All routes run the same Bitcoin software underneath, and they differ in how much is done for you. On an everyday computer, a pruned node is the lighter option. It verifies everything but keeps only the most recent blocks, so storage can be around 10 GB at a low pruning target, plus room for growth and other files. It still downloads and validates the full history; pruning does not remove that initial bandwidth requirement. However, most wallet server software needs the full ledger, which is why a dedicated node keeps it. Route What it is Strengths Trade-offs Who you still trust Bitcoin Core on your everyday computer The reference software, open source, from bitcoincore.org Free, the simplest start, includes its own wallet, can run in pruned mode on a small disk Not always on. Without pruned mode it fills most of a 1 TB disk The Bitcoin Core developers, unless you check the signatures of several builders, and your everyday computer Umbrel A home server system with an app store. Runs on your own hardware, or on a device the company sells The most polished interface, one-click apps Made by a single company. Free for personal use, but its licence is not open source (no commercial reuse) Umbrel as well, for the system, its app store and its updates. The code can be read Start9 (StartOS) An open source server system. Runs on your own hardware, or on a server the company sells Strong focus on privacy and self-hosting, good documentation Made by a single company. A little more to learn at the start Start9 as well, for the system, its marketplace and its updates. The image comes with a checksum and a signature RaspiBlitz An open source community project for the Raspberry Pi No company involved, strong Lightning tools More technical, some work on the command line Its volunteer maintainers. Build scripts are public, and the image carries the lead developer's PGP signature MyNode A node system for small computers, with a free community edition and a paid premium edition Simple web interface, many Bitcoin apps Made by a small company, some features reserved for the paid edition MyNode as well, for the system, the apps it packages and its updates The manual route Debian or Ubuntu Linux, then Bitcoin Core, then an Electrum server (electrs or Fulcrum), by following a guide You understand every part. Nothing hidden, fully open source Slow, command line only. For learners who enjoy it Only the software whose signatures you check yourself, the authors of your guide, and your hardware Umbrel and Start9 are also general home servers, which connects with chapter 10, since the same box can host your files and your passwords. A reasonable setup for most people is to try Bitcoin Core on your computer, in pruned mode, to see what a node is, then to move to a dedicated small computer with the platform whose trade-offs suit you.","href":"/book/en/node/#choose-your-route"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"A word on Lightning nodes","kicker":"Ch. 18 \u00b7 Understand","text":"The same platforms let you install a Lightning node (LND or Core Lightning) in 1 click. Treat it as a later project. A Lightning node holds real funds in channels, on a machine that is always online. Channels need their own backups, regular attention and an understanding of what happens if the disk fails, and restoring an old backup the wrong way can even cost you the funds in a channel. A Bitcoin node alone can break without any loss, because you only have to synchronise again, which is not true of a Lightning node. Learn with small amounts, and only after the rest of this chapter has become routine.","href":"/book/en/node/#a-word-on-lightning-nodes"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 1: Choose the hardware","kicker":"Ch. 18 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Choose the hardware. A used mini PC or a recent Raspberry Pi with 8 GB of memory, a 2 TB SSD from a known brand, and a network cable to your router.","href":"/book/en/node/#step-1"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 2: Install the platform","kicker":"Ch. 18 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Install the platform by following its official guide, from an image downloaded on its official website, and set a strong, unique password (chapter 2). Where the project publishes a checksum or a signature for that image, as Start9 and RaspiBlitz do, check it before you write the image to the disk. This is the step that replaces trust in the download with verification.","href":"/book/en/node/#step-2"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 3: Install the Bitcoin app and let it synchronise","kicker":"Ch. 18 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Install the Bitcoin app and let it synchronise. It takes a few days, and up to a week on slow hardware such as a Raspberry Pi. Leave it running when practical. If you need to stop, shut it down cleanly from the interface and let synchronisation resume later; do not cut the power.","href":"/book/en/node/#step-3"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 4: Install an Electrum server app","kicker":"Ch. 18 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Install an Electrum server app (electrs or Fulcrum). It builds an index so that wallets can ask your node questions, which takes several more hours, sometimes a full day.","href":"/book/en/node/#step-4"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 5: Connect your wallet","kicker":"Ch. 18 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Connect your wallet. In Sparrow, BlueWallet or Nunchuk, open the server or network settings and enter the address of your own Electrum server instead of the public one. From this step on, your wallet shows what your own node verified, and no longer what a third party told it. Zeus works differently, because it connects to a Lightning node, which is for later.","href":"/book/en/node/#step-5"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 6: Reach it from outside your home","kicker":"Ch. 18 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Reach it from outside your home over Tor or a private VPN (see chapter 10). The platforms provide a Tor address for this.","href":"/book/en/node/#step-6"},{"type":"step","part":3,"title":"Step 7: Keep it updated,","kicker":"Ch. 18 \u00b7 Step by step","text":"Keep it updated, and keep your seed backups separate from the node. A verification-only node needs no seed and should never see one. If you enable Bitcoin Core's wallet or install Lightning, protect and back up those wallets separately.","href":"/book/en/node/#step-7"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Step by step","kicker":"Ch. 18 \u00b7 Tutorial","text":"The example uses a platform with an app store, such as Umbrel or StartOS. RaspiBlitz and MyNode follow the same order. Your own work fits in a weekend, and the machine then needs a few days on its own. Choose the hardware. A used mini PC or a recent Raspberry Pi with 8 GB of memory, a 2 TB SSD from a known brand, and a network cable to your router. Install the platform by following its official guide, from an image downloaded on its official website, and set a strong, unique password (chapter 2). Where the project publishes a checksum or a signature for that image, as Start9 and RaspiBlitz do, check it before you write the image to the disk. This is the step that replaces trust in the download with verification. Install the Bitcoin app and let it synchronise. It takes a few days, and up to a week on slow hardware such as a Raspberry Pi. Leave it running when practical. If you need to stop, shut it down cleanly from the interface and let synchronisation resume later; do not cut the power. Install an Electrum server app (electrs or Fulcrum). It builds an index so that wallets can ask your node questions, which takes several more hours, sometimes a full day. Connect your wallet. In Sparrow, BlueWallet or Nunchuk, open the server or network settings and enter the address of your own Electrum server instead of the public one. From this step on, your wallet shows what your own node verified, and no longer what a third party told it. Zeus works differently, because it connects to a Lightning node, which is for later. Reach it from outside your home over Tor or a private VPN (see chapter 10). The platforms provide a Tor address for this. Keep it updated, and keep your seed backups separate from the node. A verification-only node needs no seed and should never see one. If you enable Bitcoin Core's wallet or install Lightning, protect and back up those wallets separately.","href":"/book/en/node/#step-by-step"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Mistakes to avoid","kicker":"Ch. 18 \u00b7 Watch out","text":"Using a cheap SD card or USB stick, or a disk that is too small. A node writes constantly, and SD cards and USB sticks tend to fail within months and can corrupt the data. Use a real SSD of 2 TB, because 1 TB leaves almost no margin as of September 2026, and the RaspiBolt guide now asks for 2 TB as well. No power protection. A sudden power cut can damage the database and cost you days of synchronisation. Always shut down from the interface, and consider a small battery backup (UPS). Thinking the node holds your coins. A verification-only node holds no spending keys. Bitcoin Core's optional wallet and a Lightning app do hold keys, and require their own backups. With keys kept separately, a failed verification node can be rebuilt without losing funds. Exposing it to the internet. Do not open ports on your router to reach the dashboard. Use Tor or a VPN. Giving up during the first synchronisation. It can look stuck for days while it verifies more than 15 years of transactions, which it only has to do once. Downloading software from the wrong place, or without verifying it. Get Bitcoin Core only from bitcoincore.org, and each platform from its official website. The download page of Bitcoin Core explains how to check the file against the published list of checksums, and how to check that this list carries the PGP signatures of several developers. On a platform, you rely on its maker to do this for the apps it installs, whereas on your own computer and on the manual route it is your job.","href":"/book/en/node/#mistakes-to-avoid"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Go further","kicker":"Ch. 18 \u00b7 Go further","text":"The basics: the download page of Bitcoin Core (bitcoincore.org), with the current requirements, pruned mode and the procedure to verify your download. The manual route: the RaspiBolt guide (raspibolt.org, version 4, updated in August 2026) and MiniBolt, a similar guide for ordinary PCs (minibolt.minibolt.info). Both are maintained as of September 2026. Connecting a wallet to your node: the Sparrow Wallet documentation (sparrowwallet.com/docs). Next: your checklist.","href":"/book/en/node/#go-further"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Sources","kicker":"Ch. 18 \u00b7 References","text":"Graceful shutdown, stop RPC, Bitcoin Core documentation, checked 22 October 2026. Blockchain size chart, Blockchain.com, consulted on 20 September 2026 (about 770 GB). Download Bitcoin Core, with requirements and verification of the download, Bitcoin Core project, consulted in September 2026. Signatures of the Bitcoin Core builders (guix.sigs), Bitcoin Core project on GitHub, updated at every release. Installing StartOS, with verification of the download, Start9 documentation, consulted in September 2026. RaspiBlitz FAQ, how to verify the SD card image, RaspiBlitz project, consulted in September 2026. RaspiBolt, version 4, RaspiBolt community guide, updated in August 2026. MiniBolt, MiniBolt community guide, consulted in September 2026.","href":"/book/en/node/#sources"},{"type":"action","part":3,"title":"Look in your wallet's network settings and see which server it uses today","kicker":"Ch. 18 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/node/#action-node-check-server"},{"type":"action","part":3,"title":"Verify the Bitcoin Core download, install it in pruned mode so it does not fill your disk, and watch it synchronise","kicker":"Ch. 18 \u00b7 Checklist action, 1 hour","text":"","href":"/book/en/node/#action-node-bitcoin-core-pruned"},{"type":"action","part":3,"title":"Choose a platform and the hardware for a dedicated node, with a 2 TB SSD","kicker":"Ch. 18 \u00b7 Checklist action, 1 hour","text":"","href":"/book/en/node/#action-node-choose-platform"},{"type":"action","part":3,"title":"Build the node, start the synchronisation (a few days, unattended) and add an Electrum server","kicker":"Ch. 18 \u00b7 Checklist action, weekend","text":"","href":"/book/en/node/#action-node-build-node"},{"type":"action","part":3,"title":"Point your wallet to your own node and remove the public server","kicker":"Ch. 18 \u00b7 Checklist action, 5 min","text":"","href":"/book/en/node/#action-node-connect-wallet"},{"type":"section","part":3,"title":"Your checklist","kicker":"Ch. 18 \u00b7 Act","text":"Look in your wallet's network settings and see which server it uses todayVerify the Bitcoin Core download, install it in pruned mode so it does not fill your disk, and watch it synchroniseChoose a platform and the hardware for a dedicated node, with a 2 TB SSDBuild the node, start the synchronisation (a few days, unattended) and add an Electrum serverPoint your wallet to your own node and remove the public server","href":"/book/en/node/#your-checklist"},{"type":"page","part":null,"title":"Glossary","kicker":"Book","text":"Privacy \u00b7 Autonomy \u00b7 Money and savings \u00b7 Bitcoin","href":"/book/en/glossary/"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"Encryption","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Privacy","text":"Scrambling information so that only someone holding the right key can read it.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-encryption"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"End-to-end encryption","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Privacy","text":"Encryption where only you and the person you talk to hold the keys. The service in the middle carries the message but cannot read it.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-end-to-end-encryption"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"Confidentiality","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Privacy","text":"Nobody except the 2 people talking can read the message.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-confidentiality"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"Authenticity","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Privacy","text":"The guarantee that the person at the other end is really who you think, and that the message was not altered on the way. Encryption alone does not give it.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-authenticity"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"Trusted third party","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Privacy","text":"An intermediary whose honesty the whole system depends on. A messenger that hands out its users' keys from its own directory is one, even when it encrypts end to end.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-trusted-third-party"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"Forward secrecy","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Privacy","text":"A design where a key stolen one day cannot unlock the messages exchanged before.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-forward-secrecy"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"Metadata","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Privacy","text":"Not what you say, but who you talk to, when, from where and how often. Often more revealing than the content itself.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-metadata"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"Open source","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Privacy","text":"Software with public source code and a licence allowing use, modification and redistribution. Public code alone is not enough; see the licence distinctions in chapters 10 and 11.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-open-source"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"Tracker","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Privacy","text":"A piece of code inside a site or an app that reports what you do to a third party, usually an advertising company.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-tracker"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"Fingerprinting","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Privacy","text":"Recognising your browser or device from its technical details (screen, fonts, settings), even without cookies.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-fingerprinting"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"Password manager","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Privacy","text":"An encrypted vault that creates and remembers a different strong password for every site. You remember only one.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-password-manager"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"Two-factor authentication (2FA)","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Privacy","text":"A second proof on top of your password: a code from an app, or a physical key.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-two-factor-authentication-2fa"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"SIM swap","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Privacy","text":"A fraud where someone convinces your operator to move your phone number to their SIM card, and then receives your SMS codes.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-sim-swap"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"Alias (email)","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Privacy","text":"A disposable address that forwards to your real mailbox. One per service, so a leak can be traced and switched off.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-alias-email"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"VPN","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Privacy","text":"A service that carries your traffic through its own server. It hides your activity from your internet provider and your address from the sites you visit, but the VPN company itself must be trusted.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-vpn"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"Tor","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Privacy","text":"A network that routes your traffic through several independent volunteers, so that none of them knows both who you are and what you visit.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-tor"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"Mixnet","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Privacy","text":"A network that, in addition to routing through several hops, mixes and delays traffic to hide timing patterns.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-mixnet"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"Threat model","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Privacy","text":"The honest answer to the question \"who do I want to protect myself from?\". It decides how far you need to go.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-threat-model"},{"type":"section","part":null,"title":"Privacy","kicker":"Glossary","text":"Encryption. Scrambling information so that only someone holding the right key can read it. End-to-end encryption. Encryption where only you and the person you talk to hold the keys. The service in the middle carries the message but cannot read it. Confidentiality. Nobody except the 2 people talking can read the message. Authenticity. The guarantee that the person at the other end is really who you think, and that the message was not altered on the way. Encryption alone does not give it. Trusted third party. An intermediary whose honesty the whole system depends on. A messenger that hands out its users' keys from its own directory is one, even when it encrypts end to end. Forward secrecy. A design where a key stolen one day cannot unlock the messages exchanged before. Metadata. Not what you say, but who you talk to, when, from where and how often. Often more revealing than the content itself. Open source. Software with public source code and a licence allowing use, modification and redistribution. Public code alone is not enough; see the licence distinctions in chapters 10 and 11. Tracker. A piece of code inside a site or an app that reports what you do to a third party, usually an advertising company. Fingerprinting. Recognising your browser or device from its technical details (screen, fonts, settings), even without cookies. Password manager. An encrypted vault that creates and remembers a different strong password for every site. You remember only one. Two-factor authentication (2FA). A second proof on top of your password: a code from an app, or a physical key. SIM swap. A fraud where someone convinces your operator to move your phone number to their SIM card, and then receives your SMS codes. Alias (email). A disposable address that forwards to your real mailbox. One per service, so a leak can be traced and switched off. VPN. A service that carries your traffic through its own server. It hides your activity from your internet provider and your address from the sites you visit, but the VPN company itself must be trusted. Tor. A network that routes your traffic through several independent volunteers, so that none of them knows both who you are and what you visit. Mixnet. A network that, in addition to routing through several hops, mixes and delays traffic to hide timing patterns. Threat model. The honest answer to the question \"who do I want to protect myself from?\". It decides how far you need to go.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#privacy"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"Private key","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Autonomy","text":"A long secret number that only you hold. It proves you are you, and whoever holds it controls the identity or the money behind it.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-private-key"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"Public key","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Autonomy","text":"The matching number you can share freely. Others use it to check your signature or to write to you.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-public-key"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"Passkey","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Autonomy","text":"A key pair used to log in to a website or app. The service keeps the public half. The private half stays in a device or may sync in encrypted form through a passkey provider; a hardware security key keeps it on that key.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-passkey"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"Decentralised","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Autonomy","text":"Run by many independent parties, with nobody able to switch it off, censor it or change the rules alone.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-decentralised"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"Self-hosting","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Autonomy","text":"Running a service on a machine you control instead of renting it from a company.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-self-hosting"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"Nostr","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Autonomy","text":"An open protocol for social media. Your identity is a key pair, and your posts travel through relays that anyone can run.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-nostr"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"npub and nsec","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Autonomy","text":"Your public key (npub, your public identity) and your secret key (nsec, never share it) on Nostr.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-npub-and-nsec"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"Relay","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Autonomy","text":"A simple server that stores and forwards Nostr posts. You can use several, and change at any time.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-relay"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"Zap","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Autonomy","text":"A small Lightning payment sent to someone on Nostr, as a tip or a thank you.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-zap"},{"type":"section","part":null,"title":"Autonomy","kicker":"Glossary","text":"Private key. A long secret number that only you hold. It proves you are you, and whoever holds it controls the identity or the money behind it. Public key. The matching number you can share freely. Others use it to check your signature or to write to you. Passkey. A key pair used to log in to a website or app. The service keeps the public half. The private half stays in a device or may sync in encrypted form through a passkey provider; a hardware security key keeps it on that key. Decentralised. Run by many independent parties, with nobody able to switch it off, censor it or change the rules alone. Self-hosting. Running a service on a machine you control instead of renting it from a company. Nostr. An open protocol for social media. Your identity is a key pair, and your posts travel through relays that anyone can run. npub and nsec. Your public key (npub, your public identity) and your secret key (nsec, never share it) on Nostr. Relay. A simple server that stores and forwards Nostr posts. You can use several, and change at any time. Zap. A small Lightning payment sent to someone on Nostr, as a tip or a thank you.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#autonomy"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"Inflation","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Money and savings","text":"The rise of prices over time, which is the same thing as money losing purchasing power. National currencies are designed to lose a little value every year.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-inflation"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"Counterparty risk","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Money and savings","text":"The risk that whoever holds an asset for you (a bank, a broker, an exchange) fails, or refuses to give it back.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-counterparty-risk"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"Self-custody","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Money and savings","text":"Holding an asset yourself, with no intermediary: coins in your hand, keys in your possession. No company can freeze it, lend it or lose it for you.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-self-custody"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"Custodial","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Money and savings","text":"A company holds the asset for you. Convenient, but what you own is a promise.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-custodial"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"Monero","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Money and savings","text":"A digital currency with a purpose similar to Bitcoin's, money you can hold yourself, where amounts, senders and receivers are hidden by default. Used by some advanced users as a private complement to Bitcoin. It is much less liquid and much less accepted, its much smaller network is easier for an outside entity to attack, as the block reorganisations of 2025 showed, and its development is more centralised (chapter 12).","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-monero"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"KYC (know your customer)","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Money and savings","text":"Mandatory identity checks by financial companies. They link your name to what you own in a database.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-kyc-know-your-customer"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"CBDC","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Money and savings","text":"Central bank digital currency, money issued digitally by a central bank. Who can see or control payments depends on its design and laws. For the proposed digital euro, the ECB describes offline privacy and limits on its own access to identifiable payment data; these are design commitments, not a deployed system.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-cbdc"},{"type":"section","part":null,"title":"Money and savings","kicker":"Glossary","text":"Inflation. The rise of prices over time, which is the same thing as money losing purchasing power. National currencies are designed to lose a little value every year. Counterparty risk. The risk that whoever holds an asset for you (a bank, a broker, an exchange) fails, or refuses to give it back. Self-custody. Holding an asset yourself, with no intermediary: coins in your hand, keys in your possession. No company can freeze it, lend it or lose it for you. Custodial. A company holds the asset for you. Convenient, but what you own is a promise. Monero. A digital currency with a purpose similar to Bitcoin's, money you can hold yourself, where amounts, senders and receivers are hidden by default. Used by some advanced users as a private complement to Bitcoin. It is much less liquid and much less accepted, its much smaller network is easier for an outside entity to attack, as the block reorganisations of 2025 showed, and its development is more centralised (chapter 12). KYC (know your customer). Mandatory identity checks by financial companies. They link your name to what you own in a database. CBDC. Central bank digital currency, money issued digitally by a central bank. Who can see or control payments depends on its design and laws. For the proposed digital euro, the ECB describes offline privacy and limits on its own access to identifiable payment data; these are design commitments, not a deployed system.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#money-and-savings"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"Bitcoin","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Bitcoin","text":"Digital money with no issuer and a fixed supply of 21 million. The rules are enforced by thousands of independent computers.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-bitcoin"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"Sat (satoshi)","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Bitcoin","text":"The smallest unit of bitcoin. One bitcoin is 100 million sats.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-sat-satoshi"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"Wallet","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Bitcoin","text":"An app or a device that holds your keys and signs your transactions. It does not hold coins: the coins are entries in the shared ledger.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-wallet"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"Seed phrase","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Bitcoin","text":"Recovery words for a wallet's keys, commonly 12 or 24 in BIP39, with other formats such as 20-word SLIP39 and native Electrum seeds. Recovery requires compatible software and any extra passphrase; labels, multisig configuration and Lightning state can need separate backups (chapter 14).","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-seed-phrase"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"Hardware wallet","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Bitcoin","text":"A small dedicated device that keeps your keys offline and signs transactions without exposing them to your computer or phone.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-hardware-wallet"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"Lightning","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Bitcoin","text":"A payment network built on top of Bitcoin: instant, nearly free, made for everyday amounts.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-lightning"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"On-chain","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Bitcoin","text":"A transaction recorded directly in the Bitcoin ledger. Confirmation time and fees vary. Additional confirmations reduce reversal risk; there is no absolute finality. Often suited to larger amounts.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-on-chain"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"Node","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Bitcoin","text":"A computer that keeps its own copy of the Bitcoin ledger and checks every transaction against the rules.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-node"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"Proof of work","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Bitcoin","text":"The energy miners must spend to add a block. It makes the history of transactions extremely costly to rewrite.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-proof-of-work"},{"type":"term","part":null,"title":"Peer-to-peer (P2P)","kicker":"Glossary \u00b7 Bitcoin","text":"Directly between 2 people, with no company holding the funds in the middle.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#term-peer-to-peer-p2p"},{"type":"section","part":null,"title":"Bitcoin","kicker":"Glossary","text":"Bitcoin. Digital money with no issuer and a fixed supply of 21 million. The rules are enforced by thousands of independent computers. Sat (satoshi). The smallest unit of bitcoin. One bitcoin is 100 million sats. Wallet. An app or a device that holds your keys and signs your transactions. It does not hold coins: the coins are entries in the shared ledger. Seed phrase. Recovery words for a wallet's keys, commonly 12 or 24 in BIP39, with other formats such as 20-word SLIP39 and native Electrum seeds. Recovery requires compatible software and any extra passphrase; labels, multisig configuration and Lightning state can need separate backups (chapter 14). Hardware wallet. A small dedicated device that keeps your keys offline and signs transactions without exposing them to your computer or phone. Lightning. A payment network built on top of Bitcoin: instant, nearly free, made for everyday amounts. On-chain. A transaction recorded directly in the Bitcoin ledger. Confirmation time and fees vary. Additional confirmations reduce reversal risk; there is no absolute finality. Often suited to larger amounts. Node. A computer that keeps its own copy of the Bitcoin ledger and checks every transaction against the rules. Proof of work. The energy miners must spend to add a block. It makes the history of transactions extremely costly to rewrite. Peer-to-peer (P2P). Directly between 2 people, with no company holding the funds in the middle.","href":"/book/en/glossary/#bitcoin"},{"type":"page","part":null,"title":"Contents","kicker":"Cover","text":"Your messages \u00b7 Your passwords and your accounts \u00b7 Your browser and your searches \u00b7 Your email and your aliases \u00b7 Your network \u00b7 Keys instead of accounts \u00b7 Nostr: an open network without a company in charge \u00b7 Replacing Big Tech services, one at a time \u00b7 Taking back control of your phone \u00b7 Hosting your own services \u00b7 AI without giving yourself away \u00b7 Where to keep the fruit of your work \u00b7 Your first wallet and your first sats \u00b7 Backing up your seed phrase \u00b7 Lightning and euros in everyday life \u00b7 Privacy in Bitcoin \u00b7 Hardware wallets \u00b7 Running your own node \u00b7 Introduction \u00b7 Proof of Privacy \u00b7 Proof of Autonomy \u00b7 Proof of Work \u00b7 Glossary","href":"/book/en/#contents"},{"type":"page","part":null,"title":"In this guide","kicker":"Cover","text":"18 chapters with 90 checklist actions, plus the introduction, the 3 part openings and the glossary. The text is in English. The same book is also available in Lithuanian, in this layout: Lietuvi\u0173.","href":"/book/en/#preview"},{"type":"page","part":null,"title":"Your progress","kicker":"Cover","text":"Actions done and chapters read, saved in this browser. Open a checklist. Clear progress.","href":"/book/en/#progress"},{"type":"page","part":null,"title":"Talk recordings","kicker":"Cover","text":"Why Your Internet Security Depends on Your Keys \u00b7 How the \u201cSoldier-Citizen\u201d Can Help Decentralize the World: Lessons from Russia\u2019s Invasion of Ukraine \u00b7 How Nym Works Compared to Other VPNs \u00b7 Rebuilding Social Media Without a Company in Charge using Nostr \u00b7 CBDCs, KYC, and the Fight for Monetary Privacy \u00b7 Why Centralized Money Fails: How Bitcoin Became an Alternative \u00b7 Bitcoin\u2019s 2 Flaws","href":"/book/en/#replays"},{"type":"page","part":null,"title":"About this edition","kicker":"Cover","text":"PROOF Conference, Cypher Baltics, licence, fork, independent editions.","href":"/book/en/#edition"},{"type":"page","part":null,"title":"Where to start?","kicker":"Orientation","text":"Autonomy is built gradually. Pick a concrete need, explore its chapter, then start with 1 action suited to your situation.","href":"/book/en/start/"},{"type":"page","part":null,"title":"How each chapter works","kicker":"Where to start?","text":"The need, options and limits, steps, sources. Checklist with effort tags, 1 action at a time.","href":"/book/en/start/#how-chapters-work"},{"type":"page","part":null,"title":"All actions","kicker":"Global checklist","text":"The 90 checklist actions of the 18 chapters on a single page, grouped by chapter, with the same ticks.","href":"/book/en/checklist/"},{"type":"page","part":null,"title":"Protect my accounts","kicker":"Where to start? \u00b7 Path","text":"Passwords and account security first, then your email and your aliases. Your passwords and your accounts \u00b7 Your email and your aliases","href":"/book/en/start/#path-accounts"},{"type":"page","part":null,"title":"Protect my conversations and my browsing","kicker":"Where to start? \u00b7 Path","text":"Your messengers, then your browser and your searches, then your network. Your messages \u00b7 Your browser and your searches \u00b7 Your network","href":"/book/en/start/#path-conversations"},{"type":"page","part":null,"title":"Control my data and my devices","kicker":"Where to start? \u00b7 Path","text":"Alternatives to Big Tech services, the settings of your phone, then hosting your own services. Replacing Big Tech services, one at a time \u00b7 Taking back control of your phone \u00b7 Hosting your own services \u00b7 Keys instead of accounts \u00b7 Nostr: an open network without a company in charge \u00b7 AI without giving yourself away","href":"/book/en/start/#path-data"},{"type":"page","part":null,"title":"Understand Bitcoin","kicker":"Where to start? \u00b7 Path","text":"Savings, a first wallet, backing up the seed phrase, everyday payments, privacy, hardware wallets and your own node. Where to keep the fruit of your work \u00b7 Your first wallet and your first sats \u00b7 Backing up your seed phrase \u00b7 Lightning and euros in everyday life \u00b7 Privacy in Bitcoin \u00b7 Hardware wallets \u00b7 Running your own node","href":"/book/en/start/#path-bitcoin"}];
